INSERT INTO interview_questions (bank_id, bank_version, question_id, sort_index, is_premium, family, tier, kind, tags, competency, leadership_tier, bodies, interviewer, grading)
VALUES ('1-system-design-mastery/0-fundamentals-of-system-design', '231d8cc57+37d905d9a', '1-system-design-mastery/0-fundamentals-of-system-design/12', 12, false, 'technical', 'senior', 'optimize', ARRAY['distributed-lock', 'lock-granularity', 'optimistic-concurrency']::text[], NULL, NULL, $iv${"typescript":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng TypeScript/NestJS. Khi checkout, decrementStock acquire CÙNG một Redis lock key toàn cục (lock:inventory) trước khi đụng vào database, bất kể SKU nào đang bị trừ kho — \"cho chắc\". Dưới tải checkout đồng thời trên nhiều SKU khác nhau, write throughput flatline thấp hơn hẳn mức N replica đáng lẽ phải đạt được, và thêm replica cũng không cải thiện được gì. Hãy optimize nó (nói ra, không có IDE).","givenCode":"```typescript\n// inventory.service.ts — một lock toàn cục canh mọi lần ghi của mọi SKU\n@Injectable()\nexport class InventoryService {\n    constructor(\n        private readonly redis: Redis,\n        private readonly repo: Repository<InventoryEntity>,\n    ) {}\n\n    async decrementStock(sku: string, qty: number): Promise<void> {\n        const acquired = await this.redis.set('lock:inventory', '1', 'NX', 'EX', 5)\n        if (!acquired) throw new ConflictException('inventory busy, retry')\n        try {\n            const row = await this.repo.findOneBy({ sku })\n            if (!row || row.stock < qty) throw new BadRequestException('out of stock')\n            row.stock -= qty\n            await this.repo.save(row)\n        } finally {\n            await this.redis.del('lock:inventory')\n        }\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in TypeScript/NestJS. On checkout, decrementStock acquires the SAME global Redis lock key (lock:inventory) before touching the database, no matter which product SKU is being decremented. Optimize it (spoken, no IDE).","givenCode":"```typescript\n// inventory.service.ts — one global lock guards every SKU's write\n@Injectable()\nexport class InventoryService {\n    constructor(\n        private readonly redis: Redis,\n        private readonly repo: Repository<InventoryEntity>,\n    ) {}\n\n    async decrementStock(sku: string, qty: number): Promise<void> {\n        const acquired = await this.redis.set('lock:inventory', '1', 'NX', 'EX', 5)\n        if (!acquired) throw new ConflictException('inventory busy, retry')\n        try {\n            const row = await this.repo.findOneBy({ sku })\n            if (!row || row.stock < qty) throw new BadRequestException('out of stock')\n            row.stock -= qty\n            await this.repo.save(row)\n        } finally {\n            await this.redis.del('lock:inventory')\n        }\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"java":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng Java/Spring Boot. Khi checkout, decrementStock acquire CÙNG một Redisson lock toàn cục (\"inventory-lock\") trước khi đụng vào database, bất kể SKU nào đang bị trừ kho — \"cho chắc\". Dưới tải checkout đồng thời trên nhiều SKU khác nhau, write throughput flatline thấp hơn hẳn mức N replica đáng lẽ phải đạt được, và thêm replica cũng không cải thiện được gì. Hãy optimize nó (nói ra, không có IDE).","givenCode":"```java\n// InventoryService.java — một Redisson lock toàn cục canh mọi lần ghi của mọi SKU\n@Service\npublic class InventoryService {\n    private final RedissonClient redisson;\n    private final InventoryRepository repo;\n\n    public InventoryService(RedissonClient redisson, InventoryRepository repo) {\n        this.redisson = redisson;\n        this.repo = repo;\n    }\n\n    public void decrementStock(String sku, int qty) throws InterruptedException {\n        RLock lock = redisson.getLock(\"inventory-lock\");\n        if (!lock.tryLock(2, 5, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"inventory busy, retry\");\n        }\n        try {\n            InventoryEntity row = repo.findBySku(sku)\n                    .orElseThrow(() -> new IllegalArgumentException(\"unknown sku\"));\n            if (row.getStock() < qty) throw new IllegalStateException(\"out of stock\");\n            row.setStock(row.getStock() - qty);\n            repo.save(row);\n        } finally {\n            lock.unlock();\n        }\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in Java/Spring Boot. On checkout, decrementStock acquires the SAME global Redisson lock (\"inventory-lock\") before touching the database, no matter which product SKU is being decremented. Optimize it (spoken, no IDE).","givenCode":"```java\n// InventoryService.java — one global Redisson lock guards every SKU's write\n@Service\npublic class InventoryService {\n    private final RedissonClient redisson;\n    private final InventoryRepository repo;\n\n    public InventoryService(RedissonClient redisson, InventoryRepository repo) {\n        this.redisson = redisson;\n        this.repo = repo;\n    }\n\n    public void decrementStock(String sku, int qty) throws InterruptedException {\n        RLock lock = redisson.getLock(\"inventory-lock\");\n        if (!lock.tryLock(2, 5, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {\n            throw new IllegalStateException(\"inventory busy, retry\");\n        }\n        try {\n            InventoryEntity row = repo.findBySku(sku)\n                    .orElseThrow(() -> new IllegalArgumentException(\"unknown sku\"));\n            if (row.getStock() < qty) throw new IllegalStateException(\"out of stock\");\n            row.setStock(row.getStock() - qty);\n            repo.save(row);\n        } finally {\n            lock.unlock();\n        }\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"csharp":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng C#/ASP.NET Core. Khi checkout, DecrementStockAsync acquire CÙNG một Redis lock key toàn cục (lock:inventory) trước khi đụng vào database, bất kể SKU nào đang bị trừ kho — \"cho chắc\". Dưới tải checkout đồng thời trên nhiều SKU khác nhau, write throughput flatline thấp hơn hẳn mức N replica đáng lẽ phải đạt được, và thêm replica cũng không cải thiện được gì. Hãy optimize nó (nói ra, không có IDE).","givenCode":"```csharp\n// InventoryService.cs — một lock Redis toàn cục canh mọi lần ghi của mọi SKU\npublic class InventoryService\n{\n    private readonly IDatabase _redis;\n    private readonly InventoryDbContext _db;\n\n    public InventoryService(IConnectionMultiplexer mux, InventoryDbContext db)\n    {\n        _redis = mux.GetDatabase();\n        _db = db;\n    }\n\n    public async Task DecrementStockAsync(string sku, int qty)\n    {\n        var acquired = await _redis.StringSetAsync(\"lock:inventory\", \"1\", TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5), When.NotExists);\n        if (!acquired) throw new InvalidOperationException(\"inventory busy, retry\");\n        try\n        {\n            var row = await _db.Inventory.FirstOrDefaultAsync(i => i.Sku == sku);\n            if (row is null || row.Stock < qty) throw new InvalidOperationException(\"out of stock\");\n            row.Stock -= qty;\n            await _db.SaveChangesAsync();\n        }\n        finally\n        {\n            await _redis.KeyDeleteAsync(\"lock:inventory\");\n        }\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in C#/ASP.NET Core. On checkout, DecrementStockAsync acquires the SAME global Redis lock key (lock:inventory) before touching the database, no matter which product SKU is being decremented. Optimize it (spoken, no IDE).","givenCode":"```csharp\n// InventoryService.cs — one global Redis lock guards every SKU's write\npublic class InventoryService\n{\n    private readonly IDatabase _redis;\n    private readonly InventoryDbContext _db;\n\n    public InventoryService(IConnectionMultiplexer mux, InventoryDbContext db)\n    {\n        _redis = mux.GetDatabase();\n        _db = db;\n    }\n\n    public async Task DecrementStockAsync(string sku, int qty)\n    {\n        var acquired = await _redis.StringSetAsync(\"lock:inventory\", \"1\", TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5), When.NotExists);\n        if (!acquired) throw new InvalidOperationException(\"inventory busy, retry\");\n        try\n        {\n            var row = await _db.Inventory.FirstOrDefaultAsync(i => i.Sku == sku);\n            if (row is null || row.Stock < qty) throw new InvalidOperationException(\"out of stock\");\n            row.Stock -= qty;\n            await _db.SaveChangesAsync();\n        }\n        finally\n        {\n            await _redis.KeyDeleteAsync(\"lock:inventory\");\n        }\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"go":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng Go. Khi checkout, DecrementStock acquire CÙNG một Redis lock key toàn cục (lock:inventory) trước khi đụng vào database, bất kể SKU nào đang bị trừ kho — \"cho chắc\". Dưới tải checkout đồng thời trên nhiều SKU khác nhau, write throughput flatline thấp hơn hẳn mức N replica đáng lẽ phải đạt được, và thêm replica cũng không cải thiện được gì. Hãy optimize nó (nói ra, không có IDE).","givenCode":"```go\n// inventory_service.go — một lock Redis toàn cục canh mọi lần ghi của mọi SKU\nfunc (s *InventoryService) DecrementStock(ctx context.Context, sku string, qty int) error {\n\tok, err := s.redis.SetNX(ctx, \"lock:inventory\", \"1\", 5*time.Second).Result()\n\tif err != nil || !ok {\n\t\treturn errors.New(\"inventory busy, retry\")\n\t}\n\tdefer s.redis.Del(ctx, \"lock:inventory\")\n\n\trow, err := s.repo.FindBySKU(ctx, sku)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tif row.Stock < qty {\n\t\treturn errors.New(\"out of stock\")\n\t}\n\trow.Stock -= qty\n\treturn s.repo.Save(ctx, row)\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in Go. On checkout, DecrementStock acquires the SAME global Redis lock key (lock:inventory) before touching the database, no matter which product SKU is being decremented. Optimize it (spoken, no IDE).","givenCode":"```go\n// inventory_service.go — one global Redis lock guards every SKU's write\nfunc (s *InventoryService) DecrementStock(ctx context.Context, sku string, qty int) error {\n\tok, err := s.redis.SetNX(ctx, \"lock:inventory\", \"1\", 5*time.Second).Result()\n\tif err != nil || !ok {\n\t\treturn errors.New(\"inventory busy, retry\")\n\t}\n\tdefer s.redis.Del(ctx, \"lock:inventory\")\n\n\trow, err := s.repo.FindBySKU(ctx, sku)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tif row.Stock < qty {\n\t\treturn errors.New(\"out of stock\")\n\t}\n\trow.Stock -= qty\n\treturn s.repo.Save(ctx, row)\n}\n```","diagram":null}}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"vi":{"followUps":["Nếu hai lần ghi đồng thời vẫn rơi vào đúng cùng một resource key ngay cả sau khi thu hẹp lock, bạn quyết định kết quả thế nào — vẫn giữ một lock theo key (giờ đã rẻ hơn nhiều), hay chuyển sang một vòng lặp retry kiểu compare-and-swap?","Bạn sẽ chọn số lượng lock shard (hay hash bucket) thế nào để contention luôn thấp mà không phải quản lý một lượng lock state cồng kềnh?"],"hints":["Tự hỏi: mọi lần ghi trong code này có thật sự cần bị loại trừ khỏi MỌI lần ghi khác, hay chỉ cần loại trừ khỏi những lần ghi vào đúng resource đó?","Hình dung N replica cùng ghi vào N dòng khác nhau, không liên quan gì nhau, tại cùng một thời điểm — lock có biết chúng không xung đột nhau không, hay vẫn nối tiếp hoá chúng?"]},"en":{"followUps":["If two concurrent writes still land on the exact SAME resource key even after narrowing the lock, how do you decide the outcome — keep a (now much cheaper) per-key lock, or move to a compare-and-swap retry loop instead?","How would you size the number of lock shards (or hash buckets) so contention stays low without ending up managing an unwieldy amount of lock state?"],"hints":["Ask: does every write in this code truly need to be excluded from EVERY other write, or only from writes to that exact same resource?","Picture N replicas all writing to N different, unrelated rows at the same instant — does the lock know they don't conflict, or does it serialize them anyway?"]}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"locales":{"vi":{"rubric":["[problemSolving] Chỉ ra rằng MỘT lock key duy nhất bọc quanh mọi lần ghi bất kể ghi vào resource nào, nên nó nối tiếp hoá (serialize) TẤT CẢ các ghi trên mọi replica thành một hàng chờ duy nhất — dù bản thân các replica đã stateless và scale ngang — đây là một shared bottleneck tự gây ra, không phải giới hạn của database hay mạng.","[technical] Đề xuất thu hẹp granularity của lock xuống đúng resource đang bị ghi (một lock theo key hoặc theo shard) để các ghi vào resource khác nhau không còn giành nhau, chỉ những ghi thật sự đồng thời vào CÙNG một resource mới cần nối tiếp hoá.","[technical] Đưa ra lựa chọn mạnh hơn: bỏ hẳn app-level lock ở những chỗ database đã tự cung cấp sẵn đúng bảo đảm cần thiết — một update có điều kiện/atomic (compare-and-swap qua một cột version, hoặc một `UPDATE ... WHERE` atomic tự kiểm tra điều kiện trong một câu lệnh) thay vì tự dựng mutual exclusion phía trên database.","[communication] Nói thẳng về trade-off: lock theo từng key (hoặc một update điều kiện atomic) đánh đổi thêm một chút phức tạp — thiết kế key/shard, hoặc xử lý retry khi \"affected rows = 0\" — để lấy lại việc ghi vào các resource không liên quan chạy song song trở lại.","[testing] Verify bằng một bài test tải đồng thời nhắm vào nhiều resource key KHÁC NHAU cùng lúc, xác nhận throughput giờ scale theo số replica thay vì flatline quanh mức mà một lock toàn cục có thể nối tiếp hoá."],"checklist":[{"text":"A single shared lock key like `lock:inventory` used for every SKU forces unrelated writes — say a checkout on SKU A and a checkout on SKU B — to contend for the same mutex, even though the two rows have nothing to do with each other.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"Because the lock's scope is not tied to the resource it protects, cluster-wide write throughput is capped by how fast one lock can be acquired and released; adding more stateless replicas cannot raise that ceiling since every replica still funnels through the same single key.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"Scoping the lock to the actual resource being written, for example `lock:inventory:${sku}`, lets checkouts on different SKUs proceed in parallel while only concurrent checkouts on the exact same SKU still serialize — which is the only case that genuinely needs mutual exclusion.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"A database can often provide the same exclusivity guarantee natively and more cheaply: a single atomic statement such as `UPDATE inventory SET stock = stock - :qty WHERE sku = :sku AND stock >= :qty` (via `repo.decrement()` or a raw query) decrements and checks the stock precondition in one round trip, removing the need for any application-level lock.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"The affected-row count from that atomic update doubles as the outcome signal: one row affected means the decrement succeeded, zero rows affected means the precondition failed (insufficient stock), so callers need a retry/handle-zero-rows path instead of a Redis lock/unlock round trip.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Either fix trades a bit of extra complexity for parallelism: per-key locking requires choosing a key or shard scheme, while the atomic-update approach pushes contention handling onto interpreting a zero-rows-affected result and retrying — but both remove cross-SKU serialization.","dimension":"communication","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"The bottleneck in this scenario is not the database or the network — the horizontally scaled, stateless replicas are already capable of higher throughput, but the single shared lock key alone turns all of their writes into a single-file queue.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"A concurrent load test hitting many different SKUs at once is the right way to confirm the fix worked: throughput should now scale up with replica count instead of flatlining near whatever one global lock could ever serialize.","dimension":"testing","critical":false,"scoreBand":10}],"keywords":["distributed lock","lock granularity","contention","optimistic concurrency","compare-and-swap"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"The problem is that every checkout uses the exact same lock key, `lock:inventory`, regardless of which SKU it's for — so a checkout on SKU A and one on SKU B end up contending for the same mutex even though they touch completely unrelated rows. Because the lock scope isn't tied to the resource being written, cluster-wide write throughput is capped by how fast one lock can be acquired and released, and adding more stateless replicas doesn't help since they all funnel through that same key. The fix is to scope the lock to the actual resource, something like `lock:inventory:${sku}`, so checkouts on different SKUs run in parallel and only concurrent checkouts on the same SKU actually serialize. Honestly, I'd go further and avoid the lock entirely — the database can give you the same exclusivity guarantee more cheaply with a single atomic statement, like `UPDATE inventory SET stock = stock - :qty WHERE sku = :sku AND stock >= :qty`. The affected-row count tells you the outcome directly: one row means the decrement succeeded, zero rows means the precondition failed, so you just need a retry or handle-zero-rows path instead of a Redis lock/unlock round trip. Both approaches trade a bit of extra complexity for parallelism — per-key locking needs a key or shard scheme, the atomic update pushes the complexity into interpreting a zero-rows result and retrying — but either removes the cross-SKU serialization. The actual bottleneck isn't the database or the network here; the replicas are already stateless and scaled out, it's the single shared lock key alone that's turning all their writes into a single-file queue. I'd validate by running a concurrent load test across many different SKUs and confirming throughput now scales with replica count instead of flatlining.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"The core issue is that the lock key `lock:inventory` is the same for every SKU, so unrelated checkouts — say SKU A and SKU B — end up serialized against each other even though they don't touch the same data. Since the lock isn't scoped to the resource, throughput is capped by how fast that one lock can cycle, and adding more replicas doesn't change that. The fix is to scope it per resource, like `lock:inventory:${sku}`, so different SKUs can proceed in parallel. Actually, I'd skip the app-level lock altogether and let the database handle it atomically — something like `UPDATE inventory SET stock = stock - :qty WHERE sku = :sku AND stock >= :qty` gives you the same exclusivity in one round trip. The real bottleneck isn't the DB or network — it's this one shared lock turning otherwise-parallel replicas into a single-file queue. I'd confirm the fix with a concurrent load test across many SKUs and check throughput scales with replica count.","sonnet":80,"haiku":80},{"level":3,"answer":"Since the lock isn't scoped to a specific SKU, all checkouts end up serialized through the same key, which caps overall throughput no matter how many replicas you have. I'd scope the lock per SKU, like `lock:inventory:${sku}`, so different products can be checked out in parallel. Alternatively, I'd just let the database handle it with an atomic update statement that decrements and checks stock in one go, instead of using a separate lock at all.","sonnet":45,"haiku":45},{"level":4,"answer":"I'd probably just make the database update atomic instead of doing a separate lock step — like combining the read and write into a single query. That should help avoid some of the race conditions without needing to manage a lock at all.","sonnet":15,"haiku":15},{"level":5,"answer":"I think the issue might just be that Redis is slow or maybe not configured with enough connections. I'd check the Redis connection pool size and maybe increase the timeout on the lock acquisition, that's probably causing the checkouts to feel slow under load.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]},"en":{"rubric":["[problemSolving] Identifies that ONE global lock key wraps every write regardless of which resource it touches, so it serializes ALL writes across every replica into a single line even though the replicas themselves are stateless and horizontally scaled — a self-inflicted shared bottleneck, not a database or network limit.","[technical] Proposes narrowing the lock's granularity to the resource actually being written (a per-key or per-shard lock) so writes to different resources stop contending, and only genuinely concurrent writes to the SAME resource still serialize.","[technical] Offers the stronger alternative: drop the app-level lock entirely where the database can already provide the guarantee natively — a conditional/atomic update (compare-and-swap via a version column, or an atomic `UPDATE ... WHERE` that checks the precondition in one statement) instead of hand-rolling mutual exclusion above the database.","[communication] States the trade-off honestly: per-key locking (or an atomic conditional update) trades a little extra complexity — key/shard design, or handling a \"zero rows affected\" retry — for writes to unrelated resources running in parallel again.","[testing] Verifies with a concurrent load test hitting many DIFFERENT resource keys at once, confirming throughput now scales with replica count instead of flatlining near what one global lock can ever serialize."],"checklist":[{"text":"A single shared lock key like `lock:inventory` used for every SKU forces unrelated writes — say a checkout on SKU A and a checkout on SKU B — to contend for the same mutex, even though the two rows have nothing to do with each other.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"Because the lock's scope is not tied to the resource it protects, cluster-wide write throughput is capped by how fast one lock can be acquired and released; adding more stateless replicas cannot raise that ceiling since every replica still funnels through the same single key.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"Scoping the lock to the actual resource being written, for example `lock:inventory:${sku}`, lets checkouts on different SKUs proceed in parallel while only concurrent checkouts on the exact same SKU still serialize — which is the only case that genuinely needs mutual exclusion.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"A database can often provide the same exclusivity guarantee natively and more cheaply: a single atomic statement such as `UPDATE inventory SET stock = stock - :qty WHERE sku = :sku AND stock >= :qty` (via `repo.decrement()` or a raw query) decrements and checks the stock precondition in one round trip, removing the need for any application-level lock.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"The affected-row count from that atomic update doubles as the outcome signal: one row affected means the decrement succeeded, zero rows affected means the precondition failed (insufficient stock), so callers need a retry/handle-zero-rows path instead of a Redis lock/unlock round trip.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Either fix trades a bit of extra complexity for parallelism: per-key locking requires choosing a key or shard scheme, while the atomic-update approach pushes contention handling onto interpreting a zero-rows-affected result and retrying — but both remove cross-SKU serialization.","dimension":"communication","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"The bottleneck in this scenario is not the database or the network — the horizontally scaled, stateless replicas are already capable of higher throughput, but the single shared lock key alone turns all of their writes into a single-file queue.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"A concurrent load test hitting many different SKUs at once is the right way to confirm the fix worked: throughput should now scale up with replica count instead of flatlining near whatever one global lock could ever serialize.","dimension":"testing","critical":false,"scoreBand":10}],"keywords":["distributed lock","lock granularity","contention","optimistic concurrency","compare-and-swap"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"The problem is that every checkout uses the exact same lock key, `lock:inventory`, regardless of which SKU it's for — so a checkout on SKU A and one on SKU B end up contending for the same mutex even though they touch completely unrelated rows. Because the lock scope isn't tied to the resource being written, cluster-wide write throughput is capped by how fast one lock can be acquired and released, and adding more stateless replicas doesn't help since they all funnel through that same key. The fix is to scope the lock to the actual resource, something like `lock:inventory:${sku}`, so checkouts on different SKUs run in parallel and only concurrent checkouts on the same SKU actually serialize. Honestly, I'd go further and avoid the lock entirely — the database can give you the same exclusivity guarantee more cheaply with a single atomic statement, like `UPDATE inventory SET stock = stock - :qty WHERE sku = :sku AND stock >= :qty`. The affected-row count tells you the outcome directly: one row means the decrement succeeded, zero rows means the precondition failed, so you just need a retry or handle-zero-rows path instead of a Redis lock/unlock round trip. Both approaches trade a bit of extra complexity for parallelism — per-key locking needs a key or shard scheme, the atomic update pushes the complexity into interpreting a zero-rows result and retrying — but either removes the cross-SKU serialization. The actual bottleneck isn't the database or the network here; the replicas are already stateless and scaled out, it's the single shared lock key alone that's turning all their writes into a single-file queue. I'd validate by running a concurrent load test across many different SKUs and confirming throughput now scales with replica count instead of flatlining.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"The core issue is that the lock key `lock:inventory` is the same for every SKU, so unrelated checkouts — say SKU A and SKU B — end up serialized against each other even though they don't touch the same data. Since the lock isn't scoped to the resource, throughput is capped by how fast that one lock can cycle, and adding more replicas doesn't change that. The fix is to scope it per resource, like `lock:inventory:${sku}`, so different SKUs can proceed in parallel. Actually, I'd skip the app-level lock altogether and let the database handle it atomically — something like `UPDATE inventory SET stock = stock - :qty WHERE sku = :sku AND stock >= :qty` gives you the same exclusivity in one round trip. The real bottleneck isn't the DB or network — it's this one shared lock turning otherwise-parallel replicas into a single-file queue. I'd confirm the fix with a concurrent load test across many SKUs and check throughput scales with replica count.","sonnet":80,"haiku":80},{"level":3,"answer":"Since the lock isn't scoped to a specific SKU, all checkouts end up serialized through the same key, which caps overall throughput no matter how many replicas you have. I'd scope the lock per SKU, like `lock:inventory:${sku}`, so different products can be checked out in parallel. Alternatively, I'd just let the database handle it with an atomic update statement that decrements and checks stock in one go, instead of using a separate lock at all.","sonnet":45,"haiku":45},{"level":4,"answer":"I'd probably just make the database update atomic instead of doing a separate lock step — like combining the read and write into a single query. That should help avoid some of the race conditions without needing to manage a lock at all.","sonnet":15,"haiku":15},{"level":5,"answer":"I think the issue might just be that Redis is slow or maybe not configured with enough connections. I'd check the Redis connection pool size and maybe increase the timeout on the lock acquisition, that's probably causing the checkouts to feel slow under load.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]}},"ideal":{"typescript":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nBottleneck không nằm ở database hay mạng — nó nằm ở chính cái lock. `lock:inventory` là MỘT key duy nhất dùng chung cho mọi SKU, nên một checkout trên SKU A và một checkout trên SKU B — hai dòng hoàn toàn không liên quan — vẫn phải giành nhau đúng một mutex; service đã stateless và scale ngang, nhưng đúng một Redis key này biến mọi lần ghi, trên toàn cluster, thành một hàng chờ nối đuôi nhau — đó chính là lý do thêm replica không giúp được gì. Cách sửa đầu tiên: thu hẹp phạm vi lock xuống đúng resource đang bị đụng vào — `lock:inventory:${sku}` thay vì `lock:inventory` — để ghi vào SKU A và SKU B không còn giành nhau nữa; chỉ hai checkout cùng đụng vào CÙNG một SKU tại cùng thời điểm mới còn nối tiếp hoá, đúng là trường hợp duy nhất thật sự cần loại trừ lẫn nhau. Tốt hơn nữa: bỏ hẳn app-level lock cho trường hợp này, vì database đã có thể tự cho đúng bảo đảm cần thiết trong một câu lệnh atomic — `UPDATE inventory SET stock = stock - :qty WHERE sku = :sku AND stock >= :qty` qua `repo.decrement()`/một raw query, rồi kiểm tra số dòng bị ảnh hưởng: 1 nghĩa là trừ kho thành công một cách atomic, 0 nghĩa là \"không đủ hàng\" — không cần round-trip tới Redis hay bất kỳ lock nào cả. Nói thẳng cái giá dù chọn cách nào: lock theo từng SKU vẫn cần một retry/backoff ngắn khi thật sự có contention trên cùng SKU; cách update atomic đẩy đúng phần xử lý đó sang \"0 dòng bị ảnh hưởng → hết hàng hoặc retry\". Verify bằng một bài test tải đồng thời nhắm vào nhiều SKU khác nhau — throughput giờ phải scale theo số replica thay vì flatline quanh mức mà một lock toàn cục có thể nối tiếp hoá.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe bottleneck isn't the database or the network — it's the lock itself. `lock:inventory` is ONE key shared by every SKU, so a checkout on SKU A and a checkout on SKU B — two completely unrelated rows — still fight over the exact same mutex; the service is stateless and horizontally scaled, but this one Redis key turns every write, cluster-wide, into a single-file line, which is exactly why adding replicas doesn't help. First fix: narrow the lock's scope to the resource actually being touched — `lock:inventory:${sku}` instead of `lock:inventory` — so SKU A and SKU B's writes stop contending; only two checkouts hitting the SAME SKU at the same instant still serialize, which is the only case that genuinely needs exclusion. Better still: drop the app-level lock for this case entirely, because the database can already give the exact guarantee needed in one atomic statement — `UPDATE inventory SET stock = stock - :qty WHERE sku = :sku AND stock >= :qty` via `repo.decrement()`/a raw query, then check the affected-row count: 1 means the decrement succeeded atomically, 0 means \"insufficient stock\" — no Redis round-trip or lock required at all. Name the cost either way: per-SKU locking still needs a short retry/backoff on genuine same-SKU contention; the atomic-update path pushes that same handling onto \"0 rows affected → out of stock or retry\". Verify with a concurrent load test hitting many different SKUs at once — throughput should now scale with replica count instead of flatlining at roughly what one global lock could ever serialize."},"java":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nBottleneck không nằm ở database hay mạng — nó nằm ở chính cái lock. `\"inventory-lock\"` là MỘT key Redisson duy nhất dùng chung cho mọi SKU, nên một checkout trên SKU A và một checkout trên SKU B — hai dòng hoàn toàn không liên quan — vẫn phải giành nhau đúng một `RLock`; service đã stateless và scale ngang, nhưng đúng một lock key này biến mọi lần ghi, trên toàn cluster, thành một hàng chờ nối đuôi nhau — đó chính là lý do thêm replica không giúp được gì. Cách sửa đầu tiên: thu hẹp phạm vi lock xuống đúng resource đang bị đụng vào — `redisson.getLock(\"inventory-lock:\" + sku)` thay vì dùng chung một tên — để ghi vào SKU A và SKU B không còn giành nhau nữa; chỉ hai checkout cùng đụng vào CÙNG một SKU tại cùng thời điểm mới còn nối tiếp hoá, đúng là trường hợp duy nhất thật sự cần loại trừ lẫn nhau. Tốt hơn nữa: bỏ hẳn app-level lock cho trường hợp này, vì database đã có thể tự cho đúng bảo đảm cần thiết trong một câu lệnh atomic — hoặc optimistic locking qua một cột `@Version` của JPA (Hibernate ném `OptimisticLockException` khi ghi bị stale, bắt lại và retry), hoặc một native `@Modifying` query `UPDATE inventory SET stock = stock - :qty WHERE sku = :sku AND stock >= :qty`, kiểm tra số dòng trả về: 1 nghĩa là trừ kho thành công một cách atomic, 0 nghĩa là \"không đủ hàng\" — không cần round-trip tới Redisson hay bất kỳ lock nào cả. Nói thẳng cái giá dù chọn cách nào: lock theo từng SKU vẫn cần một retry/backoff ngắn khi thật sự có contention trên cùng SKU; cách update atomic đẩy đúng phần xử lý đó sang \"0 dòng bị ảnh hưởng → hết hàng hoặc retry\". Verify bằng một bài test tải đồng thời nhắm vào nhiều SKU khác nhau — throughput giờ phải scale theo số replica thay vì flatline quanh mức mà một lock toàn cục có thể nối tiếp hoá.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe bottleneck isn't the database or the network — it's the lock itself. `\"inventory-lock\"` is ONE Redisson key shared by every SKU, so a checkout on SKU A and a checkout on SKU B — two completely unrelated rows — still fight over the exact same `RLock`; the service is stateless and horizontally scaled, but this one lock key turns every write, cluster-wide, into a single-file line, which is exactly why adding replicas doesn't help. First fix: narrow the lock's scope to the resource actually being touched — `redisson.getLock(\"inventory-lock:\" + sku)` instead of one shared name — so SKU A and SKU B's writes stop contending; only two checkouts hitting the SAME SKU at the same instant still serialize, which is the only case that genuinely needs exclusion. Better still: drop the app-level lock for this case entirely, because the database can already give the exact guarantee needed in one atomic statement — either optimistic locking via a JPA `@Version` column (Hibernate throws `OptimisticLockException` on a stale write, caught and retried) or a native `@Modifying` query `UPDATE inventory SET stock = stock - :qty WHERE sku = :sku AND stock >= :qty`, checking the returned row count: 1 means the decrement succeeded atomically, 0 means \"insufficient stock\" — no Redisson round-trip or lock required at all. Name the cost either way: per-SKU locking still needs a short retry/backoff on genuine same-SKU contention; the atomic-update path pushes that same handling onto \"0 rows affected → out of stock or retry\". Verify with a concurrent load test hitting many different SKUs at once — throughput should now scale with replica count instead of flatlining at roughly what one global lock could ever serialize."},"csharp":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nBottleneck không nằm ở database hay mạng — nó nằm ở chính cái lock. `\"lock:inventory\"` là MỘT key duy nhất dùng chung cho mọi SKU, nên một checkout trên SKU A và một checkout trên SKU B — hai dòng hoàn toàn không liên quan — vẫn phải giành nhau đúng một mutex; service đã stateless và scale ngang, nhưng đúng một Redis key này biến mọi lần ghi, trên toàn cluster, thành một hàng chờ nối đuôi nhau — đó chính là lý do thêm replica không giúp được gì. Cách sửa đầu tiên: thu hẹp phạm vi lock xuống đúng resource đang bị đụng vào — `$\"lock:inventory:{sku}\"` thay vì dùng chung một key — để ghi vào SKU A và SKU B không còn giành nhau nữa; chỉ hai checkout cùng đụng vào CÙNG một SKU tại cùng thời điểm mới còn nối tiếp hoá, đúng là trường hợp duy nhất thật sự cần loại trừ lẫn nhau. Tốt hơn nữa: bỏ hẳn app-level lock cho trường hợp này, vì database đã có thể tự cho đúng bảo đảm cần thiết trong một câu lệnh atomic — `ExecuteUpdateAsync` của EF Core (hoặc một raw query có tham số) phát ra `UPDATE Inventory SET Stock = Stock - {qty} WHERE Sku = {sku} AND Stock >= {qty}`, rồi kiểm tra số dòng bị ảnh hưởng trả về: 1 nghĩa là trừ kho thành công một cách atomic, 0 nghĩa là \"không đủ hàng\" — không cần round-trip tới Redis hay bất kỳ lock nào cả; một concurrency token `[Timestamp]`/`RowVersion` cộng với bắt và retry `DbUpdateConcurrencyException` là hướng optimistic locking tương đương. Nói thẳng cái giá dù chọn cách nào: lock theo từng SKU vẫn cần một retry/backoff ngắn khi thật sự có contention trên cùng SKU; cách update atomic đẩy đúng phần xử lý đó sang \"0 dòng bị ảnh hưởng → hết hàng hoặc retry\". Verify bằng một bài test tải đồng thời nhắm vào nhiều SKU khác nhau — throughput giờ phải scale theo số replica thay vì flatline quanh mức mà một lock toàn cục có thể nối tiếp hoá.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe bottleneck isn't the database or the network — it's the lock itself. `\"lock:inventory\"` is ONE key shared by every SKU, so a checkout on SKU A and a checkout on SKU B — two completely unrelated rows — still fight over the exact same mutex; the service is stateless and horizontally scaled, but this one Redis key turns every write, cluster-wide, into a single-file line, which is exactly why adding replicas doesn't help. First fix: narrow the lock's scope to the resource actually being touched — `$\"lock:inventory:{sku}\"` instead of one shared key — so SKU A and SKU B's writes stop contending; only two checkouts hitting the SAME SKU at the same instant still serialize, which is the only case that genuinely needs exclusion. Better still: drop the app-level lock for this case entirely, because the database can already give the exact guarantee needed in one atomic statement — EF Core's `ExecuteUpdateAsync` (or a raw parameterized query) issuing `UPDATE Inventory SET Stock = Stock - {qty} WHERE Sku = {sku} AND Stock >= {qty}`, then checking the returned rows-affected count: 1 means the decrement succeeded atomically, 0 means \"insufficient stock\" — no Redis round-trip or lock required at all; a `[Timestamp]`/`RowVersion` concurrency token plus a catch-and-retry on `DbUpdateConcurrencyException` is the equivalent optimistic-locking route. Name the cost either way: per-SKU locking still needs a short retry/backoff on genuine same-SKU contention; the atomic-update path pushes that same handling onto \"0 rows affected → out of stock or retry\". Verify with a concurrent load test hitting many different SKUs at once — throughput should now scale with replica count instead of flatlining at roughly what one global lock could ever serialize."},"go":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nBottleneck không nằm ở database hay mạng — nó nằm ở chính cái lock. `\"lock:inventory\"` là MỘT key duy nhất dùng chung cho mọi SKU, nên một checkout trên SKU A và một checkout trên SKU B — hai dòng hoàn toàn không liên quan — vẫn phải giành nhau đúng một `SetNX`; service đã stateless và scale ngang, nhưng đúng một Redis key này biến mọi lần ghi, trên toàn cluster, thành một hàng chờ nối đuôi nhau — đó chính là lý do thêm replica không giúp được gì. Cách sửa đầu tiên: thu hẹp phạm vi lock xuống đúng resource đang bị đụng vào — `fmt.Sprintf(\"lock:inventory:%s\", sku)` thay vì dùng chung một key — để ghi vào SKU A và SKU B không còn giành nhau nữa; chỉ hai checkout cùng đụng vào CÙNG một SKU tại cùng thời điểm mới còn nối tiếp hoá, đúng là trường hợp duy nhất thật sự cần loại trừ lẫn nhau. Tốt hơn nữa: bỏ hẳn app-level lock cho trường hợp này, vì database đã có thể tự cho đúng bảo đảm cần thiết trong một câu lệnh atomic — một raw query có tham số qua `database/sql`/`sqlx`, `UPDATE inventory SET stock = stock - $1 WHERE sku = $2 AND stock >= $1`, rồi kiểm tra `RowsAffected()`: 1 nghĩa là trừ kho thành công một cách atomic, 0 nghĩa là \"không đủ hàng\" — không cần round-trip tới Redis hay bất kỳ lock nào cả. Nói thẳng cái giá dù chọn cách nào: lock theo từng SKU vẫn cần một retry/backoff ngắn khi thật sự có contention trên cùng SKU; cách update atomic đẩy đúng phần xử lý đó sang \"0 dòng bị ảnh hưởng → hết hàng hoặc retry\". Verify bằng một bài test tải đồng thời nhắm vào nhiều SKU khác nhau — throughput giờ phải scale theo số replica thay vì flatline quanh mức mà một lock toàn cục có thể nối tiếp hoá.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe bottleneck isn't the database or the network — it's the lock itself. `\"lock:inventory\"` is ONE key shared by every SKU, so a checkout on SKU A and a checkout on SKU B — two completely unrelated rows — still fight over the exact same `SetNX`; the service is stateless and horizontally scaled, but this one Redis key turns every write, cluster-wide, into a single-file line, which is exactly why adding replicas doesn't help. First fix: narrow the lock's scope to the resource actually being touched — `fmt.Sprintf(\"lock:inventory:%s\", sku)` instead of one shared key — so SKU A and SKU B's writes stop contending; only two checkouts hitting the SAME SKU at the same instant still serialize, which is the only case that genuinely needs exclusion. Better still: drop the app-level lock for this case entirely, because the database can already give the exact guarantee needed in one atomic statement — a raw parameterized query via `database/sql`/`sqlx`, `UPDATE inventory SET stock = stock - $1 WHERE sku = $2 AND stock >= $1`, then checking `RowsAffected()`: 1 means the decrement succeeded atomically, 0 means \"insufficient stock\" — no Redis round-trip or lock required at all. Name the cost either way: per-SKU locking still needs a short retry/backoff on genuine same-SKU contention; the atomic-update path pushes that same handling onto \"0 rows affected → out of stock or retry\". Verify with a concurrent load test hitting many different SKUs at once — throughput should now scale with replica count instead of flatlining at roughly what one global lock could ever serialize."}}}$iv$::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (bank_id, bank_version, question_id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO interview_questions (bank_id, bank_version, question_id, sort_index, is_premium, family, tier, kind, tags, competency, leadership_tier, bodies, interviewer, grading)
VALUES ('1-system-design-mastery/0-fundamentals-of-system-design', '231d8cc57+37d905d9a', '1-system-design-mastery/0-fundamentals-of-system-design/13', 13, false, 'technical', 'middle', 'design-lite', ARRAY['statelessness', 'session-store', 'sticky-session']::text[], NULL, NULL, $iv${"agnostic":{"vi":{"prompt":"Service của bạn đang lưu session người dùng ngay trong RAM của process. Hệ quả là Load Balancer buộc phải ghim mọi request của một user về đúng node đang giữ session đó. Hãy phác thảo một session-store contract nhỏ để đẩy state đó ra khỏi process, biến service trở lại stateless: nêu tên các operation của contract, session sẽ sống ở đâu, và bảo vệ vì sao đây là điều kiện tiên quyết để scale out.","givenCode":null,"diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"Your service holds user sessions right in the process's local RAM. The consequence is that the Load Balancer is forced to pin every request from a given user to the exact node holding that session. Sketch a small session-store contract to push that state out of the process, making the service stateless again: name the contract's operations, where the session actually lives, and defend why this is the precondition for scaling out.","givenCode":null,"diagram":null}}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"vi":{"followUps":["Nếu session hết TTL đúng lúc một request đang xử lý dở, contract nên coi đó là văng tài khoản hay tự gia hạn — và ai chịu trách nhiệm quyết định điều đó?","Nếu store trung tâm chỉ là một instance Redis duy nhất, chẳng phải bạn vừa dời SPOF từ RAM của node sang Redis thôi sao — bạn thiết kế gì để tránh điều đó?"],"hints":["Hỏi: điều gì đang buộc Load Balancer phải ghim một user vào đúng một node — thứ gì chỉ node đó biết mà các node khác không biết?","Nếu session không còn sống trong RAM của từng node, nó cần sống ở đâu để mọi node đọc ra cùng một kết quả?"]},"en":{"followUps":["If a session's TTL expires right in the middle of an in-flight request, should the contract treat that as a logout or auto-renew it — and whose call is that?","If the central store is just a single Redis instance, haven't you just moved the SPOF from a node's RAM into Redis — what would you design to avoid that?"],"hints":["Ask: what is forcing the Load Balancer to pin a given user to one exact node — what lives there that only that node knows?","If the session no longer lives in each node's own RAM, where does it need to live so every node reads back the same result?"]}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"locales":{"vi":{"rubric":["Định vị đúng vấn đề: session sống trong RAM cục bộ buộc phải ghim user vào một node cố định (sticky session); node đó chết, hoặc user bị round-robin sang node khác không có session, là bị văng tài khoản — và đây cũng là lý do tải không còn rải đều.","Phác thảo một contract tối giản cho store: `get(sessionId)` đọc session, `set(sessionId, session, ttl)` ghi kèm thời gian sống, `delete(sessionId)` xoá — tất cả trỏ vào một nguồn tập trung dùng chung bởi mọi node (ví dụ Redis), không phải state riêng của từng process.","Giải thích statelessness là điều kiện tiên quyết để scale ngang: một khi state được đẩy ra khỏi process, node nào cũng xử lý được request bất kỳ, nên round-robin (hay Least Connections) mới thực sự hợp lệ và số node mới thêm mới cộng dồn được năng lực.","Bảo vệ trade-off một cách trung thực: đổi lấy việc bỏ sticky session là thêm một network hop cho mỗi lần đọc/ghi session, nhưng phần thưởng là scale-out không giới hạn và không mất session khi một node chết.","Nhận ra bản thân store trung tâm giờ trở thành hạ tầng dùng chung, cực kỳ quan trọng — nó phải tự có tính sẵn sàng cao (replication/cluster), nếu không sẽ chỉ dời SPOF từ RAM của node sang chính nó chứ chưa hề khử được SPOF."],"checklist":[{"text":"The problem: a session living in local RAM pins each user to the exact node holding it, forcing a sticky session.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Consequence #1: when that node dies, or the LB round-robins the user onto a different node, they are logged out immediately.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Consequence #2: sticky-session pinning also defeats the goal of spreading load evenly across nodes.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"The contract: a small shared session-store with get(sessionId), set(sessionId, session, ttl), and delete(sessionId), backed by one central in-memory store like Redis instead of per-process RAM.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Pushing state out like this makes the service stateless: any node can serve any request, so round-robin (or Least Connections when load is uneven) becomes valid.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"This statelessness is the precondition for scaling out — without it, adding nodes just adds more places for a user to get logged out rather than adding up capacity.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"The trade-off: every session read/write now costs one extra network hop to the store, in exchange for near-unbounded scale-out and no session loss when a node dies.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"The caveat: the central store is now shared, critical infrastructure — if it isn't highly available on its own (replication/cluster), the SPOF has just moved into the store, not been removed.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10}],"keywords":["statelessness","session store","sticky session","round-robin","single point of failure (SPOF)"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"The root issue is the session living in the process's own RAM, so the load balancer has to pin a user to that exact node — a sticky session — and if that node dies, or the LB round-robins them elsewhere anyway, they get logged out, plus load can't spread evenly. My fix is a small shared session-store contract: get by session id, set with a TTL, and delete on logout, all backed by something central like Redis instead of local memory. Once state is out of the process, the service is stateless, so any node can serve any request and round-robin or least-connections finally works properly, and that statelessness is the actual precondition for scaling out at all. The trade-off is an extra network hop on every session read and write, and I'd flag that this central store now becomes critical shared infrastructure — if it's not itself highly available, I've just moved the single point of failure into the store instead of removing it.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"Because the session lives in local RAM, the LB has to stick a user to one node, and that node dying or the LB rebalancing them elsewhere logs them out. I'd move sessions into a central store like Redis with get, set with expiry, and delete operations, so no node needs to hold state locally. That makes the service stateless, which means any node can handle any request and load balancing actually works evenly, at the cost of an extra network call per session lookup.","sonnet":63,"haiku":75},{"level":3,"answer":"The problem is the session only exists on one machine, so users get stuck to that machine and lose their session if it goes down. I'd put the session data in something like Redis instead, so it's shared across all the nodes. That way any server can handle the request without needing the sticky session trick.","sonnet":25,"haiku":38},{"level":4,"answer":"I'd just use a database or cache to store sessions instead of keeping them in memory. That should fix the sticky session problem somehow. Not fully sure how the load balancer routing changes after that though.","sonnet":0,"haiku":13},{"level":5,"answer":"Honestly sticky sessions aren't that bad, you could just make the load balancer smarter about not sending users to a dead node. I don't think you need to change where sessions are stored, just improve the health checks. Moving session data elsewhere sounds like unnecessary extra work.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]},"en":{"rubric":["Locates the problem precisely: a session living in local RAM forces the user to be pinned to one fixed node (sticky session); that node dying, or the user getting round-robined onto a different node without the session, means getting logged out — and it's also why load stops spreading evenly.","Sketches a minimal store contract: `get(sessionId)` reads the session, `set(sessionId, session, ttl)` writes it with an expiry, `delete(sessionId)` removes it — all pointing at one central source shared by every node (e.g. Redis), not per-process state.","Explains statelessness as the precondition for scaling out: once state is pushed out of the process, any node can handle any request, so round-robin (or Least Connections) becomes genuinely valid and each newly added node actually adds up capacity.","Defends the trade-off honestly: giving up the sticky session costs one extra network hop per session read/write, in exchange for unbounded scale-out and no session loss when a node dies.","Recognizes that the central store itself is now shared, critical infrastructure — it must be highly available on its own (replication/cluster), otherwise this design has merely moved the SPOF from a node's RAM into the store, not removed it."],"checklist":[{"text":"The problem: a session living in local RAM pins each user to the exact node holding it, forcing a sticky session.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Consequence #1: when that node dies, or the LB round-robins the user onto a different node, they are logged out immediately.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Consequence #2: sticky-session pinning also defeats the goal of spreading load evenly across nodes.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"The contract: a small shared session-store with get(sessionId), set(sessionId, session, ttl), and delete(sessionId), backed by one central in-memory store like Redis instead of per-process RAM.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Pushing state out like this makes the service stateless: any node can serve any request, so round-robin (or Least Connections when load is uneven) becomes valid.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"This statelessness is the precondition for scaling out — without it, adding nodes just adds more places for a user to get logged out rather than adding up capacity.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"The trade-off: every session read/write now costs one extra network hop to the store, in exchange for near-unbounded scale-out and no session loss when a node dies.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"The caveat: the central store is now shared, critical infrastructure — if it isn't highly available on its own (replication/cluster), the SPOF has just moved into the store, not been removed.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10}],"keywords":["statelessness","session store","sticky session","round-robin","single point of failure (SPOF)"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"The root issue is the session living in the process's own RAM, so the load balancer has to pin a user to that exact node — a sticky session — and if that node dies, or the LB round-robins them elsewhere anyway, they get logged out, plus load can't spread evenly. My fix is a small shared session-store contract: get by session id, set with a TTL, and delete on logout, all backed by something central like Redis instead of local memory. Once state is out of the process, the service is stateless, so any node can serve any request and round-robin or least-connections finally works properly, and that statelessness is the actual precondition for scaling out at all. The trade-off is an extra network hop on every session read and write, and I'd flag that this central store now becomes critical shared infrastructure — if it's not itself highly available, I've just moved the single point of failure into the store instead of removing it.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"Because the session lives in local RAM, the LB has to stick a user to one node, and that node dying or the LB rebalancing them elsewhere logs them out. I'd move sessions into a central store like Redis with get, set with expiry, and delete operations, so no node needs to hold state locally. That makes the service stateless, which means any node can handle any request and load balancing actually works evenly, at the cost of an extra network call per session lookup.","sonnet":63,"haiku":75},{"level":3,"answer":"The problem is the session only exists on one machine, so users get stuck to that machine and lose their session if it goes down. I'd put the session data in something like Redis instead, so it's shared across all the nodes. That way any server can handle the request without needing the sticky session trick.","sonnet":25,"haiku":38},{"level":4,"answer":"I'd just use a database or cache to store sessions instead of keeping them in memory. That should fix the sticky session problem somehow. Not fully sure how the load balancer routing changes after that though.","sonnet":0,"haiku":13},{"level":5,"answer":"Honestly sticky sessions aren't that bad, you could just make the load balancer smarter about not sending users to a dead node. I don't think you need to change where sessions are stored, just improve the health checks. Moving session data elsewhere sounds like unnecessary extra work.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]}},"ideal":{"agnostic":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nVấn đề: session nằm trong RAM cục bộ ghim mỗi user vào đúng node đang giữ nó — bắt buộc phải sticky session; khi node đó chết, hoặc LB route user sang node khác qua round-robin, user bị văng tài khoản ngay lập tức, và việc ghim còn phá luôn mục tiêu rải tải đều. Thiết kế của tôi là một session-store contract nhỏ, dùng chung bởi mọi node: `get(sessionId)` trả về session, `set(sessionId, session, ttl)` ghi kèm thời gian sống, `delete(sessionId)` xoá khi logout — tất cả trỏ vào một kho tập trung, in-memory, kiểu Redis, thay vì sống trong RAM riêng của từng process. Một khi state được đẩy ra ngoài như vậy, service trở nên stateless: node nào cũng xử lý được request bất kỳ, nên round-robin (hoặc Least Connections nếu tải lệch) mới thực sự hợp lệ, và đây chính là điều kiện tiên quyết để scale ngang — không có nó, thêm node chỉ thêm chỗ để user bị văng chứ không cộng dồn được năng lực. Trade-off tôi chấp nhận công khai: mỗi lần đọc/ghi session giờ tốn thêm một network hop tới store, nhưng đổi lại là scale-out gần như không giới hạn và không còn mất session khi một node chết. Tôi cũng lưu ý store trung tâm này giờ là hạ tầng dùng chung cực kỳ quan trọng — nếu không tự có tính sẵn sàng cao (replication/cluster), tôi chỉ vừa dời SPOF từ RAM của node sang chính cái store đó, chứ chưa hề khử được nó.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe problem: a session living in local RAM pins each user to the exact node holding it — forcing a sticky session; when that node dies, or the LB round-robins the user onto a different node, they're logged out immediately, and pinning also defeats the goal of spreading load evenly. My design is a small session-store contract shared by every node: `get(sessionId)` returns the session, `set(sessionId, session, ttl)` writes it with an expiry, `delete(sessionId)` removes it on logout — all pointing at one central, in-memory store like Redis instead of living in each process's own RAM. Once state is pushed out like that, the service becomes stateless: any node can serve any request, so round-robin (or Least Connections when load is uneven) is finally valid — and this statelessness is exactly the precondition for scaling out, since without it, adding nodes just adds more places for a user to get logged out rather than adding up capacity. I'd openly accept the trade-off: every session read/write now costs one extra network hop to the store, in exchange for near-unbounded scale-out and no session loss when a node dies. I'd also flag that this central store is now shared, critical infrastructure — if it isn't highly available on its own (replication/cluster), I've just moved the SPOF from a node's RAM into the store itself, not actually removed it."}}}$iv$::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (bank_id, bank_version, question_id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO interview_questions (bank_id, bank_version, question_id, sort_index, is_premium, family, tier, kind, tags, competency, leadership_tier, bodies, interviewer, grading)
VALUES ('1-system-design-mastery/0-fundamentals-of-system-design', '231d8cc57+37d905d9a', '1-system-design-mastery/0-fundamentals-of-system-design/14', 14, false, 'technical', 'senior', 'design-lite', ARRAY['health-check', 'flapping', 'failure-detection']::text[], NULL, NULL, $iv${"agnostic":{"vi":{"prompt":"Health check của load balancer bạn đang dùng chỉ là một lần ping mỗi giây với chính sách 1-strike: một lần ping fail là evict node ngay lập tức, một lần ping thành công là readmit ngay lập tức. Một node gặp một đợt GC pause ngắn hoặc một burst tải ngắn bị evict, quay lại vài giây sau, rồi lại bị evict ở lần trục trặc kế tiếp. Hãy thiết kế một \"health-check contract\" nhỏ để sửa chuyện này: mỗi node cần lưu trạng thái gì, quy tắc chuyển trạng thái ra/vào pool ra sao, và bạn sẽ đặt interval/threshold/grace timing thế nào.","givenCode":null,"diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"Your load balancer's health check is a single ping every second with a 1-strike policy: one failed ping evicts the node immediately, one successful ping readmits it immediately. A node hit by a brief GC pause or a short load burst gets evicted, comes back seconds later, gets evicted again on the next hiccup. Design a small health-check contract that fixes this: what state does each node need to track, what are the transition rules in and out of the pool, and what would you set the interval/threshold/grace timing to.","givenCode":null,"diagram":null}}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"vi":{"followUps":["Với unhealthy threshold là 3 ở interval 2 giây, một crash thật mất tới 6 giây mới bị evict — điều đó có chấp nhận được với workload này không, và bạn sẽ đổi gì nếu không?","Một node cứ flap lặp đi lặp lại — không bao giờ ổn định hẳn, cũng không bao giờ chết hẳn — có thể còn tệ hơn một node chết sạch sẽ. Contract của bạn phát hiện đúng pattern đó và xử lý nó khác với một trục trặc thoáng qua một lần như thế nào?"],"hints":["Hỏi: với chính sách 1-ping/1-strike, load balancer có phân biệt được \"node vừa tạm dừng một chút\" với \"node thật sự đã chết\" hay không — nó cần nhớ thêm thông tin gì để phân biệt được hai trường hợp đó?","Coi healthy threshold và unhealthy threshold là hai núm chỉnh riêng biệt, không phải một con số dùng chung — việc nhận lại một node có cần nhanh y hệt việc evict nó không, hay nên thận trọng hơn?"]},"en":{"followUps":["With an unhealthy threshold of 3 at a 2-second interval, a real crash takes up to 6 seconds to be evicted — is that acceptable for this workload, and what would you change if it isn't?","A node that flaps repeatedly — never fully stable, never fully dead — is arguably worse than one that's cleanly down. How would your contract detect that specific pattern and handle it differently from a normal one-off transient blip?"],"hints":["Ask: with a 1-ping/1-strike policy, can the load balancer tell \"the node just paused for a moment\" apart from \"the node is actually dead\" at all — what information would it need to remember to tell the two apart?","Treat the healthy and unhealthy thresholds as two separate dials, not one shared number — does readmitting a node need to be exactly as fast as evicting it, or should rejoining be more cautious?"]}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"locales":{"vi":{"rubric":["Chẩn đoán đúng gốc rễ: một lần ping cộng chính sách 1-strike không thể phân biệt một trục trặc thoáng qua (GC pause, một đợt CPU tăng đột biến ngắn) với một node thật sự đã chết, nên nó evict-rồi-readmit theo nhiễu — đó chính là flapping — và mỗi lần flap lại đẩy các connection dang dở sang các node còn lại, làm tải của chúng tăng vọt theo.","Thiết kế một state machine phong phú hơn nhị phân up/down — tối thiểu HEALTHY / SUSPECT / UNHEALTHY, hoặc một bộ đếm số lần fail liên tiếp tương đương — để một lần ping trượt duy nhất không còn kích hoạt eviction ngay lập tức.","Nêu được các tunable cụ thể với một điểm khởi đầu hợp lý: một check interval (ví dụ 2-5 giây), một unhealthy threshold (số lần fail liên tiếp trước khi evict, ví dụ 3) nghiêm ngặt hơn healthy threshold (số lần thành công liên tiếp trước khi readmit, ví dụ 2) — node phải tự chứng minh lại sự ổn định trước khi được nhận lại, chứ không chỉ cần trả lời đúng một lần.","Bảo vệ đánh đổi một cách tường minh: threshold càng nghiêm ngặt càng miễn nhiễm với nhiễu, nhưng cái giá trực tiếp là phản ứng chậm hơn với một node thật sự đã chết — một crash thật giờ mất thêm khoảng threshold × interval giây mới bị phát hiện và evict, và đúng khoảng thời gian đó là cái giá phải trả.","(Bonus, không bắt buộc) Nêu được một detector kiểu phi-accrual, hoặc exponential backoff khi node cứ flap lặp lại, như bước nâng cấp tiếp theo so với một threshold cố định, cho một node cứ dao động ở ngưỡng thay vì lên hẳn hoặc xuống hẳn."],"checklist":[{"text":"The root cause: a single ping with a 1-strike policy carries no memory, so it can't distinguish a transient GC pause or load spike from a real crash — it evicts immediately and readmits immediately on the very next successful ping.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"Flapping is actively harmful, not just noisy: every eviction reroutes the node's in-flight connections onto the remaining nodes, spiking their load right as the recovering node is about to rejoin and take load back.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"The design gives each node a state machine — HEALTHY, SUSPECT (recent failures, still in the pool), and UNHEALTHY (evicted) — instead of a bare boolean.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"The load balancer pings every node on a fixed interval, e.g. 2-5 seconds, frequent enough to notice real failures quickly but sparse enough not to be a load source itself.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"A node moves HEALTHY → SUSPECT → UNHEALTHY only after an unhealthy threshold of consecutive failures (e.g. 3 in a row), so a single blip no longer evicts anything.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"Getting back in is stricter: a healthy threshold of consecutive successes (e.g. 2) is required before UNHEALTHY → HEALTHY, so a node must demonstrate real recovered stability rather than answer one lucky ping.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"The trade-off: raising the unhealthy threshold buys strong immunity to noise, but a truly dead node now survives up to threshold × interval seconds longer inside the pool (e.g. up to 6 seconds at 3 failures / 2-second interval) before eviction.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"For a node that keeps hovering right at the threshold and repeatedly flaps over the long run, a fixed threshold isn't enough — that calls for a phi-accrual failure detector (confidence scored from recent response timing) or exponential backoff that makes a re-flapping node earn its way back more slowly.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10}],"keywords":["health check","flapping","failure detection","unhealthy threshold","grace period"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"The core problem is a single ping with one-strike eviction has no memory, so it can't tell a brief GC pause from an actually dead node, and every eviction reroutes in-flight traffic onto the remaining nodes right as the node is about to come back and take load again — that's what causes flapping. I'd give each node a small state machine: healthy, suspect, and unhealthy, pinging every node on something like a two to five second interval. It only drops to unhealthy after a consecutive-failure threshold, say three misses in a row, and getting back in requires a stricter consecutive-success threshold, say two, so it has to prove real stability. The honest trade-off is that a bigger threshold buys noise immunity but a truly dead node now stays in the pool for threshold times interval seconds longer — up to six seconds here — and if a node keeps hovering right at that line long-term, a fixed threshold isn't enough, that's where you'd reach for something like a phi-accrual detector or exponential backoff on re-admission.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"A single ping with one strike can't tell a temporary blip from a real failure, so it evicts and readmits the node constantly, and each eviction dumps its connections onto the other nodes which spikes their load too. I'd add a small state machine — healthy, suspect, unhealthy — pinging every few seconds, and only evict after several consecutive failures in a row, then require a couple consecutive successes before letting it back in, stricter than the eviction side. The trade-off is that raising the failure threshold makes it more resistant to noise but means a genuinely dead node stays in the pool longer before we catch it.","sonnet":88,"haiku":88},{"level":3,"answer":"The issue is the health check reacts to just one failed ping, so a node that just had a small hiccup gets kicked out and then let back in right away, and that eviction pushes its traffic onto other nodes. I'd track a few consecutive failures before evicting instead of just one, and maybe require a couple successes before letting it rejoin. There's some tradeoff between reacting fast to real failures and not overreacting to noise, but I'd need to think more about the exact numbers.","sonnet":38,"haiku":63},{"level":4,"answer":"I think you'd just check the node a couple more times before deciding it's actually down, instead of one ping. That should stop the flapping issue. I'm not sure what interval or exact thresholds to use though.","sonnet":13,"haiku":13},{"level":5,"answer":"I'd probably just make the health check ping less often so it doesn't overreact as much. Or maybe just don't evict nodes automatically and let an engineer check manually when there's an issue. I don't think a state machine is really necessary for something this simple.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]},"en":{"rubric":["Diagnoses the root cause: a single ping plus a 1-strike policy can't tell a transient hiccup (a GC pause, a momentary CPU spike) apart from a genuinely dead node, so it evicts-and-readmits on noise — that's flapping — and each flap reroutes in-flight connections onto the remaining nodes, spiking their load in turn.","Designs a state machine richer than binary up/down — at minimum HEALTHY / SUSPECT / UNHEALTHY, or an equivalent consecutive-failure counter — so a single missed ping no longer triggers an immediate eviction.","Names concrete tunables with a defensible starting point: a check interval (e.g. every 2-5s), an unhealthy threshold (consecutive failures before eviction, e.g. 3) that is stricter than the healthy threshold (consecutive successes before readmission, e.g. 2) — a node has to prove itself sane again before rejoining, not just answer once.","Defends the trade-off explicitly: a stricter threshold buys immunity to noise at the direct cost of slower reaction to a genuinely dead node — a real crash now takes roughly threshold × interval seconds longer to be detected and evicted, and that window is exactly the price being paid.","(Bonus, not required) Names a phi-accrual-style detector, or exponential backoff on repeated flapping, as the next step up from a fixed threshold for a node that keeps borderline-failing rather than being cleanly up or down."],"checklist":[{"text":"The root cause: a single ping with a 1-strike policy carries no memory, so it can't distinguish a transient GC pause or load spike from a real crash — it evicts immediately and readmits immediately on the very next successful ping.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"Flapping is actively harmful, not just noisy: every eviction reroutes the node's in-flight connections onto the remaining nodes, spiking their load right as the recovering node is about to rejoin and take load back.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"The design gives each node a state machine — HEALTHY, SUSPECT (recent failures, still in the pool), and UNHEALTHY (evicted) — instead of a bare boolean.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"The load balancer pings every node on a fixed interval, e.g. 2-5 seconds, frequent enough to notice real failures quickly but sparse enough not to be a load source itself.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"A node moves HEALTHY → SUSPECT → UNHEALTHY only after an unhealthy threshold of consecutive failures (e.g. 3 in a row), so a single blip no longer evicts anything.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"Getting back in is stricter: a healthy threshold of consecutive successes (e.g. 2) is required before UNHEALTHY → HEALTHY, so a node must demonstrate real recovered stability rather than answer one lucky ping.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"The trade-off: raising the unhealthy threshold buys strong immunity to noise, but a truly dead node now survives up to threshold × interval seconds longer inside the pool (e.g. up to 6 seconds at 3 failures / 2-second interval) before eviction.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"For a node that keeps hovering right at the threshold and repeatedly flaps over the long run, a fixed threshold isn't enough — that calls for a phi-accrual failure detector (confidence scored from recent response timing) or exponential backoff that makes a re-flapping node earn its way back more slowly.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10}],"keywords":["health check","flapping","failure detection","unhealthy threshold","grace period"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"The core problem is a single ping with one-strike eviction has no memory, so it can't tell a brief GC pause from an actually dead node, and every eviction reroutes in-flight traffic onto the remaining nodes right as the node is about to come back and take load again — that's what causes flapping. I'd give each node a small state machine: healthy, suspect, and unhealthy, pinging every node on something like a two to five second interval. It only drops to unhealthy after a consecutive-failure threshold, say three misses in a row, and getting back in requires a stricter consecutive-success threshold, say two, so it has to prove real stability. The honest trade-off is that a bigger threshold buys noise immunity but a truly dead node now stays in the pool for threshold times interval seconds longer — up to six seconds here — and if a node keeps hovering right at that line long-term, a fixed threshold isn't enough, that's where you'd reach for something like a phi-accrual detector or exponential backoff on re-admission.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"A single ping with one strike can't tell a temporary blip from a real failure, so it evicts and readmits the node constantly, and each eviction dumps its connections onto the other nodes which spikes their load too. I'd add a small state machine — healthy, suspect, unhealthy — pinging every few seconds, and only evict after several consecutive failures in a row, then require a couple consecutive successes before letting it back in, stricter than the eviction side. The trade-off is that raising the failure threshold makes it more resistant to noise but means a genuinely dead node stays in the pool longer before we catch it.","sonnet":88,"haiku":88},{"level":3,"answer":"The issue is the health check reacts to just one failed ping, so a node that just had a small hiccup gets kicked out and then let back in right away, and that eviction pushes its traffic onto other nodes. I'd track a few consecutive failures before evicting instead of just one, and maybe require a couple successes before letting it rejoin. There's some tradeoff between reacting fast to real failures and not overreacting to noise, but I'd need to think more about the exact numbers.","sonnet":38,"haiku":63},{"level":4,"answer":"I think you'd just check the node a couple more times before deciding it's actually down, instead of one ping. That should stop the flapping issue. I'm not sure what interval or exact thresholds to use though.","sonnet":13,"haiku":13},{"level":5,"answer":"I'd probably just make the health check ping less often so it doesn't overreact as much. Or maybe just don't evict nodes automatically and let an engineer check manually when there's an issue. I don't think a state machine is really necessary for something this simple.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]}},"ideal":{"agnostic":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nGốc rễ là một lần ping với chính sách 1-strike không mang theo trí nhớ nào cả: nó không thể phân biệt \"node này vừa tạm dừng một chút\" với \"node này thật sự đã chết\", nên bất kỳ GC pause hay load spike thoáng qua nào cũng bị đối xử y hệt một crash thật — evict ngay, rồi readmit ngay ở lần ping thành công kế tiếp. Đó là flapping, và nó không chỉ gây nhiễu mà còn gây hại thật sự: mỗi lần evict đẩy các connection dang dở của node đó sang các node còn lại, làm tải của chúng tăng vọt đúng lúc node \"đang hồi phục\" chuẩn bị quay lại nhận tải trở lại — cả pool cứ xáo trộn thay vì ổn định. Contract của tôi cho mỗi node một state machine nhỏ thay vì một boolean trần trụi: HEALTHY, SUSPECT (có một hoặc vài lần fail gần đây, vẫn còn trong pool), và UNHEALTHY (đã bị evict). Load balancer ping mọi node theo một interval cố định — 2-5 giây là điểm khởi đầu hợp lý, đủ thường xuyên để phát hiện lỗi thật nhanh, đủ thưa để bản thân việc ping không trở thành một nguồn tải. Một node chỉ chuyển HEALTHY → SUSPECT → UNHEALTHY sau khi đạt unhealthy threshold gồm các lần fail LIÊN TIẾP (ví dụ 3 lần liền) — một trục trặc đơn lẻ không còn evict được gì nữa. Việc quay lại pool được cố tình làm nghiêm ngặt hơn về tinh thần, không chỉ đối xứng đơn thuần: yêu cầu một healthy threshold gồm các lần thành công liên tiếp (ví dụ 2 lần) trước khi UNHEALTHY → HEALTHY, để node phải chứng minh sự ổn định đã thật sự hồi phục, chứ không chỉ may mắn trả lời đúng một lần ping, trước khi được tin tưởng giao lại tải. Đánh đổi ở đây là tường minh và cần được bảo vệ chứ không phải giấu đi: tăng unhealthy threshold mua được khả năng miễn nhiễm mạnh với nhiễu, nhưng một node thật sự đã chết giờ tồn tại lâu hơn trong pool thêm khoảng threshold × interval giây trước khi bị evict — với 3 lần fail ở interval 2 giây, đó là tới 6 giây request vẫn bị route tới một node đã chết. Khoảng thời gian đó chính là cái giá thật của việc tuning chống flapping, và nó phải được cân đo dựa trên mức độ tệ của việc \"vẫn route tới một node đã chết trong N giây\" đối với đúng service này. Với một node không chết hẳn cũng không hồi phục hẳn mà cứ dao động ngay tại ngưỡng — flap lặp đi lặp lại về lâu dài — một threshold cố định là chưa đủ; đó là trường hợp cần một failure detector kiểu phi-accrual (chấm điểm độ tin cậy dựa trên lịch sử thời gian phản hồi gần đây thay vì một ngưỡng cứng duy nhất) hoặc một exponential backoff khiến node phải \"trả giá\" nhiều hơn mỗi lần nó flap lại.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe root cause is that a single ping with a 1-strike policy carries no memory: it can't distinguish \"this node just paused for a moment\" from \"this node is actually dead\", so any transient GC pause or load spike gets treated exactly like a real crash — evict immediately, readmit immediately on the very next successful ping. That's flapping, and it's actively harmful, not just noisy: every eviction reroutes the node's in-flight connections onto the remaining nodes, spiking their load right as the \"recovering\" node is about to rejoin and take load back — the pool churns instead of settling. My contract gives each node a small state machine instead of a bare boolean: HEALTHY, SUSPECT (one or more recent failures, still in the pool), and UNHEALTHY (evicted). The load balancer pings every node on a fixed interval — 2-5 seconds is a reasonable start, frequent enough to notice real failures quickly, sparse enough not to be a load source itself. A node moves HEALTHY → SUSPECT → UNHEALTHY only after an unhealthy threshold of CONSECUTIVE failures (e.g. 3 in a row) — a single blip no longer evicts anything. Getting back in is deliberately made stricter in spirit, not just symmetric: require a healthy threshold of consecutive successes (e.g. 2) before UNHEALTHY → HEALTHY, so a node has to demonstrate real recovered stability, not just answer one lucky ping, before it's trusted with load again. The trade-off is explicit and has to be defended, not hidden: raising the unhealthy threshold buys strong immunity to noise, but a truly dead node now survives threshold × interval seconds longer inside the pool before eviction — with 3 failures at a 2-second interval that's up to 6 seconds of requests still being routed to a dead node. That window is the real price of anti-flapping tuning, and it has to be sized against how bad \"still routing to a dead node for N seconds\" actually is for this specific service. For a node that doesn't cleanly die or cleanly recover but keeps hovering right at the threshold — repeatedly flapping in and out over the long run — a fixed threshold isn't quite enough; that's the case for a phi-accrual failure detector (which scores confidence based on the recent history of response timing rather than one hard cutoff) or an exponential backoff that makes a node earn its way back in more slowly each time it re-flaps."}}}$iv$::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (bank_id, bank_version, question_id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO interview_banks (bank_id, version, content_hash, status, family, course, sort_index, difficulty, titles, descriptions, module_refs, question_count, calibration, imported_at, published_at)
VALUES ('1-system-design-mastery/18-distributed-locks-and-leader-election', '231d8cc57+37d905d9a', '5308e06cdcbc775ff680362d16c3345c15cf665e3168ecbfbf2a2a8b27b3e404', 'published', 'technical', '1-system-design-mastery', 18, 'hard', $iv${"vi":"Phỏng vấn: Distributed locks & leader election - Redlock & fencing token, bầu leader theo lease, etcd strong consensus","en":"Interview: Distributed locks & leader election — Redlock & fencing tokens, lease-based leader election, etcd strong consensus"}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"vi":"Ngân hàng gồm 15 câu phỏng vấn tư duy, trải khắp 7 loại phỏng vấn nói (theory, scenario, reasoning, debug, review, optimize, design-lite), bao phủ 3 bài học của module 18: Redlock trên nhiều node Redis độc lập với quorum acquire, validity window (ttl − elapsed − drift), release bằng Lua CAS và fencing token tăng đơn điệu (đánh đổi giữa liveness và safety); bầu leader theo lease với acquire bằng SET NX PX, renew bằng Lua CAS, step-down khi renew thất bại, term, và khoảng trống failover leaseTTL+renewInterval; và etcd strong consensus - thứ tự toàn cục của Raft, createRevision làm nguyên tố sắp thứ tự, lease ephemeral, và đọc linearizable so với serializable. Module đa ngôn ngữ (TypeScript/Java/C#/Go) - các cơ chế mang tính phổ quát, các stack minh hoạ (ioredis/Redisson/RedLock.net/redsync, etcd3/jetcd/dotnet-etcd/clientv3) chỉ là ví dụ.","en":"A 15-question reasoning-focused mock-interview bank spanning the 7 spoken-interview categories (theory, scenario, reasoning, debug, review, optimize, design-lite) across module 18's three lessons: Redlock over independent Redis nodes with quorum acquire, validity window (ttl − elapsed − drift), Lua CAS release and monotonic fencing tokens (liveness vs safety); lease-based leader election with SET NX PX acquire, Lua CAS renew, step-down-on-fail, term, and the leaseTTL+renewInterval failover window; and etcd strong consensus — Raft total order, createRevision as the ordering primitive, ephemeral leases, and linearizable vs serializable reads. Multi-language backend module (TypeScript/Java/C#/Go); the mechanisms are universal and the illustrating stacks (ioredis/Redisson/RedLock.net/redsync, etcd3/jetcd/dotnet-etcd/clientv3) are only examples."}$iv$::jsonb, ARRAY['18-distributed-locks-and-leader-election']::text[], 15, '{"mode":"dev-seed"}'::jsonb, now(), now())
ON CONFLICT (bank_id, version) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO interview_questions (bank_id, bank_version, question_id, sort_index, is_premium, family, tier, kind, tags, competency, leadership_tier, bodies, interviewer, grading)
VALUES ('1-system-design-mastery/18-distributed-locks-and-leader-election', '231d8cc57+37d905d9a', '1-system-design-mastery/18-distributed-locks-and-leader-election/0', 0, false, 'technical', 'junior', 'theory', ARRAY['fencing-tokens', 'liveness-vs-safety', 'distributed-locking']::text[], NULL, NULL, $iv${"agnostic":{"vi":{"prompt":"Một đồng nghiệp trong nhóm quả quyết: \"Redlock đã lấy được quorum trên đa số node rồi, chỉ cần giữ lock object ở phía client là đủ an toàn, không cần thêm gì nữa.\" Hãy giải thích ra tiếng cho đồng nghiệp đó nghe: vì sao quorum acquire một mình KHÔNG chặn được một stale writer, và fencing token thêm vào cơ chế gì để bịt lỗ hổng đó? Đừng chỉ nêu định nghĩa suông.","givenCode":null,"diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"A teammate insists: \"Redlock already got quorum across a majority of the nodes, so just holding the lock object client-side is enough safety? Don't just recite a definition.","givenCode":null,"diagram":null}}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"vi":{"followUps":["Nếu bộ đếm fencing token và watermark chỉ nằm trong bộ nhớ và reset về 0 khi service restart, safety còn được đảm bảo không? Bạn vá bằng cách nào?","Redlock yêu cầu các node độc lập về network/power/AZ chứ không phải Redis Cluster — điều đó liên quan thế nào đến việc quorum có còn đáng tin hay trở thành \"quorum giả\"?"],"hints":["Tự hỏi: khi một client bị pause vượt TTL rồi thức dậy, ai là bên biết lock đã bị thu hồi — client hay storage? Điều đó quyết định ai phải chịu trách nhiệm chặn cú write muộn.","Đi đúng hai cơ chế: (1) token tăng đơn điệu cấp mỗi lần grant, (2) watermark ở storage so sánh token với lastSeen để reject — rồi nối chúng lại thành lời giải thích cho đồng nghiệp."]},"en":{"followUps":["If the fencing-token counter and the watermark only live in memory and reset to 0 when the service restarts, is safety still guaranteed? How would you patch that?","Redlock requires nodes independent in network/power/AZ rather than a Redis Cluster — how does that relate to whether the quorum can still be trusted, versus becoming a \"false quorum\"?"],"hints":["Ask yourself: when a client pauses past the TTL and then wakes up, who actually knows the lock was revoked — the client or the storage? That decides who is responsible for blocking the late write.","Walk through exactly two mechanisms: (1) a monotonically increasing token issued on every grant, (2) a storage-side watermark that compares the token against lastSeen to reject — then stitch them into the explanation for your teammate."]}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"locales":{"vi":{"rubric":["Nêu được khung hai bài toán: quorum giải liveness (nhiều nhất một owner tại một thời điểm); nó KHÔNG giải safety (một writer cũ từng sở hữu lock).","Giải thích được lỗi mà quorum không bắt được: một client đã acquire lock, bị pause vượt TTL, thức dậy vẫn cầm lock object trông như hợp lệ và ghi đè lên dữ liệu của leader mới hơn.","Mô tả được fencing token: một số nguyên tăng đơn điệu, cấp mỗi lần lock được grant, gắn vào mọi lần write.","Mô tả được watermark phía storage: storage ghi lại token cao nhất từng thấy và reject mọi write có token nhỏ hơn hoặc bằng lastSeen — nên token nhỏ hơn của client bị pause sẽ thua.","Chốt được insight cốt lõi: storage không cần biết gì về lock service, nó chỉ so hai số nguyên — đó chính là lý do vì sao safety tách biệt được khỏi liveness."],"checklist":[{"text":"A distributed lock has two independent jobs — liveness and safety — enforced by two different mechanisms: quorum acquire and the fencing token.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"Quorum acquire (parallel SET NX PX across independent nodes, then counting the majority) guarantees liveness — at most one client wins the lock at any moment.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"Quorum is liveness-only: a client that legitimately acquired the lock, then paused past the TTL, can wake up still holding a client-side lock object that looks valid, even though the lock service already re-granted the lock to someone else.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"Quorum has no way to stop that late write, because the stale client's lock object doesn't know it was revoked, and storage can't tell a stale writer from a fresh one.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"The fix is a fencing token: a monotonically increasing integer issued on every grant, attached to every write.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":13},{"text":"The storage layer keeps a watermark — the highest token it has ever seen — and rejects any write whose token is less than or equal to that watermark.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":13},{"text":"A paused client inevitably carries a smaller token than the fresher leader, so its write is rejected.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":9},{"text":"The elegance is that storage knows nothing about the lock service — it just compares two integers.","dimension":"communication","critical":false,"scoreBand":9}],"keywords":["fencing token","quorum","liveness vs safety","watermark","distributed lock"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"So quorum acquire only guarantees liveness — it makes sure at most one client wins the lock at any given moment, but it says nothing about what happens after. If a client pauses past the TTL — a GC pause, a network stall — it wakes up still holding a lock object that looks perfectly valid, even though the lock service already handed the lock to someone else, and storage has no way to distinguish that stale writer from a fresh one. That's exactly what a fencing token fixes: every grant hands out a monotonically increasing number, and the storage layer keeps a watermark of the highest token it's ever seen, rejecting anything less than or equal to that. The paused client's token is always older than the new leader's, so its write gets bounced — storage doesn't even need to know the lock service exists, it's just comparing two integers. Quorum buys you liveness, fencing buys you safety, and you need both.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"Quorum just tells you one client won the lock at a given moment — it's a liveness guarantee. The problem is a client can pause past its TTL, wake up, and still think it holds the lock even though it's been reassigned, and there's nothing stopping it from writing. Fencing tokens solve that: each grant gets an increasing number, and storage rejects any write with a token lower than the last one it accepted. So the stale client's old token just gets rejected.","sonnet":88,"haiku":88},{"level":3,"answer":"Quorum means a majority of nodes agreed to give you the lock, so it should be safe. But if the client is slow and the lock expires, there could be an issue — another client might take over while the first one is still working. That's where fencing tokens come in, you attach a number to each write so you know which one is newer, and you reject the older one.","sonnet":13,"haiku":63},{"level":4,"answer":"I think fencing tokens are like a version number for the lock, you use them so writes don't conflict with each other. Quorum is just when most of the nodes agree on who has the lock, so I'd assume that's already pretty safe.","sonnet":0,"haiku":38},{"level":5,"answer":"Redlock uses Redis so it's basically the same as a normal lock, just distributed across more machines. As long as you have enough replicas agreeing, it should be fine — I don't think you really need fencing tokens on top since majority already proves it's correct.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]},"en":{"rubric":["States the two-problem framing: quorum solves liveness (at most one owner at a moment); it does NOT solve safety (a stale writer that once owned the lock).","Explains the failure quorum cannot catch: a client that acquired the lock, paused past the TTL, then woke up still holds a lock object that looks valid and overwrites a fresher leader's data.","Describes the fencing token: a monotonically increasing integer issued on each grant, attached to every write.","Describes the storage-side watermark: storage records the highest token seen and rejects any write with a token less than or equal to that watermark — so a paused client's smaller token loses.","Nails the key insight: storage needs to know nothing about the lock service; it just compares two integers, which is exactly why safety is separable from liveness."],"checklist":[{"text":"A distributed lock has two independent jobs — liveness and safety — enforced by two different mechanisms: quorum acquire and the fencing token.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"Quorum acquire (parallel SET NX PX across independent nodes, then counting the majority) guarantees liveness — at most one client wins the lock at any moment.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"Quorum is liveness-only: a client that legitimately acquired the lock, then paused past the TTL, can wake up still holding a client-side lock object that looks valid, even though the lock service already re-granted the lock to someone else.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"Quorum has no way to stop that late write, because the stale client's lock object doesn't know it was revoked, and storage can't tell a stale writer from a fresh one.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"The fix is a fencing token: a monotonically increasing integer issued on every grant, attached to every write.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":13},{"text":"The storage layer keeps a watermark — the highest token it has ever seen — and rejects any write whose token is less than or equal to that watermark.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":13},{"text":"A paused client inevitably carries a smaller token than the fresher leader, so its write is rejected.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":9},{"text":"The elegance is that storage knows nothing about the lock service — it just compares two integers.","dimension":"communication","critical":false,"scoreBand":9}],"keywords":["fencing token","quorum","liveness vs safety","watermark","distributed lock"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"So quorum acquire only guarantees liveness — it makes sure at most one client wins the lock at any given moment, but it says nothing about what happens after. If a client pauses past the TTL — a GC pause, a network stall — it wakes up still holding a lock object that looks perfectly valid, even though the lock service already handed the lock to someone else, and storage has no way to distinguish that stale writer from a fresh one. That's exactly what a fencing token fixes: every grant hands out a monotonically increasing number, and the storage layer keeps a watermark of the highest token it's ever seen, rejecting anything less than or equal to that. The paused client's token is always older than the new leader's, so its write gets bounced — storage doesn't even need to know the lock service exists, it's just comparing two integers. Quorum buys you liveness, fencing buys you safety, and you need both.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"Quorum just tells you one client won the lock at a given moment — it's a liveness guarantee. The problem is a client can pause past its TTL, wake up, and still think it holds the lock even though it's been reassigned, and there's nothing stopping it from writing. Fencing tokens solve that: each grant gets an increasing number, and storage rejects any write with a token lower than the last one it accepted. So the stale client's old token just gets rejected.","sonnet":88,"haiku":88},{"level":3,"answer":"Quorum means a majority of nodes agreed to give you the lock, so it should be safe. But if the client is slow and the lock expires, there could be an issue — another client might take over while the first one is still working. That's where fencing tokens come in, you attach a number to each write so you know which one is newer, and you reject the older one.","sonnet":13,"haiku":63},{"level":4,"answer":"I think fencing tokens are like a version number for the lock, you use them so writes don't conflict with each other. Quorum is just when most of the nodes agree on who has the lock, so I'd assume that's already pretty safe.","sonnet":0,"haiku":38},{"level":5,"answer":"Redlock uses Redis so it's basically the same as a normal lock, just distributed across more machines. As long as you have enough replicas agreeing, it should be fine — I don't think you really need fencing tokens on top since majority already proves it's correct.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]}},"ideal":{"agnostic":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nMột distributed lock có hai việc độc lập phải làm. Quorum acquire (`SET NX PX` song song trên các node độc lập, đếm majority) đảm bảo liveness — tại một thời điểm chỉ một client thắng lock. Nhưng đó chỉ là liveness thôi: một client từng acquire lock hợp lệ, sau đó bị GC-pause vượt TTL, khi thức dậy vẫn cầm một lock object phía client trông như còn hợp lệ, dù lock service đã grant lại lock đó cho người khác từ lâu. Quorum không có cách nào ngăn cú write muộn ấy, vì lock object của client cũ không biết mình đã bị revoke, còn storage thì không phân biệt được đâu là writer cũ, đâu là writer mới. Cách vá là fencing token: một số nguyên tăng đơn điệu, cấp ra mỗi lần lock được grant, gắn vào mọi lần write. Storage giữ một watermark — token cao nhất từng thấy — và reject mọi write có token nhỏ hơn hoặc bằng watermark đó. Một client bị pause chắc chắn cầm token nhỏ hơn writer mới hơn, nên write của nó bị từ chối. Điểm hay ở đây là storage không cần biết gì về lock service — nó chỉ so hai số nguyên. Đó chính là mấu chốt: quorum lo liveness, fencing token lo safety — hai lớp khác nhau cho hai bài toán khác nhau.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nA distributed lock has two independent jobs. Quorum acquire (parallel `SET NX PX` across independent nodes, then counting the majority) guarantees liveness — at most one client wins the lock at any moment. But it is liveness-only: a client that legitimately acquired the lock, then paused past the TTL, wakes up still holding a client-side lock object that looks valid, even though the lock service already re-granted the lock to someone else. Quorum has no way to stop that late write, because the stale client's lock object doesn't know it was revoked, and storage can't tell a stale writer from a fresh one. The fix is a fencing token: a monotonically increasing integer issued on every grant, attached to every write. The storage layer keeps a watermark — the highest token it has ever seen — and rejects any write whose token is less than or equal to that watermark. A paused client inevitably carries a smaller token than the fresher leader, so its write is rejected. The elegance is that storage knows nothing about the lock service; it just compares two integers. That is the whole point: quorum for liveness, fencing for safety — two different layers for two different problems."}}}$iv$::jsonb)
