INSERT INTO interview_questions (bank_id, bank_version, question_id, sort_index, is_premium, family, tier, kind, tags, competency, leadership_tier, bodies, interviewer, grading)
VALUES ('1-system-design-mastery/18-distributed-locks-and-leader-election', '231d8cc57+37d905d9a', '1-system-design-mastery/18-distributed-locks-and-leader-election/8', 8, false, 'technical', 'senior', 'debug', ARRAY['validity-window', 'clock-drift', 'lock-expiry']::text[], NULL, NULL, $iv${"typescript":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng TypeScript/NestJS. Đọc to hàm acquire này lên, chỉ ra chính xác nó cấp lock ngay khi acquiredOn >= this.quorum rồi báo NGUYÊN ttlMs là validityMs. Ngoài ra Promise.all cũng không có timeout cho từng node, nên một node chậm/chết có thể khiến cả lời gọi mất gần bằng cả ttlMs trước khi đa số node trả lời xong. Cụ thể: với ttl=5000ms mà một trong ba node mất khoảng 4800ms để trả lời, hàm này vẫn trả acquired: true và báo khoảng 5000ms validity, trong khi thực tế chỉ còn khoảng 200ms (trừ đi clock drift) là còn dùng được. Code này pass hết khi test lúc mọi node đều khỏe (elapsed rất nhỏ). Giải thích vì sao bug hoàn toàn im lặng trên happy-path đó nhưng lại nguy hiểm khi tải/độ trễ thật xuất hiện, và sửa cho đúng.","givenCode":"```typescript\n// Acquire the lock on all independent Redis nodes in parallel, then decide on quorum.\nasync acquire(resource: string, ttlMs: number): Promise<AcquireResult> {\n    const token = randomUUID()\n    // No per-node timeout here — a slow/dead node makes this take almost the whole ttlMs.\n    const results = await Promise.all(\n        this.nodes.map(async (node) => {\n            const reply = await node.set(resource, token, \"PX\", ttlMs, \"NX\")\n            return reply === \"OK\"\n        }),\n    )\n    const acquiredOn = results.filter(Boolean).length\n    if (acquiredOn >= this.quorum) {\n        const fencingToken = this.nextFencingToken(resource)\n        return {\n            acquired: true,\n            token,\n            fencingToken,\n            acquiredOn,\n            totalNodes: this.nodes.length,\n            quorum: this.quorum,\n            validityMs: ttlMs,\n        }\n    }\n    await this.releaseEverywhere(resource, token)\n    return {\n        acquired: false,\n        token,\n        acquiredOn,\n        totalNodes: this.nodes.length,\n        quorum: this.quorum,\n        validityMs: ttlMs,\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in TypeScript/NestJS. Read this acquire function out loud, and point at exactly why it grants the lock as soon as acquiredOn >= this.quorum and then reports the FULL ttlMs as validityMs. On top of that, Promise.all has no per-node timeout, so one slow or dead node can make the whole call take almost as long as ttlMs before the majority resolves. Concretely: with ttl=5000ms and one node taking roughly 4800ms to respond, this function still returns acquired: true and reports about 5000ms of validity, when in reality only about 200ms (minus clock drift) actually remains. It passes every test where all nodes are healthy (elapsed is tiny). Explain why the bug is completely silent on that happy path but dangerous once real load or latency shows up, and fix it properly.","givenCode":"```typescript\n// Acquire the lock on all independent Redis nodes in parallel, then decide on quorum.\nasync acquire(resource: string, ttlMs: number): Promise<AcquireResult> {\n    const token = randomUUID()\n    // No per-node timeout here — a slow/dead node makes this take almost the whole ttlMs.\n    const results = await Promise.all(\n        this.nodes.map(async (node) => {\n            const reply = await node.set(resource, token, \"PX\", ttlMs, \"NX\")\n            return reply === \"OK\"\n        }),\n    )\n    const acquiredOn = results.filter(Boolean).length\n    if (acquiredOn >= this.quorum) {\n        const fencingToken = this.nextFencingToken(resource)\n        return {\n            acquired: true,\n            token,\n            fencingToken,\n            acquiredOn,\n            totalNodes: this.nodes.length,\n            quorum: this.quorum,\n            validityMs: ttlMs,\n        }\n    }\n    await this.releaseEverywhere(resource, token)\n    return {\n        acquired: false,\n        token,\n        acquiredOn,\n        totalNodes: this.nodes.length,\n        quorum: this.quorum,\n        validityMs: ttlMs,\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"java":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng java. Đọc to hàm acquire này lên, chỉ ra chính xác nó cấp lock ngay khi acquiredOn >= quorum rồi báo NGUYÊN ttlMs là validityMs. Ngoài ra vòng lặp gọi f.join() cũng không có timeout riêng cho từng node, nên một node chậm/chết có thể khiến cả hàm mất gần bằng cả ttlMs trước khi đa số node trả lời xong. Cụ thể: với ttlMs=5000 mà một trong ba node mất khoảng 4800ms để trả lời, hàm này vẫn trả acquired=true và báo khoảng 5000ms validity, trong khi thực tế chỉ còn khoảng 200ms (trừ đi clock drift) là còn dùng được. Code này pass hết khi test lúc mọi node đều khỏe (elapsed rất nhỏ). Giải thích vì sao bug hoàn toàn im lặng trên happy-path đó nhưng lại nguy hiểm khi tải/độ trễ thật xuất hiện, và sửa cho đúng.","givenCode":"```java\n// Acquire the lock on all independent Redis nodes in parallel, then decide on quorum.\npublic AcquireResult acquire(String resource, long ttlMs) {\n    String token = UUID.randomUUID().toString();\n    // No per-node timeout here — a slow/dead node makes this take almost the whole ttlMs.\n    List<CompletableFuture<Boolean>> futures = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (HostPort node : nodes) {\n        futures.add(CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> setNx(node, resource, token, ttlMs)));\n    }\n    int acquiredOn = 0;\n    for (CompletableFuture<Boolean> f : futures) {\n        if (Boolean.TRUE.equals(f.join())) acquiredOn++;\n    }\n    if (acquiredOn >= quorum) {\n        long fencingToken = nextFencingToken(resource);\n        return new AcquireResult(true, token, fencingToken, acquiredOn, nodes.size(), quorum, ttlMs);\n    }\n    releaseEverywhere(resource, token);\n    return new AcquireResult(false, token, null, acquiredOn, nodes.size(), quorum, ttlMs);\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in java. Read this acquire function out loud, and point at exactly why it grants the lock as soon as acquiredOn >= quorum and then reports the FULL ttlMs as validityMs. On top of that, the f.join() loop has no per-node timeout, so one slow or dead node can make the whole function take almost as long as ttlMs before the majority resolves. Concretely: with ttlMs=5000 and one node taking roughly 4800ms to respond, this function still returns acquired=true and reports about 5000ms of validity, when in reality only about 200ms (minus clock drift) actually remains. It passes every test where all nodes are healthy (elapsed is tiny). Explain why the bug is completely silent on that happy path but dangerous once real load or latency shows up, and fix it properly.","givenCode":"```java\n// Acquire the lock on all independent Redis nodes in parallel, then decide on quorum.\npublic AcquireResult acquire(String resource, long ttlMs) {\n    String token = UUID.randomUUID().toString();\n    // No per-node timeout here — a slow/dead node makes this take almost the whole ttlMs.\n    List<CompletableFuture<Boolean>> futures = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (HostPort node : nodes) {\n        futures.add(CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> setNx(node, resource, token, ttlMs)));\n    }\n    int acquiredOn = 0;\n    for (CompletableFuture<Boolean> f : futures) {\n        if (Boolean.TRUE.equals(f.join())) acquiredOn++;\n    }\n    if (acquiredOn >= quorum) {\n        long fencingToken = nextFencingToken(resource);\n        return new AcquireResult(true, token, fencingToken, acquiredOn, nodes.size(), quorum, ttlMs);\n    }\n    releaseEverywhere(resource, token);\n    return new AcquireResult(false, token, null, acquiredOn, nodes.size(), quorum, ttlMs);\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"csharp":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng C#/ASP.NET Core. Đọc to hàm AcquireAsync này lên, chỉ ra chính xác nó cấp lock ngay khi acquiredOn >= _quorum rồi báo NGUYÊN ttlMs là ValidityMs. Ngoài ra Task.WhenAll cũng không có timeout cho từng node, nên một node chậm/chết có thể khiến cả lời gọi mất gần bằng cả ttlMs trước khi đa số node trả lời xong. Cụ thể: với ttlMs=5000 mà một trong ba node mất khoảng 4800ms để trả lời, hàm này vẫn trả Acquired=true và báo khoảng 5000ms validity, trong khi thực tế chỉ còn khoảng 200ms (trừ đi clock drift) là còn dùng được. Code này pass hết khi test lúc mọi node đều khỏe (elapsed rất nhỏ). Giải thích vì sao bug hoàn toàn im lặng trên happy-path đó nhưng lại nguy hiểm khi tải/độ trễ thật xuất hiện, và sửa cho đúng.","givenCode":"```csharp\n// Acquire the lock on all independent Redis nodes in parallel, then decide on quorum.\npublic async Task<AcquireResult> AcquireAsync(string resource, long ttlMs)\n{\n    var token = Guid.NewGuid().ToString();\n    // No per-node timeout here — a slow/dead node makes this take almost the whole ttlMs.\n    var tasks = _nodes.Select(async node =>\n        await node.GetDatabase()\n            .StringSetAsync(resource, token, TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(ttlMs), When.NotExists));\n    var results = await Task.WhenAll(tasks);\n    var acquiredOn = results.Count(r => r);\n    if (acquiredOn >= _quorum)\n    {\n        var fencingToken = NextFencingToken(resource);\n        return new AcquireResult\n        {\n            Acquired = true,\n            Token = token,\n            FencingToken = fencingToken,\n            AcquiredOn = acquiredOn,\n            TotalNodes = _nodes.Count,\n            Quorum = _quorum,\n            ValidityMs = ttlMs,\n        };\n    }\n    await ReleaseEverywhereAsync(resource, token);\n    return new AcquireResult\n    {\n        Acquired = false,\n        Token = token,\n        AcquiredOn = acquiredOn,\n        TotalNodes = _nodes.Count,\n        Quorum = _quorum,\n        ValidityMs = ttlMs,\n    };\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in C#/ASP.NET Core. Read this AcquireAsync function out loud, and point at exactly why it grants the lock as soon as acquiredOn >= _quorum and then reports the FULL ttlMs as ValidityMs. On top of that, Task.WhenAll has no per-node timeout, so one slow or dead node can make the whole call take almost as long as ttlMs before the majority resolves. Concretely: with ttlMs=5000 and one node taking roughly 4800ms to respond, this function still returns Acquired=true and reports about 5000ms of validity, when in reality only about 200ms (minus clock drift) actually remains. It passes every test where all nodes are healthy (elapsed is tiny). Explain why the bug is completely silent on that happy path but dangerous once real load or latency shows up, and fix it properly.","givenCode":"```csharp\n// Acquire the lock on all independent Redis nodes in parallel, then decide on quorum.\npublic async Task<AcquireResult> AcquireAsync(string resource, long ttlMs)\n{\n    var token = Guid.NewGuid().ToString();\n    // No per-node timeout here — a slow/dead node makes this take almost the whole ttlMs.\n    var tasks = _nodes.Select(async node =>\n        await node.GetDatabase()\n            .StringSetAsync(resource, token, TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(ttlMs), When.NotExists));\n    var results = await Task.WhenAll(tasks);\n    var acquiredOn = results.Count(r => r);\n    if (acquiredOn >= _quorum)\n    {\n        var fencingToken = NextFencingToken(resource);\n        return new AcquireResult\n        {\n            Acquired = true,\n            Token = token,\n            FencingToken = fencingToken,\n            AcquiredOn = acquiredOn,\n            TotalNodes = _nodes.Count,\n            Quorum = _quorum,\n            ValidityMs = ttlMs,\n        };\n    }\n    await ReleaseEverywhereAsync(resource, token);\n    return new AcquireResult\n    {\n        Acquired = false,\n        Token = token,\n        AcquiredOn = acquiredOn,\n        TotalNodes = _nodes.Count,\n        Quorum = _quorum,\n        ValidityMs = ttlMs,\n    };\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"go":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng go. Đọc to hàm Acquire này lên, chỉ ra chính xác nó cấp lock ngay khi acquiredOn >= s.quorum rồi báo NGUYÊN ttlMs là ValidityMs. Ngoài ra mỗi goroutine cũng không có context.WithTimeout cho lệnh của mình, nên một node chậm/chết có thể khiến wg.Wait() chờ gần bằng cả ttlMs trước khi đa số node trả lời xong. Cụ thể: với ttlMs=5000 mà một trong ba node mất khoảng 4800ms để trả lời, hàm này vẫn trả Acquired: true và báo khoảng 5000ms validity, trong khi thực tế chỉ còn khoảng 200ms (trừ đi clock drift) là còn dùng được. Code này pass hết khi test lúc mọi node đều khỏe (elapsed rất nhỏ). Giải thích vì sao bug hoàn toàn im lặng trên happy-path đó nhưng lại nguy hiểm khi tải/độ trễ thật xuất hiện, và sửa cho đúng.","givenCode":"```go\n// Acquire fires SET NX PX at every node in parallel, then decides on quorum.\nfunc (s *Service) Acquire(resource string, ttlMs int64) AcquireResult {\n    token := uuid.NewString()\n    results := make([]bool, len(s.nodes))\n    var wg sync.WaitGroup\n    for i, node := range s.nodes {\n        wg.Add(1)\n        // No per-call timeout here — a slow/dead node makes this take almost the whole ttlMs.\n        go func(i int, node *redis.Client) {\n            defer wg.Done()\n            ok, err := node.SetNX(context.Background(), resource, token, time.Duration(ttlMs)*time.Millisecond).Result()\n            results[i] = err == nil && ok\n        }(i, node)\n    }\n    wg.Wait()\n\n    acquiredOn := 0\n    for _, r := range results {\n        if r {\n            acquiredOn++\n        }\n    }\n    if acquiredOn >= s.quorum {\n        ft := s.nextFencingToken(resource)\n        return AcquireResult{\n            Acquired:     true,\n            Token:        token,\n            FencingToken: &ft,\n            AcquiredOn:   acquiredOn,\n            TotalNodes:   len(s.nodes),\n            Quorum:       s.quorum,\n            ValidityMs:   ttlMs,\n        }\n    }\n    s.releaseEverywhere(resource, token)\n    return AcquireResult{\n        Acquired:   false,\n        Token:      token,\n        AcquiredOn: acquiredOn,\n        TotalNodes: len(s.nodes),\n        Quorum:     s.quorum,\n        ValidityMs: ttlMs,\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in go. Read this Acquire function out loud, and point at exactly why it grants the lock as soon as acquiredOn >= s.quorum and then reports the FULL ttlMs as ValidityMs. On top of that, each goroutine has no context.WithTimeout on its own call, so one slow or dead node can make wg.Wait() block for almost the whole ttlMs before the majority resolves. Concretely: with ttlMs=5000 and one node taking roughly 4800ms to respond, this function still returns Acquired: true and reports about 5000ms of validity, when in reality only about 200ms (minus clock drift) actually remains. It passes every test where all nodes are healthy (elapsed is tiny). Explain why the bug is completely silent on that happy path but dangerous once real load or latency shows up, and fix it properly.","givenCode":"```go\n// Acquire fires SET NX PX at every node in parallel, then decides on quorum.\nfunc (s *Service) Acquire(resource string, ttlMs int64) AcquireResult {\n    token := uuid.NewString()\n    results := make([]bool, len(s.nodes))\n    var wg sync.WaitGroup\n    for i, node := range s.nodes {\n        wg.Add(1)\n        // No per-call timeout here — a slow/dead node makes this take almost the whole ttlMs.\n        go func(i int, node *redis.Client) {\n            defer wg.Done()\n            ok, err := node.SetNX(context.Background(), resource, token, time.Duration(ttlMs)*time.Millisecond).Result()\n            results[i] = err == nil && ok\n        }(i, node)\n    }\n    wg.Wait()\n\n    acquiredOn := 0\n    for _, r := range results {\n        if r {\n            acquiredOn++\n        }\n    }\n    if acquiredOn >= s.quorum {\n        ft := s.nextFencingToken(resource)\n        return AcquireResult{\n            Acquired:     true,\n            Token:        token,\n            FencingToken: &ft,\n            AcquiredOn:   acquiredOn,\n            TotalNodes:   len(s.nodes),\n            Quorum:       s.quorum,\n            ValidityMs:   ttlMs,\n        }\n    }\n    s.releaseEverywhere(resource, token)\n    return AcquireResult{\n        Acquired:   false,\n        Token:      token,\n        AcquiredOn: acquiredOn,\n        TotalNodes: len(s.nodes),\n        Quorum:     s.quorum,\n        ValidityMs: ttlMs,\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null}}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"vi":{"followUps":["Sau khi sửa xong, giả sử ttl chỉ còn 500ms thay vì 5000ms — cùng một hệ số drift 1% cố định đó có còn hợp lý không, hay cần điều chỉnh, và vì sao?","Giả sử phía gọi bỏ qua hoàn toàn validityMs và chỉ check acquired === true — có lớp phòng thủ nào khác, độc lập với chỗ sửa validity này, vẫn ngăn được một writer đã hết hạn ghi đè lên storage khi cửa sổ hiệu lực thật sự đã trôi qua không?"],"hints":["So sánh cái code ĐANG KIỂM (acquiredOn >= quorum) với cái nó ĐANG TRẢ VỀ (nguyên ttl gốc) — hai thứ đó có thực sự đo cùng một điều không?","Nghĩ xem điều gì có thể khiến một trong ba lệnh SET song song mất gần bằng cả ttl để trả lời — và hiện tại có gì đang chặn chuyện đó xảy ra không?"]},"en":{"followUps":["Once fixed, suppose ttl is only 500ms instead of 5000ms — does the same fixed 1% drift factor still make sense, or would you tune it, and why?","Suppose the caller ignores validityMs entirely and only checks acquired === true — what other defense layer, independent of this validity fix, still stops a stale writer from corrupting storage once the real window has expired?"],"hints":["Compare what the code CHECKS (acquiredOn >= quorum) against what it RETURNS (the raw original ttl) — are those two things actually measuring the same thing?","Think about what could make one of the three parallel SET calls take almost as long as the whole ttl to respond — and whether anything currently stops that from happening."]}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"locales":{"vi":{"rubric":["[problemSolving] Chỉ đúng chỗ hỏng: code cấp lock ngay khi đạt quorum rồi báo NGUYÊN ttl là khoảng thời gian còn dùng được, bỏ qua thời gian mà chính acquire đã tiêu tốn (elapsed) và clock drift giữa các node — nên một node chậm có thể khiến khoảng hiệu lực thật gần như bằng không.","[technical] Sửa đúng bằng validity = ttl − elapsed − drift, thêm lại điều kiện validityMs > 0 trước khi cấp lock, và đặt per-node command timeout nhỏ hơn khoảng 10% ttl để một node chết không nuốt hết cửa sổ hiệu lực; nêu thêm khuyến nghị critical section nên xong trong khoảng an toàn (thường ≤ 30% validity).","[communication] Diễn giải được vì sao bug này im lặng và nguy hiểm: acquire \"thành công\" không hề báo lỗi, nhưng khoảng hiệu lực nó cấp đã gần như hết hạn từ trước — nên hai worker khác nhau đều có thể tin rằng mình đang giữ một lock còn mới (một lỗi vi phạm an toàn, không phải một crash dễ thấy).","[testing] Đề xuất được cách kiểm chứng: tiêm một node chậm/bị treo vào bộ ba node rồi assert acquire hoặc từ chối cấp lock hoặc trả về validityMs đã co lại đúng mức, kèm một test fencing xác nhận một write đến muộn sau khi hết validity bị storage từ chối."],"checklist":[{"text":"A distributed lock's usable window is not the raw `ttlMs` passed in — it is `ttl − elapsed − drift`, where `elapsed` is however long the acquisition itself took and `drift` compensates for clock skew between the independent Redis nodes voting on the lock.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Because `Promise.all` over the per-node `SET NX PX` calls has no per-node timeout, a single slow or dead node can stretch the real acquisition time close to the full `ttlMs` before the quorum resolves — so `elapsed` can end up nearly as large as `ttl` itself.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"Hard-coding `validityMs: ttlMs` in the return value — instead of computing it from `Date.now()` captured before the parallel `SET` calls and subtracted afterward — means the reported validity is completely disconnected from how much of that window the acquisition itself already consumed.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"The grant condition should be `acquiredOn >= quorum && validityMs > 0`, not just `acquiredOn >= quorum`; when the computed validity is zero or negative the function must fail closed — releasing the lock on every node and returning `acquired: false` — exactly like the existing failure branch already does for a missed quorum.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"The bug is silent on the happy path because when all nodes are healthy, `Promise.all` resolves almost instantly, so real elapsed time is tiny and even a correct calculation would land close to `ttlMs` — meaning the missing `elapsed`/`drift` subtraction never visibly changes the result under normal conditions.","dimension":"testing","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"With `ttl=5000ms` and one node taking roughly 4800ms to respond, the real remaining validity is only about 200ms minus drift, yet the unfixed function still reports `validityMs: 5000` and `acquired: true` — so a worker believes it holds the lock for nearly five more seconds when it is already almost expired, letting a second worker acquire the same resource and both believe they hold a valid lock simultaneously (the exact split-brain scenario Redlock's quorum mechanism exists to prevent).","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Fixing the acquisition path alone is not sufficient — the `ioredis` client should also have `commandTimeout` (and `connectTimeout`) configured to well under roughly 10% of `ttlMs`, so a single dead node is bounded and cannot drag the whole `Promise.all` call out toward the full `ttl` the way it does in the unbounded version.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Even after correctly computing `validityMs`, the critical section protected by the lock should still finish comfortably inside that window — typical guidance is using no more than about 30% of it — since a shrunk validity is not license to consume every remaining millisecond, leaving margin for release and any residual jitter.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10}],"keywords":["validity window","clock drift","quorum acquire","command timeout","fencing token"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"The usable lock window isn't the raw ttlMs you passed in, it's ttl minus however long acquisition itself took minus clock drift between the Redis nodes voting on it. Because the Promise.all over the per-node SET NX PX calls has no per-node timeout, one slow or dead node can stretch that acquisition time out to almost the full ttl before quorum resolves. But the code hardcodes validityMs to ttlMs instead of computing it from Date.now() captured before and after the parallel calls, so the reported validity is completely disconnected from what actually got consumed. The grant condition should be quorum reached AND validityMs greater than zero, not just quorum — when validity comes out zero or negative it needs to fail closed, releasing the lock everywhere and returning acquired:false, same as the existing quorum-miss branch. This bug is silent on the happy path because when nodes are healthy Promise.all resolves almost instantly, so even a correct calculation lands close to ttlMs anyway. But with, say, a 5 second ttl and one node taking 4800ms, the real remaining validity is more like 200ms minus drift, and the unfixed version still reports validityMs 5000 and acquired true — so a worker thinks it has five more seconds when it's basically already expired, letting a second worker grab the same resource. On top of fixing the calculation, ioredis should have commandTimeout and connectTimeout set to well under 10% of ttlMs so one dead node can't drag things out, and the critical section itself should stay under roughly 30% of the validity window.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"Because the Promise.all over the per-node SET NX PX calls has no per-node timeout, a single slow or dead node can stretch the real acquisition time out to nearly the full ttl before quorum even resolves. The problem is the code hardcodes validityMs to ttlMs instead of computing it from Date.now() before and after the parallel calls, so the reported validity has nothing to do with how much of that window actually got used. The grant condition needs to be quorum reached and validityMs greater than zero, not just quorum, and when it computes to zero or negative it should fail closed and release the lock everywhere, the same way the quorum-miss path already does. This bug hides on the happy path because when all the nodes are healthy Promise.all resolves almost instantly, so elapsed time is tiny and even the correct calculation would land close to ttlMs anyway. I'd also set commandTimeout and connectTimeout on the ioredis client well under 10% of ttlMs so one dead node can't drag out the whole call, and keep the actual critical section under about 30% of whatever validity comes out.","sonnet":80,"haiku":80},{"level":3,"answer":"The core bug is that validityMs gets hardcoded to ttlMs instead of being computed from Date.now() captured before and after the parallel acquisition calls, so what gets reported has nothing to do with how much of the window was actually consumed getting quorum. The grant condition should require validityMs to be greater than zero in addition to reaching quorum, and when it comes out zero or negative the function needs to fail closed instead of returning acquired true. This stays hidden on the happy path because when all the nodes respond quickly, elapsed time is tiny, so even a properly computed validity would land close to ttlMs anyway — the difference only shows up once a node is slow.","sonnet":45,"haiku":45},{"level":4,"answer":"I think this bug would be pretty hard to catch just from normal testing, because when everything's healthy the calls all come back fast, so you wouldn't really notice anything wrong with the numbers under normal conditions.","sonnet":15,"haiku":15},{"level":5,"answer":"I'd probably add retries around the SET NX PX calls in case a node briefly fails to respond, and maybe log a warning if any node in the quorum takes longer than expected, just so we have visibility into slow nodes.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]},"en":{"rubric":["[problemSolving] Spots that the code grants the lock as soon as quorum is reached and then reports the FULL ttl as the usable window, ignoring both the time acquire itself consumed (elapsed) and clock drift across the nodes — so a single slow node can leave almost zero real validity.","[technical] Fixes it correctly with validity = ttl − elapsed − drift, restores the validityMs > 0 guard before granting, and adds a per-node command timeout under roughly 10% of ttl so a dead node cannot swallow the whole window; adds the guidance that the critical section should finish well inside validity (typically <= 30%).","[communication] Explains why the bug is silent and dangerous: acquire \"succeeds\" with no error at all, but the validity window it grants was already effectively gone — so two different workers can each believe they hold a fresh lock (a safety violation, not an obvious crash).","[testing] Proposes a concrete verification: inject a slow/paused node into the trio and assert acquire either refuses or returns a correctly shrunken validityMs, plus a fencing test confirming a write that arrives after validity has expired is rejected at storage."],"checklist":[{"text":"A distributed lock's usable window is not the raw `ttlMs` passed in — it is `ttl − elapsed − drift`, where `elapsed` is however long the acquisition itself took and `drift` compensates for clock skew between the independent Redis nodes voting on the lock.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Because `Promise.all` over the per-node `SET NX PX` calls has no per-node timeout, a single slow or dead node can stretch the real acquisition time close to the full `ttlMs` before the quorum resolves — so `elapsed` can end up nearly as large as `ttl` itself.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"Hard-coding `validityMs: ttlMs` in the return value — instead of computing it from `Date.now()` captured before the parallel `SET` calls and subtracted afterward — means the reported validity is completely disconnected from how much of that window the acquisition itself already consumed.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"The grant condition should be `acquiredOn >= quorum && validityMs > 0`, not just `acquiredOn >= quorum`; when the computed validity is zero or negative the function must fail closed — releasing the lock on every node and returning `acquired: false` — exactly like the existing failure branch already does for a missed quorum.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"The bug is silent on the happy path because when all nodes are healthy, `Promise.all` resolves almost instantly, so real elapsed time is tiny and even a correct calculation would land close to `ttlMs` — meaning the missing `elapsed`/`drift` subtraction never visibly changes the result under normal conditions.","dimension":"testing","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"With `ttl=5000ms` and one node taking roughly 4800ms to respond, the real remaining validity is only about 200ms minus drift, yet the unfixed function still reports `validityMs: 5000` and `acquired: true` — so a worker believes it holds the lock for nearly five more seconds when it is already almost expired, letting a second worker acquire the same resource and both believe they hold a valid lock simultaneously (the exact split-brain scenario Redlock's quorum mechanism exists to prevent).","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Fixing the acquisition path alone is not sufficient — the `ioredis` client should also have `commandTimeout` (and `connectTimeout`) configured to well under roughly 10% of `ttlMs`, so a single dead node is bounded and cannot drag the whole `Promise.all` call out toward the full `ttl` the way it does in the unbounded version.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Even after correctly computing `validityMs`, the critical section protected by the lock should still finish comfortably inside that window — typical guidance is using no more than about 30% of it — since a shrunk validity is not license to consume every remaining millisecond, leaving margin for release and any residual jitter.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10}],"keywords":["validity window","clock drift","quorum acquire","command timeout","fencing token"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"The usable lock window isn't the raw ttlMs you passed in, it's ttl minus however long acquisition itself took minus clock drift between the Redis nodes voting on it. Because the Promise.all over the per-node SET NX PX calls has no per-node timeout, one slow or dead node can stretch that acquisition time out to almost the full ttl before quorum resolves. But the code hardcodes validityMs to ttlMs instead of computing it from Date.now() captured before and after the parallel calls, so the reported validity is completely disconnected from what actually got consumed. The grant condition should be quorum reached AND validityMs greater than zero, not just quorum — when validity comes out zero or negative it needs to fail closed, releasing the lock everywhere and returning acquired:false, same as the existing quorum-miss branch. This bug is silent on the happy path because when nodes are healthy Promise.all resolves almost instantly, so even a correct calculation lands close to ttlMs anyway. But with, say, a 5 second ttl and one node taking 4800ms, the real remaining validity is more like 200ms minus drift, and the unfixed version still reports validityMs 5000 and acquired true — so a worker thinks it has five more seconds when it's basically already expired, letting a second worker grab the same resource. On top of fixing the calculation, ioredis should have commandTimeout and connectTimeout set to well under 10% of ttlMs so one dead node can't drag things out, and the critical section itself should stay under roughly 30% of the validity window.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"Because the Promise.all over the per-node SET NX PX calls has no per-node timeout, a single slow or dead node can stretch the real acquisition time out to nearly the full ttl before quorum even resolves. The problem is the code hardcodes validityMs to ttlMs instead of computing it from Date.now() before and after the parallel calls, so the reported validity has nothing to do with how much of that window actually got used. The grant condition needs to be quorum reached and validityMs greater than zero, not just quorum, and when it computes to zero or negative it should fail closed and release the lock everywhere, the same way the quorum-miss path already does. This bug hides on the happy path because when all the nodes are healthy Promise.all resolves almost instantly, so elapsed time is tiny and even the correct calculation would land close to ttlMs anyway. I'd also set commandTimeout and connectTimeout on the ioredis client well under 10% of ttlMs so one dead node can't drag out the whole call, and keep the actual critical section under about 30% of whatever validity comes out.","sonnet":80,"haiku":80},{"level":3,"answer":"The core bug is that validityMs gets hardcoded to ttlMs instead of being computed from Date.now() captured before and after the parallel acquisition calls, so what gets reported has nothing to do with how much of the window was actually consumed getting quorum. The grant condition should require validityMs to be greater than zero in addition to reaching quorum, and when it comes out zero or negative the function needs to fail closed instead of returning acquired true. This stays hidden on the happy path because when all the nodes respond quickly, elapsed time is tiny, so even a properly computed validity would land close to ttlMs anyway — the difference only shows up once a node is slow.","sonnet":45,"haiku":45},{"level":4,"answer":"I think this bug would be pretty hard to catch just from normal testing, because when everything's healthy the calls all come back fast, so you wouldn't really notice anything wrong with the numbers under normal conditions.","sonnet":15,"haiku":15},{"level":5,"answer":"I'd probably add retries around the SET NX PX calls in case a node briefly fails to respond, and maybe log a warning if any node in the quorum takes longer than expected, just so we have visibility into slow nodes.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]}},"ideal":{"typescript":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nBug không nằm ở việc đếm quorum — `acquiredOn >= this.quorum` là đúng — nó nằm ở CHỖ hàm này định nghĩa `validityMs`. Một lock KHÔNG có hiệu lực trong suốt `ttlMs`; nó chỉ an toàn trong khoảng `validity = ttl − elapsed − drift`. `elapsed` phải trừ đi chính thời gian mà `acquire` đã tiêu tốn (bao gồm cả một node chậm), còn `drift` (thường là `ttlMs * 0.01`) đền cho clock skew tương đối giữa ba voter. Đoạn code trên không tính `elapsed` (không có `Date.now()` lúc bắt đầu để trừ), không trừ drift, và gán thẳng `validityMs: ttlMs` ở cả hai nhánh — nên điều kiện cấp lock chỉ còn là `acquiredOn >= this.quorum`, thiếu hẳn vế `validityMs > 0` lẽ ra phải có. Vì sao im lặng trên happy-path: khi cả ba node đều khỏe, `Promise.all` trả về gần như tức thời, `elapsed` thực tế cực nhỏ nên dù có tính đúng thì `validityMs` cũng gần bằng `ttlMs` — bug không lộ ra chút nào. Nhưng với `ttl=5000ms` mà một node mất ~4800ms để trả lời (không có timeout nào chặn `Promise.all` chờ nó), `elapsed` thực tế đã ăn gần hết `ttl`; hàm đúng lẽ phải trả về `validityMs` chỉ còn khoảng 200ms trừ drift — nhưng code này vẫn báo `validityMs: 5000`. Một worker nhận lock tưởng mình còn gần 5 giây để làm critical section, trong khi thực tế lock đã gần hết hạn ngay lúc vừa cấp — worker thứ hai có thể acquire lại và cả hai đều tin mình đang giữ lock hợp lệ, y hệt kiểu split-brain mà Redlock lẽ ra phải ngăn. Sửa: thêm `const start = Date.now()` trước `Promise.all`, tính `const elapsed = Date.now() - start` sau khi có kết quả, rồi `const validityMs = ttlMs - elapsed - Math.floor(ttlMs * this.driftFactor)`; điều kiện cấp lock phải là `acquiredOn >= this.quorum && validityMs > 0` — khi hết hiệu lực thì fail closed (release best-effort trên mọi node) đúng như nhánh else vẫn đang làm. Song song đó phải cấu hình `commandTimeout` (và `connectTimeout`) trên client `ioredis` nhỏ hơn khoảng 10% `ttlMs`, để một node chết không thể kéo `Promise.all` gần hết `ttl` như trong kịch bản trên. Cuối cùng, kể cả sau khi sửa, critical section vẫn nên hoàn tất thoải mái bên trong `validityMs` (khuyến nghị thường là ≤ 30%) — validity co lại không có nghĩa là được phép dùng sát đến giây cuối cùng.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe bug is not in the quorum count — `acquiredOn >= this.quorum` is correct — it's in WHERE this function defines `validityMs`. A lock is not valid for the entire `ttlMs`; it is only safe for `validity = ttl − elapsed − drift`. `elapsed` must subtract the time `acquire` itself consumed (including a slow node), and `drift` (typically `ttlMs * 0.01`) compensates for relative clock skew across the three voters. The code above never computes `elapsed` (there is no `Date.now()` captured at the start to subtract from), never subtracts drift, and hard-codes `validityMs: ttlMs` in both branches — so the grant condition collapses to just `acquiredOn >= this.quorum`, missing the `validityMs > 0` clause it should have. Why it's silent on the happy path: when all three nodes are healthy, `Promise.all` resolves nearly instantly, so real `elapsed` is tiny and even a correct calculation would land `validityMs` close to `ttlMs` — the bug never surfaces. But with `ttl=5000ms` and one node taking ~4800ms to respond (nothing bounds how long `Promise.all` waits on it), real `elapsed` has eaten almost the entire `ttl`; a correct implementation would return `validityMs` around 200ms minus drift — yet this code still reports `validityMs: 5000`. A worker that gets the lock believes it has nearly 5 seconds for its critical section, when the lock is already nearly expired the moment it was granted — a second worker can acquire it right after, and both end up believing they hold a valid lock, exactly the split-brain Redlock is supposed to prevent. The fix: capture `const start = Date.now()` before `Promise.all`, compute `const elapsed = Date.now() - start` once the results are in, then `const validityMs = ttlMs - elapsed - Math.floor(ttlMs * this.driftFactor)`; the grant condition must be `acquiredOn >= this.quorum && validityMs > 0` — when validity is gone, fail closed (best-effort release on every node) exactly like the existing else branch already does. Alongside that, configure `commandTimeout` (and `connectTimeout`) on the `ioredis` client to something under roughly 10% of `ttlMs`, so a dead node cannot drag `Promise.all` out to nearly the whole `ttl` the way it does in this scenario. Finally, even after the fix, the critical section should still finish comfortably inside `validityMs` (typical guidance is <= 30%) — a shrunk validity is not license to use every last millisecond of it."},"java":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nBug không nằm ở việc đếm quorum — `acquiredOn >= quorum` là đúng — nó nằm ở CHỖ hàm này định nghĩa validity. Một lock KHÔNG có hiệu lực trong suốt `ttlMs`; nó chỉ an toàn trong khoảng `validity = ttl − elapsed − drift`. `elapsed` phải trừ đi chính thời gian mà `acquire` đã tiêu tốn (bao gồm cả một node chậm), còn `drift` (thường là `ttlMs * 0.01`) đền cho clock skew tương đối giữa ba voter. Đoạn code trên không đo `elapsed` (không có mốc thời gian bắt đầu bằng `System.nanoTime()` để trừ), không trừ drift, và truyền thẳng `ttlMs` làm giá trị validity ở cả hai nhánh return — nên điều kiện cấp lock chỉ còn `acquiredOn >= quorum`, thiếu hẳn vế `validityMs > 0` lẽ ra phải có. Vì sao im lặng trên happy-path: khi cả ba node đều khỏe, vòng lặp `f.join()` trả về gần như tức thời, `elapsed` thực tế cực nhỏ nên dù có tính đúng thì validity cũng gần bằng `ttlMs` — bug không lộ ra chút nào. Nhưng với `ttlMs=5000` mà một node mất ~4800ms để trả lời (không có gì chặn `f.join()` chờ nó), `elapsed` thực tế đã ăn gần hết ttl; hàm đúng lẽ phải trả về validity chỉ còn khoảng 200ms trừ drift — nhưng code này vẫn báo 5000ms. Một worker nhận lock tưởng mình còn gần 5 giây để làm critical section, trong khi thực tế lock đã gần hết hạn ngay lúc vừa cấp — worker thứ hai có thể acquire lại và cả hai đều tin mình đang giữ lock hợp lệ, y hệt kiểu split-brain mà Redlock lẽ ra phải ngăn. Sửa: bắt mốc `long start = System.nanoTime()` trước vòng lặp tạo future, tính `long elapsedMs = (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1_000_000` sau khi mọi future đã `join()` xong, rồi `long validityMs = ttlMs - elapsedMs - (long) Math.floor(ttlMs * driftFactor)`; điều kiện cấp lock phải là `acquiredOn >= quorum && validityMs > 0` — hết hiệu lực thì fail closed (release best-effort trên mọi node) đúng như nhánh else vẫn đang làm. Song song đó phải đặt timeout cho từng lệnh `SET` — ví dụ socket timeout ngắn trên kết nối `Jedis` dùng trong `setNx`, hoặc bọc mỗi future bằng `.orTimeout(...)` — nhỏ hơn khoảng 10% `ttlMs`, để một node chết không thể kéo vòng `join()` gần hết ttl như trong kịch bản trên. Cuối cùng, kể cả sau khi sửa, critical section vẫn nên hoàn tất thoải mái bên trong validity (khuyến nghị thường là ≤ 30%) — validity co lại không có nghĩa là được phép dùng sát đến mili-giây cuối cùng.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe bug is not in the quorum count — `acquiredOn >= quorum` is correct — it's in WHERE this function defines validity. A lock is not valid for the entire `ttlMs`; it is only safe for `validity = ttl − elapsed − drift`. `elapsed` must subtract the time `acquire` itself consumed (including a slow node), and `drift` (typically `ttlMs * 0.01`) compensates for relative clock skew across the three voters. The code above never measures `elapsed` (there is no starting timestamp captured with `System.nanoTime()` to subtract from), never subtracts drift, and passes `ttlMs` straight through as the validity value in both return branches — so the grant condition collapses to just `acquiredOn >= quorum`, missing the `validityMs > 0` clause it should have. Why it's silent on the happy path: when all three nodes are healthy, the `f.join()` loop resolves nearly instantly, so real `elapsed` is tiny and even a correct calculation would land validity close to `ttlMs` — the bug never surfaces. But with `ttlMs=5000` and one node taking ~4800ms to respond (nothing bounds how long `f.join()` waits on it), real `elapsed` has eaten almost the entire ttl; a correct implementation would return validity around 200ms minus drift — yet this code still reports 5000ms. A worker that gets the lock believes it has nearly 5 seconds for its critical section, when the lock is already nearly expired the moment it was granted — a second worker can acquire it right after, and both end up believing they hold a valid lock, exactly the split-brain Redlock is supposed to prevent. The fix: capture `long start = System.nanoTime()` before the loop that builds the futures, compute `long elapsedMs = (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1_000_000` once every future has been `join()`ed, then `long validityMs = ttlMs - elapsedMs - (long) Math.floor(ttlMs * driftFactor)`; the grant condition must be `acquiredOn >= quorum && validityMs > 0` — when validity is gone, fail closed (best-effort release on every node) exactly like the existing else branch already does. Alongside that, put a timeout on each individual `SET` call — for example a short socket timeout on the `Jedis` connection used inside `setNx`, or wrapping each future with `.orTimeout(...)` — under roughly 10% of `ttlMs`, so a dead node cannot drag the `join()` loop out to nearly the whole ttl the way it does in this scenario. Finally, even after the fix, the critical section should still finish comfortably inside validity (typical guidance is <= 30%) — a shrunk validity is not license to use every last millisecond of it."},"csharp":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nBug không nằm ở việc đếm quorum — `acquiredOn >= _quorum` là đúng — nó nằm ở CHỖ hàm này định nghĩa `ValidityMs`. Một lock KHÔNG có hiệu lực trong suốt `ttlMs`; nó chỉ an toàn trong khoảng `validity = ttl − elapsed − drift`. `elapsed` phải trừ đi chính thời gian mà `AcquireAsync` đã tiêu tốn (bao gồm cả một node chậm), còn `drift` (thường là `ttlMs * 0.01`) đền cho clock skew tương đối giữa ba voter. Đoạn code trên không đo `elapsed` (không có mốc `DateTime.UtcNow` lúc bắt đầu để trừ), không trừ drift, và gán thẳng `ttlMs` cho `ValidityMs` ở cả hai nhánh return — nên điều kiện cấp lock chỉ còn `acquiredOn >= _quorum`, thiếu hẳn vế `validityMs > 0` lẽ ra phải có. Vì sao im lặng trên happy-path: khi cả ba node đều khỏe, `Task.WhenAll` trả về gần như tức thời, `elapsed` thực tế cực nhỏ nên dù có tính đúng thì `ValidityMs` cũng gần bằng `ttlMs` — bug không lộ ra chút nào. Nhưng với `ttlMs=5000` mà một node mất ~4800ms để trả lời (không có gì chặn `Task.WhenAll` chờ nó), `elapsed` thực tế đã ăn gần hết ttl; hàm đúng lẽ phải trả về `ValidityMs` chỉ còn khoảng 200ms trừ drift — nhưng code này vẫn báo 5000ms. Một worker nhận lock tưởng mình còn gần 5 giây để làm critical section, trong khi thực tế lock đã gần hết hạn ngay lúc vừa cấp — worker thứ hai có thể acquire lại và cả hai đều tin mình đang giữ lock hợp lệ, y hệt kiểu split-brain mà Redlock lẽ ra phải ngăn. Sửa: bắt mốc `var start = DateTime.UtcNow` trước khi tạo các task, tính `var elapsed = (long)(DateTime.UtcNow - start).TotalMilliseconds` sau khi `Task.WhenAll` hoàn tất, rồi `var validityMs = ttlMs - elapsed - (long)Math.Floor(ttlMs * _driftFactor)`; điều kiện cấp lock phải là `acquiredOn >= _quorum && validityMs > 0` — hết hiệu lực thì fail closed (release best-effort trên mọi node) đúng như nhánh else vẫn đang làm. Song song đó phải cấu hình `connectTimeout`/`syncTimeout` trên `ConnectionMultiplexer` của StackExchange.Redis nhỏ hơn khoảng 10% `ttlMs` — hoặc bọc mỗi task bằng `.WaitAsync(timeout)` — để một node chết không thể kéo `Task.WhenAll` gần hết ttl như trong kịch bản trên. Cuối cùng, kể cả sau khi sửa, critical section vẫn nên hoàn tất thoải mái bên trong `ValidityMs` (khuyến nghị thường là ≤ 30%) — validity co lại không có nghĩa là được phép dùng sát đến mili-giây cuối cùng.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe bug is not in the quorum count — `acquiredOn >= _quorum` is correct — it's in WHERE this function defines `ValidityMs`. A lock is not valid for the entire `ttlMs`; it is only safe for `validity = ttl − elapsed − drift`. `elapsed` must subtract the time `AcquireAsync` itself consumed (including a slow node), and `drift` (typically `ttlMs * 0.01`) compensates for relative clock skew across the three voters. The code above never measures `elapsed` (there is no `DateTime.UtcNow` timestamp captured at the start to subtract from), never subtracts drift, and assigns `ttlMs` straight to `ValidityMs` in both return branches — so the grant condition collapses to just `acquiredOn >= _quorum`, missing the `validityMs > 0` clause it should have. Why it's silent on the happy path: when all three nodes are healthy, `Task.WhenAll` resolves nearly instantly, so real `elapsed` is tiny and even a correct calculation would land `ValidityMs` close to `ttlMs` — the bug never surfaces. But with `ttlMs=5000` and one node taking ~4800ms to respond (nothing bounds how long `Task.WhenAll` waits on it), real `elapsed` has eaten almost the entire ttl; a correct implementation would return `ValidityMs` around 200ms minus drift — yet this code still reports 5000ms. A worker that gets the lock believes it has nearly 5 seconds for its critical section, when the lock is already nearly expired the moment it was granted — a second worker can acquire it right after, and both end up believing they hold a valid lock, exactly the split-brain Redlock is supposed to prevent. The fix: capture `var start = DateTime.UtcNow` before building the tasks, compute `var elapsed = (long)(DateTime.UtcNow - start).TotalMilliseconds` once `Task.WhenAll` completes, then `var validityMs = ttlMs - elapsed - (long)Math.Floor(ttlMs * _driftFactor)`; the grant condition must be `acquiredOn >= _quorum && validityMs > 0` — when validity is gone, fail closed (best-effort release on every node) exactly like the existing else branch already does. Alongside that, configure `connectTimeout`/`syncTimeout` on the StackExchange.Redis `ConnectionMultiplexer` to something under roughly 10% of `ttlMs` — or wrap each task with `.WaitAsync(timeout)` — so a dead node cannot drag `Task.WhenAll` out to nearly the whole ttl the way it does in this scenario. Finally, even after the fix, the critical section should still finish comfortably inside `ValidityMs` (typical guidance is <= 30%) — a shrunk validity is not license to use every last millisecond of it."},"go":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nBug không nằm ở việc đếm quorum — `acquiredOn >= s.quorum` là đúng — nó nằm ở CHỖ hàm này định nghĩa `ValidityMs`. Một lock KHÔNG có hiệu lực trong suốt `ttlMs`; nó chỉ an toàn trong khoảng `validity = ttl − elapsed − drift`. `elapsed` phải trừ đi chính thời gian mà `Acquire` đã tiêu tốn (bao gồm cả một node chậm), còn `drift` (thường là `ttlMs * 0.01`) đền cho clock skew tương đối giữa ba voter. Đoạn code trên không đo `elapsedMs` (không có mốc `time.Now()` lúc bắt đầu để trừ), không trừ drift, và gán thẳng `ttlMs` cho `ValidityMs` ở cả hai nhánh return — nên điều kiện cấp lock chỉ còn `acquiredOn >= s.quorum`, thiếu hẳn vế `validityMs > 0` lẽ ra phải có. Vì sao im lặng trên happy-path: khi cả ba node đều khỏe, `wg.Wait()` trả về gần như tức thời, `elapsedMs` thực tế cực nhỏ nên dù có tính đúng thì `ValidityMs` cũng gần bằng `ttlMs` — bug không lộ ra chút nào. Nhưng với `ttlMs=5000` mà một node mất ~4800ms để trả lời (không có `context.WithTimeout` nào chặn goroutine của nó), `elapsedMs` thực tế đã ăn gần hết ttl; hàm đúng lẽ phải trả về `ValidityMs` chỉ còn khoảng 200ms trừ drift — nhưng code này vẫn báo 5000ms. Một worker nhận lock tưởng mình còn gần 5 giây để làm critical section, trong khi thực tế lock đã gần hết hạn ngay lúc vừa cấp — worker thứ hai có thể acquire lại và cả hai đều tin mình đang giữ lock hợp lệ, y hệt kiểu split-brain mà Redlock lẽ ra phải ngăn. Sửa: bắt mốc `start := time.Now()` trước khi tạo các goroutine, tính `elapsedMs := time.Since(start).Milliseconds()` sau `wg.Wait()`, rồi `validityMs := ttlMs - elapsedMs - int64(math.Floor(float64(ttlMs)*driftFactor))`; điều kiện cấp lock phải là `acquiredOn >= s.quorum && validityMs > 0` — hết hiệu lực thì fail closed (release best-effort trên mọi node) đúng như nhánh else vẫn đang làm. Song song đó phải thay `context.Background()` bằng `ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), d)` với `d` nhỏ hơn khoảng 10% `ttlMs` cho mỗi lệnh `SetNX`, để một node chết không thể kéo `wg.Wait()` gần hết ttl như trong kịch bản trên. Cuối cùng, kể cả sau khi sửa, critical section vẫn nên hoàn tất thoải mái bên trong `ValidityMs` (khuyến nghị thường là ≤ 30%) — validity co lại không có nghĩa là được phép dùng sát đến mili-giây cuối cùng.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe bug is not in the quorum count — `acquiredOn >= s.quorum` is correct — it's in WHERE this function defines `ValidityMs`. A lock is not valid for the entire `ttlMs`; it is only safe for `validity = ttl − elapsed − drift`. `elapsed` must subtract the time `Acquire` itself consumed (including a slow node), and `drift` (typically `ttlMs * 0.01`) compensates for relative clock skew across the three voters. The code above never measures `elapsedMs` (there is no `time.Now()` timestamp captured at the start to subtract from), never subtracts drift, and assigns `ttlMs` straight to `ValidityMs` in both return branches — so the grant condition collapses to just `acquiredOn >= s.quorum`, missing the `validityMs > 0` clause it should have. Why it's silent on the happy path: when all three nodes are healthy, `wg.Wait()` returns nearly instantly, so real `elapsedMs` is tiny and even a correct calculation would land `ValidityMs` close to `ttlMs` — the bug never surfaces. But with `ttlMs=5000` and one node taking ~4800ms to respond (nothing bounds its goroutine with a `context.WithTimeout`), real `elapsedMs` has eaten almost the entire ttl; a correct implementation would return `ValidityMs` around 200ms minus drift — yet this code still reports 5000ms. A worker that gets the lock believes it has nearly 5 seconds for its critical section, when the lock is already nearly expired the moment it was granted — a second worker can acquire it right after, and both end up believing they hold a valid lock, exactly the split-brain Redlock is supposed to prevent. The fix: capture `start := time.Now()` before spawning the goroutines, compute `elapsedMs := time.Since(start).Milliseconds()` after `wg.Wait()`, then `validityMs := ttlMs - elapsedMs - int64(math.Floor(float64(ttlMs)*driftFactor))`; the grant condition must be `acquiredOn >= s.quorum && validityMs > 0` — when validity is gone, fail closed (best-effort release on every node) exactly like the existing else branch already does. Alongside that, replace `context.Background()` with `ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), d)` where `d` is under roughly 10% of `ttlMs` for each `SetNX` call, so a dead node cannot drag `wg.Wait()` out to nearly the whole ttl the way it does in this scenario. Finally, even after the fix, the critical section should still finish comfortably inside `ValidityMs` (typical guidance is <= 30%) — a shrunk validity is not license to use every last millisecond of it."}}}$iv$::jsonb)
