INSERT INTO interview_questions (bank_id, bank_version, question_id, sort_index, is_premium, family, tier, kind, tags, competency, leadership_tier, bodies, interviewer, grading)
VALUES ('1-system-design-mastery/18-distributed-locks-and-leader-election', '231d8cc57+37d905d9a', '1-system-design-mastery/18-distributed-locks-and-leader-election/11', 11, false, 'technical', 'middle', 'optimize', ARRAY['linearizable-reads', 'serializable-reads', 'read-index']::text[], NULL, NULL, $iv${"typescript":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng TypeScript — khái niệm áp dụng cho mọi stack.) Một coordination service dựng trên etcd3 (Node) đọc etcd ở hot path, và p99 read latency của nó đang quá cao. Mọi read. Mặc định đó là linearizable: mỗi read phải trả một round-trip ReadIndex, nơi leader xác nhận mình vẫn còn là leader trước khi trả lời. Điểm chậm: linearizable read tốn khoảng 1 round-trip leader-to-follower mỗi lần, và service này phải trả giá đó ngay cả với những read mà một câu trả lời stale vài chục ms là hoàn toàn ổn (dashboard, metrics), trong khi những read thực sự quan trọng lại bị trộn chung, không có gì phân biệt. Hãy tư duy thành lời, không IDE: bạn cắt read latency thế nào mà không phá vỡ correctness?","givenCode":"```typescript\n// Mọi read ở đây đều mặc định dùng mode linearizable của etcd3 — kể cả dashboard\n// aggregate được poll mỗi giây, vẫn trả cùng một round-trip ReadIndex như read\n// lock-owner bên dưới, dù một con số stale vài chục ms là hoàn toàn chấp nhận được.\nasync function readLockOwner(lockKey: string) {\n  const res = await this.etcd.get(lockKey).exec()\n  return res.kvs[0]?.value.toString() ?? null\n}\n\nasync function readDashboardMetrics() {\n  const res = await this.etcd.getAll().prefix('metrics/').exec()\n  return res.kvs.map((kv) => ({ key: kv.key.toString(), value: kv.value.toString() }))\n}\n\nsetInterval(() => readDashboardMetrics(), 1000)\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated with TypeScript — the concept applies to any stack.) A coordination service built on etcd3 (Node) reads etcd on a hot path, and its p99 read latency is too high. Every read. That default is linearizable: each read pays a ReadIndex round-trip where the leader confirms it is still leader before serving. The slowness: linearizable reads cost ~1 leader-to-follower round-trip apiece, and the service pays that even for reads where a few-tens-of-ms-stale answer would be perfectly fine (dashboards, metrics), while the genuinely critical reads are mixed in with no distinction. Reason out loud, no IDE: how do you cut the read latency without breaking correctness?","givenCode":"```typescript\n// Every read here defaults to etcd3's linearizable mode — including the dashboard\n// aggregate polled once a second, which pays the same ReadIndex round-trip as the\n// lock-owner read even though a few-tens-of-ms-stale count would be perfectly fine.\nasync function readLockOwner(lockKey: string) {\n  const res = await this.etcd.get(lockKey).exec()\n  return res.kvs[0]?.value.toString() ?? null\n}\n\nasync function readDashboardMetrics() {\n  const res = await this.etcd.getAll().prefix('metrics/').exec()\n  return res.kvs.map((kv) => ({ key: kv.key.toString(), value: kv.value.toString() }))\n}\n\nsetInterval(() => readDashboardMetrics(), 1000)\n```","diagram":null}},"java":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng Java — khái niệm áp dụng cho mọi stack.) Một coordination service dựng trên jetcd đọc etcd ở hot path, và p99 read latency của nó đang quá cao. Mọi read. Mặc định đó là linearizable: mỗi read phải trả một round-trip ReadIndex, nơi leader xác nhận mình vẫn còn là leader trước khi trả lời. Điểm chậm: linearizable read tốn khoảng 1 round-trip leader-to-follower mỗi lần, và service này phải trả giá đó ngay cả với những read mà một câu trả lời stale vài chục ms là hoàn toàn ổn (dashboard, metrics), trong khi những read thực sự quan trọng lại bị trộn chung, không có gì phân biệt. Hãy tư duy thành lời, không IDE: bạn cắt read latency thế nào mà không phá vỡ correctness?","givenCode":"```java\n// Mọi read ở đây đều mặc định dùng mode linearizable của jetcd — kể cả dashboard\n// aggregate được poll mỗi giây, vẫn trả cùng một round-trip ReadIndex như read\n// lock-owner bên dưới, dù một con số stale vài chục ms là hoàn toàn chấp nhận được.\nOptional<KeyValue> readLockOwner(String lockKey) throws Exception {\n    GetResponse res = client.getKVClient()\n        .get(ByteSequence.from(lockKey, UTF_8))\n        .get(TIMEOUT_MS, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);\n    return res.getKvs().stream().findFirst();\n}\n\nList<KeyValue> readDashboardMetrics() throws Exception {\n    GetResponse res = client.getKVClient()\n        .get(ByteSequence.from(\"metrics/\", UTF_8), GetOption.builder().isPrefix(true).build())\n        .get(TIMEOUT_MS, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);\n    return res.getKvs();\n}\n\nscheduler.scheduleAtFixedRate(this::readDashboardMetrics, 0, 1, TimeUnit.SECONDS);\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in Java — the concept applies to any stack.) A coordination service built on jetcd reads etcd on a hot path, and its p99 read latency is too high. Every read. That default is linearizable: each read pays a ReadIndex round-trip where the leader confirms it is still leader before serving. The slowness: linearizable reads cost ~1 leader-to-follower round-trip apiece, and the service pays that even for reads where a few-tens-of-ms-stale answer would be perfectly fine (dashboards, metrics), while the genuinely critical reads are mixed in with no distinction. Reason out loud, no IDE: how do you cut the read latency without breaking correctness?","givenCode":"```java\n// Every read here defaults to jetcd's linearizable mode — including the dashboard\n// aggregate polled once a second, which pays the same ReadIndex round-trip as the\n// lock-owner read even though a few-tens-of-ms-stale count would be perfectly fine.\nOptional<KeyValue> readLockOwner(String lockKey) throws Exception {\n    GetResponse res = client.getKVClient()\n        .get(ByteSequence.from(lockKey, UTF_8))\n        .get(TIMEOUT_MS, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);\n    return res.getKvs().stream().findFirst();\n}\n\nList<KeyValue> readDashboardMetrics() throws Exception {\n    GetResponse res = client.getKVClient()\n        .get(ByteSequence.from(\"metrics/\", UTF_8), GetOption.builder().isPrefix(true).build())\n        .get(TIMEOUT_MS, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);\n    return res.getKvs();\n}\n\nscheduler.scheduleAtFixedRate(this::readDashboardMetrics, 0, 1, TimeUnit.SECONDS);\n```","diagram":null}},"csharp":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng C#. Mọi read. Mặc định đó là linearizable: mỗi read phải trả một round-trip ReadIndex, nơi leader xác nhận mình vẫn còn là leader trước khi trả lời. Điểm chậm: linearizable read tốn khoảng 1 round-trip leader-to-follower mỗi lần, và service này phải trả giá đó ngay cả với những read mà một câu trả lời stale vài chục ms là hoàn toàn ổn (dashboard, metrics), trong khi những read thực sự quan trọng lại bị trộn chung, không có gì phân biệt. Hãy tư duy thành lời, không IDE: bạn cắt read latency thế nào mà không phá vỡ correctness?","givenCode":"```csharp\n// Mọi read ở đây đều mặc định dùng mode linearizable của dotnet-etcd — kể cả\n// dashboard aggregate được poll mỗi giây, vẫn trả cùng một round-trip ReadIndex\n// như read lock-owner bên dưới, dù một con số stale vài chục ms là hoàn toàn ổn.\nasync Task<KeyValue?> ReadLockOwnerAsync(string lockKey)\n{\n    var res = await _client.GetAsync(new RangeRequest { Key = Bs(lockKey) });\n    return res.Kvs.Count > 0 ? res.Kvs[0] : null;\n}\n\nasync Task<IReadOnlyList<KeyValue>> ReadDashboardMetricsAsync()\n{\n    const string prefix = \"metrics/\";\n    var rangeEnd = prefix[..^1] + (char)(prefix[^1] + 1);\n    var res = await _client.GetAsync(new RangeRequest { Key = Bs(prefix), RangeEnd = Bs(rangeEnd) });\n    return res.Kvs;\n}\n\n_ = Task.Run(async () => { while (true) { await ReadDashboardMetricsAsync(); await Task.Delay(1000); } });\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated with C# — the concept applies to any stack.) A coordination service built on dotnet-etcd reads etcd on a hot path, and its p99 read latency is too high. Every read. That default is linearizable: each read pays a ReadIndex round-trip where the leader confirms it is still leader before serving. The slowness: linearizable reads cost ~1 leader-to-follower round-trip apiece, and the service pays that even for reads where a few-tens-of-ms-stale answer would be perfectly fine (dashboards, metrics), while the genuinely critical reads are mixed in with no distinction. Reason out loud, no IDE: how do you cut the read latency without breaking correctness?","givenCode":"```csharp\n// Every read here defaults to dotnet-etcd's linearizable mode — including the\n// dashboard aggregate polled once a second, which pays the same ReadIndex round-trip\n// as the lock-owner read even though a few-tens-of-ms-stale count would be fine.\nasync Task<KeyValue?> ReadLockOwnerAsync(string lockKey)\n{\n    var res = await _client.GetAsync(new RangeRequest { Key = Bs(lockKey) });\n    return res.Kvs.Count > 0 ? res.Kvs[0] : null;\n}\n\nasync Task<IReadOnlyList<KeyValue>> ReadDashboardMetricsAsync()\n{\n    const string prefix = \"metrics/\";\n    var rangeEnd = prefix[..^1] + (char)(prefix[^1] + 1);\n    var res = await _client.GetAsync(new RangeRequest { Key = Bs(prefix), RangeEnd = Bs(rangeEnd) });\n    return res.Kvs;\n}\n\n_ = Task.Run(async () => { while (true) { await ReadDashboardMetricsAsync(); await Task.Delay(1000); } });\n```","diagram":null}},"go":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng Go — khái niệm áp dụng cho mọi stack.) Một coordination service dựng trên clientv3 đọc etcd ở hot path, và p99 read latency của nó đang quá cao. Mọi read. Mặc định đó là linearizable: mỗi read phải trả một round-trip ReadIndex, nơi leader xác nhận mình vẫn còn là leader trước khi trả lời. Điểm chậm: linearizable read tốn khoảng 1 round-trip leader-to-follower mỗi lần, và service này phải trả giá đó ngay cả với những read mà một câu trả lời stale vài chục ms là hoàn toàn ổn (dashboard, metrics), trong khi những read thực sự quan trọng lại bị trộn chung, không có gì phân biệt. Hãy tư duy thành lời, không IDE: bạn cắt read latency thế nào mà không phá vỡ correctness?","givenCode":"```go\n// Mọi read ở đây đều mặc định dùng mode linearizable của clientv3 — kể cả dashboard\n// aggregate được poll mỗi giây, vẫn trả cùng một round-trip ReadIndex như read\n// lock-owner bên dưới, dù một con số stale vài chục ms là hoàn toàn chấp nhận được.\nfunc readLockOwner(ctx context.Context, cli *clientv3.Client, lockKey string) (*mvccpb.KeyValue, error) {\n\tres, err := cli.Get(ctx, lockKey)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, err\n\t}\n\tif len(res.Kvs) == 0 {\n\t\treturn nil, nil\n\t}\n\treturn res.Kvs[0], nil\n}\n\nfunc readDashboardMetrics(ctx context.Context, cli *clientv3.Client) ([]*mvccpb.KeyValue, error) {\n\tres, err := cli.Get(ctx, \"metrics/\", clientv3.WithPrefix())\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, err\n\t}\n\treturn res.Kvs, nil\n}\n\ngo func() {\n\tfor range time.Tick(time.Second) {\n\t\treadDashboardMetrics(ctx, cli)\n\t}\n}()\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in Go — the concept applies to any stack.) A coordination service built on clientv3 reads etcd on a hot path, and its p99 read latency is too high. Every read. That default is linearizable: each read pays a ReadIndex round-trip where the leader confirms it is still leader before serving. The slowness: linearizable reads cost ~1 leader-to-follower round-trip apiece, and the service pays that even for reads where a few-tens-of-ms-stale answer would be perfectly fine (dashboards, metrics), while the genuinely critical reads are mixed in with no distinction. Reason out loud, no IDE: how do you cut the read latency without breaking correctness?","givenCode":"```go\n// Every read here defaults to clientv3's linearizable mode — including the dashboard\n// aggregate polled once a second, which pays the same ReadIndex round-trip as the\n// lock-owner read even though a few-tens-of-ms-stale count would be perfectly fine.\nfunc readLockOwner(ctx context.Context, cli *clientv3.Client, lockKey string) (*mvccpb.KeyValue, error) {\n\tres, err := cli.Get(ctx, lockKey)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, err\n\t}\n\tif len(res.Kvs) == 0 {\n\t\treturn nil, nil\n\t}\n\treturn res.Kvs[0], nil\n}\n\nfunc readDashboardMetrics(ctx context.Context, cli *clientv3.Client) ([]*mvccpb.KeyValue, error) {\n\tres, err := cli.Get(ctx, \"metrics/\", clientv3.WithPrefix())\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, err\n\t}\n\treturn res.Kvs, nil\n}\n\ngo func() {\n\tfor range time.Tick(time.Second) {\n\t\treadDashboardMetrics(ctx, cli)\n\t}\n}()\n```","diagram":null}}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"vi":{"followUps":["Nếu một serializable read được phục vụ bởi một follower thiểu số bị tụt lại sau khi replicate qua một lần partition, câu trả lời có thể stale tới mức nào, và điều đó có làm bạn vẽ lại ranh giới routing không?","Nếu một read ở dashboard sau này lại được dùng để RA QUYẾT ĐỊNH, chứ không chỉ để hiển thị một con số, điều gì sẽ vỡ, và bạn phát hiện sự trôi dạt đó trước khi ship bằng cách nào?"],"hints":["Tự hỏi: leader phải xác nhận chính xác điều gì trước khi có thể trả lời an toàn một linearizable read — và vì sao việc xác nhận đó tốn một round-trip?","Chia các read trong hệ thống này thành hai nhóm: nhóm QUYẾT ĐỊNH một điều gì đó (một lock, một tuyên bố leadership) và nhóm chỉ HIỂN THỊ một điều gì đó (một con số trên dashboard). Cách chia đó có khớp với hai mode đọc không?"]},"en":{"followUps":["If a serializable read is served by a minority follower that has fallen behind on replication after a partition, how stale can the answer actually get, and does that change where you draw the routing line?","If a dashboard read later starts being used to make a decision, not just to display a number, what breaks, and how would you catch that drift before it ships?"],"hints":["Ask yourself: what exactly must the leader confirm before it can safely answer a linearizable read — and why does confirming that cost a round-trip?","Sort the reads in this system into two piles: ones that DECIDE something (a lock, a leadership claim) and ones that only DISPLAY something (a dashboard number). Does that split line up with the two read modes?"]}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"locales":{"vi":{"rubric":["[technical] Phân biệt rõ linearizable read (dùng ReadIndex: leader phải xác nhận mình vẫn còn là leader qua một majority heartbeat, tốn khoảng 1 round-trip, không ghi log) với serializable read (đọc thẳng từ replica cục bộ, gần như miễn phí, nhưng có thể stale vài chục ms).","[problemSolving] Route theo đúng nhu cầu: config quan trọng và các quyết định lock/leadership vẫn giữ linearizable; dashboard, metrics, aggregate chuyển sang serializable — cắt phần lớn read latency mà không đánh đổi một correctness bug.","[communication] Bảo vệ rule một cách rõ ràng — chỉ trả round-trip ở đúng những chỗ staleness thật sự là một correctness bug — thay vì hạ consistency trên diện rộng.","[testing] Đề xuất đo read latency ở cả hai mode và assert rằng đường serializable chỉ được dùng ở nơi chấp nhận được bounded-staleness (ví dụ một test đảm bảo quyết định lock không bao giờ đọc serializable)."],"checklist":[{"text":"A default `etcd3` read is linearizable, meaning it triggers a ReadIndex confirmation: before answering, the leader verifies via a majority heartbeat that it is still leader. No log entry is written, but the client still pays roughly one leader round-trip per read.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"Calling `.serializable()` on an `etcd3` `GetBuilder` skips the ReadIndex confirmation entirely and answers straight from whichever replica received the request. This is nearly free but can lag the true committed state by tens of milliseconds — a bounded staleness, not an unbounded one.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"Reads split into two categories by what they're used for: reads that DECIDE something (who holds a lock, who is leader, critical config) versus reads that only DISPLAY something (a dashboard count, a metrics aggregate). This distinction, not raw call frequency, determines which consistency mode a read needs.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"A stale answer on a lock-owner or leadership read can hand the same lock or leadership role to two different callers at once, so that read must stay linearizable — it cannot be switched to `.serializable()`.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"A dashboard or metrics read that is off by tens of milliseconds causes no correctness problem, only a slightly stale display value, so it is safe to switch to `.serializable()` and read from the local replica instead of paying the round-trip on every poll.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":9},{"text":"The fix is a per-call routing decision, not a global consistency downgrade: only the reads that tolerate staleness get `.serializable()` added; reads that gate a decision are left on the default linearizable path untouched.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":13},{"text":"The win can be proven by measuring p99 latency under load for both paths — the switched-over dashboard read should drop close to a local read, while the lock-owner read stays at roughly one round-trip, unchanged.","dimension":"testing","critical":false,"scoreBand":9},{"text":"The routing rule itself should be enforced by a test, not just documented: spy on the underlying `get(...).exec()` call for the lock-owner read and assert that `.serializable()` is never chained onto it, so a future edit can't silently weaken a decision-making read.","dimension":"testing","critical":true,"scoreBand":13}],"keywords":["linearizable read","serializable read","ReadIndex","read latency","stale read"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"By default an etcd3 read is linearizable — it does a ReadIndex confirmation, so the leader checks with a majority via heartbeat before answering, no log write but you still pay roughly one leader round trip. Calling .serializable() skips that and answers from whatever replica got the request, basically free, but can lag the true state by tens of milliseconds — bounded staleness, not unbounded. The real split isn't how often a read happens, it's whether the read decides something — like who holds a lock or who's leader — versus just displays something like a dashboard count. A stale answer on a lock-owner read can hand the same lock to two callers, so that has to stay linearizable, while a dashboard being a few milliseconds behind is harmless and fine to switch to .serializable(). So the fix is routing per call, not a global downgrade — only the tolerant reads get switched. I'd prove the win with p99 latency measurements on both paths, and I'd lock in the routing rule with a test that spies on the lock-owner read's call and asserts .serializable() never gets chained onto it.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"An etcd3 read is linearizable by default, meaning it does a ReadIndex confirmation — the leader checks a majority heartbeat before answering, so no log entry gets written but you still pay about one round trip. Calling .serializable() skips that check and answers from the local replica instead, which is nearly free but can lag the true committed state by tens of milliseconds. The important distinction is whether a read decides something, like who currently holds the lock or who's leader, versus a read that only displays something like a dashboard number — that's what should determine the consistency mode. A stale answer on the lock-owner read specifically is dangerous because it could hand the lock to two callers at once, so that one has to remain linearizable. I'd want a test that spies on that lock-owner call and asserts .serializable() is never accidentally chained onto it.","sonnet":60,"haiku":60},{"level":3,"answer":".serializable() skips the leader confirmation and just answers from whatever replica got the request, so it's fast but can be stale by a few tens of milliseconds. The thing that decides whether that's okay isn't how often you're calling it, it's whether the read is used to decide something or just to display something. A lock-owner or leadership read falls into the \"decide\" bucket, so if it goes stale you could end up handing the same lock to two different callers — that one has to stay on the fully consistent path.","sonnet":42,"haiku":42},{"level":4,"answer":"I think the core issue is that a leadership or lock-owner read really can't tolerate staleness, because if it returns an out-of-date answer, two different processes could both believe they're the current leader or both believe they're holding the same lock at the same moment. That's the kind of read where correctness actually depends on getting the true, right-now state, not a slightly delayed snapshot from some replica. So whatever else changes in the read path, that particular read needs to stay on the strict, fully consistent option rather than the faster one.","sonnet":14,"haiku":14},{"level":5,"answer":"Honestly I'd just add caching in front of etcd for all the reads with a short TTL, like a second or two, since etcd calls can get expensive under load. That should cut down round trips across the board without really affecting correctness, since a second of staleness shouldn't matter much for most use cases in this kind of system.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]},"en":{"rubric":["[technical] Distinguishes a linearizable read (ReadIndex: leader confirms it's still leader via a majority heartbeat, ~1 round-trip, no log write) from a serializable read (goes straight to the local replica, nearly free, possibly stale by tens of ms).","[problemSolving] Routes by need: critical config and lock/leadership decisions stay linearizable; dashboards, metrics and aggregates switch to serializable — cutting the bulk of read latency without risking a correctness bug.","[communication] Defends the rule cleanly — you pay the round-trip only where staleness would actually be a correctness bug — rather than globally weakening consistency.","[testing] Proposes measuring read latency in both modes and asserting the serializable path is used only where a bounded-staleness read is acceptable (e.g. a test that a lock decision never reads serializable)."],"checklist":[{"text":"A default `etcd3` read is linearizable, meaning it triggers a ReadIndex confirmation: before answering, the leader verifies via a majority heartbeat that it is still leader. No log entry is written, but the client still pays roughly one leader round-trip per read.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"Calling `.serializable()` on an `etcd3` `GetBuilder` skips the ReadIndex confirmation entirely and answers straight from whichever replica received the request. This is nearly free but can lag the true committed state by tens of milliseconds — a bounded staleness, not an unbounded one.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"Reads split into two categories by what they're used for: reads that DECIDE something (who holds a lock, who is leader, critical config) versus reads that only DISPLAY something (a dashboard count, a metrics aggregate). This distinction, not raw call frequency, determines which consistency mode a read needs.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"A stale answer on a lock-owner or leadership read can hand the same lock or leadership role to two different callers at once, so that read must stay linearizable — it cannot be switched to `.serializable()`.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"A dashboard or metrics read that is off by tens of milliseconds causes no correctness problem, only a slightly stale display value, so it is safe to switch to `.serializable()` and read from the local replica instead of paying the round-trip on every poll.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":9},{"text":"The fix is a per-call routing decision, not a global consistency downgrade: only the reads that tolerate staleness get `.serializable()` added; reads that gate a decision are left on the default linearizable path untouched.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":13},{"text":"The win can be proven by measuring p99 latency under load for both paths — the switched-over dashboard read should drop close to a local read, while the lock-owner read stays at roughly one round-trip, unchanged.","dimension":"testing","critical":false,"scoreBand":9},{"text":"The routing rule itself should be enforced by a test, not just documented: spy on the underlying `get(...).exec()` call for the lock-owner read and assert that `.serializable()` is never chained onto it, so a future edit can't silently weaken a decision-making read.","dimension":"testing","critical":true,"scoreBand":13}],"keywords":["linearizable read","serializable read","ReadIndex","read latency","stale read"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"By default an etcd3 read is linearizable — it does a ReadIndex confirmation, so the leader checks with a majority via heartbeat before answering, no log write but you still pay roughly one leader round trip. Calling .serializable() skips that and answers from whatever replica got the request, basically free, but can lag the true state by tens of milliseconds — bounded staleness, not unbounded. The real split isn't how often a read happens, it's whether the read decides something — like who holds a lock or who's leader — versus just displays something like a dashboard count. A stale answer on a lock-owner read can hand the same lock to two callers, so that has to stay linearizable, while a dashboard being a few milliseconds behind is harmless and fine to switch to .serializable(). So the fix is routing per call, not a global downgrade — only the tolerant reads get switched. I'd prove the win with p99 latency measurements on both paths, and I'd lock in the routing rule with a test that spies on the lock-owner read's call and asserts .serializable() never gets chained onto it.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"An etcd3 read is linearizable by default, meaning it does a ReadIndex confirmation — the leader checks a majority heartbeat before answering, so no log entry gets written but you still pay about one round trip. Calling .serializable() skips that check and answers from the local replica instead, which is nearly free but can lag the true committed state by tens of milliseconds. The important distinction is whether a read decides something, like who currently holds the lock or who's leader, versus a read that only displays something like a dashboard number — that's what should determine the consistency mode. A stale answer on the lock-owner read specifically is dangerous because it could hand the lock to two callers at once, so that one has to remain linearizable. I'd want a test that spies on that lock-owner call and asserts .serializable() is never accidentally chained onto it.","sonnet":60,"haiku":60},{"level":3,"answer":".serializable() skips the leader confirmation and just answers from whatever replica got the request, so it's fast but can be stale by a few tens of milliseconds. The thing that decides whether that's okay isn't how often you're calling it, it's whether the read is used to decide something or just to display something. A lock-owner or leadership read falls into the \"decide\" bucket, so if it goes stale you could end up handing the same lock to two different callers — that one has to stay on the fully consistent path.","sonnet":42,"haiku":42},{"level":4,"answer":"I think the core issue is that a leadership or lock-owner read really can't tolerate staleness, because if it returns an out-of-date answer, two different processes could both believe they're the current leader or both believe they're holding the same lock at the same moment. That's the kind of read where correctness actually depends on getting the true, right-now state, not a slightly delayed snapshot from some replica. So whatever else changes in the read path, that particular read needs to stay on the strict, fully consistent option rather than the faster one.","sonnet":14,"haiku":14},{"level":5,"answer":"Honestly I'd just add caching in front of etcd for all the reads with a short TTL, like a second or two, since etcd calls can get expensive under load. That should cut down round trips across the board without really affecting correctness, since a second of staleness shouldn't matter much for most use cases in this kind of system.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]}},"ideal":{"typescript":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nCả `readLockOwner` lẫn `readDashboardMetrics` đều đi qua đường đọc mặc định của `etcd3` — linearizable — nhưng chỉ `readLockOwner` mới thực sự cần điều đó. Route theo đúng nhu cầu: giữ nguyên `readLockOwner` (một câu trả lời stale ở đó có thể khiến hai owner cùng cầm lock), và thêm `.serializable()` vào `GetBuilder` của dashboard để nó đọc thẳng từ replica cục bộ thay vì trả round-trip mỗi giây.\n\n:::muted\nHai mode khác nhau thật sự ở đâu\n:::\nMột read mặc định (linearizable) của `etcd3` kích hoạt ReadIndex ngầm bên dưới: trước khi leader trả lời, nó xác nhận qua một majority heartbeat rằng mình vẫn còn là leader — không ghi log nào, nhưng client vẫn phải chờ khoảng 1 round-trip. Gọi `.serializable()` bỏ hẳn bước xác nhận đó, trả lời từ bất kỳ replica nào nhận request, gần như miễn phí nhưng có thể trễ so với trạng thái đã commit thật vài chục ms. Đó là staleness có giới hạn, không phải staleness vô hạn — chấp nhận được cho một giá trị hiển thị, không chấp nhận được cho một quyết định.\n\n:::muted\nCách sửa & cách chứng minh\n:::\n```typescript\nasync function readDashboardMetrics() {\n  const res = await this.etcd.getAll().prefix('metrics/').serializable().exec()\n  return res.kvs.map((kv) => ({ key: kv.key.toString(), value: kv.value.toString() }))\n}\n```\n`readLockOwner` giữ nguyên đường mặc định, không đụng vào. Để verify: đo p99 latency của cả hai lời gọi dưới tải — dashboard read dùng serializable phải giảm gần bằng một read local, còn lock read vẫn giữ khoảng 1 round-trip. Sau đó assert chính rule routing trong một test: gọi `readLockOwner`, spy lời gọi `get(...).exec()` bên dưới, và assert rằng `.serializable()` không bao giờ được chain vào đó.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\n`readLockOwner` and `readDashboardMetrics` both run through `etcd3`'s default read path, which is linearizable — but only `readLockOwner` needs to be. Route by need: keep `readLockOwner` exactly as-is (a stale answer there could hand out the lock to two owners), and add `.serializable()` to the dashboard's `GetBuilder` so it reads straight from the local replica instead of paying the round-trip once a second.\n\n:::muted\nHow the two modes actually differ\n:::\nA default (linearizable) `etcd3` read triggers ReadIndex under the hood: before the leader answers, it confirms via a majority heartbeat that it is still leader — no log entry is written, but the client still waits ~1 leader round-trip. Calling `.serializable()` skips that confirmation entirely and answers from whatever replica received the request, which is nearly free but can lag the true committed state by tens of milliseconds. That's a bounded staleness, not an unbounded one — acceptable for a display value, not for a decision.\n\n:::muted\nThe fix & how to prove it\n:::\n```typescript\nasync function readDashboardMetrics() {\n  const res = await this.etcd.getAll().prefix('metrics/').serializable().exec()\n  return res.kvs.map((kv) => ({ key: kv.key.toString(), value: kv.value.toString() }))\n}\n```\n`readLockOwner` stays on the default path untouched. To verify: measure p99 latency of both calls under load — the serializable dashboard read should drop close to a local read, the lock read stays at ~1 round-trip. Then assert the routing rule itself in a test: call `readLockOwner`, spy on the underlying `get(...).exec()` call, and assert `.serializable()` was never chained onto it."},"java":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nCả `readLockOwner` lẫn `readDashboardMetrics` đều đi qua đường đọc mặc định của `jetcd` — linearizable — nhưng chỉ `readLockOwner` mới thực sự cần điều đó. Route theo đúng nhu cầu: giữ nguyên `readLockOwner` (một câu trả lời stale ở đó có thể khiến hai owner cùng cầm lock), và thêm `.isSerializable(true)` vào `GetOption` của dashboard để nó đọc thẳng từ replica cục bộ thay vì trả round-trip mỗi giây.\n\n:::muted\nHai mode khác nhau thật sự ở đâu\n:::\nMột read mặc định (linearizable) của `jetcd` kích hoạt ReadIndex ngầm bên dưới: trước khi leader trả lời, nó xác nhận qua một majority heartbeat rằng mình vẫn còn là leader — không ghi log nào, nhưng client vẫn phải chờ khoảng 1 round-trip. Set `isSerializable(true)` bỏ hẳn bước xác nhận đó, trả lời từ bất kỳ replica nào nhận request, gần như miễn phí nhưng có thể trễ so với trạng thái đã commit thật vài chục ms. Đó là staleness có giới hạn, không phải staleness vô hạn — chấp nhận được cho một giá trị hiển thị, không chấp nhận được cho một quyết định.\n\n:::muted\nCách sửa & cách chứng minh\n:::\n```java\nList<KeyValue> readDashboardMetrics() throws Exception {\n    GetResponse res = client.getKVClient()\n        .get(ByteSequence.from(\"metrics/\", UTF_8), GetOption.builder()\n            .isPrefix(true)\n            .isSerializable(true)\n            .build())\n        .get(TIMEOUT_MS, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);\n    return res.getKvs();\n}\n```\n`readLockOwner` giữ nguyên `GetOption` mặc định, không đụng vào. Để verify: đo p99 latency của cả hai lời gọi dưới tải — dashboard read dùng serializable phải giảm gần bằng một read local, còn lock read vẫn giữ khoảng 1 round-trip. Sau đó assert chính rule routing trong một test: bắt lấy `GetOption` được truyền vào lời gọi lock-owner và assert `isSerializable()` là `false`.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\n`readLockOwner` and `readDashboardMetrics` both run through `jetcd`'s default read path, which is linearizable — but only `readLockOwner` needs to be. Route by need: keep `readLockOwner` exactly as-is (a stale answer there could hand out the lock to two owners), and add `.isSerializable(true)` to the dashboard's `GetOption` so it reads straight from the local replica instead of paying the round-trip once a second.\n\n:::muted\nHow the two modes actually differ\n:::\nA default (linearizable) `jetcd` read triggers ReadIndex under the hood: before the leader answers, it confirms via a majority heartbeat that it is still leader — no log entry is written, but the client still waits ~1 leader round-trip. Setting `isSerializable(true)` skips that confirmation entirely and answers from whatever replica received the request, which is nearly free but can lag the true committed state by tens of milliseconds. That's a bounded staleness, not an unbounded one — acceptable for a display value, not for a decision.\n\n:::muted\nThe fix & how to prove it\n:::\n```java\nList<KeyValue> readDashboardMetrics() throws Exception {\n    GetResponse res = client.getKVClient()\n        .get(ByteSequence.from(\"metrics/\", UTF_8), GetOption.builder()\n            .isPrefix(true)\n            .isSerializable(true)\n            .build())\n        .get(TIMEOUT_MS, TimeUnit.MILLISECONDS);\n    return res.getKvs();\n}\n```\n`readLockOwner` stays on the default `GetOption` untouched. To verify: measure p99 latency of both calls under load — the serializable dashboard read should drop close to a local read, the lock read stays at ~1 round-trip. Then assert the routing rule itself in a test: capture the `GetOption` passed into the lock-owner call and assert `isSerializable()` is `false` on it."},"csharp":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nCả `ReadLockOwnerAsync` lẫn `ReadDashboardMetricsAsync` đều đi qua đường đọc mặc định của `dotnet-etcd` — linearizable — nhưng chỉ `ReadLockOwnerAsync` mới thực sự cần điều đó. Route theo đúng nhu cầu: giữ nguyên `ReadLockOwnerAsync` (một câu trả lời stale ở đó có thể khiến hai owner cùng cầm lock), và set `Serializable = true` trên `RangeRequest` của dashboard để nó đọc thẳng từ replica cục bộ thay vì trả round-trip mỗi giây.\n\n:::muted\nHai mode khác nhau thật sự ở đâu\n:::\nMột read mặc định (linearizable) của `dotnet-etcd` kích hoạt ReadIndex ngầm bên dưới: trước khi leader trả lời, nó xác nhận qua một majority heartbeat rằng mình vẫn còn là leader — không ghi log nào, nhưng client vẫn phải chờ khoảng 1 round-trip. Set `Serializable = true` bỏ hẳn bước xác nhận đó, trả lời từ bất kỳ replica nào nhận request, gần như miễn phí nhưng có thể trễ so với trạng thái đã commit thật vài chục ms. Đó là staleness có giới hạn, không phải staleness vô hạn — chấp nhận được cho một giá trị hiển thị, không chấp nhận được cho một quyết định.\n\n:::muted\nCách sửa & cách chứng minh\n:::\n```csharp\nasync Task<IReadOnlyList<KeyValue>> ReadDashboardMetricsAsync()\n{\n    const string prefix = \"metrics/\";\n    var rangeEnd = prefix[..^1] + (char)(prefix[^1] + 1);\n    var res = await _client.GetAsync(new RangeRequest\n    {\n        Key = Bs(prefix),\n        RangeEnd = Bs(rangeEnd),\n        Serializable = true,\n    });\n    return res.Kvs;\n}\n```\n`ReadLockOwnerAsync` giữ nguyên `RangeRequest` mặc định, không đụng vào. Để verify: đo p99 latency của cả hai lời gọi dưới tải — dashboard read dùng serializable phải giảm gần bằng một read local, còn lock read vẫn giữ khoảng 1 round-trip. Sau đó assert chính rule routing trong một test: bắt lấy `RangeRequest` được truyền vào lời gọi lock-owner và assert `Serializable` là `false`.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\n`ReadLockOwnerAsync` and `ReadDashboardMetricsAsync` both run through `dotnet-etcd`'s default read path, which is linearizable — but only `ReadLockOwnerAsync` needs to be. Route by need: keep `ReadLockOwnerAsync` exactly as-is (a stale answer there could hand out the lock to two owners), and set `Serializable = true` on the dashboard's `RangeRequest` so it reads straight from the local replica instead of paying the round-trip once a second.\n\n:::muted\nHow the two modes actually differ\n:::\nA default (linearizable) `dotnet-etcd` read triggers ReadIndex under the hood: before the leader answers, it confirms via a majority heartbeat that it is still leader — no log entry is written, but the client still waits ~1 leader round-trip. Setting `Serializable = true` skips that confirmation entirely and answers from whatever replica received the request, which is nearly free but can lag the true committed state by tens of milliseconds. That's a bounded staleness, not an unbounded one — acceptable for a display value, not for a decision.\n\n:::muted\nThe fix & how to prove it\n:::\n```csharp\nasync Task<IReadOnlyList<KeyValue>> ReadDashboardMetricsAsync()\n{\n    const string prefix = \"metrics/\";\n    var rangeEnd = prefix[..^1] + (char)(prefix[^1] + 1);\n    var res = await _client.GetAsync(new RangeRequest\n    {\n        Key = Bs(prefix),\n        RangeEnd = Bs(rangeEnd),\n        Serializable = true,\n    });\n    return res.Kvs;\n}\n```\n`ReadLockOwnerAsync` stays on the default `RangeRequest` untouched. To verify: measure p99 latency of both calls under load — the serializable dashboard read should drop close to a local read, the lock read stays at ~1 round-trip. Then assert the routing rule itself in a test: capture the `RangeRequest` passed into the lock-owner call and assert `Serializable` is `false` on it."},"go":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nCả `readLockOwner` lẫn `readDashboardMetrics` đều đi qua đường đọc mặc định của `clientv3` — linearizable — nhưng chỉ `readLockOwner` mới thực sự cần điều đó. Route theo đúng nhu cầu: giữ nguyên `readLockOwner` (một câu trả lời stale ở đó có thể khiến hai owner cùng cầm lock), và thêm `clientv3.WithSerializable()` vào lời gọi `Get` của dashboard để nó đọc thẳng từ replica cục bộ thay vì trả round-trip mỗi giây.\n\n:::muted\nHai mode khác nhau thật sự ở đâu\n:::\nMột read mặc định (linearizable) của `clientv3` kích hoạt ReadIndex ngầm bên dưới: trước khi leader trả lời, nó xác nhận qua một majority heartbeat rằng mình vẫn còn là leader — không ghi log nào, nhưng client vẫn phải chờ khoảng 1 round-trip. Thêm `clientv3.WithSerializable()` bỏ hẳn bước xác nhận đó, trả lời từ bất kỳ member nào nhận request, gần như miễn phí nhưng có thể trễ so với trạng thái đã commit thật vài chục ms. Đó là staleness có giới hạn, không phải staleness vô hạn — chấp nhận được cho một giá trị hiển thị, không chấp nhận được cho một quyết định.\n\n:::muted\nCách sửa & cách chứng minh\n:::\n```go\nfunc readDashboardMetrics(ctx context.Context, cli *clientv3.Client) ([]*mvccpb.KeyValue, error) {\n\tres, err := cli.Get(ctx, \"metrics/\", clientv3.WithPrefix(), clientv3.WithSerializable())\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, err\n\t}\n\treturn res.Kvs, nil\n}\n```\n`readLockOwner` giữ nguyên lời gọi mặc định, không đụng vào. Để verify: đo p99 latency của cả hai lời gọi dưới tải — dashboard read dùng serializable phải giảm gần bằng một read local, còn lock read vẫn giữ khoảng 1 round-trip. Sau đó assert chính rule routing trong một test: bọc etcd client, bắt lấy các `clientv3.OpOption` được truyền vào lời gọi lock-owner, và assert không option nào trong đó là `clientv3.WithSerializable()`.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\n`readLockOwner` and `readDashboardMetrics` both run through `clientv3`'s default read path, which is linearizable — but only `readLockOwner` needs to be. Route by need: keep `readLockOwner` exactly as-is (a stale answer there could hand out the lock to two owners), and add `clientv3.WithSerializable()` to the dashboard's `Get` call so it reads straight from the local replica instead of paying the round-trip once a second.\n\n:::muted\nHow the two modes actually differ\n:::\nA default (linearizable) `clientv3` read triggers ReadIndex under the hood: before the leader answers, it confirms via a majority heartbeat that it is still leader — no log entry is written, but the client still waits ~1 leader round-trip. Adding `clientv3.WithSerializable()` skips that confirmation entirely and answers from whatever member received the request, which is nearly free but can lag the true committed state by tens of milliseconds. That's a bounded staleness, not an unbounded one — acceptable for a display value, not for a decision.\n\n:::muted\nThe fix & how to prove it\n:::\n```go\nfunc readDashboardMetrics(ctx context.Context, cli *clientv3.Client) ([]*mvccpb.KeyValue, error) {\n\tres, err := cli.Get(ctx, \"metrics/\", clientv3.WithPrefix(), clientv3.WithSerializable())\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn nil, err\n\t}\n\treturn res.Kvs, nil\n}\n```\n`readLockOwner` stays on the default call untouched. To verify: measure p99 latency of both calls under load — the serializable dashboard read should drop close to a local read, the lock read stays at ~1 round-trip. Then assert the routing rule itself in a test: wrap the etcd client, capture the `clientv3.OpOption`s passed into the lock-owner call, and assert none of them is `clientv3.WithSerializable()`."}}}$iv$::jsonb)
