INSERT INTO interview_questions (bank_id, bank_version, question_id, sort_index, is_premium, family, tier, kind, tags, competency, leadership_tier, bodies, interviewer, grading)
VALUES ('1-system-design-mastery/3-communication-patterns', '231d8cc57+37d905d9a', '1-system-design-mastery/3-communication-patterns/11', 11, false, 'technical', 'middle', 'optimize', ARRAY['event-sourcing', 'snapshot', 'replay-performance']::text[], NULL, NULL, $iv${"typescript":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng TypeScript. Triệu chứng: các account sống lâu (hàng nghìn event) đọc ngày càng chậm; latency tăng tuyến tính và vô hạn theo số event từng được ghi, dù phần lớn lần đọc chỉ cần số dư hiện tại. Cái chậm nằm ở chỗ: replay-từ-đầu là O(n) mỗi lần đọc, và n chỉ tăng vì log là append-only.","givenCode":"```typescript\n// account.service.ts\n@Injectable()\nexport class AccountService {\n    constructor(\n        @InjectRepository(EventRecord)\n        private readonly eventRepo: Repository<EventRecord>,\n    ) {}\n\n    async loadState(accountId: string): Promise<AccountState> {\n        const events = await this.eventRepo.find({\n            where: { aggregateId: accountId },\n            order: { version: \"ASC\" },\n        })   // ← đọc TOÀN BỘ event từng ghi cho aggregate này, từ version 1\n        return events.reduce(applyEvent, emptyState(accountId))   // ← fold(applyEvent) trên CẢ log, ở MỖI lần đọc\n    }\n\n    async getAccount(accountId: string): Promise<AccountState> {\n        return this.loadState(accountId)   // ← mỗi lần gọi GET /accounts/:id đều trả giá cho toàn bộ lịch sử\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in TypeScript. Symptom: reads on long-lived accounts (thousands of events) get progressively slower; latency grows linearly and unboundedly with the number of events ever written, even though most reads only need the current balance. The slowness: replay-from-scratch is O(n) per read and n only grows because the log is append-only.","givenCode":"```typescript\n// account.service.ts\n@Injectable()\nexport class AccountService {\n    constructor(\n        @InjectRepository(EventRecord)\n        private readonly eventRepo: Repository<EventRecord>,\n    ) {}\n\n    async loadState(accountId: string): Promise<AccountState> {\n        const events = await this.eventRepo.find({\n            where: { aggregateId: accountId },\n            order: { version: \"ASC\" },\n        })   // ← reads EVERY event ever written for this aggregate, from version 1\n        return events.reduce(applyEvent, emptyState(accountId))   // ← fold(applyEvent) over the WHOLE log, on every single read\n    }\n\n    async getAccount(accountId: string): Promise<AccountState> {\n        return this.loadState(accountId)   // ← every GET /accounts/:id call pays for the entire history\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"java":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng java. Triệu chứng: các account sống lâu (hàng nghìn event) đọc ngày càng chậm; latency tăng tuyến tính và vô hạn theo số event từng được ghi, dù phần lớn lần đọc chỉ cần số dư hiện tại. Cái chậm nằm ở chỗ: replay-từ-đầu là O(n) mỗi lần đọc, và n chỉ tăng vì log là append-only.","givenCode":"```java\n// AccountProjectionService.java\n@Service\npublic class AccountProjectionService {\n\n    @PersistenceContext\n    private EntityManager em;\n\n    public AccountState loadState(String accountId) {\n        List<EventRecord> events = em.createQuery(\n                \"SELECT e FROM EventRecord e WHERE e.aggregateId = :id ORDER BY e.version ASC\",\n                EventRecord.class)\n                .setParameter(\"id\", accountId)\n                .getResultList();   // ← lấy TOÀN BỘ event từng ghi cho aggregate này, từ version 1\n\n        return replayEvents(accountId, events);   // ← replayEvents fold CẢ danh sách, ở MỖI lần đọc\n    }\n\n    public AccountState getAccount(String accountId) {\n        return loadState(accountId);   // ← mỗi lần gọi GET /accounts/{id} đều trả giá cho toàn bộ lịch sử\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in java. Symptom: reads on long-lived accounts (thousands of events) get progressively slower; latency grows linearly and unboundedly with the number of events ever written, even though most reads only need the current balance. The slowness: replay-from-scratch is O(n) per read and n only grows because the log is append-only.","givenCode":"```java\n// AccountProjectionService.java\n@Service\npublic class AccountProjectionService {\n\n    @PersistenceContext\n    private EntityManager em;\n\n    public AccountState loadState(String accountId) {\n        List<EventRecord> events = em.createQuery(\n                \"SELECT e FROM EventRecord e WHERE e.aggregateId = :id ORDER BY e.version ASC\",\n                EventRecord.class)\n                .setParameter(\"id\", accountId)\n                .getResultList();   // ← fetches EVERY event ever written for this aggregate, from version 1\n\n        return replayEvents(accountId, events);   // ← replayEvents folds the WHOLE list, on every single read\n    }\n\n    public AccountState getAccount(String accountId) {\n        return loadState(accountId);   // ← every GET /accounts/{id} call pays for the entire history\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"csharp":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng C#. Triệu chứng: các account sống lâu (hàng nghìn event) đọc ngày càng chậm; latency tăng tuyến tính và vô hạn theo số event từng được ghi, dù phần lớn lần đọc chỉ cần số dư hiện tại. Cái chậm nằm ở chỗ: replay-từ-đầu là O(n) mỗi lần đọc, và n chỉ tăng vì log là append-only.","givenCode":"```csharp\n// AccountProjectionService.cs\nasync Task<AccountState> LoadState(EventStoreDbContext db, string accountId)\n{\n    var events = await db.Events\n        .Where(e => e.AggregateId == accountId)\n        .OrderBy(e => e.Version)\n        .ToListAsync();   // ← lấy TOÀN BỘ event từng ghi cho aggregate này, từ version 1\n\n    var state = EmptyState(accountId);\n    foreach (var row in events)\n        state = ApplyEvent(state, row.Payload, row.OccurredAt, row.Version);   // ← fold CẢ danh sách, ở MỖI lần đọc\n\n    return state;\n}\n\nasync Task<AccountState> GetAccount(EventStoreDbContext db, string accountId)\n{\n    return await LoadState(db, accountId);   // ← mỗi lần gọi GET /accounts/{id} đều trả giá cho toàn bộ lịch sử\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in C#. Symptom: reads on long-lived accounts (thousands of events) get progressively slower; latency grows linearly and unboundedly with the number of events ever written, even though most reads only need the current balance. The slowness: replay-from-scratch is O(n) per read and n only grows because the log is append-only.","givenCode":"```csharp\n// AccountProjectionService.cs\nasync Task<AccountState> LoadState(EventStoreDbContext db, string accountId)\n{\n    var events = await db.Events\n        .Where(e => e.AggregateId == accountId)\n        .OrderBy(e => e.Version)\n        .ToListAsync();   // ← fetches EVERY event ever written for this aggregate, from version 1\n\n    var state = EmptyState(accountId);\n    foreach (var row in events)\n        state = ApplyEvent(state, row.Payload, row.OccurredAt, row.Version);   // ← folds the WHOLE list, on every single read\n\n    return state;\n}\n\nasync Task<AccountState> GetAccount(EventStoreDbContext db, string accountId)\n{\n    return await LoadState(db, accountId);   // ← every GET /accounts/{id} call pays for the entire history\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"go":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng go. Triệu chứng: các account sống lâu (hàng nghìn event) đọc ngày càng chậm; latency tăng tuyến tính và vô hạn theo số event từng được ghi, dù phần lớn lần đọc chỉ cần số dư hiện tại. Cái chậm nằm ở chỗ: replay-từ-đầu là O(n) mỗi lần đọc, và n chỉ tăng vì log là append-only.","givenCode":"```go\n// loadState builds AccountState for accountID by folding the entire event log.\nfunc loadState(ctx context.Context, pool *pgxpool.Pool, accountID string) (AccountState, error) {\n\trows, err := pool.Query(ctx,\n\t\t`SELECT event_type, payload, occurred_at, version FROM events WHERE aggregate_id = $1 ORDER BY version ASC`,\n\t\taccountID,\n\t)   // ← lấy TOÀN BỘ event từng ghi cho aggregate này, từ version 1\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn AccountState{}, err\n\t}\n\tdefer rows.Close()\n\n\tstate := AccountState{AccountID: accountID}\n\tfor rows.Next() {\n\t\tvar eventType string\n\t\tvar payload map[string]interface{}\n\t\tvar occurredAt time.Time\n\t\tvar version int\n\t\tif err := rows.Scan(&eventType, &payload, &occurredAt, &version); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn AccountState{}, err\n\t\t}\n\t\tpayload[\"type\"] = eventType\n\t\tstate = applyEvent(state, payload, occurredAt, version)   // ← fold CẢ danh sách, ở MỖI lần đọc\n\t}\n\treturn state, rows.Err()\n}\n\nfunc getAccount(ctx context.Context, pool *pgxpool.Pool, accountID string) (AccountState, error) {\n\treturn loadState(ctx, pool, accountID)   // ← mỗi lần gọi GET /accounts/:id đều trả giá cho toàn bộ lịch sử\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in go. Symptom: reads on long-lived accounts (thousands of events) get progressively slower; latency grows linearly and unboundedly with the number of events ever written, even though most reads only need the current balance. The slowness: replay-from-scratch is O(n) per read and n only grows because the log is append-only.","givenCode":"```go\n// loadState builds AccountState for accountID by folding the entire event log.\nfunc loadState(ctx context.Context, pool *pgxpool.Pool, accountID string) (AccountState, error) {\n\trows, err := pool.Query(ctx,\n\t\t`SELECT event_type, payload, occurred_at, version FROM events WHERE aggregate_id = $1 ORDER BY version ASC`,\n\t\taccountID,\n\t)   // ← fetches EVERY event ever written for this aggregate, from version 1\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn AccountState{}, err\n\t}\n\tdefer rows.Close()\n\n\tstate := AccountState{AccountID: accountID}\n\tfor rows.Next() {\n\t\tvar eventType string\n\t\tvar payload map[string]interface{}\n\t\tvar occurredAt time.Time\n\t\tvar version int\n\t\tif err := rows.Scan(&eventType, &payload, &occurredAt, &version); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn AccountState{}, err\n\t\t}\n\t\tpayload[\"type\"] = eventType\n\t\tstate = applyEvent(state, payload, occurredAt, version)   // ← folds the WHOLE list, on every single read\n\t}\n\treturn state, rows.Err()\n}\n\nfunc getAccount(ctx context.Context, pool *pgxpool.Pool, accountID string) (AccountState, error) {\n\treturn loadState(ctx, pool, accountID)   // ← every GET /accounts/:id call pays for the entire history\n}\n```","diagram":null}}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"vi":{"followUps":["Bạn quyết định tần suất chụp snapshot (mỗi bao nhiêu event) dựa trên đánh đổi nào — giữa chi phí replay tiết kiệm được và overhead lưu/ghi snapshot?","Nếu một snapshot bị hỏng hoặc bị xoá nhầm, hệ thống có còn trả về đúng state không, và bạn kiểm chứng điều đó bằng phép thử nào?"],"hints":["Chi phí đang tăng vì cái gì lặp lại y hệt ở mỗi lần đọc — có phần nào của log đã \"chốt xong\" và không cần fold lại mỗi lần không?","Nghĩ đến việc lưu lại state đã tính sẵn tại một version nào đó, rồi chỉ fold tiếp phần event MỚI HƠN version đó — phần cũ không cần đụng tới nữa mỗi lần đọc."]},"en":{"followUps":["What trade-off drives your choice of snapshot frequency (every how many events) — between the replay cost saved and the overhead of storing/writing snapshots?","If a snapshot were corrupted or accidentally deleted, would the system still return the correct state, and what test would you run to confirm that?"],"hints":["What is repeating identically on every single read that's driving the cost up — is there a part of the log that's already \"settled\" and shouldn't need to be folded again each time?","Think about caching the already-computed state at some version, then only folding the events NEWER than that version — the older part never needs to be touched again on read."]}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"locales":{"vi":{"rubric":["[problemSolving] Chẩn đoán đúng chi phí: replay fold TOÀN BỘ n event trên mỗi lần đọc, nên cost là O(n) và tăng vô hạn khi event log append-only ngày càng dài — đọc current-state phải trả giá cho cả lịch sử mỗi lần, dù phần lớn request chỉ cần số dư hiện tại.","[technical] Đề xuất đúng snapshot: định kỳ persist state của aggregate tại version N (ví dụ mỗi 10 event) vào một snapshot store; khi load, bắt đầu từ state của snapshot mới nhất và chỉ replay các event SAU nó — giới hạn replay chỉ còn phần đuôi (tail) của log.","[technical] Giữ đúng tính đúng đắn: snapshot là một cache DẪN XUẤT (derived), không phải nguồn sự thật — xoá hết snapshot vẫn phải replay được từ log ra cùng một state; nó chỉ tối ưu hiệu năng đọc chứ không đụng vào cái gì được coi là dữ liệu gốc.","[communication] Nói thẳng đánh đổi: snapshot tốn thêm storage cộng cơ chế ghi/chọn snapshot và một khoảng staleness giữa các lần chụp, đổi lại chi phí replay bị chặn trên, gần như hằng số thay vì tăng vô hạn.","[testing] Xác minh: benchmark độ trễ load trên một aggregate có version cao trước/sau khi thêm snapshot (đường latency phải phẳng lại), và chứng minh việc rebuild chỉ từ log (bỏ hết snapshot) cho ra state giống hệt — khẳng định snapshot không bao giờ trở thành nguồn sự thật."],"checklist":[{"text":"Replaying an event-sourced aggregate by folding every event from version 1 on each read makes the read cost O(n) in the total number of events ever written, not in the size of the answer actually needed — so latency grows purely because the append-only log keeps getting longer.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"Because an event log is append-only, the event count n never shrinks, so a replay-from-scratch load path has no ceiling: an account that has accumulated thousands of events pays for its entire history on every single read, even when only the current balance is needed.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"A snapshot is a periodically persisted copy of the aggregate's state at a given version (for example every 10 events) written to a separate store; the load path then starts from the latest snapshot's state and folds only the events after that version, bounding replay work to the tail of the log instead of the whole history.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"A snapshot must remain a derived cache, never the source of truth: the event log stays append-only and untouched, and deleting every snapshot must still let the system rebuild identical state by replaying from the log — if the state differs without snapshots, the snapshot has silently become authoritative.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"Snapshotting introduces trade-offs: extra storage for the snapshot table, a background job that must reliably fire after each qualifying write, and a staleness window bounded by the snapshot interval (at most the events between the last snapshot and the current version), which the tail replay always closes.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Correctness of a snapshot mechanism is verified by wiping the snapshot store, forcing the load path back onto full replay, and asserting the resulting state exactly matches the snapshot-backed path — this is the test that proves the snapshot never replaced the event log as ground truth.","dimension":"testing","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"The performance benefit of snapshotting is verified by benchmarking load latency on a high-version account before and after enabling snapshots: the latency curve should flatten to roughly constant cost instead of continuing to grow linearly with the number of events.","dimension":"testing","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Fixing this kind of unbounded-replay cost starts with measuring where the cost actually comes from — confirming that the fold over the full event array, not network or serialization overhead, is what scales with n — before introducing snapshotting as the remedy.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10}],"keywords":["snapshot","replay","append-only log","O(n) replay cost","derived cache"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"The latency comes from replaying every event from version 1 on each read, so cost scales with total events ever written, not the size of the answer — and because the log is append-only that count never shrinks, so heavy accounts just get slower forever. I'd first confirm the fold over the full array is actually the scaling cost, not network or serialization, before fixing it. The fix is snapshotting: periodically persist state at a given version in a separate store, then load from the latest snapshot and fold only the events after it, bounding replay to the tail. The snapshot must stay a derived cache — the log stays untouched, and wiping snapshots and forcing full replay should still produce identical state, which is how I'd verify correctness. There's a real cost: extra storage, a background job that must fire reliably, and a staleness window bounded by the snapshot interval. I'd verify the performance win by benchmarking latency before and after — it should flatten instead of growing linearly.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"The issue is that every read replays the aggregate from the very first event, so the read cost scales directly with how many events exist for that aggregate rather than with what the caller actually needs back. Before fixing anything I'd first check that the fold over the event array is really what's driving the slowdown, not something like network or serialization. The fix is to periodically persist a snapshot of the aggregate's state at a given version in a separate store, then have loads start from the latest snapshot and only fold the events after it, so replay only covers the recent tail. It's important the snapshot stays purely a cache — I'd verify that by wiping the snapshot store, forcing a full replay, and checking the state comes out identical either way. For the performance side, I'd benchmark load latency on a heavy account before and after turning snapshots on and expect it to flatten out instead of climbing with event count.","sonnet":80,"haiku":80},{"level":3,"answer":"I'd add a snapshot — basically saving the aggregate's state every so many events into its own table, so loading can start from the latest snapshot and only replay what came after it instead of everything. The important part is that the snapshot can't become the real source of truth — the event log still has to be what everything is derived from. So to check that's actually true, I'd delete all the snapshots, force it to replay from scratch, and make sure the state it produces is exactly the same as when snapshots are in use.","sonnet":45,"haiku":45},{"level":4,"answer":"I think the way to check this is fixed would be to clear out whatever snapshot data exists and make sure the app still comes up with the same result when it has to rebuild from scratch.","sonnet":15,"haiku":15},{"level":5,"answer":"This sounds like it just needs an index on the events table, or maybe caching the API response for a few seconds. If reads are slow I'd probably put a CDN or Redis cache in front of the endpoint rather than touching how the aggregate itself loads.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]},"en":{"rubric":["[problemSolving] Correctly diagnoses the cost: replay folds ALL n events on every read, so the cost is O(n) and grows unbounded as the append-only event log keeps lengthening — a current-state read pays for the entire history every time, even though most requests only need the current balance.","[technical] Prescribes snapshotting correctly: periodically persist the aggregate's state at version N (e.g. every 10 events) into a snapshot store; on load, start from the latest snapshot's state and replay only the events AFTER it — bounding replay to the tail of the log.","[technical] Preserves correctness: a snapshot is a DERIVED cache, not the source of truth — deleting every snapshot must still let you replay from the log to the same state; it only optimizes read performance without touching what counts as the origin data.","[communication] States the trade-off honestly: a snapshot costs extra storage plus the machinery to write/pick snapshots and a staleness window between captures, in exchange for bounding replay cost to something roughly constant instead of unbounded.","[testing] Verifies: benchmarks load latency on a high-version aggregate before/after adding snapshots (the latency curve should flatten), and proves that rebuilding from the log alone (all snapshots dropped) yields identical state — confirming the snapshot never became the source of truth."],"checklist":[{"text":"Replaying an event-sourced aggregate by folding every event from version 1 on each read makes the read cost O(n) in the total number of events ever written, not in the size of the answer actually needed — so latency grows purely because the append-only log keeps getting longer.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"Because an event log is append-only, the event count n never shrinks, so a replay-from-scratch load path has no ceiling: an account that has accumulated thousands of events pays for its entire history on every single read, even when only the current balance is needed.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"A snapshot is a periodically persisted copy of the aggregate's state at a given version (for example every 10 events) written to a separate store; the load path then starts from the latest snapshot's state and folds only the events after that version, bounding replay work to the tail of the log instead of the whole history.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"A snapshot must remain a derived cache, never the source of truth: the event log stays append-only and untouched, and deleting every snapshot must still let the system rebuild identical state by replaying from the log — if the state differs without snapshots, the snapshot has silently become authoritative.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"Snapshotting introduces trade-offs: extra storage for the snapshot table, a background job that must reliably fire after each qualifying write, and a staleness window bounded by the snapshot interval (at most the events between the last snapshot and the current version), which the tail replay always closes.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Correctness of a snapshot mechanism is verified by wiping the snapshot store, forcing the load path back onto full replay, and asserting the resulting state exactly matches the snapshot-backed path — this is the test that proves the snapshot never replaced the event log as ground truth.","dimension":"testing","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"The performance benefit of snapshotting is verified by benchmarking load latency on a high-version account before and after enabling snapshots: the latency curve should flatten to roughly constant cost instead of continuing to grow linearly with the number of events.","dimension":"testing","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Fixing this kind of unbounded-replay cost starts with measuring where the cost actually comes from — confirming that the fold over the full event array, not network or serialization overhead, is what scales with n — before introducing snapshotting as the remedy.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10}],"keywords":["snapshot","replay","append-only log","O(n) replay cost","derived cache"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"The latency comes from replaying every event from version 1 on each read, so cost scales with total events ever written, not the size of the answer — and because the log is append-only that count never shrinks, so heavy accounts just get slower forever. I'd first confirm the fold over the full array is actually the scaling cost, not network or serialization, before fixing it. The fix is snapshotting: periodically persist state at a given version in a separate store, then load from the latest snapshot and fold only the events after it, bounding replay to the tail. The snapshot must stay a derived cache — the log stays untouched, and wiping snapshots and forcing full replay should still produce identical state, which is how I'd verify correctness. There's a real cost: extra storage, a background job that must fire reliably, and a staleness window bounded by the snapshot interval. I'd verify the performance win by benchmarking latency before and after — it should flatten instead of growing linearly.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"The issue is that every read replays the aggregate from the very first event, so the read cost scales directly with how many events exist for that aggregate rather than with what the caller actually needs back. Before fixing anything I'd first check that the fold over the event array is really what's driving the slowdown, not something like network or serialization. The fix is to periodically persist a snapshot of the aggregate's state at a given version in a separate store, then have loads start from the latest snapshot and only fold the events after it, so replay only covers the recent tail. It's important the snapshot stays purely a cache — I'd verify that by wiping the snapshot store, forcing a full replay, and checking the state comes out identical either way. For the performance side, I'd benchmark load latency on a heavy account before and after turning snapshots on and expect it to flatten out instead of climbing with event count.","sonnet":80,"haiku":80},{"level":3,"answer":"I'd add a snapshot — basically saving the aggregate's state every so many events into its own table, so loading can start from the latest snapshot and only replay what came after it instead of everything. The important part is that the snapshot can't become the real source of truth — the event log still has to be what everything is derived from. So to check that's actually true, I'd delete all the snapshots, force it to replay from scratch, and make sure the state it produces is exactly the same as when snapshots are in use.","sonnet":45,"haiku":45},{"level":4,"answer":"I think the way to check this is fixed would be to clear out whatever snapshot data exists and make sure the app still comes up with the same result when it has to rebuild from scratch.","sonnet":15,"haiku":15},{"level":5,"answer":"This sounds like it just needs an index on the events table, or maybe caching the API response for a few seconds. If reads are slow I'd probably put a CDN or Redis cache in front of the endpoint rather than touching how the aggregate itself loads.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]}},"ideal":{"typescript":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nĐo trước: `loadState` gọi `eventRepo.find` lấy hết event của `aggregateId` rồi `reduce(applyEvent)` trên toàn bộ mảng — với một account đã tích luỹ hàng nghìn version, mỗi lần `GET /accounts/:id` đều fold lại từ đầu, nên latency tăng tuyến tính theo đúng n = tổng số event từng ghi, không phải theo kích thước response. Sửa bằng snapshot: thêm một `SnapshotRecord` entity/bảng `account_snapshots` (lưu `accountId`, `version`, `state` dạng JSON), và một job (chạy fire-and-forget ngay sau `appendEvent`, ví dụ mỗi khi `nextVersion % 10 === 0`) serialize `AccountState` hiện tại vào đó. `loadState` viết lại thành: tìm snapshot mới nhất bằng `snapshotRepo.findOne({ where: { accountId }, order: { version: \"DESC\" } })`, nếu có thì chỉ `eventRepo.find({ where: { aggregateId: accountId, version: MoreThan(snapshot.version) } })` rồi `reduce(applyEvent, JSON.parse(snapshot.state))` — tức là chỉ fold PHẦN ĐUÔI sau snapshot, không phải fold(applyEvent) trên n event như đang có. Điểm mấu chốt cần nói rõ: `account_snapshots` là một cache dẫn xuất, không phải nguồn sự thật — `events` vẫn append-only và không đổi gì; nếu `TRUNCATE account_snapshots` rồi gọi lại `loadState`, code phải tự fallback về nhánh replay-từ-đầu và cho ra state giống hệt, đó chính là test xác nhận snapshot chưa bao giờ thay thế event log. Đánh đổi: tốn thêm một bảng, một job ghi snapshot định kỳ, và một khoảng \"staleness\" tối đa 10 event giữa lần snapshot gần nhất và version hiện tại (luôn được bù lại bằng đoạn replay tail). Xác minh bằng cách benchmark `loadState` trên một account version cao trước/sau khi bật snapshot — đường latency phải phẳng lại gần hằng số thay vì tăng theo n — và viết một test xoá sạch `account_snapshots` rồi so sánh state với bản có snapshot, phải bằng nhau tuyệt đối.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nMeasure first: `loadState` calls `eventRepo.find` for every event of `aggregateId` and then `reduce(applyEvent)` over the whole array — for an account that has accumulated thousands of versions, every `GET /accounts/:id` folds from scratch, so latency grows linearly in n = the total number of events ever written, not in the size of the response. Fix with a snapshot: add a `SnapshotRecord` entity/table `account_snapshots` (storing `accountId`, `version`, `state` as JSON), plus a job (fired fire-and-forget right after `appendEvent`, e.g. whenever `nextVersion % 10 === 0`) that serializes the current `AccountState` into it. Rewrite `loadState` to: look up the latest snapshot with `snapshotRepo.findOne({ where: { accountId }, order: { version: \"DESC\" } })`, and if one exists, only `eventRepo.find({ where: { aggregateId: accountId, version: MoreThan(snapshot.version) } })` then `reduce(applyEvent, JSON.parse(snapshot.state))` — folding only the TAIL after the snapshot, instead of folding n events like today. The point to state explicitly: `account_snapshots` is a derived cache, not the source of truth — `events` stays append-only and untouched; if you `TRUNCATE account_snapshots` and call `loadState` again, the code must fall back to the replay-from-scratch branch and produce the identical state, which is exactly the test that confirms the snapshot never replaced the event log. Trade-off: an extra table, a periodic snapshot-writing job, and a staleness window of at most 10 events between the latest snapshot and the current version (always made up for by the tail replay). Verify by benchmarking `loadState` on a high-version account before/after enabling snapshots — the latency curve should flatten to roughly constant instead of growing with n — and by writing a test that wipes `account_snapshots` and compares the resulting state against the snapshot-backed path, which must be exactly equal."},"java":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nĐo trước: `loadState` chạy JPQL lấy hết `EventRecord` của `aggregateId` rồi đưa cả `List` vào `replayEvents`, fold tuần tự qua `applyEvent` — với một account đã tích luỹ hàng nghìn version, mỗi lần `GET /accounts/{id}` đều fold lại từ đầu, nên latency tăng tuyến tính theo đúng n = tổng số event từng ghi. Sửa bằng snapshot: thêm một `@Entity AccountSnapshot` ánh xạ tới bảng `account_snapshots` (cột `accountId`, `version`, `stateJson`), và một tác vụ chạy sau `appendEvent` (ví dụ `@Async`, kích hoạt khi `nextVersion % 10 == 0`) serialize `AccountState` hiện tại vào đó. Viết lại `loadState`: query snapshot mới nhất bằng `SELECT s FROM AccountSnapshot s WHERE s.accountId = :id ORDER BY s.version DESC` (giới hạn 1 kết quả), nếu có thì chỉ query tiếp `EventRecord` với `e.version > :snapshotVersion`, rồi fold các event đó lên state đã deserialize từ snapshot — tức chỉ fold PHẦN ĐUÔI sau snapshot, không phải toàn bộ n event như hiện tại. Điểm mấu chốt cần nói rõ: `account_snapshots` là một cache dẫn xuất, không phải nguồn sự thật — bảng `events` vẫn append-only, không đổi gì; nếu `TRUNCATE account_snapshots` rồi gọi lại `loadState`, code phải tự rơi về nhánh replay-từ-đầu (không có snapshot) và cho ra state giống hệt, đó chính là test xác nhận snapshot chưa bao giờ thay thế event log. Đánh đổi: tốn thêm một bảng, một tác vụ ghi snapshot định kỳ, và một khoảng \"staleness\" tối đa 10 event giữa lần snapshot gần nhất và version hiện tại (luôn được bù lại bằng đoạn replay đuôi). Xác minh bằng cách benchmark `loadState` trên một account version cao trước/sau khi bật snapshot — đường latency phải phẳng lại gần hằng số thay vì tăng theo n — và viết một test xoá sạch `account_snapshots` rồi so sánh state với bản có snapshot, phải bằng nhau tuyệt đối.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nMeasure first: `loadState` runs a JPQL query for every `EventRecord` of `aggregateId` and passes the whole `List` into `replayEvents`, folding sequentially through `applyEvent` — for an account that has accumulated thousands of versions, every `GET /accounts/{id}` folds from scratch, so latency grows linearly in n = the total number of events ever written. Fix with a snapshot: add an `@Entity AccountSnapshot` mapped to an `account_snapshots` table (columns `accountId`, `version`, `stateJson`), plus a task run after `appendEvent` (e.g. `@Async`, triggered when `nextVersion % 10 == 0`) that serializes the current `AccountState` into it. Rewrite `loadState`: query the latest snapshot with `SELECT s FROM AccountSnapshot s WHERE s.accountId = :id ORDER BY s.version DESC` (limit 1 result); if one exists, query only `EventRecord`s with `e.version > :snapshotVersion` and fold those onto the state deserialized from the snapshot — folding only the TAIL after the snapshot, instead of all n events like today. The point to state explicitly: `account_snapshots` is a derived cache, not the source of truth — the `events` table stays append-only, untouched; if you `TRUNCATE account_snapshots` and call `loadState` again, the code must fall back to the replay-from-scratch branch (no snapshot) and produce the identical state, which is exactly the test that confirms the snapshot never replaced the event log. Trade-off: an extra table, a periodic snapshot-writing task, and a staleness window of at most 10 events between the latest snapshot and the current version (always made up for by the tail replay). Verify by benchmarking `loadState` on a high-version account before/after enabling snapshots — the latency curve should flatten to roughly constant instead of growing with n — and by writing a test that wipes `account_snapshots` and compares the resulting state against the snapshot-backed path, which must be exactly equal."},"csharp":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nĐo trước: `LoadState` truy vấn hết `db.Events` của `accountId`, sắp theo `Version`, rồi `foreach` fold tuần tự qua `ApplyEvent` — với một account đã tích luỹ hàng nghìn version, mỗi lần `GET /accounts/{id}` đều fold lại từ đầu, nên latency tăng tuyến tính theo đúng n = tổng số event từng ghi. Sửa bằng snapshot: thêm một entity `AccountSnapshot` (cột `AccountId`, `Version`, `StateJson`) ánh xạ tới bảng `account_snapshots` qua `db.Snapshots`, và một tác vụ chạy sau `AppendEvent` (fire-and-forget, ví dụ khi `nextVersion % 10 == 0`) serialize `AccountState` hiện tại vào đó. Viết lại `LoadState`: `db.Snapshots.Where(s => s.AccountId == accountId).OrderByDescending(s => s.Version).FirstOrDefaultAsync()` để lấy snapshot mới nhất, nếu có thì chỉ query tiếp `db.Events.Where(e => e.AggregateId == accountId && e.Version > snapshot.Version)`, rồi fold các event đó lên state deserialize từ snapshot thay vì `EmptyState(accountId)` — tức chỉ fold PHẦN ĐUÔI sau snapshot, không phải toàn bộ n event như hiện tại. Điểm mấu chốt cần nói rõ: `account_snapshots` là một cache dẫn xuất, không phải nguồn sự thật — bảng `Events` vẫn append-only, không đổi gì; nếu xoá sạch `account_snapshots` rồi gọi lại `LoadState`, code phải tự rơi về nhánh replay-từ-đầu và cho ra state giống hệt, đó chính là test xác nhận snapshot chưa bao giờ thay thế event log. Đánh đổi: tốn thêm một bảng, một tác vụ ghi snapshot định kỳ, và một khoảng \"staleness\" tối đa 10 event giữa lần snapshot gần nhất và version hiện tại (luôn được bù lại bằng đoạn replay đuôi). Xác minh bằng cách benchmark `LoadState` trên một account version cao trước/sau khi bật snapshot — đường latency phải phẳng lại gần hằng số thay vì tăng theo n — và viết một test xoá sạch `account_snapshots` rồi so sánh state với bản có snapshot, phải bằng nhau tuyệt đối.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nMeasure first: `LoadState` queries every row of `db.Events` for `accountId`, ordered by `Version`, then `foreach`-folds sequentially through `ApplyEvent` — for an account that has accumulated thousands of versions, every `GET /accounts/{id}` folds from scratch, so latency grows linearly in n = the total number of events ever written. Fix with a snapshot: add an `AccountSnapshot` entity (columns `AccountId`, `Version`, `StateJson`) mapped to an `account_snapshots` table via `db.Snapshots`, plus a task run after `AppendEvent` (fire-and-forget, e.g. when `nextVersion % 10 == 0`) that serializes the current `AccountState` into it. Rewrite `LoadState`: `db.Snapshots.Where(s => s.AccountId == accountId).OrderByDescending(s => s.Version).FirstOrDefaultAsync()` to get the latest snapshot; if one exists, query only `db.Events.Where(e => e.AggregateId == accountId && e.Version > snapshot.Version)`, then fold those events onto the state deserialized from the snapshot instead of `EmptyState(accountId)` — folding only the TAIL after the snapshot, instead of all n events like today. The point to state explicitly: `account_snapshots` is a derived cache, not the source of truth — the `Events` table stays append-only, untouched; if you wipe `account_snapshots` and call `LoadState` again, the code must fall back to the replay-from-scratch branch and produce the identical state, which is exactly the test that confirms the snapshot never replaced the event log. Trade-off: an extra table, a periodic snapshot-writing task, and a staleness window of at most 10 events between the latest snapshot and the current version (always made up for by the tail replay). Verify by benchmarking `LoadState` on a high-version account before/after enabling snapshots — the latency curve should flatten to roughly constant instead of growing with n — and by writing a test that wipes `account_snapshots` and compares the resulting state against the snapshot-backed path, which must be exactly equal."},"go":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nĐo trước: `loadState` query hết dòng của `events` theo `aggregate_id`, sắp theo `version ASC`, rồi `for rows.Next()` fold tuần tự qua `applyEvent` — với một account đã tích luỹ hàng nghìn version, mỗi lần `GET /accounts/:id` đều fold lại từ đầu, nên latency tăng tuyến tính theo đúng n = tổng số event từng ghi. Sửa bằng snapshot: dùng bảng `account_snapshots` đã có sẵn trong schema (cột `account_id`, `version`, `state` JSONB) nhưng đang không được đọc ở đây, cộng một hàm chạy sau `appendEvent` (ví dụ khi `nextVersion % snapshotThreshold == 0`, với `snapshotThreshold = 10`) serialize `AccountState` hiện tại vào đó. Viết lại `loadState`: trước tiên query dòng snapshot mới nhất bằng `SELECT version, state FROM account_snapshots WHERE account_id = $1 ORDER BY version DESC LIMIT 1`, nếu có thì chỉ query tiếp `events` với `version > snap.Version`, rồi fold các event đó lên state đã decode từ snapshot thay vì `AccountState{}` rỗng — tức chỉ fold PHẦN ĐUÔI sau snapshot, không phải toàn bộ n event như hiện tại; nếu KHÔNG có snapshot mới rơi về đúng nhánh replay-từ-đầu như code gốc. Điểm mấu chốt cần nói rõ: `account_snapshots` là một cache dẫn xuất, không phải nguồn sự thật — bảng `events` vẫn append-only, không đổi gì; nếu `DELETE FROM account_snapshots` rồi gọi lại `loadState`, hàm phải tự rơi về nhánh replay-từ-đầu và cho ra state giống hệt, đó chính là test xác nhận snapshot chưa bao giờ thay thế event log. Đánh đổi: tốn thêm một bảng, một tác vụ ghi snapshot định kỳ, và một khoảng \"staleness\" tối đa 10 event giữa lần snapshot gần nhất và version hiện tại (luôn được bù lại bằng đoạn replay đuôi). Xác minh bằng cách benchmark `loadState` trên một account version cao trước/sau khi bật đọc snapshot — đường latency phải phẳng lại gần hằng số thay vì tăng theo n — và viết một test xoá sạch `account_snapshots` rồi so sánh state với bản có snapshot, phải bằng nhau tuyệt đối.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nMeasure first: `loadState` queries every row of `events` for `aggregate_id`, ordered by `version ASC`, then `for rows.Next()` folds sequentially through `applyEvent` — for an account that has accumulated thousands of versions, every `GET /accounts/:id` folds from scratch, so latency grows linearly in n = the total number of events ever written. Fix with a snapshot: use the `account_snapshots` table already present in the schema (columns `account_id`, `version`, `state` JSONB) but never read here, plus a function run after `appendEvent` (e.g. when `nextVersion % snapshotThreshold == 0`, with `snapshotThreshold = 10`) that serializes the current `AccountState` into it. Rewrite `loadState`: first query the latest snapshot row with `SELECT version, state FROM account_snapshots WHERE account_id = $1 ORDER BY version DESC LIMIT 1`; if one exists, query only `events` with `version > snap.Version`, then fold those events onto the state decoded from the snapshot instead of an empty `AccountState{}` — folding only the TAIL after the snapshot, instead of all n events like today; with NO snapshot, fall back to exactly the original replay-from-scratch branch. The point to state explicitly: `account_snapshots` is a derived cache, not the source of truth — the `events` table stays append-only, untouched; if you `DELETE FROM account_snapshots` and call `loadState` again, the function must fall back to the replay-from-scratch branch and produce the identical state, which is exactly the test that confirms the snapshot never replaced the event log. Trade-off: an extra table, a periodic snapshot-writing task, and a staleness window of at most 10 events between the latest snapshot and the current version (always made up for by the tail replay). Verify by benchmarking `loadState` on a high-version account before/after wiring in snapshot reads — the latency curve should flatten to roughly constant instead of growing with n — and by writing a test that wipes `account_snapshots` and compares the resulting state against the snapshot-backed path, which must be exactly equal."}}}$iv$::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (bank_id, bank_version, question_id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO interview_questions (bank_id, bank_version, question_id, sort_index, is_premium, family, tier, kind, tags, competency, leadership_tier, bodies, interviewer, grading)
VALUES ('1-system-design-mastery/3-communication-patterns', '231d8cc57+37d905d9a', '1-system-design-mastery/3-communication-patterns/12', 12, false, 'technical', 'senior', 'optimize', ARRAY['cqrs', 'read-model-rebuild', 'zero-downtime-reindex']::text[], NULL, NULL, $iv${"typescript":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng typescript. Triệu chứng: rebuild dội tải lên Elasticsearch, các query bình thường chậm hẳn trong lúc chạy, và client thoáng thấy một index nửa-vời vì document đang được ghi thẳng vào đúng cái index mà read đang chạm tới. Đọc code cho sẵn, rồi giải thích các chi phí cộng dồn đứng sau độ chậm này, và thiết kế lại rebuild để có thể replay một lịch sử lớn mà không downtime, không làm read xuống cấp.","givenCode":"```typescript\n@Injectable()\nexport class ReadModelRebuildService {\n  constructor(\n    @InjectRepository(Customer) private readonly repo: Repository<Customer>,\n    private readonly esClient: Client,\n  ) {}\n\n  async rebuildCustomerIndex(): Promise<void> {\n    const rows = await this.repo.find(); // replay the full write-model history\n    for (const row of rows) {\n      // one document at a time, straight into the LIVE index serving GET /customer/:id\n      await this.esClient.index({\n        index: 'customers',\n        id: row.id,\n        document: { id: row.id, name: row.name, email: row.email },\n        refresh: true, // forces a costly segment flush on every single document\n      });\n    }\n  }\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in typescript. Symptoms: the rebuild hammers Elasticsearch, ordinary queries slow to a crawl while it runs, and clients transiently see a half-populated index because documents are being written into the very index reads are hitting. Read the given code, then explain the compounding costs behind the slowdown, and redesign the rebuild so a large history can be replayed with no downtime and no degraded reads.","givenCode":"```typescript\n@Injectable()\nexport class ReadModelRebuildService {\n  constructor(\n    @InjectRepository(Customer) private readonly repo: Repository<Customer>,\n    private readonly esClient: Client,\n  ) {}\n\n  async rebuildCustomerIndex(): Promise<void> {\n    const rows = await this.repo.find(); // replay the full write-model history\n    for (const row of rows) {\n      // one document at a time, straight into the LIVE index serving GET /customer/:id\n      await this.esClient.index({\n        index: 'customers',\n        id: row.id,\n        document: { id: row.id, name: row.name, email: row.email },\n        refresh: true, // forces a costly segment flush on every single document\n      });\n    }\n  }\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"java":{"vi":{"prompt":"Service rebuild dưới đây replay TOÀN BỘ lịch sử customer từ write model vào thẳng index Elasticsearch đang LIVE mà GET /customer/{id} vẫn đang đọc. Triệu chứng: rebuild dội tải lên Elasticsearch, các query bình thường chậm hẳn trong lúc chạy, và client thoáng thấy một index nửa-vời vì document đang được ghi thẳng vào đúng cái index mà read đang chạm tới. Đọc code cho sẵn, rồi giải thích các chi phí cộng dồn đứng sau độ chậm này, và thiết kế lại rebuild để có thể replay một lịch sử lớn mà không downtime, không làm read xuống cấp.","givenCode":"```java\n@Service\n@RequiredArgsConstructor\npublic class ReadModelRebuildService {\n    private final CustomerRepository repo;      // JPA repository over the write model\n    private final ElasticsearchClient esClient;  // live read model\n\n    public void rebuildCustomerIndex() throws IOException {\n        List<Customer> rows = repo.findAll(); // replay the full write-model history\n        for (Customer row : rows) {\n            // one document at a time, straight into the LIVE index serving GET /customer/{id}\n            esClient.index(IndexRequest.of(req -> req\n                    .index(INDEX)\n                    .id(row.getId())\n                    .document(new CustomerDoc(row.getId(), row.getName(), row.getEmail()))\n                    .refresh(Refresh.True) // forces a costly segment flush on every single document\n            ));\n        }\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in java. Symptoms: the rebuild hammers Elasticsearch, ordinary queries slow to a crawl while it runs, and clients transiently see a half-populated index because documents are being written into the very index reads are hitting. Read the given code, then explain the compounding costs behind the slowdown, and redesign the rebuild so a large history can be replayed with no downtime and no degraded reads.","givenCode":"```java\n@Service\n@RequiredArgsConstructor\npublic class ReadModelRebuildService {\n    private final CustomerRepository repo;      // JPA repository over the write model\n    private final ElasticsearchClient esClient;  // live read model\n\n    public void rebuildCustomerIndex() throws IOException {\n        List<Customer> rows = repo.findAll(); // replay the full write-model history\n        for (Customer row : rows) {\n            // one document at a time, straight into the LIVE index serving GET /customer/{id}\n            esClient.index(IndexRequest.of(req -> req\n                    .index(INDEX)\n                    .id(row.getId())\n                    .document(new CustomerDoc(row.getId(), row.getName(), row.getEmail()))\n                    .refresh(Refresh.True) // forces a costly segment flush on every single document\n            ));\n        }\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"csharp":{"vi":{"prompt":"Service rebuild dưới đây replay TOÀN BỘ lịch sử customer từ write model (AppDbContext trên PostgreSQL) vào thẳng index Elasticsearch đang LIVE mà GET /customer/{id} vẫn đang đọc. Triệu chứng: rebuild dội tải lên Elasticsearch, các query bình thường chậm hẳn trong lúc chạy, và client thoáng thấy một index nửa-vời vì document đang được ghi thẳng vào đúng cái index mà read đang chạm tới. Đọc code cho sẵn, rồi giải thích các chi phí cộng dồn đứng sau độ chậm này, và thiết kế lại rebuild để có thể replay một lịch sử lớn mà không downtime, không làm read xuống cấp.","givenCode":"```csharp\npublic class ReadModelRebuildService\n{\n    private readonly AppDbContext _db;        // write model (PostgreSQL)\n    private readonly ElasticsearchClient _es; // live read model\n\n    public async Task RebuildCustomerIndexAsync()\n    {\n        var rows = await _db.Customers.ToListAsync(); // replay the full write-model history\n\n        foreach (var row in rows)\n        {\n            // one document at a time, straight into the LIVE index serving GET /customer/{id}\n            await _es.IndexAsync(new CustomerDoc(row.Id, row.Name, row.Email), idx => idx\n                .Index(\"customers\")\n                .Id(row.Id)\n                .Refresh(Refresh.True)); // forces a costly segment flush on every single document\n        }\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in C#/ASP.NET Core. Symptoms: the rebuild hammers Elasticsearch, ordinary queries slow to a crawl while it runs, and clients transiently see a half-populated index because documents are being written into the very index reads are hitting. Read the given code, then explain the compounding costs behind the slowdown, and redesign the rebuild so a large history can be replayed with no downtime and no degraded reads.","givenCode":"```csharp\npublic class ReadModelRebuildService\n{\n    private readonly AppDbContext _db;        // write model (PostgreSQL)\n    private readonly ElasticsearchClient _es; // live read model\n\n    public async Task RebuildCustomerIndexAsync()\n    {\n        var rows = await _db.Customers.ToListAsync(); // replay the full write-model history\n\n        foreach (var row in rows)\n        {\n            // one document at a time, straight into the LIVE index serving GET /customer/{id}\n            await _es.IndexAsync(new CustomerDoc(row.Id, row.Name, row.Email), idx => idx\n                .Index(\"customers\")\n                .Id(row.Id)\n                .Refresh(Refresh.True)); // forces a costly segment flush on every single document\n        }\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"go":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng go. Hàm dưới đây replay TOÀN BỘ lịch sử customer từ write model (PostgreSQL) vào thẳng collection MongoDB đang LIVE mà GET /customer/{id} vẫn đang đọc. Triệu chứng: rebuild làm MongoDB bão hoà, các query bình thường chậm hẳn trong lúc chạy, và client thoáng thấy một collection nửa-vời vì document đang được ghi thẳng vào đúng cái collection mà read đang chạm tới. Đọc code cho sẵn, rồi giải thích các chi phí cộng dồn đứng sau độ chậm này, và thiết kế lại rebuild để có thể replay một lịch sử lớn mà không downtime, không làm read xuống cấp.","givenCode":"```go\nfunc RebuildCustomerReadModel(ctx context.Context, db *pgxpool.Pool, col *mongo.Collection) error {\n\trows, err := db.Query(ctx, `SELECT id, name, email FROM customers`) // replay the full write-model history\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tdefer rows.Close()\n\n\tjournal := true\n\twc := writeconcern.Majority()\n\twc.Journal = &journal // forces an fsync-acknowledge on every single write\n\n\tliveCol, err := col.Clone(options.Collection().SetWriteConcern(wc)) // same LIVE collection GET /customer/{id} reads from\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\n\tfor rows.Next() {\n\t\tvar id, name, email string\n\t\tif err := rows.Scan(&id, &name, &email); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn err\n\t\t}\n\t\t// one document at a time\n\t\tfilter := bson.M{\"id\": id}\n\t\tupdate := bson.M{\"$set\": bson.M{\"id\": id, \"name\": name, \"email\": email}}\n\t\tif _, err := liveCol.UpdateOne(ctx, filter, update, options.Update().SetUpsert(true)); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn err\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn rows.Err()\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in go — the concept applies to any stack.) This module's Go stack keeps its read model in MongoDB rather than Elasticsearch, but the exact same rebuild mistake shows up in the exact same shape. The function below replays the ENTIRE customer history from the write model (PostgreSQL) into the LIVE MongoDB collection that GET /customer/{id} is still reading from. Symptoms: the rebuild saturates MongoDB, ordinary queries slow to a crawl while it runs, and clients transiently see a half-populated collection because documents are being written into the very collection reads are hitting. Read the given code, then explain the compounding costs behind the slowdown, and redesign the rebuild so a large history can be replayed with no downtime and no degraded reads.","givenCode":"```go\nfunc RebuildCustomerReadModel(ctx context.Context, db *pgxpool.Pool, col *mongo.Collection) error {\n\trows, err := db.Query(ctx, `SELECT id, name, email FROM customers`) // replay the full write-model history\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\tdefer rows.Close()\n\n\tjournal := true\n\twc := writeconcern.Majority()\n\twc.Journal = &journal // forces an fsync-acknowledge on every single write\n\n\tliveCol, err := col.Clone(options.Collection().SetWriteConcern(wc)) // same LIVE collection GET /customer/{id} reads from\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\n\tfor rows.Next() {\n\t\tvar id, name, email string\n\t\tif err := rows.Scan(&id, &name, &email); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn err\n\t\t}\n\t\t// one document at a time\n\t\tfilter := bson.M{\"id\": id}\n\t\tupdate := bson.M{\"$set\": bson.M{\"id\": id, \"name\": name, \"email\": email}}\n\t\tif _, err := liveCol.UpdateOne(ctx, filter, update, options.Update().SetUpsert(true)); err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn err\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\treturn rows.Err()\n}\n```","diagram":null}}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"vi":{"followUps":["Trong lúc rebuild theo batch đang chạy, các write vẫn tiếp tục đổ vào write model — làm sao đảm bảo các event phát ra TRONG LÚC rebuild không bị mất tính đến khi bạn swap read sang store mới?","Nếu store bạn đang rebuild vào không có sẵn cơ chế atomic alias/rename, bạn sẽ giả lập một cú swap atomic an toàn thế nào?"],"hints":["Tách riêng ba thứ đang xảy ra ở MỖI lần gọi trong vòng lặp: round-trip mạng, bản thân việc ghi, và flush/acknowledge bị ép buộc. Chỉ riêng batch sẽ không sửa được cái nào trong ba cái đó?","Nếu client đang đọc đúng cái index/collection mà bạn đang ghi rebuild vào, một query chạy đúng giữa chừng sẽ thấy gì?"]},"en":{"followUps":["Writes keep landing on the write model while your batched rebuild is running — how do you make sure events published DURING the rebuild aren't lost by the time you swap reads over to the new store?","If the store you're rebuilding into has no built-in atomic alias/rename mechanism, how would you fake an atomic swap safely?"],"hints":["Separate the three things happening on every single call in the loop: the network round-trip, the write itself, and the forced flush/acknowledge. Which of those would batching ALONE not fix?","If clients are reading from the exact same index/collection you are writing the rebuild into, what does a query see if it runs exactly halfway through?"]}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"locales":{"vi":{"rubric":["[problemSolving] Chẩn đoán cả BA chi phí cộng dồn cùng lúc: bị ép flush/acknowledge sau mỗi lần ghi, ghi từng document một thay vì theo batch, và rebuild thẳng vào read-model store đang LIVE phục vụ query — chứ không chỉ bắt đúng một trong ba, tách rời nhau.","[technical] Nêu đúng cơ chế: ép store flush bền vững sau MỖI lần ghi (refresh theo từng document của search index, hay acknowledge ghi journal theo từng write của document store) vốn là thao tác đắt, đáng ra chỉ nên trả giá MỘT LẦN cho cả batch, không phải một lần cho mỗi document.","[technical] Đề xuất đúng cách sửa: batch lại và nới lỏng flush theo-từng-write — ghi theo bulk/batch, tạm nới độ bền theo-từng-write của store trong lúc rebuild (ví dụ tăng refresh interval của search index, hoặc hạ xuống write concern nhẹ hơn), để chỉ flush một lần ở cuối thay vì một lần mỗi document.","[technical] Đề xuất đúng zero-downtime reindex: dựng một store MỚI (index/collection) tách riêng, replay toàn bộ lịch sử của write model vào đó, rồi atomically trỏ read sang store mới (alias swap, atomic rename) — không bao giờ sửa trực tiếp read model đang phục vụ, để client không bao giờ thấy kết quả nửa-vời.","[communication] Trình bày rõ lập luận đúng-đắn của CQRS: read model là một projection dẫn xuất, idempotent (upsert-theo-id) từ write model, nên rebuild lại từ đầu LUÔN an toàn — giải thích điều này để trấn an rằng rebuild toàn bộ không phải một hack rủi ro một-lần.","[testing] Xác minh cách sửa: đo latency query live trong lúc rebuild (phải giữ phẳng với cách dùng store phụ), xác nhận việc swap sang store mới là atomic (không có khoảng-hở kết quả nửa-vời), và kiểm số bản ghi/spot-check của store vừa rebuild so với write model trước khi swap."],"checklist":[{"text":"The rebuild's slowdown comes from three costs compounding together, not from a single bottleneck: a forced flush on every single write, one network round-trip per document instead of batching, and writing directly into the live index that ordinary queries are still hitting.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"Passing `refresh: true` on every `esClient.index()` call forces Elasticsearch to flush a new searchable segment after each individual document — an operation expensive enough that it is meant to be paid once per batch, not once per row, so over a real-sized history it adds up to thousands of unnecessary flushes.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"Indexing documents one at a time instead of through the bulk API wastes a full network round-trip per row, multiplying overhead across the entire history being replayed.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"The fix for the write-side cost is to switch to the bulk API (`esClient.helpers.bulk` or `esClient.bulk`) to batch hundreds of documents per request, drop `refresh: true`, and optionally set `refresh_interval: -1` on the target index during the load, refreshing once at the end — this removes almost all the flush and round-trip overhead.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Rebuilding into the same `'customers'` index that `GET /customer/:id` reads from makes every in-flight query compete with the rebuild for that index's resources, and a query landing mid-rebuild sees whatever partial subset has been re-indexed so far — a half-populated read model exposed to live traffic.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"A zero-downtime reindex builds a brand-new index (e.g. `customers_v2`) off to the side, bulk-loads the entire history into it, and then atomically repoints the `customers` alias from the old index to the new one via a single `indices.updateAliases` call, so the swap is instantaneous and clients never observe a partial index.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"This full rebuild is safe specifically because of CQRS: the read model in Elasticsearch is a derived, idempotent, upsert-by-id projection of the write model in Postgres, so the write model itself is never touched and a from-scratch reindex risks nothing on the source of truth.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Verifying the fix means confirming query latency stays flat throughout the bulk-and-swap rebuild, that the alias flip happens as a single atomic call with no window where `customers` resolves to nothing, and that the new index's document count matches `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM customers` before the alias is flipped.","dimension":"testing","critical":false,"scoreBand":10}],"keywords":["bulk write batching","per-document refresh flush","zero-downtime reindex","alias swap","idempotent upsert projection"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"There are really three costs stacking on top of each other here. First, refresh: true on every index() call forces a new searchable segment flush after each document — that's meant to happen once per batch, not once per row, so across a full history it's thousands of unnecessary flushes. Second, indexing one document at a time instead of using the bulk API burns a full network round-trip per row. The fix for both is to switch to the bulk API, drop refresh: true, and optionally set refresh_interval: -1 on the index during the load, refreshing once at the end. Third, the rebuild writes directly into the same customers index that GET /customer/:id reads from, so live queries are competing with the rebuild and can see a half-populated index mid-rebuild. The safe pattern is to build a new customers_v2 index off to the side, bulk-load the full history into it, and then atomically repoint the customers alias with a single indices.updateAliases call so clients never see a partial state. This is safe to do at all because of CQRS — Elasticsearch is just a derived, idempotent, upsert-by-id projection of the Postgres write model, so re-deriving it touches nothing on the source of truth. I'd verify by watching query latency stay flat throughout, confirming the alias flip is a single atomic call with no gap, and checking the new index's doc count matches SELECT COUNT(*) before flipping.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"I'd say there are two real problems compounding here. One is that refresh: true is being passed on every single index() call, which forces Elasticsearch to flush a searchable segment after each document — that's an expensive operation meant to happen once per batch, not per row, so over a full history it adds up to a huge amount of overhead. The other, bigger one is that the rebuild writes straight into the same customers index that GET /customer/:id is reading from, so live queries are competing with the rebuild and can see a half-populated index while it's running. The safer approach is to build a separate customers_v2 index, load the full history into that instead, and then atomically flip the customers alias over to it in one indices.updateAliases call, so there's no window where the live index is partial. This is safe to do because Elasticsearch here is just a derived, idempotent projection of the Postgres data — the source of truth is untouched by a full rebuild. I'd verify by checking latency stays flat during the rebuild and that the new index's count matches Postgres before the alias flips.","sonnet":80,"haiku":80},{"level":3,"answer":"Looking at this, the refresh: true flag on every write is probably a big part of it — that forces a flush each time instead of batching, which gets expensive fast. Also it looks like the rebuild is writing into the same index that's actually serving live reads, so queries are probably competing with it and could see incomplete data while it's running. I'd probably build a separate index for the rebuild and then switch over to it once it's done, instead of writing into the live one directly.","sonnet":45,"haiku":45},{"level":4,"answer":"I think the fix here is basically to build a new index somewhere else and then just point things over to it once it's ready, instead of writing into the one that's already live.","sonnet":15,"haiku":15},{"level":5,"answer":"This is probably just an Elasticsearch cluster sizing problem — maybe it needs more shards or a bigger heap size. I'd look at scaling up the cluster or adding more nodes so it can handle the load during the rebuild.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]},"en":{"rubric":["[problemSolving] Diagnoses the three compounding costs together: a forced per-document flush/acknowledge on every single write, single-document round-trips instead of batching, and rebuilding directly into the LIVE read-model store that is still serving queries — not just one of the three in isolation.","[technical] States the mechanism precisely: forcing the store to durably flush after every write (a search index's per-document refresh, or a document store's per-write journaled acknowledge) is a genuinely expensive operation meant to be paid once per batch, not once per document.","[technical] Prescribes batching plus relaxing the per-write flush: write in bulk/batches and temporarily loosen the store's per-write durability setting during the rebuild (e.g. raise a search index's refresh interval, or drop to a lighter write concern), forcing one flush at the very end instead of one per document.","[technical] Prescribes a zero-downtime reindex: build a brand-new store (index/collection) off to the side by replaying the write model's history into it, then atomically point reads at the new store (an alias swap, an atomic rename) — never mutating the in-service read model in place, so clients never see a half-populated result.","[communication] Frames the CQRS correctness argument clearly: the read model is a derived, idempotent (upsert-by-id) projection of the write model, so rebuilding it from scratch is always safe — explaining this to reassure that a full rebuild isn't a risky one-off hack.","[testing] Verifies the fix: measures live query latency during the rebuild (should stay flat with the side-store approach), confirms the swap to the new store is atomic (no partial-result window), and validates the rebuilt store's record count/spot-checks against the write model before swapping."],"checklist":[{"text":"The rebuild's slowdown comes from three costs compounding together, not from a single bottleneck: a forced flush on every single write, one network round-trip per document instead of batching, and writing directly into the live index that ordinary queries are still hitting.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"Passing `refresh: true` on every `esClient.index()` call forces Elasticsearch to flush a new searchable segment after each individual document — an operation expensive enough that it is meant to be paid once per batch, not once per row, so over a real-sized history it adds up to thousands of unnecessary flushes.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"Indexing documents one at a time instead of through the bulk API wastes a full network round-trip per row, multiplying overhead across the entire history being replayed.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"The fix for the write-side cost is to switch to the bulk API (`esClient.helpers.bulk` or `esClient.bulk`) to batch hundreds of documents per request, drop `refresh: true`, and optionally set `refresh_interval: -1` on the target index during the load, refreshing once at the end — this removes almost all the flush and round-trip overhead.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Rebuilding into the same `'customers'` index that `GET /customer/:id` reads from makes every in-flight query compete with the rebuild for that index's resources, and a query landing mid-rebuild sees whatever partial subset has been re-indexed so far — a half-populated read model exposed to live traffic.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"A zero-downtime reindex builds a brand-new index (e.g. `customers_v2`) off to the side, bulk-loads the entire history into it, and then atomically repoints the `customers` alias from the old index to the new one via a single `indices.updateAliases` call, so the swap is instantaneous and clients never observe a partial index.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"This full rebuild is safe specifically because of CQRS: the read model in Elasticsearch is a derived, idempotent, upsert-by-id projection of the write model in Postgres, so the write model itself is never touched and a from-scratch reindex risks nothing on the source of truth.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Verifying the fix means confirming query latency stays flat throughout the bulk-and-swap rebuild, that the alias flip happens as a single atomic call with no window where `customers` resolves to nothing, and that the new index's document count matches `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM customers` before the alias is flipped.","dimension":"testing","critical":false,"scoreBand":10}],"keywords":["bulk write batching","per-document refresh flush","zero-downtime reindex","alias swap","idempotent upsert projection"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"There are really three costs stacking on top of each other here. First, refresh: true on every index() call forces a new searchable segment flush after each document — that's meant to happen once per batch, not once per row, so across a full history it's thousands of unnecessary flushes. Second, indexing one document at a time instead of using the bulk API burns a full network round-trip per row. The fix for both is to switch to the bulk API, drop refresh: true, and optionally set refresh_interval: -1 on the index during the load, refreshing once at the end. Third, the rebuild writes directly into the same customers index that GET /customer/:id reads from, so live queries are competing with the rebuild and can see a half-populated index mid-rebuild. The safe pattern is to build a new customers_v2 index off to the side, bulk-load the full history into it, and then atomically repoint the customers alias with a single indices.updateAliases call so clients never see a partial state. This is safe to do at all because of CQRS — Elasticsearch is just a derived, idempotent, upsert-by-id projection of the Postgres write model, so re-deriving it touches nothing on the source of truth. I'd verify by watching query latency stay flat throughout, confirming the alias flip is a single atomic call with no gap, and checking the new index's doc count matches SELECT COUNT(*) before flipping.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"I'd say there are two real problems compounding here. One is that refresh: true is being passed on every single index() call, which forces Elasticsearch to flush a searchable segment after each document — that's an expensive operation meant to happen once per batch, not per row, so over a full history it adds up to a huge amount of overhead. The other, bigger one is that the rebuild writes straight into the same customers index that GET /customer/:id is reading from, so live queries are competing with the rebuild and can see a half-populated index while it's running. The safer approach is to build a separate customers_v2 index, load the full history into that instead, and then atomically flip the customers alias over to it in one indices.updateAliases call, so there's no window where the live index is partial. This is safe to do because Elasticsearch here is just a derived, idempotent projection of the Postgres data — the source of truth is untouched by a full rebuild. I'd verify by checking latency stays flat during the rebuild and that the new index's count matches Postgres before the alias flips.","sonnet":80,"haiku":80},{"level":3,"answer":"Looking at this, the refresh: true flag on every write is probably a big part of it — that forces a flush each time instead of batching, which gets expensive fast. Also it looks like the rebuild is writing into the same index that's actually serving live reads, so queries are probably competing with it and could see incomplete data while it's running. I'd probably build a separate index for the rebuild and then switch over to it once it's done, instead of writing into the live one directly.","sonnet":45,"haiku":45},{"level":4,"answer":"I think the fix here is basically to build a new index somewhere else and then just point things over to it once it's ready, instead of writing into the one that's already live.","sonnet":15,"haiku":15},{"level":5,"answer":"This is probably just an Elasticsearch cluster sizing problem — maybe it needs more shards or a bigger heap size. I'd look at scaling up the cluster or adding more nodes so it can handle the load during the rebuild.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]}},"ideal":{"typescript":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nĐoạn rebuild cho sẵn chạy đúng nhưng ba chi phí cộng dồn khiến nó vừa chậm vừa không an toàn. Thứ nhất, `refresh: true` trên mỗi lần gọi `esClient.index()` ép Elasticsearch flush một segment có thể search được sau MỖI document — đây vốn là thao tác đắt, đáng ra chỉ nên trả giá một lần cho cả batch chứ không phải một lần cho mỗi dòng; với một lịch sử cỡ thật, đó là hàng nghìn lần flush thừa. Thứ hai, index từng document một qua mỗi lần gọi lãng phí một round-trip mạng cho mỗi dòng thay vì đẩy nhiều document trong một request. Thứ ba, và nặng nhất: `rebuildCustomerIndex` ghi vào đúng index `'customers'` mà `GET /customer/:id` đang đọc — mọi query đang chạy giờ phải cạnh tranh tài nguyên của index đó với rebuild, và một query rơi đúng giữa lúc rebuild sẽ chỉ thấy phần đã được index lại tính đến thời điểm đó, tức một read model nửa-vời. Cách sửa phải chạm cả ba đòn bẩy. (1) Ngừng gọi `index()` cho từng dòng — dùng bulk API (`esClient.helpers.bulk` hoặc `esClient.bulk`) để gộp hàng trăm document mỗi request, và bỏ hẳn `refresh: true` trong lúc rebuild; `refresh_interval` mặc định của Elasticsearch đã tự refresh định kỳ, và bạn còn có thể đặt thêm `refresh_interval: -1` trên index đích trong lúc bulk-load, rồi refresh một lần duy nhất ở cuối. Chỉ riêng bước này đã loại gần hết chi phí flush lẫn round-trip. (2) Không bao giờ rebuild thẳng vào index đang live — dựng một index hoàn toàn mới (`customers_v2`), bulk-load toàn bộ lịch sử vào đó, rồi atomically trỏ lại alias `customers` từ index cũ sang index mới chỉ bằng một lệnh `indices.updateAliases` duy nhất. Read luôn resolve qua alias, nên cú swap là tức thời và client không bao giờ thấy một index nửa-vời. (3) Điều này an toàn chính là nhờ CQRS: read model là một projection dẫn xuất, idempotent (upsert-theo-`id`) từ write model trên Postgres — write model không bao giờ chạm vào Elasticsearch, nên reindex lại từ đầu không rủi ro gì cho nguồn sự thật. Xác minh: chạy cùng một load profile lên bản rebuild-từng-document cũ và cách bulk + index-phụ mới, xác nhận latency query giữ phẳng suốt lần chạy thứ hai; xác nhận việc lật alias là một lệnh atomic duy nhất, không có khoảng-hở nào mà `customers` resolve ra rỗng; và so số lượng document của index mới với `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM customers` trước khi lật alias.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe given rebuild runs correctly but three costs compound to make it both slow and unsafe. First, `refresh: true` on every `esClient.index()` call forces Elasticsearch to flush a new searchable segment after each single document — a genuinely expensive operation meant to be paid once per batch, not once per row; over any real-sized history that's thousands of unnecessary flushes. Second, indexing one document per call wastes a network round-trip per row instead of pushing many documents in one request. Third, and worst: `rebuildCustomerIndex` writes into the SAME `'customers'` index that `GET /customer/:id` reads from — every in-flight query is now competing with the rebuild for that index's resources, and a query landing mid-rebuild sees whatever subset has been re-indexed so far, i.e. a half-populated read model. The fix touches all three levers. (1) Stop calling `index()` per row — use the bulk API (`esClient.helpers.bulk` or `esClient.bulk`) to batch hundreds of documents per request, and drop `refresh: true` entirely during the rebuild; Elasticsearch's default `refresh_interval` already refreshes periodically, and you can additionally set `refresh_interval: -1` on the target index while bulk-loading, then refresh once at the end. This alone removes almost all the flush and round-trip overhead. (2) Never rebuild into the live index — build a brand-new index (`customers_v2`), bulk-load the entire history into it, then atomically repoint the `customers` alias from the old index to the new one with a single `indices.updateAliases` call. Reads always resolve through the alias, so the swap is instantaneous and clients never see a partial index. (3) This is safe specifically because of CQRS: the read model is a derived, idempotent (upsert-by-`id`) projection of the write model in Postgres — the write model never touches Elasticsearch, so a from-scratch reindex risks nothing on the source of truth. Verify it: run the same load profile against the old per-document rebuild and the new bulk + side-index approach, and confirm query latency stays flat throughout the second run; confirm the alias flip is a single atomic call with no window where `customers` resolves to nothing; and diff the new index's document count against `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM customers` before flipping the alias."},"java":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nĐoạn rebuild cho sẵn chạy đúng nhưng ba chi phí cộng dồn khiến nó vừa chậm vừa không an toàn. Thứ nhất, `.refresh(Refresh.True)` trên mỗi lần gọi `esClient.index()` ép Elasticsearch flush một segment có thể search được sau MỖI document — đây vốn là thao tác đắt, đáng ra chỉ nên trả giá một lần cho cả batch chứ không phải một lần cho mỗi dòng; với toàn bộ lịch sử từ `repo.findAll()`, đó là hàng nghìn lần flush thừa. Thứ hai, mỗi dòng một `IndexRequest` lãng phí một round-trip mạng cho mỗi document thay vì đẩy nhiều document trong một lần gọi. Thứ ba, và nặng nhất: vòng lặp ghi vào đúng `INDEX` (`\"customers\"`) mà `GET /customer/{id}` đang đọc — mọi query đang chạy giờ phải cạnh tranh tài nguyên của index đó với rebuild, và một query rơi đúng giữa lúc rebuild sẽ chỉ thấy phần đã được index lại tính đến thời điểm đó, tức một read model nửa-vời. Cách sửa phải chạm cả ba đòn bẩy. (1) Ngừng gọi `index()` cho từng dòng — gom các row JPA thành từng batch (vài trăm dòng mỗi lần) và đẩy qua `BulkIngester` của Elasticsearch Java API Client (hoặc tự dựng một `BulkRequest` gồm nhiều `BulkOperation`, gửi trong một lần gọi), và bỏ hẳn `.refresh(Refresh.True)` trong lúc load — để `refresh_interval` mặc định của index tự refresh định kỳ, hoặc nới rộng tường minh (ví dụ `-1`) trên index đích trong lúc bulk-load rồi refresh một lần duy nhất ở cuối. Chỉ riêng bước này đã loại gần hết chi phí flush lẫn round-trip. (2) Không bao giờ rebuild thẳng vào index đang live — dựng một index hoàn toàn mới (`customers_v2`), nạp toàn bộ lịch sử qua `BulkIngester`, rồi atomically trỏ lại alias `customers` từ index cũ sang index mới chỉ bằng một lệnh `esClient.indices().updateAliases(...)` duy nhất. `GET /customer/{id}` luôn resolve qua alias, nên cú swap là tức thời và client không bao giờ thấy một index nửa-vời. (3) Điều này an toàn chính là nhờ CQRS: read model là một projection dẫn xuất, idempotent (upsert-theo-`id`) từ write model trên Postgres — phía write không bao giờ chạm vào Elasticsearch, nên reindex lại từ đầu không rủi ro gì cho nguồn sự thật (`repo.findAll()` luôn sẵn đó để replay lại). Xác minh: chạy cùng một load profile lên bản rebuild-từng-document cũ và cách `BulkIngester` + index-phụ mới, xác nhận latency query giữ phẳng suốt lần chạy thứ hai; xác nhận việc lật alias là một lệnh atomic duy nhất, không có khoảng-hở nào mà `customers` resolve ra rỗng; và so số lượng document của index mới với `repo.count()` trước khi lật alias.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe given rebuild runs correctly but three costs compound to make it both slow and unsafe. First, `.refresh(Refresh.True)` on every single `esClient.index()` call forces Elasticsearch to flush a new searchable segment after each document — a genuinely expensive operation meant to be paid once per batch, not once per row; over `repo.findAll()`'s full history that's thousands of unnecessary flushes. Second, one `IndexRequest` per row wastes a network round-trip per document instead of pushing many documents in one call. Third, and worst: the loop writes into the SAME `INDEX` (`\"customers\"`) that `GET /customer/{id}` reads from — every in-flight query now competes with the rebuild for that index's resources, and a query landing mid-rebuild sees whatever subset has been re-indexed so far, i.e. a half-populated read model. The fix touches all three levers. (1) Stop calling `index()` per row — collect the JPA rows into batches (e.g. a few hundred at a time) and submit them through the Elasticsearch Java API Client's `BulkIngester` (or hand-build a `BulkRequest` with one `BulkOperation` per document and send it in one call), and drop `.refresh(Refresh.True)` entirely during the load — let the index's default `refresh_interval` do its periodic refresh, or explicitly widen it (e.g. `-1`) on the target index while bulk-loading, then refresh once at the end. This alone removes almost all the flush and round-trip overhead. (2) Never rebuild into the live index — create a brand-new index (`customers_v2`), `BulkIngester`-load the entire history into it, then atomically repoint the `customers` alias from the old index to the new one via a single `esClient.indices().updateAliases(...)` call. `GET /customer/{id}` always resolves through the alias, so the swap is instantaneous and clients never see a partial index. (3) This is safe specifically because of CQRS: the read model is a derived, idempotent (upsert-by-`id`) projection of the write model in Postgres — the write side never touches Elasticsearch, so a from-scratch reindex risks nothing on the source of truth (`repo.findAll()` is always there to replay from). Verify it: run the same load profile against the old per-document rebuild and the new `BulkIngester` + side-index approach, and confirm query latency stays flat throughout the second run; confirm the alias flip is a single atomic call with no window where `customers` resolves to nothing; and diff the new index's document count against `repo.count()` before flipping the alias."},"csharp":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nĐoạn rebuild cho sẵn chạy đúng nhưng ba chi phí cộng dồn khiến nó vừa chậm vừa không an toàn. Thứ nhất, `.Refresh(Refresh.True)` trên mỗi lần gọi `IndexAsync` ép Elasticsearch flush một segment có thể search được sau MỖI document — đây vốn là thao tác đắt, đáng ra chỉ nên trả giá một lần cho cả batch chứ không phải một lần cho mỗi dòng; với toàn bộ lịch sử từ `_db.Customers.ToListAsync()`, đó là hàng nghìn lần flush thừa. Thứ hai, mỗi dòng một lần gọi `IndexAsync` lãng phí một round-trip mạng cho mỗi document thay vì đẩy nhiều document trong một request. Thứ ba, và nặng nhất: vòng lặp ghi vào đúng index `\"customers\"` mà `GET /customer/{id}` đang đọc — mọi query đang chạy giờ phải cạnh tranh tài nguyên của index đó với rebuild, và một query rơi đúng giữa lúc rebuild sẽ chỉ thấy phần đã được index lại tính đến thời điểm đó, tức một read model nửa-vời. Cách sửa phải chạm cả ba đòn bẩy. (1) Ngừng gọi `IndexAsync` cho từng dòng — gom các row EF Core thành từng chunk (vài trăm dòng mỗi lần) và đẩy qua `BulkAsync` của client .NET Elasticsearch (nhiều `IndexOperation<CustomerDoc>` mỗi lần gọi, hoặc dùng helper `BulkAllObservable` cho lịch sử rất lớn), và bỏ hẳn `.Refresh(Refresh.True)` trong lúc load — `refresh_interval` mặc định của index đã tự refresh định kỳ, và bạn còn có thể nới rộng thêm (ví dụ `-1`) trên index đích trong lúc bulk-load rồi refresh một lần duy nhất ở cuối. Chỉ riêng bước này đã loại gần hết chi phí flush lẫn round-trip. (2) Không bao giờ rebuild thẳng vào index đang live — dựng một index hoàn toàn mới (`customers_v2`), bulk-load toàn bộ lịch sử vào đó qua `BulkAllAsync`/`BulkAsync`, rồi atomically trỏ lại alias `customers` từ index cũ sang index mới chỉ bằng một lệnh `_es.Indices.UpdateAliasesAsync(...)` duy nhất. `GET /customer/{id}` luôn resolve qua alias, nên cú swap là tức thời và client không bao giờ thấy một index nửa-vời. (3) Điều này an toàn chính là nhờ CQRS: read model là một projection dẫn xuất, idempotent (upsert-theo-`Id`) từ write model trên Postgres — phía write không bao giờ chạm vào Elasticsearch, nên reindex lại từ đầu không rủi ro gì cho nguồn sự thật (`_db.Customers` luôn sẵn đó để replay lại). Xác minh: chạy cùng một load profile lên bản rebuild-từng-document cũ và cách bulk + index-phụ mới, xác nhận latency query giữ phẳng suốt lần chạy thứ hai; xác nhận việc lật alias là một lệnh atomic duy nhất, không có khoảng-hở nào mà `customers` resolve ra rỗng; và so số lượng document của index mới với `await _db.Customers.CountAsync()` trước khi lật alias.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe given rebuild runs correctly but three costs compound to make it both slow and unsafe. First, `.Refresh(Refresh.True)` on every single `IndexAsync` call forces Elasticsearch to flush a new searchable segment after each document — a genuinely expensive operation meant to be paid once per batch, not once per row; over `_db.Customers.ToListAsync()`'s full history that's thousands of unnecessary flushes. Second, one `IndexAsync` call per row wastes a network round-trip per document instead of pushing many documents in one request. Third, and worst: the loop writes into the SAME `\"customers\"` index that `GET /customer/{id}` reads from — every in-flight query now competes with the rebuild for that index's resources, and a query landing mid-rebuild sees whatever subset has been re-indexed so far, i.e. a half-populated read model. The fix touches all three levers. (1) Stop calling `IndexAsync` per row — batch the EF Core rows into chunks (e.g. a few hundred at a time) and submit each chunk through the Elasticsearch .NET client's `BulkAsync` (many `IndexOperation<CustomerDoc>`s per call, or the `BulkAllObservable` helper for very large histories), and drop `.Refresh(Refresh.True)` entirely during the load — the index's default `refresh_interval` already refreshes periodically, and you can additionally widen it (e.g. `-1`) on the target index while bulk-loading, then refresh once at the end. This alone removes almost all the flush and round-trip overhead. (2) Never rebuild into the live index — create a brand-new index (`customers_v2`), bulk-load the entire history into it via `BulkAllAsync`/`BulkAsync`, then atomically repoint the `customers` alias from the old index to the new one with a single `_es.Indices.UpdateAliasesAsync(...)` call. `GET /customer/{id}` always resolves through the alias, so the swap is instantaneous and clients never see a partial index. (3) This is safe specifically because of CQRS: the read model is a derived, idempotent (upsert-by-`Id`) projection of the write model in Postgres — the write side never touches Elasticsearch, so a from-scratch reindex risks nothing on the source of truth (`_db.Customers` is always there to replay from). Verify it: run the same load profile against the old per-document rebuild and the new bulk + side-index approach, and confirm query latency stays flat throughout the second run; confirm the alias flip is a single atomic call with no window where `customers` resolves to nothing; and diff the new index's document count against `await _db.Customers.CountAsync()` before flipping the alias."},"go":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nĐoạn rebuild cho sẵn chạy đúng nhưng ba chi phí cộng dồn khiến nó vừa chậm vừa không an toàn — cùng bản chất bug như bản Elasticsearch của bài toán này, chỉ khoác áo MongoDB. Thứ nhất, `wc.Journal = &journal` trên một write concern majority ép MongoDB fsync-acknowledge MỖI lần gọi `UpdateOne` trước khi trả về — tương đương trực tiếp với refresh flush theo-từng-document của search index: một cam kết bền vững vốn đắt, đáng ra chỉ nên trả giá một lần cho cả batch chứ không phải một lần cho mỗi dòng; với toàn bộ lịch sử đọc từ Postgres, đó là hàng nghìn lần fsync thừa. Thứ hai, mỗi dòng một lần `UpdateOne` lãng phí một round-trip mạng cho mỗi document thay vì đẩy nhiều document trong một lần gọi. Thứ ba, và nặng nhất: vòng lặp ghi vào đúng collection `col`/`liveCol` mà `GET /customer/{id}` đang đọc — mọi query đang chạy giờ phải cạnh tranh tài nguyên của collection đó với rebuild, và một query rơi đúng giữa lúc rebuild sẽ chỉ thấy phần đã được upsert tính đến thời điểm đó, tức một read model nửa-vời. Cách sửa phải chạm cả ba đòn bẩy. (1) Ngừng gọi `UpdateOne` cho từng dòng — gom các row Postgres thành từng batch (vài trăm dòng mỗi lần) và đẩy qua `col.BulkWrite(ctx, models, options.BulkWrite().SetOrdered(false))`, với `models` là một slice các `mongo.NewUpdateOneModel()`, và bỏ write concern majority+journaled theo-từng-op trong lúc load — write concern acknowledged mặc định của collection là đủ giữa chừng rebuild; chỉ trả giá cho một cam kết bền vững mạnh hơn đúng một lần, ở cuối, nếu thật sự cần. Chỉ riêng bước này đã loại gần hết chi phí fsync lẫn round-trip. (2) Không bao giờ rebuild thẳng vào collection đang live — dựng một collection phụ hoàn toàn mới (`customers_rebuild`), `BulkWrite` toàn bộ lịch sử vào đó, rồi atomically swap nó vào bằng một lệnh `db.RunCommand(ctx, bson.D{{Key: \"renameCollection\", Value: \"app.customers_rebuild\"}, {Key: \"to\", Value: \"app.customers\"}, {Key: \"dropTarget\", Value: true}})` duy nhất — atomic collection rename của MongoDB đóng đúng vai trò mà alias swap của Elasticsearch đóng. `GET /customer/{id}` không bao giờ thấy một collection nửa-vời vì nó không hề chạm vào collection phụ cho đến khi rename hoàn tất. (3) Điều này an toàn chính là nhờ CQRS: read model là một projection dẫn xuất, idempotent (upsert-theo-`id`) từ write model trên Postgres — phía write không bao giờ chạm vào MongoDB, nên rebuild lại từ đầu không rủi ro gì cho nguồn sự thật (`SELECT * FROM customers` luôn sẵn đó để replay lại). Xác minh: chạy cùng một load profile lên bản rebuild-từng-document cũ và cách `BulkWrite` + collection-phụ mới, xác nhận latency query giữ phẳng suốt lần chạy thứ hai; xác nhận việc rename là một thao tác atomic duy nhất, không có khoảng-hở nào mà `customers` bị thiếu hoặc rỗng; và so số lượng document của collection vừa rebuild với `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM customers` trước khi rename.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe given rebuild runs correctly but three costs compound to make it both slow and unsafe — the same nature of bug as the Elasticsearch version of this problem, just wearing MongoDB's clothes. First, `wc.Journal = &journal` on a majority write concern forces MongoDB to fsync-acknowledge every single `UpdateOne` call before it returns — the direct analog of a search index's per-document refresh flush: a genuinely expensive durability guarantee meant to be paid once per batch, not once per row; over the full history read from Postgres that's thousands of unnecessary fsyncs. Second, one `UpdateOne` per row wastes a network round-trip per document instead of pushing many documents in one call. Third, and worst: the loop writes into the SAME `col`/`liveCol` collection that `GET /customer/{id}` reads from — every in-flight query now competes with the rebuild for that collection's resources, and a query landing mid-rebuild sees whatever subset has been upserted so far, i.e. a half-populated read model. The fix touches all three levers. (1) Stop calling `UpdateOne` per row — collect the Postgres rows into batches (e.g. a few hundred at a time) and submit them through `col.BulkWrite(ctx, models, options.BulkWrite().SetOrdered(false))`, where `models` is a slice of `mongo.NewUpdateOneModel()`s, and drop the per-op journaled majority write concern during the load — the collection's default acknowledged write concern is enough mid-rebuild; only pay for a stronger durability guarantee once, at the very end, if truly needed. This alone removes almost all the fsync and round-trip overhead. (2) Never rebuild into the live collection — create a brand-new side collection (`customers_rebuild`), `BulkWrite` the entire history into it, then atomically swap it in with a single `db.RunCommand(ctx, bson.D{{Key: \"renameCollection\", Value: \"app.customers_rebuild\"}, {Key: \"to\", Value: \"app.customers\"}, {Key: \"dropTarget\", Value: true}})` — MongoDB's atomic collection rename plays exactly the role Elasticsearch's alias swap plays. `GET /customer/{id}` never sees a half-populated collection because it never touches the side collection until the rename completes. (3) This is safe specifically because of CQRS: the read model is a derived, idempotent (upsert-by-`id`) projection of the write model in Postgres — the write side never touches MongoDB, so a from-scratch rebuild risks nothing on the source of truth (`SELECT * FROM customers` is always there to replay from). Verify it: run the same load profile against the old per-document rebuild and the new `BulkWrite` + side-collection approach, and confirm query latency stays flat throughout the second run; confirm the rename is a single atomic operation with no window where `customers` is missing or empty; and diff the rebuilt collection's document count against `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM customers` before renaming."}}}$iv$::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (bank_id, bank_version, question_id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO interview_questions (bank_id, bank_version, question_id, sort_index, is_premium, family, tier, kind, tags, competency, leadership_tier, bodies, interviewer, grading)
VALUES ('1-system-design-mastery/3-communication-patterns', '231d8cc57+37d905d9a', '1-system-design-mastery/3-communication-patterns/13', 13, false, 'technical', 'middle', 'design-lite', ARRAY['gateway-aggregation', 'bff', 'partial-failure']::text[], NULL, NULL, $iv${"agnostic":{"vi":{"prompt":"Màn hình \"chi tiết đơn hàng\" trên mobile app cần cùng lúc ba mảnh dữ liệu: thông tin đơn hàng (order service), tên/giá sản phẩm (product service), và tên người mua (user service). Hiện tại app mobile tự gọi thẳng cả ba service này bằng ba request tuần tự qua mạng di động. Hãy phác thảo MỘT endpoint tổng hợp (aggregation) đặt ở gateway/BFF: client chỉ gọi một request duy nhất, endpoint đó fan-out tới cả ba service rồi gộp kết quả thành một payload đúng hình dạng màn hình mobile cần. Định nghĩa rõ input/output của endpoint, cách bạn gọi ba service bên trong, xử lý khi MỘT nhánh (ví dụ product service) timeout hoặc lỗi, cách chặn latency không bị kéo dài bởi nhánh chậm nhất, và bảo vệ vì sao phần tổng hợp này nên nằm ở một BFF dành riêng cho mobile thay vì nhét thẳng vào API gateway dùng chung cho mọi loại client.","givenCode":null,"diagram":"```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    Mobile[\"Mobile client\"]\n    Mobile -.->|\"GET order-detail?orderId=42 — ?\"| Entry[\"? một endpoint tổng hợp ?\"]\n    Entry -.-> OrderSvc[\"Order Service\"]\n    Entry -.-> ProductSvc[\"Product Service\"]\n    Entry -.-> UserSvc[\"User Service\"]\n```"},"en":{"prompt":"The \"order detail\" screen in a mobile app needs three pieces of data at once: order info (order service), the product's name/price (product service), and the buyer's name (user service). Today the mobile app calls all three services directly with three sequential requests. Sketch ONE aggregation endpoint sitting at the gateway/BFF: the client makes a single call, and that endpoint fans out to all three services and merges the results into one payload shaped for the mobile screen. Define the endpoint's input/output, how you call the three services internally, how you handle it when ONE branch times out or errors, how you bound overall latency so it isn't dragged out by the slowest branch, and defend why this aggregation should live in a dedicated mobile BFF rather than being stuffed into the shared API gateway that serves every client type.","givenCode":null,"diagram":"```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    Mobile[\"Mobile client\"]\n    Mobile -.->|\"GET order-detail?orderId=42 — ?\"| Entry[\"? one aggregation endpoint ?\"]\n    Entry -.-> OrderSvc[\"Order Service\"]\n    Entry -.-> ProductSvc[\"Product Service\"]\n    Entry -.-> UserSvc[\"User Service\"]\n```"}}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"vi":{"followUps":["Nếu nhánh product-service timeout còn order + user thành công, bạn trả về đúng HTTP status và hình dạng payload nào, và mobile client dựa vào đâu để biết chính xác phần nào đang thiếu?","Sau này thêm một mảnh dữ liệu \"trạng thái vận chuyển\" (shipping service) vào đúng màn hình chi tiết đơn hàng này — lời gọi fan-out mới đó nên vào thẳng BFF hiện tại hay đặt ở đâu khác, và vì sao?"],"hints":["Nghĩ về mạng di động yếu: gọi ba request tuần tự tốn bao nhiêu round-trip so với một request duy nhất trả về đúng mọi thứ màn hình cần?","Nếu một trong ba service sập, endpoint tổng hợp có nên trả lỗi 500 cho toàn bộ không, hay trả những gì gọi được và đánh dấu rõ phần thiếu?"]},"en":{"followUps":["If the product-service branch times out while order and user both succeed, exactly what HTTP status and payload shape do you return, and how does the mobile client know precisely which section is missing?","Later you add a \"shipping status\" piece of data to this same order-detail screen — does that new fan-out call go straight into the existing BFF, or somewhere else, and why?"],"hints":["Think about a weak mobile connection: how many round-trips does calling three services sequentially cost versus one single call that returns exactly what the screen needs?","If one of the three services goes down, should the aggregation endpoint return a flat 500 for the whole thing, or return what it could and clearly flag what's missing?"]}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"locales":{"vi":{"rubric":["Định nghĩa đúng hợp đồng aggregation: một lời gọi client (`GET order-detail?orderId=`) fan-out tới ba service rồi gộp kết quả thành một payload đúng hình dạng màn hình mobile cần — thay ba round-trip bằng một, quan trọng nhất trên mạng di động yếu.","Xử lý rõ partial failure: một nhánh lỗi (ví dụ product service sập) KHÔNG được làm hỏng toàn bộ response — thiết kế payload dạng partial (trả về phần gọi thành công, đánh dấu phần thiếu/lỗi) thay vì trả thẳng 500 kiểu tất-cả-hoặc-không-gì.","Chặn latency và bảo vệ gateway: tổng latency bị quyết định bởi nhánh CHẬM NHẤT, nên gọi ba service SONG SONG kèm timeout riêng cho mỗi nhánh, và cân nhắc circuit breaker để một service chết không treo cả request tổng hợp.","Giữ mỏng và bảo vệ ranh giới BFF: gộp dữ liệu buộc gateway phải biết hình dạng của nhiều service (tăng coupling) — nên giữ logic gộp mỏng và đẩy phần tổng hợp riêng-theo-client này vào một BFF dành riêng cho mobile, thay vì nhồi vào một gateway dùng chung phải chiều mọi loại client.","Tôn trọng ranh giới trách nhiệm: BFF chỉ gộp/cắt gọt dữ liệu ở tầng transport, KHÔNG ôm business logic (rule đó vẫn thuộc về service sở hữu nó, ví dụ rule đơn hàng luôn khởi tạo `PENDING` vẫn nằm ở order service) — nếu không nó sẽ biến thành một monolith trá hình."],"checklist":[{"text":"Build ONE aggregation endpoint (e.g. GET /order-detail?orderId=) that the mobile client calls once, instead of three sequential round-trips to order/product/user services over a weak connection.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Internally the endpoint fans out to the three services in PARALLEL, each branch carrying its own timeout, so overall latency is bounded by the slowest branch rather than the sum of all three.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Add a circuit breaker per branch so one dead/slow service fails fast instead of hanging the whole endpoint.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"On partial failure (e.g. product service times out), don't return a 500 for the whole response — return what succeeded (order + user) and flag the failed section so the client can show a placeholder/skeleton for just that part.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Merge the results into one payload shaped specifically for the order-detail screen.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Put this aggregation in a dedicated mobile BFF rather than the shared API gateway: merging forces knowledge of three services' shapes; stuffing that into the shared gateway would force it to know every client's shape and keep growing, whereas a per-client BFF keeps the merge thin at the cost of one more artifact — an acceptable trade.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"The BFF must never absorb business logic — it only composes/trims data at the transport layer; a rule like \"order always starts PENDING\" belongs to the order service, not the BFF, or the BFF becomes a disguised monolith hard to test/scale.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16}],"keywords":["gateway aggregation","backend for frontend (BFF)","partial-failure response","circuit breaker","latency bound by slowest branch"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"I'd put a single aggregation endpoint, something like GET /order-detail?orderId=, inside a dedicated mobile BFF rather than the shared gateway. Internally it fans out to order, product, and user services in parallel with its own timeout per branch, plus a circuit breaker per branch so one dead service doesn't hang the whole request — since the overall latency is bound by whichever branch is slowest, parallel with timeouts is basically mandatory. If, say, product times out, I wouldn't fail the whole response with a 500 — I'd return what succeeded, order and user, and just flag the product section as missing so the client can render a placeholder for that one piece. And I'd keep this merge logic out of the shared gateway specifically because merging forces that layer to know every client's shape — mobile, web, partner — so a per-client BFF keeps it thin, as long as the BFF itself never absorbs business logic, that stays in the order service.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"I'd expose one endpoint on a mobile BFF that takes an orderId and internally calls order, product, and user services in parallel, each with a timeout, instead of the client making three sequential calls. If one branch like product fails, I'd still return the parts that succeeded and mark that section as failed rather than erroring the whole thing. I'd put this in a dedicated BFF instead of the shared gateway because the gateway shouldn't have to know the shape every different client needs.","sonnet":71,"haiku":71},{"level":3,"answer":"So I'd add one endpoint that the mobile app calls, and behind it we call the three services and combine the results before sending back. I think we should call them in parallel so it's faster, and probably need some kind of timeout so it doesn't wait forever on one of them. This should go in the BFF layer I guess, though I'm less sure why it can't just live in the gateway.","sonnet":29,"haiku":43},{"level":4,"answer":"We could add an endpoint that just calls all three services and puts the data together for the mobile app. If one fails we'd probably just show an error or retry it. I'm not too sure why it matters where exactly this lives, gateway or BFF, they seem similar to me.","sonnet":29,"haiku":29},{"level":5,"answer":"Honestly the simplest fix might be to just have the mobile app cache the data locally so it doesn't need three calls every time. Or maybe reduce the number of round trips by making the mobile app smarter about when it re-fetches. I don't think we need a new endpoint for this.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]},"en":{"rubric":["Defines the aggregation contract correctly: one client call (`GET order-detail?orderId=`) fans out to three services and merges the results into a single payload shaped for the mobile screen — trading three round-trips for one, which matters most on a weak mobile network.","Handles partial failure explicitly: one failing branch (say the product service goes down) must NOT break the whole response — design a partial-response payload (return what succeeded, flag the missing/failed section) instead of a flat all-or-nothing 500.","Bounds latency and protects the gateway: overall latency is dictated by the SLOWEST branch, so call the three services in PARALLEL with a per-branch timeout, and consider a circuit breaker so one dead service doesn't hang the whole aggregate request.","Keeps it thin and defends the BFF boundary: merging data forces the gateway to know the shape of several services (more coupling) — keep the merge logic thin and push this per-client aggregation into a dedicated mobile BFF instead of stuffing it into one shared gateway that must please every client type.","Respects the responsibility boundary: the BFF only composes/trims data at the transport layer, it does NOT absorb business logic (that rule still belongs to the owning service — e.g. an order always starting `PENDING` still lives in the order service) — otherwise it turns into a disguised monolith."],"checklist":[{"text":"Build ONE aggregation endpoint (e.g. GET /order-detail?orderId=) that the mobile client calls once, instead of three sequential round-trips to order/product/user services over a weak connection.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Internally the endpoint fans out to the three services in PARALLEL, each branch carrying its own timeout, so overall latency is bounded by the slowest branch rather than the sum of all three.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Add a circuit breaker per branch so one dead/slow service fails fast instead of hanging the whole endpoint.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"On partial failure (e.g. product service times out), don't return a 500 for the whole response — return what succeeded (order + user) and flag the failed section so the client can show a placeholder/skeleton for just that part.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Merge the results into one payload shaped specifically for the order-detail screen.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Put this aggregation in a dedicated mobile BFF rather than the shared API gateway: merging forces knowledge of three services' shapes; stuffing that into the shared gateway would force it to know every client's shape and keep growing, whereas a per-client BFF keeps the merge thin at the cost of one more artifact — an acceptable trade.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"The BFF must never absorb business logic — it only composes/trims data at the transport layer; a rule like \"order always starts PENDING\" belongs to the order service, not the BFF, or the BFF becomes a disguised monolith hard to test/scale.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16}],"keywords":["gateway aggregation","backend for frontend (BFF)","partial-failure response","circuit breaker","latency bound by slowest branch"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"I'd put a single aggregation endpoint, something like GET /order-detail?orderId=, inside a dedicated mobile BFF rather than the shared gateway. Internally it fans out to order, product, and user services in parallel with its own timeout per branch, plus a circuit breaker per branch so one dead service doesn't hang the whole request — since the overall latency is bound by whichever branch is slowest, parallel with timeouts is basically mandatory. If, say, product times out, I wouldn't fail the whole response with a 500 — I'd return what succeeded, order and user, and just flag the product section as missing so the client can render a placeholder for that one piece. And I'd keep this merge logic out of the shared gateway specifically because merging forces that layer to know every client's shape — mobile, web, partner — so a per-client BFF keeps it thin, as long as the BFF itself never absorbs business logic, that stays in the order service.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"I'd expose one endpoint on a mobile BFF that takes an orderId and internally calls order, product, and user services in parallel, each with a timeout, instead of the client making three sequential calls. If one branch like product fails, I'd still return the parts that succeeded and mark that section as failed rather than erroring the whole thing. I'd put this in a dedicated BFF instead of the shared gateway because the gateway shouldn't have to know the shape every different client needs.","sonnet":71,"haiku":71},{"level":3,"answer":"So I'd add one endpoint that the mobile app calls, and behind it we call the three services and combine the results before sending back. I think we should call them in parallel so it's faster, and probably need some kind of timeout so it doesn't wait forever on one of them. This should go in the BFF layer I guess, though I'm less sure why it can't just live in the gateway.","sonnet":29,"haiku":43},{"level":4,"answer":"We could add an endpoint that just calls all three services and puts the data together for the mobile app. If one fails we'd probably just show an error or retry it. I'm not too sure why it matters where exactly this lives, gateway or BFF, they seem similar to me.","sonnet":29,"haiku":29},{"level":5,"answer":"Honestly the simplest fix might be to just have the mobile app cache the data locally so it doesn't need three calls every time. Or maybe reduce the number of round trips by making the mobile app smarter about when it re-fetches. I don't think we need a new endpoint for this.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]}},"ideal":{"agnostic":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nTôi sẽ dựng một endpoint tổng hợp duy nhất, ví dụ `GET /order-detail?orderId=`, đặt ở một BFF riêng cho mobile chứ không nhét vào API gateway dùng chung. Bên trong, nó fan-out SONG SONG tới ba service — order, product, user — mỗi nhánh mang timeout riêng, rồi gộp kết quả thành một payload đúng hình dạng màn hình chi tiết đơn hàng cần (trạng thái đơn, tên/giá sản phẩm, tên người mua) trong một response, thay vì bắt mobile tự gọi ba request tuần tự và chịu ba round-trip trên mạng yếu. Vì latency của cả request tổng hợp bị quyết định bởi nhánh chậm nhất, gọi song song kèm timeout là bắt buộc, và tôi thêm circuit breaker cho từng nhánh để một service chết không treo cả endpoint — nó fail fast thay vì kéo dài chờ đợi. Khi một nhánh lỗi, ví dụ product service timeout, tôi không trả 500 cho toàn bộ response; tôi trả về những gì gọi thành công (order + user) và đánh dấu rõ phần product là thiếu/lỗi để mobile tự quyết định hiển thị skeleton hay placeholder cho đúng phần đó — một partial response, không phải tất-cả-hoặc-không-gì. Tôi cố tình đặt việc gộp này ở một BFF riêng cho mobile chứ không phải API gateway dùng chung: gộp dữ liệu buộc lớp đó phải biết hình dạng của ba service khác nhau, tức thêm coupling; nếu nhồi hết vào gateway dùng chung, gateway sẽ phải biết hình dạng của MỌI client (web, mobile, đối tác) và phình to dần — trong khi một BFF theo từng loại client giữ phần gộp mỏng và đúng ngay tại nơi cần nó, đổi lại là thêm một artifact phải bảo trì, một sự đánh đổi chấp nhận được. Và điều BFF này tuyệt đối không được làm là ôm business logic — nó chỉ gộp/cắt gọt dữ liệu ở tầng transport; rule đơn hàng luôn khởi tạo `PENDING` vẫn thuộc về order service, không phải BFF, nếu không BFF sẽ biến thành một monolith trá hình khó test và khó scale.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nI'd build a single aggregation endpoint, e.g. `GET /order-detail?orderId=`, sitting in a dedicated mobile BFF rather than the shared API gateway. Internally it fans out in PARALLEL to three services — order, product, user — each branch carrying its own timeout, then merges the results into one payload shaped exactly for the order-detail screen (order status, product name/price, buyer name) in a single response, instead of forcing the mobile app to make three sequential calls and eat three round-trips over a weak connection. Because the aggregate request's latency is dictated by the slowest branch, parallel calls with per-branch timeouts are mandatory, and I'd add a circuit breaker per branch so one dead service doesn't hang the whole endpoint — it fails fast instead of piling up waiting. When one branch fails, say the product service times out, I don't return a 500 for the whole response; I return what succeeded (order + user) and clearly flag the product section as missing/failed, so the mobile client can decide whether to show a skeleton or a placeholder for just that section — a partial response, not all-or-nothing. I deliberately put this merge in a dedicated mobile BFF rather than the shared API gateway: merging data forces that layer to know the shape of three different services, which is extra coupling; if you stuff it into the shared gateway, the gateway ends up needing to know the shape of EVERY client (web, mobile, partner) and keeps growing, whereas a per-client-type BFF keeps the merge thin and located exactly where it's needed, at the cost of one more artifact to maintain — an acceptable trade. And the one thing this BFF must never do is absorb business logic — it only composes/trims data at the transport layer; the rule that an order always starts `PENDING` still belongs to the order service, not the BFF, or the BFF turns into a disguised monolith that's hard to test and scale."}}}$iv$::jsonb)
