INSERT INTO interview_questions (bank_id, bank_version, question_id, sort_index, is_premium, family, tier, kind, tags, competency, leadership_tier, bodies, interviewer, grading)
VALUES ('1-system-design-mastery/5-rabbitmq-and-job-queues', '231d8cc57+37d905d9a', '1-system-design-mastery/5-rabbitmq-and-job-queues/4', 4, false, 'technical', 'middle', 'reasoning', ARRAY['exponential-backoff', 'retry-storm', 'jitter']::text[], NULL, NULL, $iv${"agnostic":{"vi":{"prompt":"Worker của một job queue xử lý job thất bại — downstream (vd SMTP gửi email) đang quá tải tạm thời. Bạn hãy phân tích ra tiếng: vì sao queue nên retry job đó sau một khoảng chờ tăng dần (exponential) thay vì retry lại ngay lập tức, và thứ DUY NHẤT bạn còn cần thêm bên trên exponential backoff để sống sót qua một outage thật là gì? Đồng thời giải thích cụ thể: ai là người thực sự lên lịch cho lần retry đó?","givenCode":null,"diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"A job queue's worker fails to process a job — the downstream (e.g. an SMTP service) is temporarily overloaded. Reason out loud: why should the queue retry that job after a growing (exponential) delay instead of retrying it again immediately, and what is the ONE thing you still need on top of exponential backoff to survive a real outage where many jobs failed together at once? Also explain concretely who actually schedules that retry?","givenCode":null,"diagram":null}}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"vi":{"followUps":["Bạn chọn số lần thử tối đa và mức trần cho backoff (cap) như thế nào để retry không kéo dài vô tận trong một outage kéo dài hàng giờ?","Nếu job đó không idempotent (vd trừ tiền hai lần nếu chạy lại), retry với backoff+jitter còn an toàn không — bạn cần thêm gì?"],"hints":["Vẽ timeline của 100 job cùng fail tại t=0. Nếu backoff không có jitter, chúng retry ở đúng những mốc giây nào — và điều gì xảy ra tại chính những mốc đó?","Nghĩ về việc ai đang giữ biến đếm attemptsMade và ai đang gọi setTimeout/schedule cho lần thử kế tiếp — có phải là dòng code bạn viết trong handler không?"]},"en":{"followUps":["How would you pick the max number of attempts and a cap on the backoff delay so retries don't keep stretching out for hours during a long outage?","If the job isn't idempotent (e.g. it would double-charge a customer on a re-run), is retrying with backoff+jitter still safe — what else do you need?"],"hints":["Sketch a timeline of 100 jobs all failing at t=0. Without jitter, at exactly which second-marks do they all retry — and what happens to the downstream at those exact moments?","Think about who is holding the attemptsMade counter and who is calling setTimeout/scheduling the next attempt — is that a line of code you wrote inside the handler?"]}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"locales":{"vi":{"rubric":["Retry ngay lập tức, nhân với nhiều worker cùng lúc, sẽ đổ thêm tải lên một downstream vốn đã đang chật vật — kéo nó lún sâu hơn, tạo thành death spiral / retry storm.","Exponential backoff (vd 1s → 2s → 4s) giãn các lần retry ra theo thời gian, cho downstream khoảng trống để hồi phục thay vì bị dội lại ngay tức khắc.","Chỉ riêng backoff vẫn chưa đủ: mọi job cùng fail trong một outage sẽ retry đồng pha với nhau (cùng ở mốc 1s, cùng ở mốc 2s, ...) — một thundering herd; jitter (ngẫu nhiên hoá khoảng chờ, vd ±20%) làm chúng lệch pha nhau để không đâm sầm vào downstream cùng lúc lần nữa.","Chính queue tự lên lịch retry, không phải worker: handler của worker chỉ throw khi fail; cơ chế của queue tăng biến đếm số lần thử (attemptsMade) và re-enqueue job sau khoảng backoff đó — không có vòng lặp retry tự viết tay bên trong handler.","Nối lại với DLQ: backoff + jitter chỉ mua thêm thời gian hồi phục, nhưng số lần thử vẫn phải có giới hạn — khi attemptsMade chạm mức tối đa, job chuyển sang Dead Letter Queue thay vì retry vô hạn."],"checklist":[{"text":"Retrying immediately, multiplied across N workers, piles more load onto an already-overloaded/down downstream, driving a death spiral.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":19},{"text":"Exponential backoff (1s, 2s, 4s) spreads the retries out over time so the downstream gets room to recover.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":19},{"text":"After a real outage, every job that failed together will retry back in phase at exactly the same delays, producing a new thundering herd that is merely delayed.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":19},{"text":"Jitter (e.g. randomizing the wait by roughly ±20%) de-synchronizes the jobs so they don't slam the downstream again all at once.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":19},{"text":"The worker never writes its own retry loop — it just throws on failure; the queue mechanism itself increments attemptsMade, waits the backoff delay with jitter, and re-enqueues the job, since the retry policy belongs to the queue, not the handler.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":12},{"text":"Once attemptsMade hits the configured max, the job is pushed to a DLQ instead of retrying indefinitely.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":12}],"keywords":["exponential backoff","jitter","retry storm","thundering herd","attemptsMade"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"Retrying immediately across N workers just piles more load onto a downstream that's already struggling, so it spirals further down. Exponential backoff — 1s, 2s, 4s — spaces retries out so the downstream gets room to recover, but everything that failed together during the outage will now retry back in phase at those same delays, which is just a delayed thundering herd. So on top of backoff you need jitter — randomizing the wait, say plus or minus twenty percent — so the jobs desynchronize instead of hitting the downstream again all at once. Mechanically, the worker doesn't write any retry loop itself — the handler just throws on failure, and the queue mechanism increments attemptsMade, waits the backoff-plus-jitter delay, and re-enqueues the job on its own; once attemptsMade hits the configured max, it goes to a DLQ instead of retrying forever.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"If everything retries immediately, you're just hammering a downstream that's already overloaded, so exponential backoff spaces those retries out over time. The catch is that all the jobs that failed together will still be in sync at each backoff step, so you'd want jitter too — some randomness in the delay — so they spread out instead of retrying in a wave. I believe the queue itself handles the retry scheduling rather than the worker's own code, incrementing some attempt counter and waiting before trying again. After enough failed attempts I'd expect it to go to a dead letter queue instead of retrying indefinitely.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":3,"answer":"Retrying right away would probably make things worse since the downstream is already overloaded, so backing off with increasing delays makes sense. I think you'd also want some randomness added so jobs don't all retry at the exact same time, though I'm not fully sure why that matters beyond 'avoiding collisions'. As for who schedules it, I'd guess the queue system handles that automatically rather than the worker writing its own timer, but I'm not certain.","sonnet":50,"haiku":50},{"level":4,"answer":"I think waiting longer between retries is better than retrying right away, but I'm not sure what else you'd need on top of that. I'd guess the worker code handles when to retry, maybe with a simple delay.","sonnet":0,"haiku":17},{"level":5,"answer":"I'd just have the worker retry immediately in a loop until it succeeds, since eventually the downstream should come back up and it doesn't need anything fancier than that.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]},"en":{"rubric":["Retrying immediately, multiplied across many workers, piles more load onto a downstream that is already struggling — pushing it further down, a death spiral / retry storm.","Exponential backoff (e.g. 1s to 2s to 4s) spreads the retries out over time, giving the downstream room to recover instead of being hammered again right away.","Backoff alone is not enough: every job that failed together during one outage retries in phase (all at the 1s mark, all at the 2s mark, ...) — a thundering herd; jitter (randomizing the delay, e.g. plus/minus ~20%) de-synchronizes them so they don't slam the downstream again at the same instant.","The queue schedules the retry itself, not the worker: the worker's handler only throws on failure; the queue mechanism increments the attempt count (attemptsMade) and re-enqueues the job after the backoff delay — there is no hand-rolled retry loop inside the handler.","Ties back to the DLQ: backoff plus jitter only buys recovery time — the number of attempts still needs a ceiling, so once attemptsMade hits the max the job moves to a Dead Letter Queue instead of retrying forever."],"checklist":[{"text":"Retrying immediately, multiplied across N workers, piles more load onto an already-overloaded/down downstream, driving a death spiral.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":19},{"text":"Exponential backoff (1s, 2s, 4s) spreads the retries out over time so the downstream gets room to recover.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":19},{"text":"After a real outage, every job that failed together will retry back in phase at exactly the same delays, producing a new thundering herd that is merely delayed.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":19},{"text":"Jitter (e.g. randomizing the wait by roughly ±20%) de-synchronizes the jobs so they don't slam the downstream again all at once.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":19},{"text":"The worker never writes its own retry loop — it just throws on failure; the queue mechanism itself increments attemptsMade, waits the backoff delay with jitter, and re-enqueues the job, since the retry policy belongs to the queue, not the handler.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":12},{"text":"Once attemptsMade hits the configured max, the job is pushed to a DLQ instead of retrying indefinitely.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":12}],"keywords":["exponential backoff","jitter","retry storm","thundering herd","attemptsMade"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"Retrying immediately across N workers just piles more load onto a downstream that's already struggling, so it spirals further down. Exponential backoff — 1s, 2s, 4s — spaces retries out so the downstream gets room to recover, but everything that failed together during the outage will now retry back in phase at those same delays, which is just a delayed thundering herd. So on top of backoff you need jitter — randomizing the wait, say plus or minus twenty percent — so the jobs desynchronize instead of hitting the downstream again all at once. Mechanically, the worker doesn't write any retry loop itself — the handler just throws on failure, and the queue mechanism increments attemptsMade, waits the backoff-plus-jitter delay, and re-enqueues the job on its own; once attemptsMade hits the configured max, it goes to a DLQ instead of retrying forever.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"If everything retries immediately, you're just hammering a downstream that's already overloaded, so exponential backoff spaces those retries out over time. The catch is that all the jobs that failed together will still be in sync at each backoff step, so you'd want jitter too — some randomness in the delay — so they spread out instead of retrying in a wave. I believe the queue itself handles the retry scheduling rather than the worker's own code, incrementing some attempt counter and waiting before trying again. After enough failed attempts I'd expect it to go to a dead letter queue instead of retrying indefinitely.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":3,"answer":"Retrying right away would probably make things worse since the downstream is already overloaded, so backing off with increasing delays makes sense. I think you'd also want some randomness added so jobs don't all retry at the exact same time, though I'm not fully sure why that matters beyond 'avoiding collisions'. As for who schedules it, I'd guess the queue system handles that automatically rather than the worker writing its own timer, but I'm not certain.","sonnet":50,"haiku":50},{"level":4,"answer":"I think waiting longer between retries is better than retrying right away, but I'm not sure what else you'd need on top of that. I'd guess the worker code handles when to retry, maybe with a simple delay.","sonnet":0,"haiku":17},{"level":5,"answer":"I'd just have the worker retry immediately in a loop until it succeeds, since eventually the downstream should come back up and it doesn't need anything fancier than that.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]}},"ideal":{"agnostic":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nRetry ngay lập tức, nhân với N worker cùng lúc, chỉ đổ thêm tải lên một downstream vốn đã đang sập — đẩy nó lún sâu hơn, tạo death spiral. Exponential backoff (1s, 2s, 4s) giãn các lần retry ra theo thời gian để downstream có khoảng trống hồi phục. Nhưng sau một outage, tất cả job từng fail cùng lúc sẽ lại retry đồng pha ở đúng những mốc thời gian đó — một thundering herd mới, chỉ là bị trì hoãn. Vì vậy cần thêm jitter: ngẫu nhiên hoá khoảng chờ (vd ±20%) để các job lệch pha nhau, không đồng loạt dội vào downstream một lần nữa. Về cơ chế: worker không tự viết retry loop — handler chỉ throw khi fail; chính queue tăng attemptsMade, chờ đúng khoảng backoff (có jitter), rồi tự re-enqueue job. Chính sách retry là việc của queue, không phải của handler. Và vì attempts không thể tăng mãi, khi attemptsMade chạm ngưỡng tối đa, job được đẩy sang DLQ thay vì retry vô hạn.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nRetrying immediately, multiplied across N workers, only piles more load onto a downstream that is already down — driving a death spiral. Exponential backoff (1s, 2s, 4s) spreads the retries out over time so the downstream gets room to recover. But after an outage, every job that failed together will retry back in phase at exactly those same delays — a new thundering herd, just delayed. So you also need jitter: randomize the wait (e.g. plus/minus ~20%) so the jobs de-synchronize instead of slamming the downstream again all at once. Mechanically: the worker never writes its own retry loop — the handler just throws on failure; the queue itself increments attemptsMade, waits the backoff delay (with jitter), and re-enqueues the job. The retry policy belongs to the queue, not to the handler. And since attempts can't grow forever, once attemptsMade hits the configured max, the job is pushed to a DLQ instead of retrying indefinitely."}}}$iv$::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (bank_id, bank_version, question_id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO interview_questions (bank_id, bank_version, question_id, sort_index, is_premium, family, tier, kind, tags, competency, leadership_tier, bodies, interviewer, grading)
VALUES ('1-system-design-mastery/5-rabbitmq-and-job-queues', '231d8cc57+37d905d9a', '1-system-design-mastery/5-rabbitmq-and-job-queues/5', 5, false, 'technical', 'senior', 'reasoning', ARRAY['publisher-confirms', 'amqp-transactions', 'delivery-guarantee']::text[], NULL, NULL, $iv${"agnostic":{"vi":{"prompt":"Một đồng nghiệp junior viết code publish message vào exchange bằng basic.publish thông thường rồi coi như xong — \"gửi rồi thì chắc chắn tới nơi\". Bạn hãy phản biện: kiểu publish \"bắn rồi quên\" (fire-and-forget) này thiếu bảo đảm gì, khoảng hở đó thực sự gây hại khi nào; rồi so sánh hai cách lấy lại bảo đảm durable-write.","givenCode":null,"diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"A junior teammate publishes into an exchange with a plain basic.publish and calls it done.","givenCode":null,"diagram":null}}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"vi":{"followUps":["Nếu bạn bật publisher confirms nhưng queue đích lại là classic queue không durable, khi broker restart chuyện gì xảy ra — vì sao confirm không cứu được bạn ở đây?","Với quorum queue (Raft, xác nhận khi đa số 2/3 bản sao ghi xong), ack của publisher confirms trả về ngay sau khi leader ghi cục bộ, hay phải đợi tới khi đạt majority — điều đó thay đổi gì về latency so với một queue durable đơn giản?"],"hints":["Tự hỏi: sau một lời gọi publish trần, producer thực sự nhận được tín hiệu gì từ broker — có hay không?","So sánh chi phí round-trip của `tx.commit` (đồng bộ, từng cái một) với dòng ack/nack bất đồng bộ của publisher confirms — cách nào cho phép producer tiếp tục publish trong lúc chờ xác nhận?"]},"en":{"followUps":["If you enable publisher confirms but the target is a non-durable classic queue, what happens on a broker restart — why doesn't the confirm save you here?","With a quorum queue (Raft, acknowledged once 2 of 3 replicas have written), does the publisher-confirm ack come back right after the leader writes locally, or only once a majority is reached — how does that change latency versus a plain durable queue?"],"hints":["Ask yourself: after a bare publish call, what signal does the producer actually get back from the broker — any at all?","Compare the round-trip cost of `tx.commit` (synchronous, one at a time) against the async stream of ack/nack under publisher confirms — which one lets the producer keep publishing while waiting for confirmation?"]}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"locales":{"vi":{"rubric":["Chỉ ra `basic.publish` thông thường là fire-and-forget: producer không nhận được tín hiệu nào báo broker đã lưu message; nếu broker crash đúng lúc giữa publish và ghi persist xong, message mất trong im lặng mà producer không hề biết.","Nêu được transaction (`tx.select`/`tx.commit`) cho bảo đảm tất-cả-hoặc-không-gì nhưng đồng bộ — mỗi lần commit là một round-trip chờ broker xác nhận xong mới publish tiếp, nên throughput giảm mạnh khi cần publish nhiều.","Publisher confirms cho đúng tín hiệu durable-write đó nhưng bất đồng bộ: mỗi message nhận một ack/nack riêng gửi về sau, producer không phải đứng chờ mà tiếp tục publish message kế tiếp — nhanh hơn hẳn transaction, nên gần như luôn được chọn trong thực tế.","Chốt được điều kiện để confirm có ý nghĩa: exchange/queue phải durable và message phải publish với cờ persistent (muốn chịu được mất cả một node thì queue còn phải là quorum queue); confirm vào một queue transient thì bảo đảm đó rỗng tuếch."],"checklist":[{"text":"A bare basic.publish is fire-and-forget — the producer publishes and moves on with no signal that the broker actually wrote the message to disk.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"If the broker crashes between the publish and finishing the persist, the message vanishes silently and the producer has no way to know.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"AMQP transactions (tx.select/tx.commit) restore the guarantee: tx.select opens a transaction and tx.commit confirms the whole batch of publishes all-or-nothing.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"tx.commit is synchronous — each commit must round-trip and wait for the broker's confirmation before publishing again, so throughput collapses at scale.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Publisher confirms solve this: enabling confirm.select gives every published message its own later ack/nack asynchronously, without blocking the producer.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Confirms provide the same durable-write guarantee as transactions but much faster, which is why confirms are almost always preferred in practice over transactions.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"A confirm only means something if the exchange and queue are declared durable and the message is published with the persistent flag — otherwise it just confirms storage into something that disappears on a broker restart.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"To survive losing an entire node (not just a restart), the queue must also be a quorum queue, replicated via Raft and acknowledged once a majority of replicas have written.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10}],"keywords":["publisher confirms","AMQP transactions","fire-and-forget","durable queue","persistent message"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"A bare basic.publish is fire-and-forget — the producer sends it and moves on with no signal the broker actually wrote it to disk, so if the broker crashes right between the publish and the persist, the message just vanishes silently. AMQP transactions are the first fix — tx.select opens one, tx.commit confirms the batch — but commit is synchronous, so every commit has to round-trip before you can publish again, which collapses throughput once volume goes up. Publisher confirms solve that: enable confirm.select and every message gets its own ack or nack later, asynchronously, so the producer keeps firing the next message without blocking — same durability guarantee, much faster, which is why confirms are almost always preferred in practice. But that confirm only means something if the exchange and queue are declared durable and the message is published with the persistent flag, otherwise it's an empty guarantee that disappears on a broker restart; and to survive losing an entire node rather than just a restart, the queue needs to be a quorum queue so the ack only comes back once a majority of replicas have actually written it.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"A plain basic.publish doesn't guarantee the broker actually stored the message — if it crashes right after, that message is just gone and the producer never finds out. Transactions with tx.select and tx.commit fix that but they're synchronous, so you have to wait for each commit before sending the next message, which really hurts throughput. Publisher confirms give you the same durability guarantee but asynchronously — you keep publishing and handle the acks/nacks as they come back — so that's usually the better choice. I'd also want to make sure the queue and exchange are actually durable and the message is marked persistent, otherwise the confirm doesn't really mean much if the broker restarts.","sonnet":75,"haiku":88},{"level":3,"answer":"I think basic.publish alone doesn't confirm the message was actually saved, so if something goes wrong right after publishing you could lose it without knowing. Transactions can give you a durability guarantee but I believe they're slower since you wait for each one to commit. Publisher confirms are the alternative and I think they're faster because they're not blocking, so that's probably the preferred approach. I'm not fully sure what else needs to be true underneath for a confirm to actually mean the message is safe though — maybe something about the queue being persistent.","sonnet":25,"haiku":50},{"level":4,"answer":"I think publish alone might not be fully reliable but I'm not sure exactly what's missing. I've heard of publisher confirms being a thing but I don't know how they compare to transactions or which one's better.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0},{"level":5,"answer":"I'd say if basic.publish returns without an error then it worked and the message got there — that's usually enough for most use cases without adding transactions or confirms.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]},"en":{"rubric":["Points out plain `basic.publish` is fire-and-forget: the producer gets no signal that the broker actually stored the message; if the broker crashes exactly between the publish and finishing the persist, the message is lost silently and the producer never knows.","Names transactions (`tx.select`/`tx.commit`) as giving an all-or-nothing guarantee but synchronous — each commit is a round-trip waiting on the broker's confirmation before you can publish again, so throughput collapses once you need to publish a lot.","Publisher confirms give the same durable-write signal but asynchronously: each message gets its own later ack/nack, so the producer keeps publishing the next message while confirms stream back — much faster than transactions, which is why confirms are almost always the practical choice.","Nails the condition for the confirm to mean anything: the exchange/queue must be durable and the message published with the persistent flag (and, to survive losing a whole node, the queue must be a quorum queue); confirming into a transient queue makes the guarantee worthless."],"checklist":[{"text":"A bare basic.publish is fire-and-forget — the producer publishes and moves on with no signal that the broker actually wrote the message to disk.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"If the broker crashes between the publish and finishing the persist, the message vanishes silently and the producer has no way to know.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"AMQP transactions (tx.select/tx.commit) restore the guarantee: tx.select opens a transaction and tx.commit confirms the whole batch of publishes all-or-nothing.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"tx.commit is synchronous — each commit must round-trip and wait for the broker's confirmation before publishing again, so throughput collapses at scale.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Publisher confirms solve this: enabling confirm.select gives every published message its own later ack/nack asynchronously, without blocking the producer.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"Confirms provide the same durable-write guarantee as transactions but much faster, which is why confirms are almost always preferred in practice over transactions.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"A confirm only means something if the exchange and queue are declared durable and the message is published with the persistent flag — otherwise it just confirms storage into something that disappears on a broker restart.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":16},{"text":"To survive losing an entire node (not just a restart), the queue must also be a quorum queue, replicated via Raft and acknowledged once a majority of replicas have written.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10}],"keywords":["publisher confirms","AMQP transactions","fire-and-forget","durable queue","persistent message"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"A bare basic.publish is fire-and-forget — the producer sends it and moves on with no signal the broker actually wrote it to disk, so if the broker crashes right between the publish and the persist, the message just vanishes silently. AMQP transactions are the first fix — tx.select opens one, tx.commit confirms the batch — but commit is synchronous, so every commit has to round-trip before you can publish again, which collapses throughput once volume goes up. Publisher confirms solve that: enable confirm.select and every message gets its own ack or nack later, asynchronously, so the producer keeps firing the next message without blocking — same durability guarantee, much faster, which is why confirms are almost always preferred in practice. But that confirm only means something if the exchange and queue are declared durable and the message is published with the persistent flag, otherwise it's an empty guarantee that disappears on a broker restart; and to survive losing an entire node rather than just a restart, the queue needs to be a quorum queue so the ack only comes back once a majority of replicas have actually written it.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"A plain basic.publish doesn't guarantee the broker actually stored the message — if it crashes right after, that message is just gone and the producer never finds out. Transactions with tx.select and tx.commit fix that but they're synchronous, so you have to wait for each commit before sending the next message, which really hurts throughput. Publisher confirms give you the same durability guarantee but asynchronously — you keep publishing and handle the acks/nacks as they come back — so that's usually the better choice. I'd also want to make sure the queue and exchange are actually durable and the message is marked persistent, otherwise the confirm doesn't really mean much if the broker restarts.","sonnet":75,"haiku":88},{"level":3,"answer":"I think basic.publish alone doesn't confirm the message was actually saved, so if something goes wrong right after publishing you could lose it without knowing. Transactions can give you a durability guarantee but I believe they're slower since you wait for each one to commit. Publisher confirms are the alternative and I think they're faster because they're not blocking, so that's probably the preferred approach. I'm not fully sure what else needs to be true underneath for a confirm to actually mean the message is safe though — maybe something about the queue being persistent.","sonnet":25,"haiku":50},{"level":4,"answer":"I think publish alone might not be fully reliable but I'm not sure exactly what's missing. I've heard of publisher confirms being a thing but I don't know how they compare to transactions or which one's better.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0},{"level":5,"answer":"I'd say if basic.publish returns without an error then it worked and the message got there — that's usually enough for most use cases without adding transactions or confirms.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]}},"ideal":{"agnostic":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nPublish trần bằng `basic.publish` là fire-and-forget — producer gọi xong là quên, không hề nhận tín hiệu báo broker đã thực sự lưu message xuống đĩa. Nếu broker crash đúng khoảng giữa lúc publish và lúc ghi persist xong, message biến mất trong im lặng, và producer không có cách nào biết. Cách đầu tiên AMQP đưa ra để lấy lại bảo đảm là transaction: `tx.select` mở một transaction, `tx.commit` xác nhận toàn bộ batch publish trong đó theo kiểu tất-cả-hoặc-không-gì. Nhưng `tx.commit` là một lời gọi đồng bộ — mỗi lần commit phải round-trip chờ broker xác nhận xong mới được publish tiếp, nên khi cần publish nhiều, throughput sụp đổ. Publisher confirms giải quyết đúng vấn đề đó: bật `confirm.select`, mỗi message publish ra nhận lại một ack/nack riêng, nhưng bất đồng bộ — producer không phải đứng chờ, cứ tiếp tục bắn message tiếp theo, rồi xử lý các ack/nack đổ về sau. Cùng một bảo đảm durable-write như transaction nhưng nhanh hơn nhiều, đó là lý do trong thực tế gần như luôn chọn confirms, transaction hiếm khi đáng đánh đổi throughput. Nhưng cái confirm đó chỉ có ý nghĩa khi đối tượng phía dưới thật sự bền: exchange và queue phải khai báo durable, message phải publish với cờ persistent — nếu không, confirm vẫn trả về \"đã lưu\" nhưng lưu vào một thứ sẽ biến mất khi broker restart, tức là một bảo đảm rỗng tuếch. Muốn chịu được mất cả một node chứ không chỉ restart, queue đó còn phải là quorum queue (replicate qua Raft, xác nhận khi đa số bản sao đã ghi) — lúc đó ack của publisher confirms mới thực sự đối ứng với một message sống sót qua sự cố.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nA bare `basic.publish` is fire-and-forget — the producer calls it and moves on, with no signal that the broker actually wrote the message to disk. If the broker crashes exactly between the publish and finishing the persist, the message vanishes silently and the producer has no way to know. AMQP's first attempt at getting that guarantee back is transactions: `tx.select` opens a transaction, `tx.commit` confirms the whole batch of publishes inside it all-or-nothing. But `tx.commit` is a synchronous call — each commit has to round-trip and wait for the broker's confirmation before you can publish again, so throughput collapses once you need to publish a lot. Publisher confirms solve exactly that: enable `confirm.select` and every published message gets its own later ack/nack, but asynchronously — the producer doesn't block, it keeps firing the next message and processes the acks/nacks as they stream back. Same durable-write guarantee as transactions, much faster, which is why in practice confirms are almost always the choice and transactions are rarely worth the throughput cost. But that confirm only means something if what's underneath is actually durable: the exchange and queue must be declared durable and the message published with the persistent flag — otherwise the confirm still says \"stored\" but into something that disappears on a broker restart, i.e. an empty guarantee. To survive losing an entire node, not just a restart, the queue also has to be a quorum queue (replicated via Raft, acknowledged once a majority of replicas have written) — only then does the publisher-confirm ack truly correspond to a message that survives a real failure."}}}$iv$::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (bank_id, bank_version, question_id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO interview_questions (bank_id, bank_version, question_id, sort_index, is_premium, family, tier, kind, tags, competency, leadership_tier, bodies, interviewer, grading)
VALUES ('1-system-design-mastery/5-rabbitmq-and-job-queues', '231d8cc57+37d905d9a', '1-system-design-mastery/5-rabbitmq-and-job-queues/6', 6, false, 'technical', 'senior', 'reasoning', ARRAY['two-layer-limiting', 'edge-vs-worker', 'backpressure']::text[], NULL, NULL, $iv${"agnostic":{"vi":{"prompt":"Hệ thống rate-limit của bạn đã chặn traffic vượt hạn mức ngay ở tầng edge bằng Token Bucket, trả về 429. Một đồng nghiệp lập luận rằng worker-side limiter giờ là dư thừa, và đề xuất bỏ đi cho gọn code, vì \"edge đã chặn hết ai vượt hạn mức rồi.\" Bạn hãy tư duy thành tiếng: vì sao hệ thống vẫn cần CẢ HAI tầng thay vì chỉ giữ tầng edge?","givenCode":null,"diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"Your rate-limited system already rejects excess traffic at the edge with a Token Bucket, returning 429. A colleague argues that the worker-side limiter is now redundant, and proposes removing it to simplify the codebase, since \"the edge already blocks anyone over the limit.\" Reason out loud: why does the system still need BOTH layers rather than just the edge one?","givenCode":null,"diagram":null}}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"vi":{"followUps":["Nếu bỏ tầng worker và chỉ giữ edge, kịch bản cụ thể nào khiến DB hoặc third-party API sập dù mọi request đều hợp lệ và đã qua được edge?","Ngược lại, nếu bỏ tầng edge và chỉ giữ worker limiter, điều gì xảy ra với chính API/service khi bị spam bởi request vô danh chưa xác thực?"],"hints":["Hỏi \"edge chặn cái gì, ở thời điểm nào\" và \"worker giới hạn cái gì, ở thời điểm nào\" — hai câu trả lời đó có giống nhau không?","Hình dung 50 job được enqueue tức thì, tất cả đều hợp lệ và đã qua edge, nhưng downstream chỉ chịu được 10 job/giây — ai giữ tốc độ đó nếu không phải worker limiter?"]},"en":{"followUps":["If you drop the worker layer and keep only the edge, what concrete scenario still takes down the DB or a third-party API even though every request is legitimate and already passed the edge?","Conversely, if you drop the edge layer and keep only the worker limiter, what happens to the API/service itself when it's spammed with unauthenticated, anonymous requests?"],"hints":["Ask \"what does the edge reject, and at what point in time\" versus \"what does the worker throttle, and at what point in time\" — are those two answers the same thing?","Picture 50 jobs enqueued instantly, all legitimate and already past the edge, but downstream can only sustain 10 jobs/second — who enforces that rate if not the worker limiter?"]}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"locales":{"vi":{"rubric":["Chỉ ra tầng edge (token bucket) chặn traffic vượt hạn mức TRƯỚC KHI nó vào hệ thống, trả 429 — nó bảo vệ chính API khỏi spam/abuse.","Nhận ra một burst HỢP LỆ (đã lọt qua được edge) vẫn có thể sinh ra khối lượng công việc downstream nặng (DB, API bên thứ ba); worker limiter giới hạn tốc độ XỬ LÝ để bảo vệ downstream, việc mà edge không hề động tới.","Giải thích hai tầng tách rời tốc độ CHẤP NHẬN khỏi tốc độ XỬ LÝ: hệ thống có thể nhận một burst nhưng vẫn drain nó ở tốc độ cố định (ví dụ 10 job/giây).","Chỉ ra worker limiter được chia sẻ qua Redis, nên scale thêm worker KHÔNG nhân đôi tốc độ downstream — đúng cái cap chia sẻ đó là mục đích khi cần bảo vệ một quota downstream cố định."],"checklist":[{"text":"Edge token bucket protects the API from abusive volume by rejecting excess requests upfront with 429.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":12},{"text":"A legitimate burst that gets through the edge can still translate into heavy work against a database or third-party quota — something the edge layer never touches.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":18},{"text":"The worker limiter exists exactly for that: throttling the downstream processing rate.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":17},{"text":"Together the two layers decouple how fast you ACCEPT work from how fast you PROCESS it.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":17},{"text":"That decoupling lets the system absorb a burst yet drain it at a safe rate (e.g. 10 jobs/second via BullMQ).","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":12},{"text":"Because the worker limiter is enforced in shared Redis, adding more workers doesn't raise the downstream rate — exactly what's needed when guarding a fixed downstream capacity.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":12},{"text":"Dropping the worker layer misreads the problem: the edge answers 'should this request proceed at all,' while the worker answers a different question — 'what rate can downstream actually sustain' — and the two questions cannot be collapsed into one.","dimension":"communication","critical":false,"scoreBand":12}],"keywords":["edge layer","worker limiter","decouple accept/process rate","shared Redis limiter","backpressure"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"I'd push back on removing the worker limiter. The edge token bucket answers one question — should this request even be accepted — and it rejects abusive volume with a 429 before it enters the system at all. But a burst that legitimately gets past the edge can still turn into heavy downstream work against a database or a third-party API with its own quota, and the edge layer never sees that at all — that's exactly the problem the worker-side limiter, like a BullMQ processor limiter, is solving. Together the two layers decouple how fast you accept work from how fast you drain it, so a burst gets absorbed at the edge but processed at a safe fixed rate, say 10 jobs a second. And because the worker limiter lives in shared Redis rather than per-process, spinning up more workers doesn't raise that downstream rate, which is exactly what you want when the thing you're protecting has a fixed capacity — so 'the edge already blocks people' misses that the two layers are answering completely different questions and can't collapse into one.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"I don't think you can drop the worker limiter. The edge bucket is about whether a request gets in at all and returns 429 if you're over the limit, but once a burst is accepted, processing it can still hammer a database or a third-party API that has its own rate limit — the edge never looks at that. The worker-side limiter is what caps how fast jobs actually get processed downstream, so you get to accept a burst but still drain it slowly and safely. If you removed it, a legitimate burst that passes the edge could still overload whatever's downstream, so really the two layers are protecting different things and you need both.","sonnet":86,"haiku":86},{"level":3,"answer":"I think the edge limiter and the worker limiter are doing kind of different things, though I'm not 100% sure exactly how. My guess is the edge one just stops too many requests from coming in, and the worker one is more about not overloading whatever the jobs are actually calling, like a database. So even if requests get accepted at the edge, maybe processing them all at once downstream could still cause problems, which is probably why you'd still want the worker limiter. I'm not sure I could explain the exact mechanism of how removing it would break things though.","sonnet":0,"haiku":57},{"level":4,"answer":"Um, I guess there might be some difference between limiting requests coming in versus limiting jobs being processed, but honestly they sound pretty similar to me. Maybe the worker one is a backup in case the edge one misses something? I'm not really sure why you'd need both.","sonnet":0,"haiku":43},{"level":5,"answer":"Honestly I think the colleague's right — if the edge already returns 429 for anyone over the limit, then by the time a job reaches the worker it's already been filtered, so having a second limiter there just seems like duplicate logic. I'd probably just remove it to keep the codebase simpler since the edge should be catching the excess traffic anyway.","sonnet":0,"haiku":14}]},"en":{"rubric":["States the edge layer (token bucket) rejects excess traffic BEFORE it enters the system, returning 429 — it protects the API itself from spam/abuse.","Recognizes a LEGITIMATE burst that passes the edge can still generate heavy downstream work (DB, third-party API); the worker limiter throttles the PROCESSING rate to protect downstream — something the edge never touches.","Explains the two layers decouple the accept-rate from the process-rate: you can accept a burst yet still drain it at a fixed rate (e.g. 10 jobs/second).","Notes the worker limiter is shared through Redis, so scaling out more workers does NOT multiply the downstream rate — that shared cap is exactly the point when protecting a fixed downstream quota."],"checklist":[{"text":"Edge token bucket protects the API from abusive volume by rejecting excess requests upfront with 429.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":12},{"text":"A legitimate burst that gets through the edge can still translate into heavy work against a database or third-party quota — something the edge layer never touches.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":18},{"text":"The worker limiter exists exactly for that: throttling the downstream processing rate.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":17},{"text":"Together the two layers decouple how fast you ACCEPT work from how fast you PROCESS it.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":17},{"text":"That decoupling lets the system absorb a burst yet drain it at a safe rate (e.g. 10 jobs/second via BullMQ).","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":12},{"text":"Because the worker limiter is enforced in shared Redis, adding more workers doesn't raise the downstream rate — exactly what's needed when guarding a fixed downstream capacity.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":12},{"text":"Dropping the worker layer misreads the problem: the edge answers 'should this request proceed at all,' while the worker answers a different question — 'what rate can downstream actually sustain' — and the two questions cannot be collapsed into one.","dimension":"communication","critical":false,"scoreBand":12}],"keywords":["edge layer","worker limiter","decouple accept/process rate","shared Redis limiter","backpressure"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"I'd push back on removing the worker limiter. The edge token bucket answers one question — should this request even be accepted — and it rejects abusive volume with a 429 before it enters the system at all. But a burst that legitimately gets past the edge can still turn into heavy downstream work against a database or a third-party API with its own quota, and the edge layer never sees that at all — that's exactly the problem the worker-side limiter, like a BullMQ processor limiter, is solving. Together the two layers decouple how fast you accept work from how fast you drain it, so a burst gets absorbed at the edge but processed at a safe fixed rate, say 10 jobs a second. And because the worker limiter lives in shared Redis rather than per-process, spinning up more workers doesn't raise that downstream rate, which is exactly what you want when the thing you're protecting has a fixed capacity — so 'the edge already blocks people' misses that the two layers are answering completely different questions and can't collapse into one.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"I don't think you can drop the worker limiter. The edge bucket is about whether a request gets in at all and returns 429 if you're over the limit, but once a burst is accepted, processing it can still hammer a database or a third-party API that has its own rate limit — the edge never looks at that. The worker-side limiter is what caps how fast jobs actually get processed downstream, so you get to accept a burst but still drain it slowly and safely. If you removed it, a legitimate burst that passes the edge could still overload whatever's downstream, so really the two layers are protecting different things and you need both.","sonnet":86,"haiku":86},{"level":3,"answer":"I think the edge limiter and the worker limiter are doing kind of different things, though I'm not 100% sure exactly how. My guess is the edge one just stops too many requests from coming in, and the worker one is more about not overloading whatever the jobs are actually calling, like a database. So even if requests get accepted at the edge, maybe processing them all at once downstream could still cause problems, which is probably why you'd still want the worker limiter. I'm not sure I could explain the exact mechanism of how removing it would break things though.","sonnet":0,"haiku":57},{"level":4,"answer":"Um, I guess there might be some difference between limiting requests coming in versus limiting jobs being processed, but honestly they sound pretty similar to me. Maybe the worker one is a backup in case the edge one misses something? I'm not really sure why you'd need both.","sonnet":0,"haiku":43},{"level":5,"answer":"Honestly I think the colleague's right — if the edge already returns 429 for anyone over the limit, then by the time a job reaches the worker it's already been filtered, so having a second limiter there just seems like duplicate logic. I'd probably just remove it to keep the codebase simpler since the edge should be catching the excess traffic anyway.","sonnet":0,"haiku":14}]}},"ideal":{"agnostic":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nTầng token bucket ở edge bảo vệ API khỏi lượng traffic lạm dụng, bằng cách chặn ngay các request vượt hạn mức với 429. Nhưng một burst HỢP LỆ lọt qua được edge vẫn có thể biến thành khối lượng công việc nặng dồn vào database hoặc quota của bên thứ ba — điều mà tầng edge không hề động tới; đó chính là việc của worker limiter, giới hạn tốc độ xử lý downstream. Kết hợp lại, hai tầng tách rời tốc độ bạn CHẤP NHẬN công việc khỏi tốc độ bạn XỬ LÝ nó, nên hệ thống hấp thụ được burst nhưng vẫn drain ở tốc độ an toàn (ví dụ 10 job/giây qua BullMQ). Vì worker limiter được thực thi trong Redis dùng chung, thêm worker không làm tăng tốc độ downstream — đúng thứ bạn muốn khi cần bảo vệ một năng lực downstream cố định. Bỏ tầng worker để \"gọn code\" là hiểu sai vấn đề: edge trả lời câu hỏi \"request này có được đi tiếp không\", còn worker trả lời câu hỏi hoàn toàn khác — \"downstream có thể chịu được tốc độ xử lý nào\" — hai câu hỏi này không thể gộp làm một.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe edge token bucket protects the API from abusive volume by rejecting excess requests up front with 429. But a legitimate burst that gets through the edge can still translate into heavy work against a database or a third-party quota — something the edge layer never touches; that is exactly what the worker limiter is for, throttling the downstream processing rate. Together, the two layers decouple how fast you ACCEPT work from how fast you PROCESS it, so the system absorbs a burst yet drains it at a safe rate (e.g. 10 jobs/second via BullMQ). Because the worker limiter is enforced in shared Redis, adding more workers doesn't raise the downstream rate — exactly what you want when guarding a fixed downstream capacity. Dropping the worker layer to \"simplify the code\" misreads the problem: the edge answers \"should this request proceed at all,\" while the worker answers a completely different question — \"what rate can downstream actually sustain\" — and the two questions cannot be collapsed into one."}}}$iv$::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (bank_id, bank_version, question_id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO interview_questions (bank_id, bank_version, question_id, sort_index, is_premium, family, tier, kind, tags, competency, leadership_tier, bodies, interviewer, grading)
VALUES ('1-system-design-mastery/5-rabbitmq-and-job-queues', '231d8cc57+37d905d9a', '1-system-design-mastery/5-rabbitmq-and-job-queues/7', 7, false, 'technical', 'middle', 'debug', ARRAY['ack-ordering', 'message-loss', 'at-least-once']::text[], NULL, NULL, $iv${"typescript":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng TypeScript. Production: một số đơn hàng \"biến mất\" mỗi khi worker bị restart. Hãy đọc code thành tiếng, chỉ ra root cause, và giải thích cách sửa cùng việc thứ tự ack liên quan thế nào đến delivery guarantee.","givenCode":"```typescript\nasync function runOrderWorker(channel: amqp.Channel): Promise<void> {\n  await channel.assertExchange(EXCHANGES.DIRECT, ExchangeKind.Direct, { durable: true })\n  await channel.assertQueue(QUEUES.ORDERS_PROCESS, { durable: true })\n  await channel.bindQueue(QUEUES.ORDERS_PROCESS, EXCHANGES.DIRECT, ORDERS_ROUTING_KEY)\n  await channel.prefetch(1)\n  await channel.consume(QUEUES.ORDERS_PROCESS, async (msg) => {\n    if (!msg) return\n    // Ack right away — the broker now considers this message DONE.\n    channel.ack(msg)\n    const order = JSON.parse(msg.content.toString())\n    await fulfillmentService.fulfill(order) // throws, or the process can crash, mid-flight\n  })\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in TypeScript. In production, some orders \"vanish\" every time the worker restarts. Read the code out loud, locate the root cause, and explain the fix and how ack ordering relates to the delivery guarantee.","givenCode":"```typescript\nasync function runOrderWorker(channel: amqp.Channel): Promise<void> {\n  await channel.assertExchange(EXCHANGES.DIRECT, ExchangeKind.Direct, { durable: true })\n  await channel.assertQueue(QUEUES.ORDERS_PROCESS, { durable: true })\n  await channel.bindQueue(QUEUES.ORDERS_PROCESS, EXCHANGES.DIRECT, ORDERS_ROUTING_KEY)\n  await channel.prefetch(1)\n  await channel.consume(QUEUES.ORDERS_PROCESS, async (msg) => {\n    if (!msg) return\n    // Ack right away — the broker now considers this message DONE.\n    channel.ack(msg)\n    const order = JSON.parse(msg.content.toString())\n    await fulfillmentService.fulfill(order) // throws, or the process can crash, mid-flight\n  })\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"java":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng Java. Production: một số đơn hàng \"biến mất\" mỗi khi worker bị restart. Hãy đọc code thành tiếng, chỉ ra root cause, và giải thích cách sửa cùng việc ack liên quan thế nào đến delivery guarantee.","givenCode":"```java\n@Bean\npublic SimpleRabbitListenerContainerFactory ordersListenerContainerFactory(ConnectionFactory connectionFactory) {\n    SimpleRabbitListenerContainerFactory factory = new SimpleRabbitListenerContainerFactory();\n    factory.setConnectionFactory(connectionFactory);\n    factory.setPrefetchCount(1);\n    // Auto-ack: the broker marks the message done the instant it hands it over, before this method even runs.\n    factory.setAcknowledgeMode(AcknowledgeMode.NONE);\n    return factory;\n}\n\n@RabbitListener(queues = Topology.QUEUE_ORDERS_PROCESS, containerFactory = \"ordersListenerContainerFactory\")\npublic void onOrder(byte[] body) {\n    Order order = parseOrder(body);\n    fulfillmentService.fulfill(order); // throws, or the process can crash, mid-flight\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in Java. In production, some orders \"vanish\" every time the worker restarts. Read the code out loud, locate the root cause, and explain the fix and how ack relates to the delivery guarantee.","givenCode":"```java\n@Bean\npublic SimpleRabbitListenerContainerFactory ordersListenerContainerFactory(ConnectionFactory connectionFactory) {\n    SimpleRabbitListenerContainerFactory factory = new SimpleRabbitListenerContainerFactory();\n    factory.setConnectionFactory(connectionFactory);\n    factory.setPrefetchCount(1);\n    // Auto-ack: the broker marks the message done the instant it hands it over, before this method even runs.\n    factory.setAcknowledgeMode(AcknowledgeMode.NONE);\n    return factory;\n}\n\n@RabbitListener(queues = Topology.QUEUE_ORDERS_PROCESS, containerFactory = \"ordersListenerContainerFactory\")\npublic void onOrder(byte[] body) {\n    Order order = parseOrder(body);\n    fulfillmentService.fulfill(order); // throws, or the process can crash, mid-flight\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"csharp":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng C#. Production: một số đơn hàng \"biến mất\" mỗi khi worker bị restart. Hãy đọc code thành tiếng, chỉ ra root cause, và giải thích cách sửa cùng việc thứ tự ack liên quan thế nào đến delivery guarantee.","givenCode":"```csharp\nvar consumer = new AsyncEventingBasicConsumer(channel);\nconsumer.ReceivedAsync += async (sender, ea) =>\n{\n    // Ack right away — the broker now considers this message DONE.\n    await channel.BasicAckAsync(ea.DeliveryTag, false);\n    var order = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<Order>(ea.Body.Span);\n    await fulfillmentService.FulfillAsync(order); // throws, or the process can crash, mid-flight\n};\nawait channel.BasicConsumeAsync(queueOrdersProcess, autoAck: false, consumer);\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in C#. In production, some orders \"vanish\" every time the worker restarts. Read the code out loud, locate the root cause, and explain the fix and how ack ordering relates to the delivery guarantee.","givenCode":"```csharp\nvar consumer = new AsyncEventingBasicConsumer(channel);\nconsumer.ReceivedAsync += async (sender, ea) =>\n{\n    // Ack right away — the broker now considers this message DONE.\n    await channel.BasicAckAsync(ea.DeliveryTag, false);\n    var order = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<Order>(ea.Body.Span);\n    await fulfillmentService.FulfillAsync(order); // throws, or the process can crash, mid-flight\n};\nawait channel.BasicConsumeAsync(queueOrdersProcess, autoAck: false, consumer);\n```","diagram":null}},"go":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng Go. Production: một số đơn hàng \"biến mất\" mỗi khi worker bị restart. Hãy đọc code thành tiếng, chỉ ra root cause, và giải thích cách sửa cùng việc thứ tự ack liên quan thế nào đến delivery guarantee.","givenCode":"```go\nmsgs, err := ch.Consume(queueOrdersProcess, \"\", false, false, false, false, nil)\nif err != nil {\n    log.Fatal(err)\n}\nfor d := range msgs {\n    // Ack right away — the broker now considers this message DONE.\n    _ = d.Ack(false)\n    var order Order\n    _ = json.Unmarshal(d.Body, &order)\n    fulfillOrder(order) // panics, or the process can crash, mid-flight\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in Go. In production, some orders \"vanish\" every time the worker restarts. Read the code out loud, locate the root cause, and explain the fix and how ack ordering relates to the delivery guarantee.","givenCode":"```go\nmsgs, err := ch.Consume(queueOrdersProcess, \"\", false, false, false, false, nil)\nif err != nil {\n    log.Fatal(err)\n}\nfor d := range msgs {\n    // Ack right away — the broker now considers this message DONE.\n    _ = d.Ack(false)\n    var order Order\n    _ = json.Unmarshal(d.Body, &order)\n    fulfillOrder(order) // panics, or the process can crash, mid-flight\n}\n```","diagram":null}}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"vi":{"followUps":["Nếu muốn tránh xử lý lại toàn bộ công việc từ đầu khi redeliver (ví dụ side-effect không idempotent), bạn thiết kế idempotency cho consumer này thế nào?","`prefetch` liên quan gì đến mức độ thiệt hại của bug này — prefetch càng cao thì khi worker crash, có bao nhiêu message đang bị coi là \"đã xong\" nhưng thực ra chưa được xử lý?"],"hints":["Hỏi: ai đang giữ message lúc này — broker hay bộ nhớ của consumer? Broker chỉ coi message là \"xong\" và xoá nó khi nào?","Vẽ lại timeline: ack chạy TRƯỚC hay SAU dòng code thực sự xử lý message? Nếu worker crash ngay sau ack nhưng trước khi xử lý xong, message đó còn tồn tại ở đâu không?"]},"en":{"followUps":["If you want to avoid redoing all the work from scratch on redelivery (e.g. a non-idempotent side effect), how would you design idempotency for this consumer?","How does `prefetch` relate to the blast radius of this bug — the higher the prefetch, the more messages could be sitting \"considered done\" but not actually processed when the worker crashes?"],"hints":["Ask: who is holding the message right now — the broker or the consumer's memory? When does the broker consider the message \"done\" and delete it?","Sketch the timeline: does the ack run BEFORE or AFTER the line that actually processes the message? If the worker crashes right after the ack but before processing finishes, where is that message now?"]}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"locales":{"vi":{"rubric":["[problemSolving] Chỉ ra đúng root cause: việc ack (hoặc auto-ack) diễn ra TRƯỚC khi xử lý xong, nên broker xoá message khỏi queue trước khi công việc thực sự hoàn tất — worker crash giữa chừng là mất luôn message đó.","[technical] Đưa ra cách sửa: chỉ ack SAU KHI xử lý thành công; khi thất bại thì nack (requeue, hoặc route sang dead-letter exchange) để broker redeliver — đây chính là thứ tạo nên delivery at-least-once.","[communication] Định khung đây là một BLOCKER về correctness/mất dữ liệu (không phải nitpick về style), và phát biểu rõ invariant: một message chỉ thực sự bị xoá khỏi queue KHI consumer đã ack.","[testing] Đề xuất một test giết worker (hoặc throw) giữa chừng xử lý, TRƯỚC khi ack, rồi assert rằng message được redeliver sang consumer khác chứ không bị mất."],"checklist":[{"text":"RabbitMQ only removes a message from a queue once the consumer acknowledges it; until then the message stays in an \"unacked\" state and the broker can redeliver it if the consumer connection drops.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"In the given consumer, `channel.ack(msg)` runs immediately on delivery, before `await fulfillmentService.fulfill(order)` has even started — so the broker marks the message done and deletes it before any real processing has happened.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"Because the ack already fired, a thrown error inside `fulfill` or a process crash (OOM, deploy restart) that happens while `fulfill` is still running leaves no unacked copy of the message anywhere, so the order is permanently lost with no chance of redelivery.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"This is exactly how RabbitMQ's at-least-once delivery guarantee gets silently downgraded to at-most-once: the guarantee only holds as long as a message is deleted strictly after processing succeeds, never before.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"The fix is to move `channel.ack(msg)` to after `fulfillmentService.fulfill(order)` resolves successfully, wrapping the call in a try/catch so the ack only fires on the success path.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"On failure, the consumer should call `channel.nack(msg, false, false)` to send the message to a dead-letter exchange, or nack with `requeue: true` for transient failures worth retrying, instead of acking a message that was never actually processed.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Correct behavior can be verified by deliberately throwing inside `fulfill` (or killing the worker) before the ack executes and confirming the message is redelivered to another consumer, or lands in the dead-letter queue, rather than disappearing.","dimension":"testing","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"The bug is a data-loss correctness issue, not a style nit, and its severity scales with `prefetch`: a higher prefetch count means more messages can be sitting acked-and-considered-done in memory when the worker crashes, widening the blast radius of lost orders.","dimension":"communication","critical":false,"scoreBand":10}],"keywords":["ack","at-least-once","redelivery","prefetch","dead-letter exchange"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"The core issue is that channel.ack(msg) fires immediately when the message arrives, before await fulfillmentService.fulfill(order) even starts running — so the broker considers the message done and deletes it before any real work has happened. Normally RabbitMQ only drops a message once it's acked, keeping it in an unacked state that gets redelivered if the consumer disconnects. Here, because the ack already went out, if fulfill throws or the process crashes mid-call, there's no unacked copy left anywhere, so the order is gone for good. That silently turns the at-least-once guarantee into at-most-once, since the guarantee only holds if deletion happens strictly after success. The fix is moving the ack to after fulfill resolves, inside a try/catch, so it only fires on the success path; on failure we should nack — either requeue:true for transient errors or send straight to a dead-letter exchange for poison messages. I'd verify this by throwing inside fulfill on purpose and confirming the message gets redelivered or dead-lettered instead of vanishing. And this gets worse with higher prefetch, since more messages can be sitting acked-and-forgotten in memory when a crash happens.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"Looking at the consumer, the ack happens right when the message comes in — before await fulfillmentService.fulfill(order) has even kicked off. So if that call throws, or the whole process dies partway through, the order never actually gets processed but there's nothing left to retry it because we already told the broker we're done with it. The fix is straightforward: only ack after fulfill actually succeeds, wrapped in a try/catch, and nack it on failure — with requeue for stuff worth retrying or straight to a dead-letter queue otherwise. To check this is fixed, I'd force an error inside fulfill and make sure the message comes back around or ends up dead-lettered rather than just disappearing. It's also worth flagging that cranking up prefetch makes this riskier, since more in-flight messages could be lost at once if a worker dies.","sonnet":80,"haiku":80},{"level":3,"answer":"If fulfill throws, or the worker crashes while that call is running, the order ends up lost with nothing to fall back on. I'd fix it by only calling ack once fulfill has actually finished successfully, instead of doing it right away. To make sure that's actually fixed, I'd force an error inside fulfill and check that the message doesn't just disappear.","sonnet":45,"haiku":45},{"level":4,"answer":"I guess to check if this works we'd want to throw an error somewhere in there and see what happens to the message.","sonnet":15,"haiku":15},{"level":5,"answer":"I'd probably focus on adding better logging around the order fulfillment step so we can see what's happening, and maybe add a retry wrapper around the whole consumer function.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]},"en":{"rubric":["[problemSolving] Identifies the root cause: the ack (or auto-ack) happens BEFORE processing, so the broker removes the message before the work is done — a crash mid-flight loses it.","[technical] Fix: ack only AFTER successful processing; on failure nack (requeue, or route to a dead-letter exchange) so the broker redelivers — this is what makes delivery at-least-once.","[communication] Frames it as a correctness / data-loss BLOCKER (not a style nit) and states the invariant plainly: a message is only removed from the queue once the consumer acks.","[testing] Proposes a test that kills the worker (or throws) mid-processing before the ack and asserts the message is redelivered to another consumer rather than lost."],"checklist":[{"text":"RabbitMQ only removes a message from a queue once the consumer acknowledges it; until then the message stays in an \"unacked\" state and the broker can redeliver it if the consumer connection drops.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"In the given consumer, `channel.ack(msg)` runs immediately on delivery, before `await fulfillmentService.fulfill(order)` has even started — so the broker marks the message done and deletes it before any real processing has happened.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"Because the ack already fired, a thrown error inside `fulfill` or a process crash (OOM, deploy restart) that happens while `fulfill` is still running leaves no unacked copy of the message anywhere, so the order is permanently lost with no chance of redelivery.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"This is exactly how RabbitMQ's at-least-once delivery guarantee gets silently downgraded to at-most-once: the guarantee only holds as long as a message is deleted strictly after processing succeeds, never before.","dimension":"technical","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"The fix is to move `channel.ack(msg)` to after `fulfillmentService.fulfill(order)` resolves successfully, wrapping the call in a try/catch so the ack only fires on the success path.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"On failure, the consumer should call `channel.nack(msg, false, false)` to send the message to a dead-letter exchange, or nack with `requeue: true` for transient failures worth retrying, instead of acking a message that was never actually processed.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Correct behavior can be verified by deliberately throwing inside `fulfill` (or killing the worker) before the ack executes and confirming the message is redelivered to another consumer, or lands in the dead-letter queue, rather than disappearing.","dimension":"testing","critical":true,"scoreBand":15},{"text":"The bug is a data-loss correctness issue, not a style nit, and its severity scales with `prefetch`: a higher prefetch count means more messages can be sitting acked-and-considered-done in memory when the worker crashes, widening the blast radius of lost orders.","dimension":"communication","critical":false,"scoreBand":10}],"keywords":["ack","at-least-once","redelivery","prefetch","dead-letter exchange"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"The core issue is that channel.ack(msg) fires immediately when the message arrives, before await fulfillmentService.fulfill(order) even starts running — so the broker considers the message done and deletes it before any real work has happened. Normally RabbitMQ only drops a message once it's acked, keeping it in an unacked state that gets redelivered if the consumer disconnects. Here, because the ack already went out, if fulfill throws or the process crashes mid-call, there's no unacked copy left anywhere, so the order is gone for good. That silently turns the at-least-once guarantee into at-most-once, since the guarantee only holds if deletion happens strictly after success. The fix is moving the ack to after fulfill resolves, inside a try/catch, so it only fires on the success path; on failure we should nack — either requeue:true for transient errors or send straight to a dead-letter exchange for poison messages. I'd verify this by throwing inside fulfill on purpose and confirming the message gets redelivered or dead-lettered instead of vanishing. And this gets worse with higher prefetch, since more messages can be sitting acked-and-forgotten in memory when a crash happens.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"Looking at the consumer, the ack happens right when the message comes in — before await fulfillmentService.fulfill(order) has even kicked off. So if that call throws, or the whole process dies partway through, the order never actually gets processed but there's nothing left to retry it because we already told the broker we're done with it. The fix is straightforward: only ack after fulfill actually succeeds, wrapped in a try/catch, and nack it on failure — with requeue for stuff worth retrying or straight to a dead-letter queue otherwise. To check this is fixed, I'd force an error inside fulfill and make sure the message comes back around or ends up dead-lettered rather than just disappearing. It's also worth flagging that cranking up prefetch makes this riskier, since more in-flight messages could be lost at once if a worker dies.","sonnet":80,"haiku":80},{"level":3,"answer":"If fulfill throws, or the worker crashes while that call is running, the order ends up lost with nothing to fall back on. I'd fix it by only calling ack once fulfill has actually finished successfully, instead of doing it right away. To make sure that's actually fixed, I'd force an error inside fulfill and check that the message doesn't just disappear.","sonnet":45,"haiku":45},{"level":4,"answer":"I guess to check if this works we'd want to throw an error somewhere in there and see what happens to the message.","sonnet":15,"haiku":15},{"level":5,"answer":"I'd probably focus on adding better logging around the order fulfillment step so we can see what's happening, and maybe add a retry wrapper around the whole consumer function.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]}},"ideal":{"typescript":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nBug nằm ở thứ tự gọi `channel.ack(msg)`, không phải logic của `fulfillmentService.fulfill`. RabbitMQ chỉ thực sự xoá một message khỏi queue khi consumer ack nó — trước đó, message vẫn \"unacked\" và broker có thể redeliver nếu consumer chết. Ở đây `channel.ack(msg)` được gọi NGAY khi nhận message, trước khi `await fulfillmentService.fulfill(order)` chạy xong. Vì vậy ngay khoảnh khắc ack được gửi, broker coi message là đã xử lý xong và xoá nó khỏi queue — bất kể `fulfill` có thực sự chạy xong hay không. Nếu `fulfill` throw, hoặc worker process crash (do OOM, deploy restart, v.v.) đúng lúc đang chạy `fulfill`, message đã bị xoá từ trước đó rồi, nên không còn cách nào redeliver nó — đơn hàng biến mất vĩnh viễn, và vì đây là bug xảy ra ở MỌI lần crash giữa chừng, production thấy đơn hàng \"biến mất\" đều đặn mỗi khi worker restart. Đây là cách phá vỡ at-least-once: guarantee này chỉ đứng vững khi invariant \"message chỉ bị xoá SAU KHI consumer xác nhận đã xử lý xong\" được giữ nguyên. Cách sửa: di chuyển `channel.ack(msg)` xuống SAU khi `await fulfillmentService.fulfill(order)` resolve thành công, bọc trong try/catch — nếu `fulfill` throw thì gọi `channel.nack(msg, false, false)` để route sang dead-letter exchange (hoặc `requeue: true` nếu lỗi tạm thời và muốn thử lại), thay vì ack. Verify bằng cách throw có chủ đích bên trong `fulfill` trước khi ack chạy, rồi assert rằng message được redeliver sang một consumer khác (hoặc rơi vào DLQ) thay vì biến mất.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe bug is in when `channel.ack(msg)` is called, not in `fulfillmentService.fulfill`'s logic. RabbitMQ only actually removes a message from the queue once the consumer acks it — before that, the message stays \"unacked\" and the broker can redeliver it if the consumer dies. Here `channel.ack(msg)` fires IMMEDIATELY on delivery, before `await fulfillmentService.fulfill(order)` has finished. So the instant the ack goes out, the broker treats the message as done and deletes it — regardless of whether `fulfill` actually completes. If `fulfill` throws, or the worker process crashes (OOM, a deploy restart, etc.) exactly while `fulfill` is running, the message was already deleted before that point, so there is no way to redeliver it — the order vanishes for good, and because this happens on every crash mid-flight, production sees orders \"disappear\" like clockwork on every worker restart. This is exactly how at-least-once gets broken: the guarantee only holds while the invariant \"a message is deleted only AFTER the consumer confirms it finished processing\" stays intact. The fix: move `channel.ack(msg)` to AFTER `await fulfillmentService.fulfill(order)` resolves successfully, wrapped in try/catch — if `fulfill` throws, call `channel.nack(msg, false, false)` to route to a dead-letter exchange (or `requeue: true` if the failure is transient and worth retrying) instead of acking. Verify by deliberately throwing inside `fulfill` before the ack runs, then asserting the message is redelivered to another consumer (or lands in the DLQ) instead of vanishing."},"java":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nBug nằm ở `AcknowledgeMode.NONE` trên container factory, không phải logic của `fulfillmentService.fulfill`. Spring AMQP có ba acknowledge mode: `AUTO` (mặc định, tự ack SAU KHI method của listener return bình thường — an toàn), `MANUAL` (code tự gọi `channel.basicAck` khi muốn), và `NONE` — chế độ mà bug này dùng. `NONE` nghĩa là RabbitMQ.Client tự động gửi ack ngay khi message được giao cho consumer, hoàn toàn độc lập với việc `onOrder` có chạy xong hay không; nói cách khác, broker xoá message khỏi queue TRƯỚC CẢ KHI listener method bắt đầu chạy. Nếu `fulfillmentService.fulfill` throw, hoặc JVM/worker process crash đúng lúc đang chạy `fulfill`, message đã bị xoá từ trước rồi — không còn gì để redeliver, đơn hàng biến mất vĩnh viễn. Vì đây là hành vi xảy ra ở MỌI lần crash giữa chừng, production thấy đơn hàng \"biến mất\" đều đặn mỗi khi worker restart. Đây chính là cách phá vỡ at-least-once: guarantee chỉ đứng vững khi message chỉ bị xoá SAU KHI consumer xác nhận xử lý xong, còn `NONE` xoá nó ngay khi giao, bất kể kết quả xử lý. Cách sửa: đổi `AcknowledgeMode` sang `MANUAL` và tự gọi ack SAU KHI `fulfillmentService.fulfill(order)` chạy thành công (dùng `ChannelAwareMessageListener`, hoặc lấy `Channel`/delivery tag qua header để gọi `channel.basicAck`), và gọi `channel.basicNack(deliveryTag, false, false)` khi fulfill throw để route sang dead-letter exchange thay vì mất trắng — hoặc đơn giản hơn, đổi về `AUTO` (mặc định) nếu không cần kiểm soát ack thủ công, vì `AUTO` tự ack sau khi method return thành công và tự để message reject/requeue khi method throw. Verify bằng cách throw có chủ đích trong `fulfill` rồi assert message được redeliver sang consumer khác (hoặc rơi vào DLQ) thay vì biến mất.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe bug is `AcknowledgeMode.NONE` on the container factory, not `fulfillmentService.fulfill`'s logic. Spring AMQP has three acknowledge modes: `AUTO` (the default — acks automatically AFTER the listener method returns normally, which is safe), `MANUAL` (the code calls `channel.basicAck` explicitly), and `NONE` — the mode this bug uses. `NONE` means the RabbitMQ.Client automatically sends the ack the moment the message is handed to the consumer, completely independent of whether `onOrder` ever finishes; in other words, the broker deletes the message from the queue BEFORE the listener method even starts running. If `fulfillmentService.fulfill` throws, or the JVM/worker process crashes exactly while `fulfill` is running, the message was already deleted before that point — there is nothing left to redeliver, and the order vanishes for good. Because this happens on every crash mid-flight, production sees orders \"disappear\" like clockwork on every worker restart. This is exactly how at-least-once gets broken: the guarantee only holds while a message is deleted only AFTER the consumer confirms it finished processing, whereas `NONE` deletes it the instant it is handed over, regardless of the outcome. The fix: switch `AcknowledgeMode` to `MANUAL` and ack explicitly AFTER `fulfillmentService.fulfill(order)` succeeds (via a `ChannelAwareMessageListener`, or by pulling the `Channel`/delivery tag from a header to call `channel.basicAck`), and call `channel.basicNack(deliveryTag, false, false)` when `fulfill` throws to route to a dead-letter exchange instead of losing the message outright — or, more simply, switch back to `AUTO` (the default) if manual ack control isn't needed, since `AUTO` acks after the method returns successfully and lets the message be rejected/requeued when the method throws. Verify by deliberately throwing inside `fulfill` and asserting the message is redelivered to another consumer (or lands in the DLQ) instead of vanishing."},"csharp":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nBug nằm ở thứ tự gọi `channel.BasicAckAsync(ea.DeliveryTag, false)`, không phải logic của `fulfillmentService.FulfillAsync`. Dù consumer khai báo `autoAck: false` — đúng bước cần thiết để tự kiểm soát ack — nhưng code lại gọi `BasicAckAsync` NGAY khi nhận message, trước khi `await fulfillmentService.FulfillAsync(order)` chạy xong. RabbitMQ chỉ thực sự xoá message khỏi queue khi nhận được ack; ở đây ack được gửi trước, nên broker coi message đã xử lý xong và xoá nó ngay lập tức — bất kể `FulfillAsync` có chạy xong thật hay không. Nếu `FulfillAsync` throw, hoặc process crash đúng lúc đang chạy nó, message đã bị xoá từ trước đó rồi, không còn gì để redeliver — đơn hàng biến mất vĩnh viễn, và vì đây là bug xảy ra ở MỌI lần crash giữa chừng, production thấy đơn hàng \"biến mất\" đều đặn mỗi khi worker restart. Đây là cách phá vỡ at-least-once: guarantee chỉ đứng vững khi invariant \"message chỉ bị xoá SAU KHI consumer xác nhận xử lý xong\" được giữ nguyên — `autoAck: false` chỉ tắt auto-ack ở tầng client, không tự sửa được việc gọi ack thủ công sai chỗ. Cách sửa: di chuyển `await channel.BasicAckAsync(ea.DeliveryTag, false)` xuống SAU khi `await fulfillmentService.FulfillAsync(order)` hoàn tất thành công, bọc trong try/catch — nếu `FulfillAsync` throw thì gọi `channel.BasicNackAsync(ea.DeliveryTag, false, requeue: false)` để route sang dead-letter exchange thay vì ack. Verify bằng cách throw có chủ đích bên trong `FulfillAsync` trước khi ack chạy, rồi assert rằng message được redeliver sang một consumer khác (hoặc rơi vào DLQ) thay vì biến mất.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe bug is in when `channel.BasicAckAsync(ea.DeliveryTag, false)` is called, not in `fulfillmentService.FulfillAsync`'s logic. Even though the consumer declares `autoAck: false` — the right step for taking manual control of acking — the code then calls `BasicAckAsync` IMMEDIATELY on delivery, before `await fulfillmentService.FulfillAsync(order)` has finished. RabbitMQ only actually removes a message from the queue once it receives the ack; here the ack goes out first, so the broker treats the message as processed and deletes it right away — regardless of whether `FulfillAsync` actually completes. If `FulfillAsync` throws, or the process crashes exactly while it is running, the message was already deleted before that point, leaving nothing to redeliver — the order vanishes for good, and because this happens on every crash mid-flight, production sees orders \"disappear\" like clockwork on every worker restart. This is exactly how at-least-once gets broken: the guarantee only holds while the invariant \"a message is deleted only AFTER the consumer confirms it finished processing\" stays intact — `autoAck: false` only turns off client-side auto-ack, it does not fix a manual ack call placed in the wrong spot. The fix: move `await channel.BasicAckAsync(ea.DeliveryTag, false)` to AFTER `await fulfillmentService.FulfillAsync(order)` completes successfully, wrapped in try/catch — if `FulfillAsync` throws, call `channel.BasicNackAsync(ea.DeliveryTag, false, requeue: false)` to route to a dead-letter exchange instead of acking. Verify by deliberately throwing inside `FulfillAsync` before the ack runs, then asserting the message is redelivered to another consumer (or lands in the DLQ) instead of vanishing."},"go":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nBug nằm ở thứ tự gọi `d.Ack(false)`, không phải logic của `fulfillOrder`. Dù `ch.Consume` truyền `autoAck=false` — đúng bước cần thiết để tự kiểm soát ack — nhưng vòng lặp lại gọi `d.Ack(false)` NGAY khi nhận delivery, trước khi `fulfillOrder(order)` chạy xong. RabbitMQ chỉ thực sự xoá message khỏi queue khi nhận được ack; ở đây ack được gửi trước, nên broker coi message đã xử lý xong và xoá nó ngay lập tức, bất kể `fulfillOrder` có chạy xong thật hay không. Nếu `fulfillOrder` panic, hoặc process crash đúng lúc đang chạy nó, message đã bị xoá từ trước đó rồi — không còn gì để redeliver, đơn hàng biến mất vĩnh viễn, và vì đây là bug xảy ra ở MỌI lần crash giữa chừng, production thấy đơn hàng \"biến mất\" đều đặn mỗi khi worker restart. Đây là cách phá vỡ at-least-once: guarantee chỉ đứng vững khi invariant \"message chỉ bị xoá SAU KHI consumer xác nhận xử lý xong\" được giữ nguyên — `autoAck=false` chỉ tắt auto-ack ở tầng client, không tự sửa được việc gọi ack thủ công sai chỗ. Cách sửa: di chuyển `d.Ack(false)` xuống SAU khi `fulfillOrder(order)` chạy xong thành công — dùng `defer`/`recover` hoặc đổi `fulfillOrder` trả về `error` để kiểm tra tường minh — và gọi `d.Nack(false, false)` khi xử lý thất bại để route sang dead-letter exchange thay vì ack. Verify bằng cách cho `fulfillOrder` panic (hoặc trả lỗi) có chủ đích trước khi ack chạy, rồi assert rằng message được redeliver sang một consumer khác (hoặc rơi vào DLQ) thay vì biến mất.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe bug is in when `d.Ack(false)` is called, not in `fulfillOrder`'s logic. Even though `ch.Consume` passes `autoAck=false` — the right step for taking manual control of acking — the loop then calls `d.Ack(false)` IMMEDIATELY on delivery, before `fulfillOrder(order)` has finished. RabbitMQ only actually removes a message from the queue once it receives the ack; here the ack goes out first, so the broker treats the message as processed and deletes it right away, regardless of whether `fulfillOrder` actually completes. If `fulfillOrder` panics, or the process crashes exactly while it is running, the message was already deleted before that point — there is nothing left to redeliver, the order vanishes for good, and because this happens on every crash mid-flight, production sees orders \"disappear\" like clockwork on every worker restart. This is exactly how at-least-once gets broken: the guarantee only holds while the invariant \"a message is deleted only AFTER the consumer confirms it finished processing\" stays intact — `autoAck=false` only turns off client-side auto-ack, it does not fix a manual ack call placed in the wrong spot. The fix: move `d.Ack(false)` to AFTER `fulfillOrder(order)` completes successfully — using `defer`/`recover`, or changing `fulfillOrder` to return an `error` for explicit checking — and call `d.Nack(false, false)` on failure to route to a dead-letter exchange instead of acking. Verify by making `fulfillOrder` panic (or return an error) deliberately before the ack runs, then asserting the message is redelivered to another consumer (or lands in the DLQ) instead of vanishing."}}}$iv$::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (bank_id, bank_version, question_id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO interview_questions (bank_id, bank_version, question_id, sort_index, is_premium, family, tier, kind, tags, competency, leadership_tier, bodies, interviewer, grading)
VALUES ('1-system-design-mastery/5-rabbitmq-and-job-queues', '231d8cc57+37d905d9a', '1-system-design-mastery/5-rabbitmq-and-job-queues/8', 8, false, 'technical', 'senior', 'debug', ARRAY['dead-letter-queue', 'attempts-exhausted', 'transient-vs-permanent']::text[], NULL, NULL, $iv${"typescript":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng TypeScript. Bug: một job được cấu hình 3 lần retry bị dead-letter ngay sau lần thất bại ĐẦU TIÊN (vốn chỉ tạm thời), rồi lại bị dead-letter thêm ở lần 2 và lần 3, khiến DLQ đầy những bản ghi trùng lặp của các job lẽ ra sẽ thành công ở lần retry sau; những lỗi tạm thời thật sự bị xử lý sai thành lỗi vĩnh viễn.","givenCode":"```typescript\nworker.on(\"failed\", async (job: Job | undefined, err: Error) => {\n    if (!job) {\n        return\n    }\n    // Pushes to the DLQ on every failed attempt -- no check against\n    // job.attemptsMade or the job's configured max attempts.\n    await dlqQueue.add(\"dead\", {\n        data: job.data,\n        failedReason: err.message,\n    })\n})\n// A job configured with attempts: 3 is dead-lettered on its FIRST failure,\n// then AGAIN on the 2nd and 3rd -- even if a later attempt succeeds.\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in TypeScript. Bug: a job configured for 3 retries is dead-lettered after its FIRST transient failure, and again on the 2nd and 3rd, so the DLQ fills with duplicates of jobs that would have succeeded on a later retry, and genuinely transient failures are wrongly handled as permanent.","givenCode":"```typescript\nworker.on(\"failed\", async (job: Job | undefined, err: Error) => {\n    if (!job) {\n        return\n    }\n    // Pushes to the DLQ on every failed attempt -- no check against\n    // job.attemptsMade or the job's configured max attempts.\n    await dlqQueue.add(\"dead\", {\n        data: job.data,\n        failedReason: err.message,\n    })\n})\n// A job configured with attempts: 3 is dead-lettered on its FIRST failure,\n// then AGAIN on the 2nd and 3rd -- even if a later attempt succeeds.\n```","diagram":null}},"java":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng Java. Bug: một job được cấu hình 3 lần retry bị dead-letter ngay sau lần thất bại ĐẦU TIÊN (vốn chỉ tạm thời), rồi lại bị dead-letter thêm ở lần 2 và lần 3, khiến DLQ đầy những bản ghi trùng lặp của các job lẽ ra sẽ thành công ở lần retry sau; những lỗi tạm thời thật sự bị xử lý sai thành lỗi vĩnh viễn.","givenCode":"```java\npublic class DlqJobFilter extends JobDefaultFilter {\n    private final RedisCommands<String, String> redis;\n\n    public DlqJobFilter(RedisCommands<String, String> redis) {\n        this.redis = redis;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void onStateElection(Job job, JobState newState) {\n        if (!(newState instanceof FailedState failed)) {\n            return;\n        }\n        // Pushes to the DLQ on every FAILED state -- never checks whether\n        // JobRunr still has retries left, or whether this failure is final.\n        String dead = \"{\\\"data\\\":{\\\"jobId\\\":\\\"\" + job.getId() + \"\\\"},\"\n            + \"\\\"failedReason\\\":\\\"\" + failed.getExceptionMessage() + \"\\\"}\";\n        redis.rpush(\"dlq:email\", dead);\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in Java. Bug: a job configured for 3 retries is dead-lettered after its FIRST transient failure, and again on the 2nd and 3rd, so the DLQ fills with duplicates of jobs that would have succeeded on a later retry, and genuinely transient failures are wrongly handled as permanent.","givenCode":"```java\npublic class DlqJobFilter extends JobDefaultFilter {\n    private final RedisCommands<String, String> redis;\n\n    public DlqJobFilter(RedisCommands<String, String> redis) {\n        this.redis = redis;\n    }\n\n    @Override\n    public void onStateElection(Job job, JobState newState) {\n        if (!(newState instanceof FailedState failed)) {\n            return;\n        }\n        // Pushes to the DLQ on every FAILED state -- never checks whether\n        // JobRunr still has retries left, or whether this failure is final.\n        String dead = \"{\\\"data\\\":{\\\"jobId\\\":\\\"\" + job.getId() + \"\\\"},\"\n            + \"\\\"failedReason\\\":\\\"\" + failed.getExceptionMessage() + \"\\\"}\";\n        redis.rpush(\"dlq:email\", dead);\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"csharp":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng C#. Bug: một job được cấu hình 3 lần retry bị dead-letter ngay sau lần thất bại ĐẦU TIÊN (vốn chỉ tạm thời), rồi lại bị dead-letter thêm ở lần 2 và lần 3, khiến DLQ đầy những bản ghi trùng lặp của các job lẽ ra sẽ thành công ở lần retry sau; những lỗi tạm thời thật sự bị xử lý sai thành lỗi vĩnh viễn.","givenCode":"```csharp\npublic class DlqServerFilter : IServerFilter\n{\n    private readonly IDatabase _redis;\n\n    public DlqServerFilter(IDatabase redis) => _redis = redis;\n\n    public void OnPerforming(PerformingContext context) { }\n\n    public void OnPerformed(PerformedContext context)\n    {\n        if (context.Exception == null) return;\n\n        // Fires after every attempt -- including ones AutomaticRetry will\n        // still retry -- never checks whether this was the job's last attempt.\n        var args = context.BackgroundJob.Job.Args;\n        var to = args.Count > 0 ? args[0]?.ToString() : null;\n        var record = JsonSerializer.Serialize(new\n        {\n            data = new { to },\n            failedReason = context.Exception.Message,\n        });\n        _redis.ListRightPush(\"dlq:email\", record);\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in C#. Bug: a job configured for 3 retries is dead-lettered after its FIRST transient failure, and again on the 2nd and 3rd, so the DLQ fills with duplicates of jobs that would have succeeded on a later retry, and genuinely transient failures are wrongly handled as permanent.","givenCode":"```csharp\npublic class DlqServerFilter : IServerFilter\n{\n    private readonly IDatabase _redis;\n\n    public DlqServerFilter(IDatabase redis) => _redis = redis;\n\n    public void OnPerforming(PerformingContext context) { }\n\n    public void OnPerformed(PerformedContext context)\n    {\n        if (context.Exception == null) return;\n\n        // Fires after every attempt -- including ones AutomaticRetry will\n        // still retry -- never checks whether this was the job's last attempt.\n        var args = context.BackgroundJob.Job.Args;\n        var to = args.Count > 0 ? args[0]?.ToString() : null;\n        var record = JsonSerializer.Serialize(new\n        {\n            data = new { to },\n            failedReason = context.Exception.Message,\n        });\n        _redis.ListRightPush(\"dlq:email\", record);\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"go":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng Go. Bug: một job được cấu hình 3 lần retry bị dead-letter ngay sau lần thất bại ĐẦU TIÊN (vốn chỉ tạm thời), rồi lại bị dead-letter thêm ở lần 2 và lần 3, khiến DLQ đầy những bản ghi trùng lặp của các job lẽ ra sẽ thành công ở lần retry sau; những lỗi tạm thời thật sự bị xử lý sai thành lỗi vĩnh viễn.","givenCode":"```go\nmux.HandleFunc(taskSendEmail, func(ctx context.Context, t *asynq.Task) error {\n    var p emailPayload\n    _ = json.Unmarshal(t.Payload(), &p)\n    time.Sleep(time.Duration(cfg.processDelayMs) * time.Millisecond)\n    if p.ForceFail || rand.Float64() < cfg.failRate {\n        // Pushes to the DLQ on every failed attempt, before the retry chain\n        // even runs -- never checks the retry count against the max.\n        dead, _ := json.Marshal(map[string]any{\"data\": p, \"failedReason\": \"Simulated SMTP failure\"})\n        rdb.RPush(ctx, \"dlq:email\", dead)\n        return errors.New(\"Simulated SMTP failure\")\n    }\n    log.Printf(\"job completed: sent email to %s\", p.To)\n    return nil\n})\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in Go. Bug: a job configured for 3 retries is dead-lettered after its FIRST transient failure, and again on the 2nd and 3rd, so the DLQ fills with duplicates of jobs that would have succeeded on a later retry, and genuinely transient failures are wrongly handled as permanent.","givenCode":"```go\nmux.HandleFunc(taskSendEmail, func(ctx context.Context, t *asynq.Task) error {\n    var p emailPayload\n    _ = json.Unmarshal(t.Payload(), &p)\n    time.Sleep(time.Duration(cfg.processDelayMs) * time.Millisecond)\n    if p.ForceFail || rand.Float64() < cfg.failRate {\n        // Pushes to the DLQ on every failed attempt, before the retry chain\n        // even runs -- never checks the retry count against the max.\n        dead, _ := json.Marshal(map[string]any{\"data\": p, \"failedReason\": \"Simulated SMTP failure\"})\n        rdb.RPush(ctx, \"dlq:email\", dead)\n        return errors.New(\"Simulated SMTP failure\")\n    }\n    log.Printf(\"job completed: sent email to %s\", p.To)\n    return nil\n})\n```","diagram":null}}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"vi":{"followUps":["Nếu cùng một side effect (một alert, một khoản charge, một thông báo) bắn ra mỗi lần job này bị dead-letter sai, thiệt hại thực tế của việc bắn trùng là gì, và bạn sẽ làm side effect đó idempotent thế nào bất kể logic DLQ hoạt động ra sao?","Giả sử job chỉ được cấu hình 1 lần thử (không có retry nào), liệu kiểm tra \"đã cạn số lần thử\" còn ý nghĩa không, hay mọi lần thất bại mặc nhiên trở thành một bản ghi DLQ? Điều đó nói lên gì về khi nào việc cấu hình retry thực sự đáng giá?"],"hints":["Handler đã chạy ở mọi lần thất bại, còn hàng đợi vẫn tự lên lịch retry kèm backoff — vậy điều gì thật sự nên là điều kiện để đẩy lên DLQ, nếu không phải \"cứ thất bại là đẩy\"?","So sánh số lần đã thất bại với số lần thử được cấu hình cho job; chỉ khi hai con số này bằng nhau thì lỗi mới thật sự trở thành vĩnh viễn thay vì tạm thời."]},"en":{"followUps":["If the same side effect (an alert, a charge, a notification) fires every time this job is wrongly dead-lettered, what real damage can duplicate firing cause, and how would you make that side effect idempotent regardless of how the DLQ logic behaves?","Suppose a job is configured with only 1 attempt (no retries at all) — does the \"attempts exhausted\" check still matter, or does every failure become a DLQ entry by definition? What does that tell you about when configuring retries is actually worth it?"],"hints":["The handler already runs on every failed attempt, and the queue keeps re-scheduling a retry with backoff on its own — so what should actually gate pushing to the DLQ, if not \"any failure at all\"?","Compare how many attempts have already failed against how many attempts the job was configured with; only once those two numbers are equal has this failure become permanent rather than transient."]}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"locales":{"vi":{"rubric":["[problemSolving] Phát hiện handler đã cho dead-letter job ở MỌI lần thất bại vì không kiểm tra số lần đã thất bại so với số lần thử tối đa được cấu hình cho job, khiến một lỗi tạm thời bị xử lý như lỗi vĩnh viễn và cùng một job bị đẩy lên DLQ nhiều hơn một lần.","[technical] Nêu đúng cách sửa: chỉ đẩy job lên DLQ khi retry đã cạn (số lần đã thử bằng đúng số lần tối đa được cấu hình); trước thời điểm đó, hàng đợi vẫn đang tự lên lịch retry kèm backoff, nên chỉ những lỗi thật sự vĩnh viễn (poison message) mới nên chạm tới DLQ.","[communication] Giải thích rõ DLQ tồn tại ĐỂ LÀM GÌ — cô lập các lỗi vĩnh viễn/poison để chúng không chặn các job lành mạnh phía sau — và vì sao dead-letter quá sớm phá vỡ mục đích đó, có thể khiến side effect gắn với job bị bắn nhiều hơn một lần.","[testing] Đề xuất bài test cụ thể: một job thất bại vài lần rồi thành công không bao giờ được lên DLQ, còn một job luôn thất bại phải cho ra đúng một bản ghi DLQ khi mọi lần thử được cấu hình đã dùng hết."],"checklist":[{"text":"A BullMQ `worker.on(\"failed\", ...)` listener fires once for every failed attempt of a job, not only on the final one, so it can run several times across a single job's retry lifecycle.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":17},{"text":"Pushing to the dead-letter queue unconditionally inside that listener — without comparing `job.attemptsMade` to the job's configured max attempts — dead-letters the job after its very first failure, while BullMQ's own backoff-driven retry is still running independently in the background.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":17},{"text":"A job configured with `attempts: 3` that fails once and later succeeds still leaves a stale DLQ entry for a failure that ultimately resolved fine, and a job that fails all three times produces three duplicate DLQ entries for what is really one underlying failure.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"The fix is to gate the push behind `if (job.attemptsMade >= max)` where `max = job.opts.attempts ?? 1`, so a job only reaches the DLQ once its retries are truly exhausted; before that point the queue is already handling the retry on its own and the listener has nothing useful to do.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":17},{"text":"A dead-letter queue exists to isolate permanent, poison failures from healthy jobs so they don't clog the queue; dead-lettering a job on its first transient failure defeats that purpose by mixing recoverable blips in with genuinely broken jobs.","dimension":"communication","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"If anything downstream consumes the DLQ — an alert, a replay job, a charge — each premature or duplicate entry re-triggers that consumer, so a side effect tied to the job that isn't idempotent can fire more than once for the same underlying failure.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"Correct behavior can be verified with two cases: a job that fails a couple of times and then succeeds should never produce a DLQ entry, and a job that fails on every attempt should produce exactly one DLQ entry, added only after the final configured attempt is spent.","dimension":"testing","critical":true,"scoreBand":16}],"keywords":["dead-letter queue","attempts exhausted","transient failure","permanent failure","retry backoff"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"The failed listener in BullMQ fires on every single failed attempt, not just the last one, so pushing to the dead-letter queue unconditionally inside it dead-letters the job after its very first failure — while BullMQ's own retry/backoff is still going in the background. That means a job configured with attempts: 3 that fails once and then succeeds still leaves a stale DLQ entry for something that resolved fine, and a job that fails all three times produces three duplicate entries for one real failure. The fix is to check job.attemptsMade against the configured max — job.opts.attempts ?? 1 — and only push once attemptsMade reaches that ceiling. A dead-letter queue exists to isolate genuinely poison jobs from healthy retries, so dead-lettering on the first blip defeats that purpose, and if anything downstream consumes the DLQ — an alert, a replay, a charge — a non-idempotent side effect could fire multiple times for the same underlying failure. I'd verify by testing a job that fails twice then succeeds and confirming no DLQ entry appears, plus a job that fails every attempt and confirming exactly one entry lands after the last attempt.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"The failed event actually fires on every failed attempt, not just the final one, so if we push to the DLQ every time that listener runs without checking how many attempts are left, we're dead-lettering jobs while the retry logic is still working in the background. I'd fix it by comparing job.attemptsMade to job.opts.attempts and only pushing once we've hit that max. It's also worth noting that if something downstream reacts to DLQ entries — sending an alert or triggering a replay — and that action isn't idempotent, we could end up triggering it more than once for what's really one failure. To verify, I'd run a job through a couple of failures and confirm the DLQ stays empty until attempts are actually exhausted.","sonnet":78,"haiku":78},{"level":3,"answer":"It looks like we're pushing to the dead-letter queue every time the failed listener fires, without checking whether the job has any retries left. I think the fix is just to compare attemptsMade against the max attempts configured on the job and only push once that's actually exhausted.","sonnet":34,"haiku":34},{"level":4,"answer":"Maybe we could add a check on attemptsMade before pushing to the DLQ, something like only doing it once it hits the max.","sonnet":17,"haiku":17},{"level":5,"answer":"I'd probably want to add more monitoring around queue depth and maybe set up alerts for when jobs start failing so we can catch these issues faster.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]},"en":{"rubric":["[problemSolving] Spots that the given handler dead-letters on EVERY failed attempt because it never checks the number of failed attempts so far against the job's configured max attempts, so a transient failure is treated as permanent and the same job gets pushed to the DLQ more than once.","[technical] States the fix: only push a job to the DLQ once its retries are exhausted (the number of attempts made equals the configured max); before that point the queue is still self-scheduling retries with backoff on its own, so only genuinely permanent (poison) failures should ever reach the DLQ.","[communication] Explains what a DLQ is FOR — isolating permanent/poison failures so they don't block the healthy jobs behind them — and why dead-lettering too early defeats that purpose and can cause a side effect tied to the job to fire more than once.","[testing] Proposes a concrete test: a job that fails a couple of times then succeeds should never reach the DLQ, and a job that always fails should produce exactly one DLQ entry once every configured attempt is spent."],"checklist":[{"text":"A BullMQ `worker.on(\"failed\", ...)` listener fires once for every failed attempt of a job, not only on the final one, so it can run several times across a single job's retry lifecycle.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":17},{"text":"Pushing to the dead-letter queue unconditionally inside that listener — without comparing `job.attemptsMade` to the job's configured max attempts — dead-letters the job after its very first failure, while BullMQ's own backoff-driven retry is still running independently in the background.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":17},{"text":"A job configured with `attempts: 3` that fails once and later succeeds still leaves a stale DLQ entry for a failure that ultimately resolved fine, and a job that fails all three times produces three duplicate DLQ entries for what is really one underlying failure.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"The fix is to gate the push behind `if (job.attemptsMade >= max)` where `max = job.opts.attempts ?? 1`, so a job only reaches the DLQ once its retries are truly exhausted; before that point the queue is already handling the retry on its own and the listener has nothing useful to do.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":17},{"text":"A dead-letter queue exists to isolate permanent, poison failures from healthy jobs so they don't clog the queue; dead-lettering a job on its first transient failure defeats that purpose by mixing recoverable blips in with genuinely broken jobs.","dimension":"communication","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"If anything downstream consumes the DLQ — an alert, a replay job, a charge — each premature or duplicate entry re-triggers that consumer, so a side effect tied to the job that isn't idempotent can fire more than once for the same underlying failure.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":11},{"text":"Correct behavior can be verified with two cases: a job that fails a couple of times and then succeeds should never produce a DLQ entry, and a job that fails on every attempt should produce exactly one DLQ entry, added only after the final configured attempt is spent.","dimension":"testing","critical":true,"scoreBand":16}],"keywords":["dead-letter queue","attempts exhausted","transient failure","permanent failure","retry backoff"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"The failed listener in BullMQ fires on every single failed attempt, not just the last one, so pushing to the dead-letter queue unconditionally inside it dead-letters the job after its very first failure — while BullMQ's own retry/backoff is still going in the background. That means a job configured with attempts: 3 that fails once and then succeeds still leaves a stale DLQ entry for something that resolved fine, and a job that fails all three times produces three duplicate entries for one real failure. The fix is to check job.attemptsMade against the configured max — job.opts.attempts ?? 1 — and only push once attemptsMade reaches that ceiling. A dead-letter queue exists to isolate genuinely poison jobs from healthy retries, so dead-lettering on the first blip defeats that purpose, and if anything downstream consumes the DLQ — an alert, a replay, a charge — a non-idempotent side effect could fire multiple times for the same underlying failure. I'd verify by testing a job that fails twice then succeeds and confirming no DLQ entry appears, plus a job that fails every attempt and confirming exactly one entry lands after the last attempt.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"The failed event actually fires on every failed attempt, not just the final one, so if we push to the DLQ every time that listener runs without checking how many attempts are left, we're dead-lettering jobs while the retry logic is still working in the background. I'd fix it by comparing job.attemptsMade to job.opts.attempts and only pushing once we've hit that max. It's also worth noting that if something downstream reacts to DLQ entries — sending an alert or triggering a replay — and that action isn't idempotent, we could end up triggering it more than once for what's really one failure. To verify, I'd run a job through a couple of failures and confirm the DLQ stays empty until attempts are actually exhausted.","sonnet":78,"haiku":78},{"level":3,"answer":"It looks like we're pushing to the dead-letter queue every time the failed listener fires, without checking whether the job has any retries left. I think the fix is just to compare attemptsMade against the max attempts configured on the job and only push once that's actually exhausted.","sonnet":34,"haiku":34},{"level":4,"answer":"Maybe we could add a check on attemptsMade before pushing to the DLQ, something like only doing it once it hits the max.","sonnet":17,"haiku":17},{"level":5,"answer":"I'd probably want to add more monitoring around queue depth and maybe set up alerts for when jobs start failing so we can catch these issues faster.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]}},"ideal":{"typescript":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nBug nằm ở handler xử lý sự kiện thất bại, không phải ở engine retry của BullMQ. `worker.on(\"failed\", ...)` fire một lần cho MỌI lần thử thất bại, không chỉ lần cuối, và handler đã cho push lên `dlqQueue` một cách vô điều kiện ở mỗi lần gọi — nó không hề đọc `job.attemptsMade` hay so sánh với `job.opts.attempts` (số lần thử tối đa được cấu hình). Vì vậy một job tạo với `attempts: 3` nhận một bản ghi DLQ ngay sau lần thất bại ĐẦU TIÊN, trong khi BullMQ vẫn đang tự retry nó ở background kèm exponential backoff, hoàn toàn độc lập với listener này; nếu thất bại tiếp thì có bản ghi THỨ HAI, và THỨ BA nếu thất bại lần ba — hoặc, nếu một lần retry sau đó thành công, các bản ghi trước đó vẫn nằm lại như bản trùng của một job thực ra đã xử lý xong. Mọi lỗi tạm thời (một SMTP timeout mà lần retry sau sẽ tự hết) rốt cuộc trông y hệt một lỗi vĩnh viễn, và nếu có gì đó tiêu thụ `dlqQueue` khi nó đầy dần (một alert, một job replay), side effect của cùng một lỗi gốc có thể bị bắn nhiều hơn một lần. Cách sửa: chỉ push khi retry đã thực sự cạn — đọc `const max = job.opts.attempts ?? 1` và chỉ push khi `job.attemptsMade >= max`; trước thời điểm đó, retry tự lên lịch kèm backoff của BullMQ đã đang chạy rồi, nên handler này không cần làm gì thêm. Chỉ một điều kiện đó là toàn bộ khác biệt giữa việc coi lỗi là tạm thời (để nó tự retry) và vĩnh viễn (dead-letter nó). Verify với hai job: một job thất bại đúng hai lần rồi thành công — assert `dlqQueue` không bao giờ nhận bản ghi cho nó — và một job luôn thất bại — assert đúng một bản ghi xuất hiện khi cả 3 lần thử đã dùng hết, không phải ba.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe bug is in the failed-event handler, not in BullMQ's retry engine. `worker.on(\"failed\", ...)` fires once for EVERY failed attempt, not just the last one, and the given handler pushes to `dlqQueue` unconditionally on each call — it never reads `job.attemptsMade` or compares it against `job.opts.attempts` (the configured max). So a job created with `attempts: 3` gets a DLQ entry after its FIRST failure, while BullMQ is still going to retry it in the background with exponential backoff completely independent of this listener; if it fails again it gets a SECOND entry, and a THIRD if it fails a third time — or, if a later attempt actually succeeds, the earlier entries are stale duplicates of a job that resolved fine. Every transient blip (a flaky SMTP timeout the next retry would clear) ends up looking exactly like a permanent failure, and if anything consumes `dlqQueue` as it fills (an alert, a replay job), the same underlying failure's side effects can fire more than once. The fix: only push once retries are truly exhausted — read `const max = job.opts.attempts ?? 1` and gate the push behind `if (job.attemptsMade >= max)`; before that point BullMQ's own backoff-driven retry is already running, so this handler has nothing useful to do. That single condition is the whole difference between treating the failure as transient (let it retry) and permanent (dead-letter it). Verify with two jobs: one that fails exactly twice then succeeds — assert `dlqQueue` never receives an entry for it — and one that always fails — assert exactly one entry appears once all 3 attempts are spent, not three."},"java":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nBug nằm ở `DlqJobFilter`, không phải logic của handler `@Job(retries = 3)`. `onStateElection` fire ở mọi lần chuyển state, và JobRunr bầu một job sang `FailedState` ở MỌI lần thử thất bại — kể cả những lần nó vẫn còn sắp retry — trước khi JobRunr tách riêng quyết định (dựa trên `retries` cấu hình trên `@Job`) có nên reschedule job hay để nó thất bại vĩnh viễn. Filter đã cho coi BẤT KỲ `FailedState` nào cũng là cuối cùng và ghi một bản ghi chết vào `dlq:email` một cách vô điều kiện, nên một job với `retries = 3` nhận một bản ghi DLQ sau lần thất bại ĐẦU TIÊN, thêm một bản sau lần 2, và thêm một bản sau lần 3 nếu vẫn thất bại — hoặc, nếu một lần thử sau đó thành công, các bản ghi trước vẫn nằm lại trong `dlq:email` như bản trùng của một job thực ra đã xong. Mọi lỗi SMTP tạm thời rốt cuộc trông y hệt một lỗi vĩnh viễn. Cách sửa: chỉ ghi bản ghi chết khi job đã thực sự dùng hết số lần retry — đếm số lần job đã chuyển vào `FailedState` (lịch sử thất bại của nó) và so với `retries` cấu hình cho job, chỉ ghi vào `dlq:email` khi con số đó chạm ngưỡng đó; trước đó thì không làm gì và để cơ chế tự lên lịch retry của JobRunr tiếp tục chạy bên dưới. Verify với một job thất bại hai lần rồi thành công — assert `dlq:email` không bao giờ nhận bản ghi cho nó — và một job luôn thất bại — assert đúng một bản ghi xuất hiện trong `dlq:email` khi cả 3 lần retry đã dùng hết, không phải ba.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe bug is in `DlqJobFilter`, not in the `@Job(retries = 3)` handler's logic. `onStateElection` fires on every state transition, and JobRunr elects a job into `FailedState` on EVERY failed attempt — including ones it is still about to retry — before it separately decides, using the `retries` configured on `@Job`, whether to reschedule the job or leave it permanently failed. The given filter treats ANY `FailedState` as final and writes a dead record to `dlq:email` unconditionally, so a job with `retries = 3` gets a DLQ entry after its FIRST failure, another after the 2nd, and another after the 3rd if it keeps failing — or, if a later attempt succeeds, the earlier entries stay in `dlq:email` as stale duplicates of a job that completed fine. Every transient SMTP blip ends up looking exactly like a permanent failure. The fix: only write the dead record once this job has actually run out of retries — track how many `FailedState` transitions this job has already accumulated (its failure history) and compare that count against the job's configured `retries`, writing to `dlq:email` only once the count reaches that ceiling; before that, do nothing and let JobRunr's own retry scheduling continue underneath. Verify with a job that fails twice then succeeds — assert `dlq:email` never gets an entry for it — and one that always fails — assert exactly one entry appears in `dlq:email` once all 3 retries are spent, not three."},"csharp":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nBug nằm ở `DlqServerFilter`, không phải ở bản thân `[AutomaticRetry]`. `IServerFilter.OnPerformed` fire sau MỌI lần thực thi — thành công hay thất bại — nên chỉ kiểm tra `context.Exception == null` vẫn để lọt những lần thử mà `[AutomaticRetry(Attempts = 2, ...)]` còn sắp retry, không chỉ lần cuối. Một job với `Attempts = 2` (2 lần retry sau lần chạy đầu, tổng 3 lần thử) nhận một bản ghi `dlq:email` ngay sau lần thất bại ĐẦU TIÊN — trước cả khi Hangfire kịp áp dụng backoff cho retry — thêm một bản sau lần 2, hoặc, nếu retry sau đó thành công, bản ghi trước vẫn nằm lại như bản trùng lỗi thời. Mọi lỗi tạm thời trông y hệt lỗi vĩnh viễn, và nếu có gì tiêu thụ `dlq:email` khi bản ghi xuất hiện (một alert, một job replay), side effect của cùng lỗi gốc có thể bị bắn nhiều hơn một lần. Cách sửa là chuyển việc ghi DLQ ra khỏi `OnPerformed` và đưa vào đường state-election mà Hangfire đã cung cấp sẵn cho đúng việc này: cài `IElectStateFilter.OnStateElection` và chỉ ghi vào `dlq:email` khi `context.CandidateState is FailedState` — Hangfire chỉ bầu một job sang `FailedState` sau khi `AutomaticRetry` đã cạn toàn bộ số lần thử cấu hình, nên theo cấu trúc đó filter này không thể nào fire sớm. Verify với một job thất bại một lần rồi thành công — assert `dlq:email` vẫn trống — và một job luôn thất bại — assert đúng một bản ghi `dlq:email` xuất hiện khi cả 3 lần thử (lần chạy đầu cộng 2 lần retry) đã dùng hết.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe bug is in `DlqServerFilter`, not in `[AutomaticRetry]` itself. `IServerFilter.OnPerformed` fires after EVERY execution attempt — success or failure — so checking only `context.Exception == null` still lets through attempts that `[AutomaticRetry(Attempts = 2, ...)]` is about to retry, not just the final one. A job with `Attempts = 2` (2 retries after the initial run, 3 attempts total) gets a `dlq:email` record on its FIRST failure — before Hangfire has even applied the retry backoff — another after the 2nd, or, if the retry eventually succeeds, the earlier record just sits there as a stale duplicate. Every transient failure looks permanent, and if anything consumes `dlq:email` as entries land (an alert, a replay job), the same underlying failure's side effects can fire more than once. The fix is to move the DLQ write out of `OnPerformed` and into the state-election path Hangfire already exposes for exactly this: implement `IElectStateFilter.OnStateElection` and write to `dlq:email` only when `context.CandidateState is FailedState` — Hangfire elects a job into `FailedState` only after `AutomaticRetry` has exhausted every configured attempt, so by construction that filter can never fire early. Verify with a job that fails once then succeeds — assert `dlq:email` stays empty — and one that always fails — assert exactly one `dlq:email` entry appears once all 3 attempts (the initial run plus its 2 retries) are spent."},"go":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nBug nằm ở nhánh xử lý thất bại của handler, không phải ở engine retry của asynq. Mỗi lần lỗi giả lập được kích hoạt, handler vừa push một bản ghi chết lên `dlq:email` VỪA vẫn return một error thường — nên chuỗi retry riêng của asynq (`MaxRetry`, exponential backoff) vẫn chạy bên dưới, hoàn toàn độc lập với việc ghi DLQ. Một job enqueue với `asynq.MaxRetry(2)` (tổng 3 lần thử) nhận một bản ghi `dlq:email` ngay ở lần thất bại ĐẦU TIÊN — rất lâu trước khi asynq kịp lên lịch retry có backoff — thêm một bản ở lần 2 và lần 3 nếu vẫn thất bại, hoặc, nếu một lần thử sau đó thành công, bản ghi chết trước đó vẫn nằm lại như bản trùng lỗi thời. Mọi lỗi SMTP tạm thời bị xử lý như vĩnh viễn, và `dlq:email` đầy dần những bản trùng của các job thực ra đã xử lý xong. Cách sửa: đọc số lần đã thử bằng `asynq.GetRetryCount(ctx)` và ngưỡng cấu hình bằng `asynq.GetMaxRetry(ctx)`, chỉ push lên `dlq:email` (rồi `return asynq.SkipRetry` để dừng chuỗi retry) khi `retried >= maxRetry`; trước đó chỉ cần return error và để cơ chế retry tự backoff của asynq chạy. Verify với một job thất bại hai lần rồi thành công — assert `dlq:email` không bao giờ nhận bản ghi cho nó — và một job luôn thất bại — assert đúng một bản ghi `dlq:email` xuất hiện khi cả 3 lần thử đã dùng hết, không phải ba.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThe bug is in the handler's failure branch, not in asynq's retry engine. Every time the simulated failure triggers, the handler pushes a dead record onto `dlq:email` AND still returns a plain error — so asynq's own retry chain (`MaxRetry`, exponential backoff) keeps running underneath, completely independent of the DLQ write. A job enqueued with `asynq.MaxRetry(2)` (3 attempts total) gets a `dlq:email` entry on its FIRST failure — long before asynq has even scheduled the backoff-delayed retry — another on the 2nd and 3rd if it keeps failing, or, if a later attempt succeeds, the earlier dead record just sits there as a stale duplicate. Every transient SMTP blip is treated as permanent, and `dlq:email` fills with duplicates of jobs that would have completed fine. The fix: read the attempt count with `asynq.GetRetryCount(ctx)` and the configured ceiling with `asynq.GetMaxRetry(ctx)`, and only push to `dlq:email` (then `return asynq.SkipRetry` to stop the chain) once `retried >= maxRetry`; before that, just return the error and let asynq's own backoff-driven retry run. Verify with a job that fails twice then succeeds — assert `dlq:email` never receives an entry for it — and one that always fails — assert exactly one `dlq:email` entry appears once all 3 attempts are spent, not three."}}}$iv$::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (bank_id, bank_version, question_id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO interview_questions (bank_id, bank_version, question_id, sort_index, is_premium, family, tier, kind, tags, competency, leadership_tier, bodies, interviewer, grading)
VALUES ('1-system-design-mastery/5-rabbitmq-and-job-queues', '231d8cc57+37d905d9a', '1-system-design-mastery/5-rabbitmq-and-job-queues/9', 9, false, 'technical', 'senior', 'review', ARRAY['check-then-decrement', 'race-condition', 'lua-atomicity']::text[], NULL, NULL, $iv${"typescript":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng TypeScript. Test pass vì test chạy tuần tự từng request một. Smell: khi có concurrency thật, hai request cùng đọc trúng một giá trị cũ, cùng kết luận \"còn trong limit,\" và cùng decrement, khiến client vượt quá limit; một biến thể khác còn giữ counter trong một in-memory map riêng của từng instance, nhân limit lên theo số pod. Hãy review PR này THÀNH TIẾNG như một PR review thật: điều gì bạn đánh dấu là blocker, điều gì chỉ là nitpick, và bạn đề xuất thay bằng gì?","givenCode":"```typescript\n// rate-limiter.service.ts\nexport class RateLimiter {\n  constructor(\n    private readonly redis: Redis,\n    private readonly config: AppConfig,\n  ) {}\n\n  // Two separate round-trips: read the count, decide, then write it back.\n  async checkRate(clientKey: string): Promise<boolean> {\n    const key = `rl:${clientKey}`\n    const current = await this.redis.get(key)\n    const remaining = current === null ? this.config.rateCapacity : parseInt(current, 10)\n\n    if (remaining <= 0) {\n      return false\n    }\n\n    await this.redis.set(key, remaining - 1, \"EX\", 60)\n    return true\n  }\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in TypeScript. Tests pass because they run requests one at a time. Smell: under real concurrency two requests both read the same stale value, both decide \"under the limit,\" and both decrement, so the client exceeds the limit; a variant also keeps the counter in a per-instance in-memory map, multiplying the limit by the pod count. Review this PR OUT LOUD like a real PR review: what do you flag as a blocker, what's just a nitpick, and what would you propose instead?","givenCode":"```typescript\n// rate-limiter.service.ts\nexport class RateLimiter {\n  constructor(\n    private readonly redis: Redis,\n    private readonly config: AppConfig,\n  ) {}\n\n  // Two separate round-trips: read the count, decide, then write it back.\n  async checkRate(clientKey: string): Promise<boolean> {\n    const key = `rl:${clientKey}`\n    const current = await this.redis.get(key)\n    const remaining = current === null ? this.config.rateCapacity : parseInt(current, 10)\n\n    if (remaining <= 0) {\n      return false\n    }\n\n    await this.redis.set(key, remaining - 1, \"EX\", 60)\n    return true\n  }\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"java":{"vi":{"prompt":"PR này cài rate limiter ở tầng application code: nó đọc Redis (GET rl:<id>) để lấy số lượt còn lại, so sánh với limit ngay trong app, rồi ghi RIÊNG một lần nữa (SET/DECR) để lưu số mới. Test pass vì test chạy tuần tự từng request một. Smell: khi có concurrency thật, hai request cùng đọc trúng một giá trị cũ, cùng kết luận \"còn trong limit,\" và cùng decrement, khiến client vượt quá limit; một biến thể khác còn giữ counter trong một in-memory map riêng của từng instance, nhân limit lên theo số pod. Hãy review PR này THÀNH TIẾNG như một PR review thật: điều gì bạn đánh dấu là blocker, điều gì chỉ là nitpick, và bạn đề xuất thay bằng gì?","givenCode":"```java\n// RateLimiterService.java\n@Service\npublic class RateLimiterService {\n\n    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;\n    private final AppProperties props;\n\n    public RateLimiterService(StringRedisTemplate redis, AppProperties props) {\n        this.redis = redis;\n        this.props = props;\n    }\n\n    // Two separate round-trips: read the count, decide, then write it back.\n    public boolean checkRate(String clientKey) {\n        String key = \"rl:\" + clientKey;\n        String current = redis.opsForValue().get(key);\n        long remaining = current == null ? props.getRate().getCapacity() : Long.parseLong(current);\n\n        if (remaining <= 0) {\n            return false;\n        }\n\n        redis.opsForValue().set(key, String.valueOf(remaining - 1), Duration.ofSeconds(60));\n        return true;\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in Java. Tests pass because they run requests one at a time. Smell: under real concurrency two requests both read the same stale value, both decide \"under the limit,\" and both decrement, so the client exceeds the limit; a variant also keeps the counter in a per-instance in-memory map, multiplying the limit by the pod count. Review this PR OUT LOUD like a real PR review: what do you flag as a blocker, what's just a nitpick, and what would you propose instead?","givenCode":"```java\n// RateLimiterService.java\n@Service\npublic class RateLimiterService {\n\n    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;\n    private final AppProperties props;\n\n    public RateLimiterService(StringRedisTemplate redis, AppProperties props) {\n        this.redis = redis;\n        this.props = props;\n    }\n\n    // Two separate round-trips: read the count, decide, then write it back.\n    public boolean checkRate(String clientKey) {\n        String key = \"rl:\" + clientKey;\n        String current = redis.opsForValue().get(key);\n        long remaining = current == null ? props.getRate().getCapacity() : Long.parseLong(current);\n\n        if (remaining <= 0) {\n            return false;\n        }\n\n        redis.opsForValue().set(key, String.valueOf(remaining - 1), Duration.ofSeconds(60));\n        return true;\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"csharp":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng C#. Test pass vì test chạy tuần tự từng request một. Smell: khi có concurrency thật, hai request cùng đọc trúng một giá trị cũ, cùng kết luận \"còn trong limit,\" và cùng decrement, khiến client vượt quá limit; một biến thể khác còn giữ counter trong một in-memory map riêng của từng instance, nhân limit lên theo số pod. Hãy review PR này THÀNH TIẾNG như một PR review thật: điều gì bạn đánh dấu là blocker, điều gì chỉ là nitpick, và bạn đề xuất thay bằng gì?","givenCode":"```csharp\n// RateLimiter.cs\npublic sealed class RateLimiter\n{\n    private readonly IDatabase _redis;\n    private readonly int _capacity;\n\n    public RateLimiter(IConnectionMultiplexer mux, IConfiguration config)\n    {\n        _redis = mux.GetDatabase();\n        _capacity = config.GetValue(\"Rate:Capacity\", 5);\n    }\n\n    // Two separate round-trips: read the count, decide, then write it back.\n    public async Task<bool> CheckRateAsync(string clientKey)\n    {\n        var key = $\"rl:{clientKey}\";\n        var current = await _redis.StringGetAsync(key);\n        var remaining = current.IsNullOrEmpty ? _capacity : (int)current;\n\n        if (remaining <= 0)\n        {\n            return false;\n        }\n\n        await _redis.StringSetAsync(key, remaining - 1, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(60));\n        return true;\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in C#. Tests pass because they run requests one at a time. Smell: under real concurrency two requests both read the same stale value, both decide \"under the limit,\" and both decrement, so the client exceeds the limit; a variant also keeps the counter in a per-instance in-memory map, multiplying the limit by the pod count. Review this PR OUT LOUD like a real PR review: what do you flag as a blocker, what's just a nitpick, and what would you propose instead?","givenCode":"```csharp\n// RateLimiter.cs\npublic sealed class RateLimiter\n{\n    private readonly IDatabase _redis;\n    private readonly int _capacity;\n\n    public RateLimiter(IConnectionMultiplexer mux, IConfiguration config)\n    {\n        _redis = mux.GetDatabase();\n        _capacity = config.GetValue(\"Rate:Capacity\", 5);\n    }\n\n    // Two separate round-trips: read the count, decide, then write it back.\n    public async Task<bool> CheckRateAsync(string clientKey)\n    {\n        var key = $\"rl:{clientKey}\";\n        var current = await _redis.StringGetAsync(key);\n        var remaining = current.IsNullOrEmpty ? _capacity : (int)current;\n\n        if (remaining <= 0)\n        {\n            return false;\n        }\n\n        await _redis.StringSetAsync(key, remaining - 1, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(60));\n        return true;\n    }\n}\n```","diagram":null}},"go":{"vi":{"prompt":"(Minh hoạ bằng Go. Test pass vì test chạy tuần tự từng request một. Smell: khi có concurrency thật, hai request cùng đọc trúng một giá trị cũ, cùng kết luận \"còn trong limit,\" và cùng decrement, khiến client vượt quá limit; một biến thể khác còn giữ counter trong một in-memory map riêng của từng instance, nhân limit lên theo số pod. Hãy review PR này THÀNH TIẾNG như một PR review thật: điều gì bạn đánh dấu là blocker, điều gì chỉ là nitpick, và bạn đề xuất thay bằng gì?","givenCode":"```go\n// rate_limiter.go\n\n// Two separate round-trips: read the count, decide, then write it back.\nfunc (l *RateLimiter) CheckRate(ctx context.Context, clientKey string) (bool, error) {\n    key := \"rl:\" + clientKey\n\n    val, err := l.rdb.Get(ctx, key).Result()\n    remaining := l.capacity\n    if err == nil {\n        remaining, _ = strconv.Atoi(val)\n    }\n\n    if remaining <= 0 {\n        return false, nil\n    }\n\n    err = l.rdb.Set(ctx, key, remaining-1, 60*time.Second).Err()\n    return true, err\n}\n```","diagram":null},"en":{"prompt":"(Illustrated in Go. Tests pass because they run requests one at a time. Smell: under real concurrency two requests both read the same stale value, both decide \"under the limit,\" and both decrement, so the client exceeds the limit; a variant also keeps the counter in a per-instance in-memory map, multiplying the limit by the pod count. Review this PR OUT LOUD like a real PR review: what do you flag as a blocker, what's just a nitpick, and what would you propose instead?","givenCode":"```go\n// rate_limiter.go\n\n// Two separate round-trips: read the count, decide, then write it back.\nfunc (l *RateLimiter) CheckRate(ctx context.Context, clientKey string) (bool, error) {\n    key := \"rl:\" + clientKey\n\n    val, err := l.rdb.Get(ctx, key).Result()\n    remaining := l.capacity\n    if err == nil {\n        remaining, _ = strconv.Atoi(val)\n    }\n\n    if remaining <= 0 {\n        return false, nil\n    }\n\n    err = l.rdb.Set(ctx, key, remaining-1, 60*time.Second).Err()\n    return true, err\n}\n```","diagram":null}}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"vi":{"followUps":["Redis còn có `WATCH`/`MULTI`/`EXEC` (optimistic locking) như một cách khác để làm check-then-write atomic — cách này so với một Lua script duy nhất thì khác nhau ở đâu, và khi nào nên chọn cách nào?","Giả sử limiter giờ cần đếm theo từng cặp (client, endpoint) thay vì chỉ theo client — thiết kế key và input của script sẽ đổi thế nào, và lập luận về atomicity còn đúng nữa không?"],"hints":["Hình dung hai request đến cùng một mili-giây cho cùng một client — lần theo chính xác từng request đọc gì, theo thứ tự nào, và ghi gì; chỗ nào việc đọc-ghi của chúng chồng lên nhau?","Biến thể dùng in-memory map chạy đúng với 1 instance nhưng vỡ với N instance — khi có nhiều hơn một tiến trình mỗi cái giữ một bản sao \"con số đếm\" riêng, thì bản sao nào mới thực sự là limit?"]},"en":{"followUps":["Redis also offers `WATCH`/`MULTI`/`EXEC` (optimistic locking) as another way to make a check-then-write atomic — how does that compare to a single Lua script, and when would you prefer one over the other?","Say the limiter now needs to track requests per (client, endpoint) pair instead of just per client — how does the key design and the script's inputs change, and does the atomicity argument still hold?"],"hints":["Picture two requests landing in the same millisecond for the same client — trace exactly what each one reads, in what order, and what each one writes; where do their reads and writes interleave?","The in-memory-map variant works fine with 1 instance but breaks with N — once more than one process holds its own copy of \"the count,\" whose copy is actually the limit?"]}}$iv$::jsonb, $iv${"locales":{"vi":{"rubric":["[problemSolving] Chỉ ra đúng race condition: check-then-decrement bị tách thành một lần đọc và một lần ghi riêng biệt, nên hai request đồng thời cùng đọc trúng con số cũ và cùng được cho pass — vượt limit khi có tải thật, mà test single-threaded không bao giờ bắt được.","[technical] Đề xuất gộp toàn bộ read-refill-compare-decrement-write vào MỘT Lua script (EVALSHA) để Redis chạy nó single-threaded và atomic, không có chỗ nào chen ngang; đồng thời đưa state về một Redis key dùng chung để mọi instance cùng tuân theo một limit duy nhất.","[communication] Review đúng chất senior: gọi thẳng limiter không atomic là BLOCKER (một bug correctness thật sự khi có concurrency), tách rõ khỏi các nitpick (đặt tên, magic number), và hỏi lại ý định thay vì áp đặt.","[testing] Đề xuất một concurrency test — bắn N request song song rồi assert đúng bằng capacity request thành công — vì test single-threaded hiện tại không thể bắt được lỗi này."],"checklist":[{"text":"Splitting a rate limiter into a separate `redis.get` to read the count and a later `redis.set` to write it back creates a check-then-decrement gap: nothing prevents another request from reading the same stale count in between the two round-trips.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"When two concurrent requests both read `remaining === 1` before either one writes, both pass the `remaining <= 0` check and both set the key to `0` — one request that should have been rejected gets through, and the limit is silently exceeded.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"A test suite that issues requests sequentially can never expose this bug, because there is no interleaving between separate single-threaded calls — the race only appears under real concurrency.","dimension":"testing","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"The underlying fix is to make the whole read-compare-decrement-write sequence a single indivisible operation instead of three separate calls to Redis with the event loop free to interleave other requests in between.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Loading a Lua script once via `redis.script(\"LOAD\", ...)` and invoking it with `redis.evalsha(sha, 1, key, ...)` moves the entire read-decide-write logic onto Redis, which executes Lua scripts single-threaded and atomically, so no other request can slip in between the read and the write.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"Storing the counter in a per-instance in-memory map is a second, independent bug: each pod counts requests on its own, so N replicas behind a load balancer let a client through at N times the intended limit — the counter needs to live in one shared Redis key, not per-instance memory.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"A non-atomic rate limiter is a correctness blocker, not a nitpick, and should be called out as such and kept separate from smaller nitpicks like a hardcoded TTL that could be a named constant.","dimension":"communication","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Verifying an atomicity fix requires a concurrency test that fires many requests in parallel for the same key and asserts exactly the configured capacity of them succeed, since a sequential test structurally cannot exercise the interleaving window.","dimension":"testing","critical":true,"scoreBand":14}],"keywords":["atomic Lua script","check-then-decrement","race condition","shared Redis state","EVALSHA"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"The core bug is that reading the count with redis.get and writing it back later with redis.set are two separate round-trips, so there's a window where two concurrent requests can both read remaining === 1, both pass the check, and both write 0 — letting one request through that should've been rejected. A sequential test suite can never catch this because there's no interleaving between single-threaded calls; it only shows up under real concurrency. The real fix is making the whole read-decide-write sequence one atomic operation, which in Redis means loading a Lua script with SCRIPT LOAD and calling it via EVALSHA, since Redis executes scripts single-threaded with no interleaving possible. There's a second, separate bug too: storing the counter in an in-memory map means each pod counts independently, so N replicas behind a load balancer let a client through N times over the limit — that counter needs to live in a shared Redis key. This is a correctness blocker, not a nitpick, and I'd keep it separate from smaller things like a hardcoded TTL. To verify, I'd fire many concurrent requests at the same key and assert exactly the configured capacity succeeds, since a sequential test structurally can't exercise the race window.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"The problem is that get and set happen as two separate calls to Redis, so two requests can both read the same remaining count before either one writes back, and both end up passing the check and setting it to 0 — so the limit gets exceeded. Moving that logic into a Lua script loaded once and run with evalsha would fix it, since Redis runs scripts atomically on a single thread. There's also a second bug here: the counter is stored in memory per instance, so if there are multiple replicas running, each one tracks its own count and the actual limit ends up multiplied by however many pods there are — that needs to be a shared Redis key instead. This is a real correctness issue, not something to lump in with smaller style nits. To verify it, I'd run a bunch of concurrent requests against the same key and check that exactly the intended number get through.","sonnet":80,"haiku":80},{"level":3,"answer":"I think using a Lua script with evalsha would help here since Redis runs those atomically. Also I noticed the counter is being kept in memory on the instance, which is going to be a problem if there's more than one replica running — that really needs to be in Redis so it's shared. To test it I'd probably fire off a bunch of requests at the same time and see how many actually get through.","sonnet":42,"haiku":42},{"level":4,"answer":"I think we'd want to write a test that sends a bunch of requests at once to check it's working properly.","sonnet":14,"haiku":14},{"level":5,"answer":"I'd suggest adding better error handling around the Redis calls and maybe logging when a client hits the rate limit so we have visibility into it.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]},"en":{"rubric":["[problemSolving] Identifies the race: check-then-decrement is split across a separate read and write, so two concurrent requests both read the stale count and both pass — over the limit under load, invisible to single-threaded tests.","[technical] Proposes packing the whole read-refill-compare-decrement-write into ONE Lua script (EVALSHA) so Redis runs it single-threaded and atomically with no interleave, and putting the state in one shared Redis key so all instances enforce one limit.","[communication] Reviews like a senior: names the non-atomic limiter as a BLOCKER (a real correctness bug under concurrency), separates it from nitpicks (naming, magic numbers), and asks about intent rather than decreeing.","[testing] Proposes a concurrency test — fire N parallel requests and assert exactly capacity succeed — since the current single-threaded test cannot catch this."],"checklist":[{"text":"Splitting a rate limiter into a separate `redis.get` to read the count and a later `redis.set` to write it back creates a check-then-decrement gap: nothing prevents another request from reading the same stale count in between the two round-trips.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"When two concurrent requests both read `remaining === 1` before either one writes, both pass the `remaining <= 0` check and both set the key to `0` — one request that should have been rejected gets through, and the limit is silently exceeded.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"A test suite that issues requests sequentially can never expose this bug, because there is no interleaving between separate single-threaded calls — the race only appears under real concurrency.","dimension":"testing","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"The underlying fix is to make the whole read-compare-decrement-write sequence a single indivisible operation instead of three separate calls to Redis with the event loop free to interleave other requests in between.","dimension":"problemSolving","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Loading a Lua script once via `redis.script(\"LOAD\", ...)` and invoking it with `redis.evalsha(sha, 1, key, ...)` moves the entire read-decide-write logic onto Redis, which executes Lua scripts single-threaded and atomically, so no other request can slip in between the read and the write.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"Storing the counter in a per-instance in-memory map is a second, independent bug: each pod counts requests on its own, so N replicas behind a load balancer let a client through at N times the intended limit — the counter needs to live in one shared Redis key, not per-instance memory.","dimension":"technical","critical":true,"scoreBand":14},{"text":"A non-atomic rate limiter is a correctness blocker, not a nitpick, and should be called out as such and kept separate from smaller nitpicks like a hardcoded TTL that could be a named constant.","dimension":"communication","critical":false,"scoreBand":10},{"text":"Verifying an atomicity fix requires a concurrency test that fires many requests in parallel for the same key and asserts exactly the configured capacity of them succeed, since a sequential test structurally cannot exercise the interleaving window.","dimension":"testing","critical":true,"scoreBand":14}],"keywords":["atomic Lua script","check-then-decrement","race condition","shared Redis state","EVALSHA"],"star":[],"ownershipSignal":null,"exampleResults":[{"level":1,"answer":"The core bug is that reading the count with redis.get and writing it back later with redis.set are two separate round-trips, so there's a window where two concurrent requests can both read remaining === 1, both pass the check, and both write 0 — letting one request through that should've been rejected. A sequential test suite can never catch this because there's no interleaving between single-threaded calls; it only shows up under real concurrency. The real fix is making the whole read-decide-write sequence one atomic operation, which in Redis means loading a Lua script with SCRIPT LOAD and calling it via EVALSHA, since Redis executes scripts single-threaded with no interleaving possible. There's a second, separate bug too: storing the counter in an in-memory map means each pod counts independently, so N replicas behind a load balancer let a client through N times over the limit — that counter needs to live in a shared Redis key. This is a correctness blocker, not a nitpick, and I'd keep it separate from smaller things like a hardcoded TTL. To verify, I'd fire many concurrent requests at the same key and assert exactly the configured capacity succeeds, since a sequential test structurally can't exercise the race window.","sonnet":100,"haiku":100},{"level":2,"answer":"The problem is that get and set happen as two separate calls to Redis, so two requests can both read the same remaining count before either one writes back, and both end up passing the check and setting it to 0 — so the limit gets exceeded. Moving that logic into a Lua script loaded once and run with evalsha would fix it, since Redis runs scripts atomically on a single thread. There's also a second bug here: the counter is stored in memory per instance, so if there are multiple replicas running, each one tracks its own count and the actual limit ends up multiplied by however many pods there are — that needs to be a shared Redis key instead. This is a real correctness issue, not something to lump in with smaller style nits. To verify it, I'd run a bunch of concurrent requests against the same key and check that exactly the intended number get through.","sonnet":80,"haiku":80},{"level":3,"answer":"I think using a Lua script with evalsha would help here since Redis runs those atomically. Also I noticed the counter is being kept in memory on the instance, which is going to be a problem if there's more than one replica running — that really needs to be in Redis so it's shared. To test it I'd probably fire off a bunch of requests at the same time and see how many actually get through.","sonnet":42,"haiku":42},{"level":4,"answer":"I think we'd want to write a test that sends a bunch of requests at once to check it's working properly.","sonnet":14,"haiku":14},{"level":5,"answer":"I'd suggest adding better error handling around the Redis calls and maybe logging when a client hits the rate limit so we have visibility into it.","sonnet":0,"haiku":0}]}},"ideal":{"typescript":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nPR này pass test và chạy được — nhưng \"chạy được\" chỉ chứng minh happy path một request, và đó chính là điều cần nêu ra đầu tiên. Có hai blocker thật sự, không phải nitpick. Thứ nhất, `checkRate` là một check-then-decrement kinh điển bị tách qua hai round-trip: `redis.get(key)` đọc số lượt còn lại, app tự tính `remaining` và tự quyết định ngay trong JavaScript, rồi `redis.set(...)` ghi giá trị mới trở lại — không gì ngăn một request khác đọc trúng CÙNG giá trị cũ ở giữa. Bắn hai request đồng thời khi `remaining === 1`: cả hai lần `get` đều trả về `1`, cả hai đều qua được điều kiện `remaining <= 0`, cả hai đều `set` key về `0` — một request lẽ ra phải bị từ chối đã lọt qua, và client âm thầm vượt quá limit. Điều này vô hình với test hiện tại vì test bắn request tuần tự, nên không bao giờ có sự chồng lấn để lộ ra lỗi. Gốc rễ chung: toàn bộ chuỗi read-decide-write cần là MỘT thao tác không thể chia cắt, còn hiện tại đó là ba lệnh Redis riêng biệt trong khi event loop của JavaScript vẫn rảnh để lên lịch cho request khác chen vào giữa. Cách sửa chính là pattern module này dạy: gộp toàn bộ logic vào một Lua script duy nhất (`token-bucket.lua` của module) load một lần qua `redis.script(\"LOAD\", ...)` rồi gọi bằng `redis.evalsha(sha, 1, key, ...)` — Redis chạy mỗi Lua script single-threaded và atomic, nên đọc, so sánh, và decrement diễn ra như một thao tác duy nhất, không request nào chen được vào giữa. Biến thể in-memory map là blocker thứ hai đi kèm: giữ counter trong một `Map` cục bộ của tiến trình nghĩa là mỗi pod tự đếm riêng, nên 4 replica sau load balancer để lọt client với 4 lần limit dự định — counter phải nằm ở một Redis key dùng chung, không phải bộ nhớ riêng từng instance. Một nitpick không chặn merge đi kèm: TTL hard-code `\"EX\", 60` nên đặt tên constant thay vì magic number.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThis PR passes tests and runs — but \"it runs\" only proves the single-request happy path, and that's exactly what to raise first. There are two real blockers, not nitpicks. First, `checkRate` is a classic check-then-decrement split across two round-trips: `redis.get(key)` reads the count, the app computes `remaining` and decides in JavaScript, then `redis.set(...)` writes the new value back — nothing stops another request from reading the SAME stale count in between. Fire two concurrent requests when `remaining === 1`: both `get` calls return `1`, both pass the `remaining <= 0` check, both `set` the key to `0` — one request that should have been rejected got through, and the client silently exceeded the limit. This is invisible to the current tests because they issue requests sequentially, so there is never an interleaving to expose. The common root: the whole read-decide-write sequence needs to be ONE indivisible operation, and right now it is three separate calls to Redis with the JavaScript event loop free to schedule other requests in between. The fix is exactly the pattern this module teaches: move the entire logic into a single Lua script (the module's `token-bucket.lua`) loaded once via `redis.script(\"LOAD\", ...)` and invoked with `redis.evalsha(sha, 1, key, ...)` — Redis executes each Lua script single-threaded and atomically, so read, compare, and decrement happen as one operation with no other request able to slip in between. The in-memory-map variant is a second blocker on top: keeping the counter in a process-local `Map` means each pod counts independently, so 4 replicas behind a load balancer let a client through at 4x the intended limit — the counter has to live in one shared Redis key, not per-instance memory. One non-blocking nitpick to mention alongside: the hardcoded `\"EX\", 60` TTL could be a named constant instead of a magic number."},"java":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nPR này pass test và chạy được — nhưng \"chạy được\" chỉ chứng minh happy path một request, và đó chính là điều cần nêu ra đầu tiên. Có hai blocker thật sự, không phải nitpick. Thứ nhất, `checkRate` là một check-then-decrement kinh điển bị tách qua hai round-trip: `redis.opsForValue().get(key)` đọc số lượt còn lại qua một connection, thread tự tính `remaining` và tự quyết định ngay trong Java thuần, rồi `redis.opsForValue().set(...)` ghi giá trị mới trở lại qua một connection khác — không gì ngăn một thread khác (hay một pod khác) đọc trúng CÙNG giá trị cũ ở giữa. Bắn hai request đồng thời khi `remaining == 1`: cả hai lần `get` đều trả về `\"1\"`, cả hai đều qua được điều kiện `remaining <= 0`, cả hai đều `set` key về `\"0\"` — một request lẽ ra phải bị từ chối đã lọt qua, và client âm thầm vượt quá limit. Điều này vô hình với test hiện tại vì test bắn request tuần tự, nên không bao giờ có sự chồng lấn để lộ ra lỗi. Gốc rễ chung: toàn bộ chuỗi read-decide-write cần là MỘT thao tác không thể chia cắt, còn hiện tại đó là hai round-trip Redis riêng biệt mà không có lock hay transaction nào bao trùm. Cách sửa chính là pattern module này dạy: gộp toàn bộ logic vào một Lua script duy nhất (`token-bucket.lua` của module) bọc thành `RedisScript<List>` load một lần qua `RedisScript.of(src, List.class)` rồi thực thi bằng `redis.execute(tokenBucket, Collections.singletonList(key), ...)` — Redis chạy mỗi Lua script single-threaded và atomic, nên đọc, so sánh, và decrement diễn ra như một thao tác duy nhất, không request nào chen được vào giữa. Biến thể in-memory map là blocker thứ hai đi kèm: giữ counter trong một field `ConcurrentHashMap` nghĩa là mỗi instance JVM tự đếm riêng, nên 4 replica sau load balancer để lọt client với 4 lần limit dự định — counter phải nằm ở một Redis key dùng chung, không phải heap memory riêng từng instance. Một nitpick không chặn merge đi kèm: TTL hard-code `Duration.ofSeconds(60)` nên đặt tên constant thay vì magic number.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThis PR passes tests and runs — but \"it runs\" only proves the single-request happy path, and that's exactly what to raise first. There are two real blockers, not nitpicks. First, `checkRate` is a classic check-then-decrement split across two round-trips: `redis.opsForValue().get(key)` reads the count over one connection, the thread computes `remaining` and decides in plain Java, then `redis.opsForValue().set(...)` writes the new value back over a second connection — nothing stops another thread (or another pod) from reading the SAME stale count in between. Fire two concurrent requests when `remaining == 1`: both `get` calls return `\"1\"`, both pass the `remaining <= 0` check, both `set` the key to `\"0\"` — one request that should have been rejected got through, and the client silently exceeded the limit. This is invisible to the current tests because they issue requests sequentially, so there is never an interleaving to expose. The common root: the whole read-decide-write sequence needs to be ONE indivisible operation, and right now it is two separate round-trips to Redis with no lock or transaction spanning them. The fix is exactly the pattern this module teaches: move the entire logic into a single Lua script (the module's `token-bucket.lua`) wrapped as a `RedisScript<List>` loaded once via `RedisScript.of(src, List.class)` and executed with `redis.execute(tokenBucket, Collections.singletonList(key), ...)` — Redis executes each Lua script single-threaded and atomically, so read, compare, and decrement happen as one operation with no other request able to slip in between. The in-memory-map variant is a second blocker on top: keeping the counter in a `ConcurrentHashMap` field means each JVM instance counts independently, so 4 replicas behind a load balancer let a client through at 4x the intended limit — the counter has to live in one shared Redis key, not per-instance heap memory. One non-blocking nitpick to mention alongside: the hardcoded `Duration.ofSeconds(60)` TTL could be a named constant instead of a magic number."},"csharp":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nPR này pass test và chạy được — nhưng \"chạy được\" chỉ chứng minh happy path một request, và đó chính là điều cần nêu ra đầu tiên. Có hai blocker thật sự, không phải nitpick. Thứ nhất, `CheckRateAsync` là một check-then-decrement kinh điển bị tách qua hai round-trip: `_redis.StringGetAsync(key)` đọc số lượt còn lại, app tự tính `remaining` và tự quyết định ở phía .NET, rồi `_redis.StringSetAsync(...)` ghi giá trị mới trở lại — không gì ngăn một request khác đọc trúng CÙNG giá trị cũ ở giữa. Bắn hai request đồng thời khi `remaining == 1`: cả hai lần `StringGetAsync` đều trả về `1`, cả hai đều qua được điều kiện `remaining <= 0`, cả hai đều `StringSetAsync` key về `0` — một request lẽ ra phải bị từ chối đã lọt qua, và client âm thầm vượt quá limit. Điều này vô hình với test hiện tại vì test bắn request tuần tự, nên không bao giờ có sự chồng lấn để lộ ra lỗi. Gốc rễ chung: toàn bộ chuỗi read-decide-write cần là MỘT thao tác không thể chia cắt, còn hiện tại đó là hai round-trip Redis riêng biệt mà không có lock hay transaction nào bao trùm. Cách sửa chính là pattern module này dạy: gộp toàn bộ logic vào một Lua script duy nhất (`token-bucket.lua` của module) load một lần qua `IServer.ScriptLoadAsync` rồi gọi bằng `_redis.ScriptEvaluateAsync(luaSha, new RedisKey[] { key }, ...)` — Redis chạy mỗi Lua script single-threaded và atomic, nên đọc, so sánh, và decrement diễn ra như một thao tác duy nhất, không request nào chen được vào giữa. Biến thể in-memory map là blocker thứ hai đi kèm: giữ counter trong một field `ConcurrentDictionary` nghĩa là mỗi tiến trình instance tự đếm riêng, nên 4 replica sau load balancer để lọt client với 4 lần limit dự định — counter phải nằm ở một Redis key dùng chung, không phải bộ nhớ riêng từng tiến trình. Một nitpick không chặn merge đi kèm: TTL hard-code `TimeSpan.FromSeconds(60)` nên đặt tên constant thay vì magic number.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThis PR passes tests and runs — but \"it runs\" only proves the single-request happy path, and that's exactly what to raise first. There are two real blockers, not nitpicks. First, `CheckRateAsync` is a classic check-then-decrement split across two round-trips: `_redis.StringGetAsync(key)` reads the count, the app computes `remaining` and decides on the .NET side, then `_redis.StringSetAsync(...)` writes the new value back — nothing stops another request from reading the SAME stale count in between. Fire two concurrent requests when `remaining == 1`: both `StringGetAsync` calls return `1`, both pass the `remaining <= 0` check, both `StringSetAsync` the key to `0` — one request that should have been rejected got through, and the client silently exceeded the limit. This is invisible to the current tests because they issue requests sequentially, so there is never an interleaving to expose. The common root: the whole read-decide-write sequence needs to be ONE indivisible operation, and right now it is two separate round-trips to Redis with no lock or transaction spanning them. The fix is exactly the pattern this module teaches: move the entire logic into a single Lua script (the module's `token-bucket.lua`) loaded once via `IServer.ScriptLoadAsync` and invoked with `_redis.ScriptEvaluateAsync(luaSha, new RedisKey[] { key }, ...)` — Redis executes each Lua script single-threaded and atomically, so read, compare, and decrement happen as one operation with no other request able to slip in between. The in-memory-map variant is a second blocker on top: keeping the counter in a `ConcurrentDictionary` field means each process instance counts independently, so 4 replicas behind a load balancer let a client through at 4x the intended limit — the counter has to live in one shared Redis key, not per-instance process memory. One non-blocking nitpick to mention alongside: the hardcoded `TimeSpan.FromSeconds(60)` TTL could be a named constant instead of a magic number."},"go":{"vi":":::muted\nChốt\n:::\nPR này pass test và chạy được — nhưng \"chạy được\" chỉ chứng minh happy path một request, và đó chính là điều cần nêu ra đầu tiên. Có hai blocker thật sự, không phải nitpick. Thứ nhất, `CheckRate` là một check-then-decrement kinh điển bị tách qua hai round-trip: `l.rdb.Get(ctx, key)` đọc số lượt còn lại, goroutine tự tính `remaining` và tự quyết định ngay trong Go thuần, rồi `l.rdb.Set(...)` ghi giá trị mới trở lại — không gì ngăn một goroutine khác (hay một pod khác) đọc trúng CÙNG giá trị cũ ở giữa. Bắn hai request đồng thời khi `remaining == 1`: cả hai lần `Get` đều trả về `\"1\"`, cả hai đều qua được điều kiện `remaining <= 0`, cả hai đều `Set` key về `0` — một request lẽ ra phải bị từ chối đã lọt qua, và client âm thầm vượt quá limit. Điều này vô hình với test hiện tại vì test bắn request tuần tự, nên không bao giờ có sự chồng lấn để lộ ra lỗi. Gốc rễ chung: toàn bộ chuỗi read-decide-write cần là MỘT thao tác không thể chia cắt, còn hiện tại đó là hai round-trip Redis riêng biệt mà không có gì bao trùm. Cách sửa chính là pattern module này dạy: gộp toàn bộ logic vào một Lua script duy nhất (`token-bucket.lua` của module, nhúng qua `//go:embed`), bọc thành `redis.NewScript(tokenBucketSrc)` rồi chạy bằng `script.Run(ctx, l.rdb, []string{key}, ...)` — go-redis tự cache SHA và dùng `EVALSHA`, còn Redis chạy mỗi Lua script single-threaded và atomic, nên đọc, so sánh, và decrement diễn ra như một thao tác duy nhất, không request nào chen được vào giữa. Biến thể in-memory map là blocker thứ hai đi kèm: giữ counter trong một `map[string]int` cục bộ của tiến trình (dù có `sync.Mutex` bảo vệ) nghĩa là mỗi replica tự đếm riêng, nên 4 pod sau load balancer để lọt client với 4 lần limit dự định — counter phải nằm ở một Redis key dùng chung, không phải bộ nhớ riêng từng tiến trình. Một nitpick không chặn merge đi kèm: TTL hard-code `60*time.Second` nên đặt tên constant thay vì magic number.","en":":::muted\nBottom line\n:::\nThis PR passes tests and runs — but \"it runs\" only proves the single-request happy path, and that's exactly what to raise first. There are two real blockers, not nitpicks. First, `CheckRate` is a classic check-then-decrement split across two round-trips: `l.rdb.Get(ctx, key)` reads the count, the goroutine computes `remaining` and decides in plain Go, then `l.rdb.Set(...)` writes the new value back — nothing stops another goroutine (or another pod) from reading the SAME stale count in between. Fire two concurrent requests when `remaining == 1`: both `Get` calls return `\"1\"`, both pass the `remaining <= 0` check, both `Set` the key to `0` — one request that should have been rejected got through, and the client silently exceeded the limit. This is invisible to the current tests because they issue requests sequentially, so there is never an interleaving to expose. The common root: the whole read-decide-write sequence needs to be ONE indivisible operation, and right now it is two separate round-trips to Redis with nothing spanning them. The fix is exactly the pattern this module teaches: move the entire logic into a single Lua script (the module's `token-bucket.lua`, embedded via `//go:embed`), wrapped as a `redis.NewScript(tokenBucketSrc)` and run with `script.Run(ctx, l.rdb, []string{key}, ...)` — go-redis caches the SHA and uses `EVALSHA` automatically, and Redis executes each Lua script single-threaded and atomically, so read, compare, and decrement happen as one operation with no other request able to slip in between. The in-memory-map variant is a second blocker on top: keeping the counter in a process-local `map[string]int` (even guarded by a `sync.Mutex`) means each replica counts independently, so 4 pods behind a load balancer let a client through at 4x the intended limit — the counter has to live in one shared Redis key, not per-instance process memory. One non-blocking nitpick to mention alongside: the hardcoded `60*time.Second` TTL could be a named constant instead of a magic number."}}}$iv$::jsonb)
